window.DEEP_STORIES["electricity"] = {"slug":"electricity","title":"Electricity & the Light Bulb","emoji":"💡","tagline":"From crackling amber to glowing cities: how humans tamed lightning","intro":"Flip a switch and the room fills with light. It feels like nothing, but it took people more than two thousand years to get there. In this Deep Dive you will travel from ancient Greeks rubbing amber to make straw jump, through daring experimenters who stored sparks in glass jars and flew kites in thunderstorms, to Alessandro Volta's first battery and Michael Faraday's magical spinning magnets. Then comes the great light bulb race, where Joseph Swan, Thomas Edison and many others battled to make a glow that would not burn out. You will see the first power stations switch on, watch Edison, Tesla and Westinghouse fight the War of the Currents, and follow the wires as they spread into a giant grid that changed homes, factories and whole countries. Finally we look at the problems electricity brought, the tiny LEDs that replaced old bulbs, and the wind and sunshine that may power our future. Twenty parts. Let's light it up!","outline":[{"n":1,"title":"A World Lit by Flames","when":"Prehistory – 1870s"},{"n":2,"title":"Amber, Electric Fish and Rubbing Machines","when":"about 600 BCE – 1740s"},{"n":3,"title":"Bottled Lightning and Benjamin Franklin","when":"1745 – 1760s"},{"n":4,"title":"Frog Legs and the First Battery","when":"1780 – 1801"},{"n":5,"title":"Davy's Dazzling Arc and Ørsted's Needle","when":"1800 – 1827"},{"n":6,"title":"Faraday's Magic Ring","when":"1831 – 1880s"},{"n":7,"title":"Electricity Goes to Work","when":"1837 – 1879"},{"n":8,"title":"The Long Race to a Gentle Glow","when":"1802 – 1879"},{"n":9,"title":"Joseph Swan Lights Up Newcastle","when":"1850 – 1883"},{"n":10,"title":"Edison's Invention Factory","when":"1876 – 1892"},{"n":11,"title":"Inside the Bulb: How It Works","when":"Science + 1897 – 1925"},{"n":12,"title":"The First Power Stations","when":"1878 – 1890"},{"n":13,"title":"AC, DC and the Magic of Transformers","when":"Science + 1881 – 1886"},{"n":14,"title":"The War of the Currents","when":"1884 – 1896"},{"n":15,"title":"Wiring the World: The Grid","when":"1889 – 1960s"},{"n":16,"title":"Switched On: How Life Changed","when":"1879 – 1960s"},{"n":17,"title":"Shocks, Smoke and Blackouts","when":"1880s – today"},{"n":18,"title":"Beyond the Filament: From Neon to LED","when":"1907 – today"},{"n":19,"title":"Power from Sun, Wind and Water","when":"1839 – today"},{"n":20,"title":"The Electric Future — and the Whole Story","when":"Today – the future"}],"people":[["Thales of Miletus (about 624 – about 546 BCE)","Greek thinker traditionally said to have noticed rubbed amber attracting objects; none of his writings survive"],["Scribonius Largus (about 1 – about 50 CE)","Roman doctor who recorded using shocks from torpedo rays as a treatment"],["William Gilbert (1544–1603)","English physician who wrote De Magnete (1600) and coined 'electricus'"],["Otto von Guericke (1602–1686)","German scientist and mayor of Magdeburg who built an early friction machine with a sulfur ball"],["Stephen Gray (1666–1736)","English dyer and amateur scientist who discovered that electricity travels through conductors"],["Charles François de Cisternay du Fay (1698–1739)","French chemist who found two kinds of electricity"],["Ewald Georg von Kleist (1700–1748)","German cleric who invented a charge-storing jar in 1745"],["Pieter van Musschenbroek (1692–1761)","Dutch professor in Leiden whose 1746 jar became known as the Leyden jar"],["Jean-Antoine Nollet (1700–1770)","French abbé and showman of electrical experiments"],["Benjamin Franklin (1706–1790)","American printer, scientist and statesman; showed lightning is electrical and invented the lightning rod"],["Thomas-François Dalibard (1709–1778)","French naturalist who first carried out Franklin's lightning experiment (May 1752)"],["Georg Wilhelm Richmann (1711–1753)","Baltic German physicist in St Petersburg killed while studying lightning"],["Luigi Galvani (1737–1798)","Italian doctor whose frog-leg experiments led to the idea of 'animal electricity'"],["Alessandro Volta (1745–1827)","Italian physicist who invented the voltaic pile, the first battery giving steady current (1800)"],["William Nicholson (1753–1815) and Anthony Carlisle (1768–1840)","English scientists who first split water with electricity (1800)"],["Humphry Davy (1778–1829)","English chemist who discovered elements by electrolysis and demonstrated the arc light"],["Hans Christian Ørsted (1777–1851)","Danish physicist who discovered that electric current affects a compass (1820)"],["André-Marie Ampère (1775–1836)","French physicist who described the laws of electric currents and magnetism"],["Georg Simon Ohm (1789–1854)","German physicist who discovered the relationship between voltage, current and resistance"],["William Sturgeon (1783–1850)","English inventor of the electromagnet (1825)"],["Joseph Henry (1797–1878)","American scientist who built powerful electromagnets and discovered induction independently"],["Michael Faraday (1791–1867)","English scientist who invented the first electric motor and discovered electromagnetic induction"],["Hippolyte Pixii (1808–1835)","French instrument maker who built an early hand-cranked generator (1832)"],["James Clerk Maxwell (1831–1879)","Scottish physicist whose equations united electricity, magnetism and light"],["Heinrich Hertz (1857–1894)","German physicist who first made and detected radio waves"],["William Fothergill Cooke (1806–1879) and Charles Wheatstone (1802–1875)","British inventors of an early electric telegraph"],["Samuel Morse (1791–1872)","American painter and inventor who developed Morse code and a telegraph system"],["Werner von Siemens (1816–1892)","German engineer and industrialist who developed the self-exciting dynamo and electric railways"],["Zénobe Gramme (1826–1901)","Belgian inventor of the ring dynamo"],["Pavel Yablochkov (1847–1894)","Russian engineer who invented the 'electric candle' arc lamp"],["Charles F. Brush (1849–1929)","American inventor of arc lighting systems and an early wind turbine"],["Warren de la Rue (1815–1889)","British astronomer and chemist who made an early platinum incandescent lamp"],["Frederick de Moleyns (1804–1854)","received the first British patent for an incandescent lamp (1841)"],["John Wellington Starr (about 1822–1846)","American who experimented with carbon lamps in a vacuum"],["Alexander Lodygin (1847–1923)","Russian engineer who patented a carbon incandescent lamp"],["Henry Woodward and Mathew Evans (active 1870s)","Canadians who patented an incandescent lamp in 1874"],["Hermann Sprengel (1834–1906)","German-born chemist who invented the mercury vacuum pump"],["Joseph Swan (1828–1914)","English chemist who developed a practical carbon-filament lamp and lit the first homes and theatre"],["Charles Stearn (active 1870s)","helped Swan achieve better vacuums in his bulbs"],["William Armstrong (1810–1900)","English industrialist whose house, Cragside, was lit by hydroelectric power and Swan lamps"],["Richard D'Oyly Carte (1844–1901)","theatre owner who lit the Savoy Theatre with electricity"],["Thomas Edison (1847–1931)","American inventor who led the team that made a long-lasting practical bulb and a complete electric lighting system"],["Francis Upton (1852–1921)","mathematician on Edison's team who calculated how the lighting system should work"],["Charles Batchelor (1845–1910)","Edison's chief experimental assistant"],["John Kruesi (1843–1899)","Swiss-born machinist who built many of Edison's devices"],["Ludwig Böhm (active 1870s–1880s)","German glassblower who made Edison's early bulbs"],["Lewis Latimer (1848–1928)","African American inventor and draftsman who improved carbon filament manufacturing"],["Hiram Maxim (1840–1916)","American-born inventor who ran a rival lighting company"],["J. J. Thomson (1856–1940)","British physicist who discovered the electron (1897)"],["Alexander Just (1872–1937) and Franjo Hanaman (1878–1941)","inventors of the tungsten filament lamp (1904)"],["William D. Coolidge (1873–1975)","American engineer who made tungsten into bendable wire for filaments"],["Irving Langmuir (1881–1957)","American chemist who invented the gas-filled coiled-filament lamp"],["Lucien Gaulard (1850–1888) and John Dixon Gibbs (1834–1912)","developed an early AC transformer system"],["Károly Zipernowsky (1853–1942), Ottó Bláthy (1860–1939) and Miksa Déri (1854–1938)","Hungarian engineers at Ganz who built practical closed-core transformers"],["William Stanley (1858–1916)","American engineer who built the first practical AC lighting system in the USA (1886)"],["George Westinghouse (1846–1914)","American industrialist who championed AC power"],["Nikola Tesla (1856–1943)","Serbian-American inventor of the AC induction motor and polyphase system"],["Galileo Ferraris (1847–1897)","Italian physicist who independently invented an induction motor"],["Harold P. Brown (1857–1944)","American engineer who campaigned against AC with cruel public demonstrations"],["Mikhail Dolivo-Dobrovolsky (1862–1919)","Russian engineer who developed three-phase AC equipment"],["Oskar von Miller (1855–1934)","German engineer who organised the Lauffen–Frankfurt power line"],["Sebastian Ziani de Ferranti (1864–1930)","British engineer who designed the high-voltage Deptford power station"],["Charles Parsons (1854–1931)","Anglo-Irish engineer who invented the modern steam turbine"],["Frank J. Sprague (1857–1934)","American engineer who built the first large successful electric tram system"],["Hubert Cecil Booth (1871–1955)","British engineer who invented the powered vacuum cleaner"],["Wilhelm Röntgen (1845–1923)","German physicist who discovered X-rays"],["Georges Claude (1870–1960)","French engineer who invented neon lighting"],["Edmund Germer (1901–1987)","German inventor who co-patented the fluorescent lamp"],["Edward Hammer (1931–2012)","American engineer who designed the spiral compact fluorescent lamp"],["Henry Joseph Round (1881–1966)","English engineer who first reported light from a semiconductor (1907)"],["Oleg Losev (1903–1942)","Russian scientist who studied light-emitting diodes in the 1920s"],["Nick Holonyak Jr. (1928–2022)","American engineer who made the first visible-light LED"],["Isamu Akasaki (1929–2021), Hiroshi Amano (born 1960) and Shuji Nakamura (born 1954)","Japanese-born scientists who made efficient blue LEDs"],["Edmond Becquerel (1820–1891)","French physicist who discovered the photovoltaic effect"],["Charles Fritts (1850–1903)","American inventor who made early selenium solar cells"],["James Blyth (1839–1906)","Scottish engineer who built one of the first electricity-making wind turbines"],["John B. Goodenough (1922–2023), M. Stanley Whittingham (born 1941) and Akira Yoshino (born 1948)","chemists who developed the lithium-ion battery"]],"glossary":[["static electricity","electric charge that builds up on a surface and stays there until it jumps away as a spark"],["charge","a property of tiny particles; positive and negative charges attract, and like charges push apart"],["electron","a tiny negatively charged particle in every atom; moving electrons carry current in metal wires"],["conductor","a material electricity flows through easily, like copper"],["insulator","a material that blocks electricity, like rubber, glass or plastic"],["circuit","a complete loop that electricity can flow around"],["current","the flow of electric charge, measured in amps (A)"],["voltage","the 'push' that drives current, measured in volts (V)"],["resistance","how much a material holds back current, measured in ohms (Ω)"],["battery","a device that turns chemical energy into electrical energy (Franklin first used the word for a row of Leyden jars)"],["electromagnet","a coil of wire that becomes a magnet when current flows through it"],["electromagnetic induction","making electricity by moving a magnet and a wire relative to each other (or by changing a magnetic field)"],["generator (dynamo)","a machine that turns motion into electricity using induction"],["filament","the thin wire inside an incandescent bulb that glows when hot"],["incandescence","glowing because something is very hot"],["direct current (DC)","electricity that flows in one direction only"],["alternating current (AC)","electricity that switches direction many times a second"],["transformer","a device with two coils that raises or lowers AC voltage"],["grid","the network of power stations, power lines and substations that delivers electricity"],["LED (light-emitting diode)","a tiny semiconductor chip that gives off light without a hot filament"]],"parts":[{"n":1,"title":"A World Lit by Flames","when":"Prehistory – 1870s","summary":"Long ago, when the Sun went down, the world went dark. People lit their homes with fires, smoky candles, smelly whale-oil lamps and flickering gas jets. Every light was a tiny fire, and every fire could be dangerous.","sections":[{"heading":"When the Sun Went Down","paragraphs":["Imagine it is a winter evening in a farming village in England about three hundred years ago. It is only five o'clock, but the Sun has already slipped behind the hills. There are no streetlights. There is no glow from the next town on the horizon. Unless the Moon is up, the lane outside your door is so dark that you cannot see your own hand. If you need to cross the yard to check on the animals, you carry a lantern with a single candle inside it, and the circle of light it makes is about the size of a kitchen table.","Indoors, the brightest thing in the room is the fire in the hearth. Your family sits close to it, partly for warmth and partly because it is the only place where you can see well enough to mend a sock or whittle a stick. Your mother might light one candle for the evening, but candles cost money, so she will blow it out the moment the work is done. Reading a book at night is a luxury. Most people simply went to bed early, and in the darkest months of the year that could mean a very long night indeed.","For almost all of human history, this was normal. The day was for working and the night was for sleeping, because there was very little else you could do in the dark. Farmers, fishers and travellers planned their lives around the Moon. A full Moon meant you could walk home safely after a market or a wedding. A new Moon meant you stayed in. Towns had night watchmen who walked the streets calling out the hour, and in many cities people who went out after dark were expected to carry their own light.","Darkness was not just inconvenient. It was dangerous. People tripped down stairs, fell into ditches and rivers, and were robbed in unlit alleys. And the only way to fight the dark was to set something on fire. Every candle, every lamp and every torch was a small, open flame, sitting in houses built of wood, thatch and cloth. When a candle was knocked over in the night, a whole street could burn.","In this first part of our story we will meet all the different flames people used to push back the night, from Ice Age lamps burning animal fat to the gas lights and kerosene lamps of the 1800s. Each one was a little better than the last. But each one was still fire. Keep that in mind, because the real hero of this Deep Dive is a strange, invisible force that could one day make light with no flame at all."]},{"heading":"Fire, Fat and the First Lamps","paragraphs":["The very first artificial light was the campfire. Our ancestors were using fire tens of thousands of years ago, and probably much longer. A fire gave warmth, cooked food, scared away animals and let a group of people see each other's faces after sunset. A burning branch pulled from the fire became the first torch, a light you could carry.","Torches have a problem, though. They burn quickly, drip, spark and smoke, and they are hard to use inside a cave or hut without filling it with fumes. So people invented something cleverer: the lamp. A lamp is simply a container that holds fuel, usually fat or oil, with a wick that soaks up the fuel and burns slowly at its tip. Instead of burning a whole stick at once, you burn a little bit of fuel at a time.","Archaeologists have found curved stone lamps from more than ten thousand years ago. One famous example was discovered in 1940 at Lascaux in France, the cave whose walls are covered in paintings of horses, deer and bulls. Ice Age artists needed light to paint deep underground, and hollowed-out stones filled with animal fat, with a wick of moss or plant fibre, gave them a steady, smoky glow. Picture an artist kneeling in the cold, the lamp spluttering beside them, painting a galloping horse by a light about as bright as a birthday candle.","Some archaeologists think people also used seashells as lamps more than six thousand years ago. A shell makes a perfect little bowl for oil. In the ancient Sumerian cities of what is now Iraq, people even carved lamps out of alabaster stone in the shape of shells, around 2600 BCE. Later, potters began making lamps of baked clay. The earliest were simple round bowls with a pinched edge to hold the wick. Over the centuries they grew spouts, handles and lids.","By Roman times, clay oil lamps were everywhere. Factories in northern Italy and southern Gaul (modern France) turned them out by the thousand between the first and third centuries CE, and ships carried them all over the Roman Empire. Many were stamped with the maker's name and decorated with gods, gladiators, animals or chariot races. Museums today are full of them, because they were cheap, common and tough enough to survive in the ground for two thousand years.","What did people burn? Whatever their land provided. Around the Mediterranean it was mostly olive oil, which burns fairly cleanly. Elsewhere people used sesame, castor, flax, walnut and almond oils, or animal fuels such as butter, ghee, fish oil, seal oil and whale oil. Fish and animal oils smoked more than olive oil, which may be why some ancient lamps had several wick spouts to make up for a dimmer flame.","An ancient oil lamp gave a small, yellow, wobbling flame. It was enough to find your way to bed or eat supper by, but not enough to light a whole room brightly. Rich families might have a dozen lamps hanging from bronze stands. Poor families might have one, or none."]},{"heading":"Candles: Tallow, Beeswax and Rushlights","paragraphs":["Candles are a lamp with the container taken away. The fuel is a solid block of fat or wax, and the wick runs through the middle. When the wick is lit, the heat melts a little pool of fuel at the top, the wick soaks it up, and the flame burns it. A candle needs no bowl and spills no oil, which made candles easy to carry and store.","The Romans made candles by dipping a wick again and again into melted tallow or beeswax. Tallow is hard animal fat, usually from cattle or sheep. In China, candles may have been made more than two thousand years ago, possibly from whale fat. But for centuries, oil lamps stayed more common in the Mediterranean, partly because beeswax was so expensive.","In medieval Europe there were two very different kinds of candle. Beeswax candles burned with a clean, bright flame and smelled sweet. They were costly, so they were used mostly in churches and in the homes of the rich. Ordinary households burned tallow candles. Tallow was cheap because every butcher had it, but it smelled of burning meat fat, smoked badly, dripped greasy blobs and needed its wick trimmed often, or the flame would sink into a sooty, flickering stub. By the 1200s candle-making was an organised craft in England and France, with guilds of chandlers, as candle-makers were called.","The very poorest people often could not afford even tallow candles. Instead they made rushlights. In summer or autumn they gathered tall rushes from damp meadows, peeled away most of the green outer skin, and dried the white, spongy pith inside. Then they dipped it in whatever fat they had, often bacon grease saved from cooking. The result was a thin, fragile stick that was clamped in a little iron holder and lit.","A naturalist named Gilbert White, who lived in the village of Selborne in Hampshire, described rushlights in a famous letter written in 1789. He timed one that was about 72 centimetres long and found it burned for 57 minutes, giving what he called 'a good clear light'. Rushlights were still used in parts of rural Britain until the end of the 1800s. Think about that: in the same years that factories and railways were changing the world, some families were still lighting their evenings with greasy reeds.","In the 1700s and 1800s, candles got better. A waxy substance from sperm whales, called spermaceti, made hard, bright, clean-burning candles. In 1823 the French chemists Michel Eugène Chevreul and Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac found how to separate a harder material called stearin from animal fat, which made firmer candles that gave a brighter light. In 1825 a braided wick was introduced that curled over and trimmed itself as it burned, so nobody had to keep snipping it. In 1834 Joseph Morgan built a machine that moulded candles quickly, and in the 1850s the Scottish chemist James Young began producing paraffin wax from coal and oil shale. Paraffin wax soon became the most common candle material, and it still is today.","The candle was so important that it became a unit of measurement. For a long time, the brightness of a lamp was described in 'candlepower', meaning how many candles it equalled. The modern scientific unit of light intensity is called the candela, the Latin word for candle, and a typical candle gives out roughly one candela. When later in this story you hear that an early electric lamp equalled sixteen candles, you will know exactly what people meant."]},{"heading":"Sailing Around the World for Lamp Oil","paragraphs":["In the 1700s and 1800s, one of the best lamp fuels came from the sea. Whale oil burned brighter and cleaner than most animal fats, and the oil from sperm whales was the finest of all. Light, in other words, came from one of the largest animals on Earth.","The island of Nantucket, off the coast of Massachusetts, began whaling in 1690. At first its sailors hunted whales close to shore. As those whales grew scarce, Nantucket crews sailed farther and farther, and by the 1700s whaling had become a big, deep-sea business. After the American Revolution ended in 1783 the industry recovered, and the town of New Bedford grew into the busiest whaling port of all.","Whalers especially wanted sperm whales. Inside a sperm whale's enormous head is a waxy liquid called spermaceti, which burns with a very bright flame and made the best candles money could buy. To find sperm whales, ships sailed to the far side of the planet: down around South America into the Pacific, to the waters off Peru and Ecuador, across the equator and as far as Japan. A single voyage could last years. Sailors left home as teenagers and came back as young men, if they came back at all.","Life on a whaling ship was hard and dangerous. Men rowed small wooden boats right up to a whale, threw harpoons by hand, and then had to survive the giant animal's struggle. Afterwards the crew cut up the whale and boiled its blubber in huge iron pots on deck to make oil, which was stored in barrels below. About ten thousand seamen worked on American whaling ships during the industry's busiest years, including more than three thousand African American sailors. Crews were paid not with wages but with a share, or 'lay', of the profits from the oil, so a voyage that found few whales could leave a sailor with almost nothing after years at sea.","In the mid-1800s, the 'golden age' of American whaling, whale oil lit homes, shops, lighthouses and factories, and it also oiled the machines of the Industrial Revolution. A clever new lamp helped too. In 1780 the Swiss inventor Aimé Argand designed a lamp with a hollow, tube-shaped wick and a glass chimney. Air flowed up through the middle of the wick as well as around the outside, so the oil burned more completely, with a brighter, steadier flame and much less smoke. Argand lamps quickly replaced older oil lamps in homes that could afford them.","But hunting whales for light came at a terrible cost to the whales. Population after population was hunted down, and whalers had to sail ever farther to find more. Today we know that several whale species were pushed close to extinction by centuries of hunting. In the end it was not kindness that saved the sperm whales from the lamp-oil trade. It was a cheaper fuel pumped out of the ground, which we will meet in a moment."]},{"heading":"A Flame Through a Pipe: The Gas Light Revolution","paragraphs":["Around 1792, in the mining town of Redruth in Cornwall, a Scottish engineer named William Murdoch tried an experiment that would change the look of cities. Murdoch worked for the famous steam-engine firm of Boulton and Watt, looking after their engines in the Cornish tin and copper mines. In his spare time he heated coal in a closed iron container, called a retort. When coal is heated without enough air to burn, it gives off a gas that can be lit. Murdoch piped this coal gas through tubes and lit it at the other end. By about 1794 he was lighting rooms of his house this way. His home in Redruth is often called the first house lit by gas.","Murdoch was not the only person playing with coal gas. A Dutch professor, Jean-Pierre Minckelers, had written about flammable gases in 1784, and one of his students later said he sometimes used gas to light his lecture room in Leuven. In France, Philippe Lebon lit his house in Paris with gas from wood in 1801. As we will see again and again in this Deep Dive, big ideas often occur to several people at once. But Murdoch was the one who turned gas lighting into a practical, working system.","In 1798 Murdoch moved back to Boulton and Watt's Soho Foundry near Birmingham and lit part of the main building with gas. In 1802, to celebrate a peace treaty between Britain and France called the Peace of Amiens, he lit up the outside of the factory for crowds to see. Then, starting in 1805, he lit an entire cotton mill in Manchester, belonging to the firm of Philips and Lee. It began with 50 gas lights and eventually had 904. Mill owners loved gas: it was brighter than candles, cheaper to run, and the workers could keep spinning cotton long after dark. In 1808 the Royal Society gave Murdoch its Rumford Medal for his work. Oddly, he never patented his gas lighting, so he never earned a fortune from it.","Lighting a single mill was one thing. Lighting a whole city meant building gasworks, digging up streets and laying pipes under them. A German-born showman and promoter named Frederick Albert Winsor set out to do just that in London. He gave dramatic demonstrations of gas light, including one at the Lyceum Theatre in 1804, and pushed for a company to supply gas through pipes. Parliament approved the plan in 1810, and on 30 April 1812 the Gas Light and Coke Company received its royal charter from King George III. It was the first company of its kind in the world, set up to make gas at a central works and pipe it to customers.","On the last night of 1813, Westminster Bridge in London was lit with gas lamps. People came out just to stare. Gas lighting spread quickly: Baltimore, in the United States, lit its first gas street lamp in February 1817, and Paris began lighting streets with gas in 1820. By 1823 many towns and cities across Britain had gasworks. A new job appeared, too: the lamplighter, who walked the streets every evening with a long pole to turn on and light each lamp, and came back at dawn to put them out.","Gas light changed how cities felt at night. Shops could stay open later. Theatres glowed. Streets felt safer. Still, gas had a dark side. The gas made from coal was poisonous to breathe, and if a pipe leaked or a tap was left open, a room could fill up and explode at the touch of a flame. Gas flames gave off heat, fumes and soot that blackened ceilings and wilted houseplants. And in theatres, where hundreds of gas jets lit the stage, fires were frighteningly common. Several hundred theatres in Europe and America burned down during the 1800s.","Late in the century, in 1891, the Austrian chemist Carl Auer von Welsbach invented the gas mantle, a little fabric net soaked in special chemicals. Heated by a gas flame, the mantle glowed a brilliant white and made gas lights several times brighter. Gas lamps would fight back against electric light for years thanks to this invention. Some cities, including parts of London, still keep a few gas streetlamps burning today for their old-fashioned charm."]},{"heading":"Rock Oil and the Kerosene Revolution","paragraphs":["Gas lighting needed pipes, and pipes only made sense in towns. Out in the countryside, on farms and in small villages, people still depended on candles and oil lamps. What they needed was a cheap liquid fuel that burned brightly. They found it underground.","In 1846 a Canadian scientist named Abraham Gesner is reported to have shown off a lamp fuel he distilled from bitumen, a black, tar-like mineral. He later named it kerosene. It was meant to be a cheaper substitute for whale oil. In 1853, in the city of Lemberg (now Lviv in Ukraine), a Polish pharmacist named Ignacy Łukasiewicz made kerosene from petroleum, the black 'rock oil' that seeped out of the ground in some places, and built a lamp to burn it. His lamp is often called the first modern kerosene lamp. In Britain the same fuel came to be called paraffin.","The real explosion came in the United States. People in western Pennsylvania had long noticed oil oozing up from the ground and floating on streams. A Yale chemist, Benjamin Silliman Jr., studied this oil and reported in 1855 that it could be refined into an excellent lamp fuel. A company called the Seneca Oil Company hired a former railroad conductor, Edwin Drake, to find a way to get the oil out in large amounts. With the help of a blacksmith and well-driller named William A. Smith, Drake drilled into the ground beside Oil Creek near Titusville, Pennsylvania. On 27 August 1859, at a depth of 69.5 feet (about 21 metres), they struck oil.","Drake's well pumped up about 12 to 20 barrels a day. That does not sound like much today, but it started a rush. Within months, wooden derricks were sprouting all along Oil Creek, and the price of oil tumbled. Drake's well was not the first oil well in the world, but it showed that drilling could provide a steady, large supply, and it launched the American oil industry. Almost all of that early oil was refined into kerosene for lamps. Petrol for cars was, at first, a nearly useless leftover.","A kerosene lamp works like this. The fuel sits in a glass or metal bowl at the bottom. A flat cloth wick dips into it, and the kerosene creeps up the wick by capillary action, the same way water creeps up a paper towel. A small knob raises or lowers the wick to make the flame bigger or smaller. A tall glass chimney around the flame creates an upward draught that pulls in fresh air, so the fuel burns more completely and brightly. Because kerosene is thin and runny, the fuel tank could sit below the flame, which made the lamps simple and cheap.","Cheap is the key word. Kerosene lamps brought decent light to millions of homes that had never been able to afford much more than a candle or two. Families could read, sew, study and talk in the evening. From the time of the American Civil War onward, kerosene steadily pushed whale oil out of the market, and the great whaling fleets shrank. Kerosene lamps are still used today in places without electricity, in parts of Africa and Asia, though the World Health Organization warns that their fumes are harmful to breathe."]},{"heading":"Smoke, Soot and the Price of Light","paragraphs":["By the 1870s, a well-off family in London, Paris or New York had more ways to light their home than ever before: gas jets on the walls, kerosene lamps on the tables and candles for carrying upstairs. Yet every one of these lights had the same basic problems, because every one of them was a fire.","First, flames were dim. A kerosene lamp gives somewhere between about 8 and 100 lumens, depending on its size. A lumen is a measure of how much light something gives out. A single ordinary household LED bulb today gives around 800 lumens, the light of dozens of candles, from something that stays cool enough to touch. A Victorian reading lamp would seem gloomy to us.","Second, flames were dirty. Candles and lamps gave off smoke and soot that blackened ceilings, curtains and paintings and had to be scrubbed off. Glass chimneys needed cleaning every day. Wicks needed trimming. Gas and kerosene left a smell in the air, and burning anything uses up oxygen and fills a closed room with stuffy, unhealthy fumes. Many people also blamed hours of reading by flickering flames for headaches and tired eyes.","Third, flames were dangerous. A lamp knocked off a table, a candle left by a curtain or a child's sleeve brushing a flame could start a deadly fire. Kerosene that had been mixed with more explosive fuels could flare up. Gas leaks could cause explosions. Theatres, mills and crowded homes all knew the terror of fire spreading from a single light.","And fourth, light was expensive. This is easy to forget. Economists Roger Fouquet and Peter Pearson studied seven hundred years of lighting in Britain, adding up the cost of fuel, the cost of the lamps and how efficiently they turned fuel into light. Their work shows that the price of a fixed amount of light has fallen enormously, so that today the same amount of light costs only a tiny fraction of what it did in 1800. Another economist, William Nordhaus, made a similar point in the 1990s, showing that ordinary price statistics hugely underestimate how much cheaper light has become. For a working family in the early 1800s, even a couple of candles each evening was a real expense, which is why so many simply went to bed when it got dark.","Meanwhile, scientists had been studying a very different kind of energy for over two hundred years. They knew how to make it spark and crackle. They had stored it in jars, drawn it down from thunderclouds, and made it flow through wires. By the 1870s a few dazzling electric arc lamps were even lighting streets and lighthouses. But nobody yet had a way to use electricity to light an ordinary living room safely, softly and cheaply. That challenge, and the people who solved it, is what this Deep Dive is all about.","So here is the big question. Every light in human history, from the Lascaux lamp to Murdoch's gas jets, had been a fire. What if light could come without any flame at all? The answer, strangely enough, began with a piece of rubbed amber, thousands of years ago."]}],"deepDive":"How much better was each new light? Scientists compare lamps by luminous efficacy: lumens of light produced for each watt of power used. A candle releases roughly 80 watts of heat but only about 13 lumens of light, an efficacy of well under 1 lumen per watt. Oil and kerosene lamps did somewhat better, and Welsbach's 1891 gas mantle was a big jump because the glowing mantle turned more of the flame's heat into visible light. Early carbon-filament electric bulbs managed only a few lumens per watt, and later tungsten bulbs about 12 to 15, yet they were still far cleaner and safer than flames. Modern LEDs often exceed 100 lumens per watt. So, measured in light per unit of energy, lighting has improved by a factor of hundreds. Economic historians argue about how to measure what this meant for people. In a famous 1996 study, William Nordhaus pointed out that standard price indexes track the cost of goods like candles and lamp oil, not the cost of the light itself, and so they miss most of the improvement. Roger Fouquet and Peter Pearson rebuilt the British record back to 1300, combining fuel prices, equipment costs and efficiency. Their data show light becoming dramatically cheaper over the 1800s and 1900s while people used vastly more of it: as light got cheaper, people did not simply save money, they bought far more light. Economists call this kind of effect a rebound. Historians also debate the credit for gas lighting. Minckelers is said to have lit his lecture room with gas in the 1780s, Lebon demonstrated gas in Paris in 1801, and Murdoch lit his Cornish house in the early 1790s; Murdoch's achievement was turning the idea into reliable industrial systems, while promoters such as Winsor created the business model of gas piped from a central works. That pattern, with many inventors and a few system-builders, will appear again with the electric light.","funFact":"The scientific unit of light intensity, the candela, is named after the candle, and an ordinary candle gives out roughly one candela.","check":{"q":"Who lit his house in Redruth, Cornwall with coal gas in the 1790s?","a":"William Murdoch","wrong":["Edwin Drake","Aimé Argand","Thomas Edison"]},"sources":[{"title":"William Murdoch","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Murdoch"},{"title":"Gas lighting","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gas_lighting"},{"title":"Gas Light and Coke Company","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gas_Light_and_Coke_Company"},{"title":"Oil lamp","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oil_lamp"},{"title":"Candle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candle"},{"title":"Rushlight","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rushlight"},{"title":"History of whaling","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_whaling"},{"title":"Drake Well","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drake_Well"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/01-1.jpg","alt":"An ancient Roman terracotta oil lamp with a decorated top, a filling hole and a wick spout (Metropolitan Museum of Art).","file":"Terracotta oil lamp MET DP232.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terracotta_oil_lamp_MET_DP232.jpg","author":"This file was donated to Wikimedia Commons as part of a project by the…","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/01-2.jpg","alt":"Portrait of William Murdoch (1754–1839), the Scottish engineer who pioneered lighting with coal gas.","file":"William Murdock.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Murdock.jpg","author":"unattributed","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/01-3.jpg","alt":"A Victorian kerosene table lamp with a blue glass fuel bowl and an etched glass globe shade, in a museum.","file":"Lamp, kerosene (AM 1965.111-1).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lamp,_kerosene_(AM_1965.111-1).jpg","author":"API data Catalogue record Photo","license":"CC BY 4.0"}],"words":4154},{"n":2,"title":"Amber, Electric Fish and Rubbing Machines","when":"about 600 BCE – 1740s","summary":"Rub a piece of golden amber with fur and it can pick up bits of straw like magic. For two thousand years people wondered why. Then curious scientists built spinning balls that made sparks and sent electricity racing along a thread.","sections":[{"heading":"The Golden Stone That Grabbed Straw","paragraphs":["Pick up a piece of amber and it feels surprisingly warm and light for a stone. That is because it is not really a stone at all. Amber is fossilised tree resin, the sticky, sweet-smelling goo that oozes out of a pine tree when its bark is damaged. Millions of years ago, blobs of resin dripped down ancient trees, sometimes trapping insects, spiders or tiny leaves. Buried under layers of sand and mud, squeezed and warmed for ages, the resin slowly hardened into amber. Some pieces are around 100 million years old, from the time of the dinosaurs.","The most famous amber in Europe comes from the shores of the Baltic Sea. Storms wash it up on the beaches of what are now Poland, Lithuania and the Russian region of Kaliningrad, where about 90 percent of the world's minable amber lies. Since prehistoric times people have collected these honey-coloured lumps and traded them far to the south along routes that historians call the Amber Road. By Roman times, Baltic amber was being carried all the way to the Mediterranean, where it was carved into beads, charms and jewellery.","Somewhere along the way, people noticed something odd. If you polish or rub a piece of amber with a dry cloth or a scrap of fur, it starts to attract light things. Bits of straw, dry leaves, feathers and dust jump up and cling to it. Rub it hard enough in very dry air and you might even hear a tiny crackle or see a faint spark in the dark. To an ancient jeweller or merchant this must have seemed magical. A stone that reaches out and grabs things, without touching them!","You can try a modern version yourself. Rub a plastic comb or balloon on a woollen jumper or your hair, then hold it near some tiny pieces of tissue paper. They leap up and stick. That pull is exactly what ancient people saw with amber. Today we call it static electricity: electric charge that builds up on a surface and stays there until it leaks away or jumps off as a spark. But it would take more than two thousand years for anyone to work out what was really going on.","Here is a puzzle that took a very long time to solve. The straw or tissue paper is not charged at all, so why should it be pulled toward the amber? The answer is that a charged object nudges the tiny charges inside nearby things. When negatively charged amber comes close to a scrap of straw, it pushes some of the straw's negative charges slightly away to the far side, leaving the near side a little positive. Because opposite charges attract and the near side is closer, the pull wins over the push, and up jumps the straw. The same trick lets a rubbed balloon cling to a wall."]},{"heading":"Thales: Tradition, Not Proof","paragraphs":["If you look up who discovered static electricity, many books give a confident answer: Thales of Miletus, around 600 BCE. Thales was a real person, a Greek thinker who lived in Miletus, a busy trading city on the coast of what is now Turkey. Later Greeks counted him as one of the Seven Sages, the wise men of early Greece, and many historians call him one of the first philosophers, because he tried to explain the world through nature rather than through stories about gods.","But here is the problem. Not a single word that Thales himself wrote has survived. Everything we know about him comes from people writing much later. The philosopher Aristotle, writing around two hundred years after Thales, reported that Thales thought a magnet, the natural magnetic stone called lodestone, must have a kind of soul, because it could move iron. Much of the rest of Thales's story comes from a writer named Diogenes Laërtius, who lived about eight hundred years after Thales and sometimes repeated unreliable tales.","The link between Thales and amber is a tradition that grew up in these later writings. It may contain a grain of truth, since Greek thinkers clearly did know that rubbed amber attracts small things. But we cannot say for certain that Thales was the first to notice it, or that he did any experiments with it at all. It is a bit like the famous story that Thales predicted a solar eclipse in 585 BCE, which comes from the historian Herodotus and which many modern historians doubt.","So the fair way to say it is this: according to tradition, Thales of Miletus noticed that amber rubbed with fur attracts light objects. Probably many ordinary people noticed it long before any philosopher wrote about it. What the Greeks did give us for certain is the name. Their word for amber was elektron. Some scholars link it to an old Greek word for the shining Sun, perhaps because of amber's warm, golden glow, and in one Greek myth amber was formed from the tears of grieving sisters of Phaethon, the boy who crashed the chariot of the Sun.","Two thousand years later, that ancient word for a golden stone would become the root of the words electric, electricity, electron and electronics. Every time you charge a phone, you are using a word that began as the Greek name for fossil tree resin."]},{"heading":"Fish That Fight with Lightning","paragraphs":["Long before anyone used the word electricity, people had felt its sting in a very different way: from fish. Certain fish can make their own electric shocks, and fishermen around the world learned to respect them.","In ancient Egypt, the Nile catfish could give a painful jolt. Egyptian texts from around 2750 BCE refer to a fish they called the thunderer of the Nile. In the Mediterranean, Greeks and Romans knew the electric ray, a flat, round fish that lies half-buried in the sand on the sea floor, waiting for small fish to swim by. When prey comes close, the ray zaps it. Depending on the species, electric rays can produce shocks of about 8 to 220 volts. Touch one by accident and your hand and arm go numb. The Romans called this fish torpedo, from a Latin verb meaning to be stiffened or paralysed. (Much later, the underwater weapon was named after the fish.)","The numbing power of the torpedo was so well known that the Greek philosopher Plato used it as a joke. In his dialogue called the Meno, a young man complains that talking to the famous thinker Socrates leaves his mind as numb as if he had been stung by a torpedo fish.","Some ancient doctors tried to put the shocks to use. The Roman physician Scribonius Largus worked at the court of the emperor Claudius. Around 46 to 47 CE he collected 271 medical recipes into a book called Compositiones. One of them was unusual. For a bad headache, he advised placing a live torpedo fish on the painful spot until the numbness took the pain away. For gout, a painful swelling of the joints, the patient was told to stand at the seashore with a living electric fish under their foot. Scribonius Largus did not know anything about electricity, but today he is sometimes mentioned as an early example of using electrical stimulation of nerves to treat pain, an idea that modern medicine really does use in some devices.","How can a fish make electricity? Electric fish have special organs made of stacks of flat cells called electrocytes. When a nerve signal reaches them, tiny charged particles called ions rush through the cell walls, making one side of each cell slightly positive and the other slightly negative. Each cell gives only a small voltage, but thousands of them stacked in a row add up, just like batteries placed end to end in a torch. The champion is the electric eel of South America: one species can produce up to about 860 volts.","For a very long time, nobody connected these shocking fish with the attraction of rubbed amber. One was a strange animal power and the other a curiosity of a jewel. Only in the 1700s did scientists show that the fish shocks were electrical, and, as we will see in a later part, the stacked cells of electric fish even helped inspire the first battery."]},{"heading":"Dr Gilbert and His Little Earth","paragraphs":["For about two thousand years after the Greeks, people knew that rubbed amber attracted straw and that lodestone attracted iron, and most of them assumed these were two versions of the same mysterious pull. The man who finally untangled them was an English doctor named William Gilbert.","Gilbert was born around 1544 in Colchester, in Essex, and studied at St John's College, Cambridge, becoming a doctor of medicine in 1569. He moved to London, built a successful medical practice and rose to become president of the Royal College of Physicians in 1600. From 1601 he served as one of the personal physicians of Queen Elizabeth I. But his great passion was magnets, and he spent years testing them.","In 1600 he published his findings in a book written in Latin, the international language of scholars. Its short title is De Magnete, meaning On the Magnet. What made the book special was its method. Gilbert did not just repeat what ancient authors had said. He did experiments, again and again, and he scoffed at writers who copied old stories without testing them. Today he is often counted among the first true experimental scientists in England.","One of his most famous experiments used a terrella, Latin for little Earth: a ball carved out of lodestone. Gilbert moved a small compass needle around the ball and found that it behaved just like a compass on the real Earth, pointing toward the poles and tipping downward more and more as it got closer to them. From this he made a bold claim: the whole Earth is a giant magnet. That, he said, is why compasses point north. He was right.","This mattered enormously to sailors. In 1600, English, Dutch, Spanish and Portuguese ships were crossing oceans, and their navigators depended on compasses without really knowing why the needles pointed north. Some people believed that compasses were pulled by a magnetic star, or by a great magnetic mountain or island somewhere in the far north. Gilbert's terrella experiments replaced those legends with a clear, testable explanation.","Gilbert then turned to amber. To study its pull carefully he invented an instrument called the versorium, from a Latin word meaning to turn around. It was simply a light metal needle balanced on a point, a bit like a compass needle but not magnetised. When he brought a rubbed object near it, the needle swung toward the object. This made the versorium the first electroscope, a tool for detecting electric charge.","With his versorium Gilbert tested dozens of materials. He discovered that amber was not special at all. Glass, sulphur, sealing wax, resin and many gemstones, such as diamond and sapphire, also attracted light things after rubbing. Metals, on the other hand, did not seem to. He noticed that the effect worked best in dry, cold weather and faded in damp air or near a flame. And he found a key difference from magnetism: a magnet pulled only on iron, but a rubbed piece of amber or glass pulled on almost anything light, from straw to scraps of metal to drops of water.","To name this group of attracting materials, Gilbert invented the Latin word electricus, meaning like amber, from the Greek elektron. He called the substances electrics. Gilbert thought electricity and magnetism were completely separate forces. Later scientists would show they are deeply connected, but separating them was a vital first step. A few decades later, in 1646, the English writer Sir Thomas Browne used the English word electricity in print, and it soon caught on.","Gilbert died in London in 1603, the same year as Queen Elizabeth, possibly from the plague. He never lived to see what his electrics would become. But the unit of magnetomotive force, the gilbert, is named in his honour."]},{"heading":"The Mayor and His Spinning Sulphur Ball","paragraphs":["Rubbing amber by hand only makes a tiny amount of charge. To study electricity properly, scientists needed a way to make lots of it. The first person to build a machine for the job was a German engineer, scientist and politician named Otto von Guericke.","Guericke was the mayor of the city of Magdeburg from 1646 to 1678, at a time when the city was rebuilding after being destroyed during the Thirty Years' War. He is most famous for his experiments with air pressure. He pumped the air out of two large copper half-spheres, the Magdeburg hemispheres, fitted together, and showed that teams of horses pulling in opposite directions could not tear them apart, because the air outside pressed them together so hard.","Guericke was also curious about why things attract each other, and in the early 1660s he built a remarkable device. He made a ball of sulphur, a yellow, brittle mineral and mounted it on an iron rod so that it could be turned. As the ball spun, he rubbed it with his dry hand. The sulphur became strongly charged, far more than a rubbed lump of amber.","Guericke found that the charged ball pulled in feathers, bits of paper and other light objects, and that sometimes, after touching the ball, they were pushed away again. He had glimpsed repulsion as well as attraction, an important clue that would puzzle scientists for decades. He described his experiments in his book Experimenta Nova, published in 1672. Interestingly, Guericke did not think of his globe mainly as an electrical machine. He was using it to imitate the forces he believed held the Earth and the heavens together. Still, it is often called the first electrostatic generator, a machine for building up static electricity.","Others soon improved the idea. Around 1705 the English instrument-maker Francis Hauksbee, who worked for the Royal Society in London, spun a glass globe with the air pumped out of it. When he rubbed it, the inside of the globe glowed with an eerie purple light. Without knowing it, Hauksbee had made a distant ancestor of neon signs and fluorescent tubes. Later in the 1700s, friction machines with spinning glass globes, cylinders or plates, rubbed by leather pads, became standard equipment in every serious laboratory and many fashionable drawing rooms."]},{"heading":"Stephen Gray Sends Electricity on a Journey","paragraphs":["The next big step came not from a professor but from a cloth dyer. Stephen Gray was born in 1666 in Canterbury, in Kent, and trained in his family's dyeing trade. But he loved science. He taught himself astronomy, ground his own lenses, built his own telescope, and helped John Flamsteed, the first Astronomer Royal, with observations of the stars. In his old age he was poor and lived as a pensioner at the Charterhouse in London, a charity home for elderly men.","In 1729 Gray was experimenting with a long glass tube, which he charged by rubbing it. To keep dust out, he had put corks in the ends. To his surprise, he found that the corks also attracted feathers, even though he had not rubbed them. The electricity, or 'electric virtue' as people then said, had travelled from the glass into the cork. Gray wondered how far it could go. He pushed a stick into the cork, then hung an ivory ball on the end of the stick, and the ball attracted feathers too. Then he tried longer and longer pieces of string.","His rooms soon became too small, so Gray took his experiments to the country house of a friend, Granville Wheler, in Kent. There they hung a long packthread, a strong twine, across rooms and along galleries and out into the grounds. At first the experiment failed when they held the thread up with other pieces of string: the electricity escaped through the supports. Then they tried holding it up with thin threads of silk, and it worked. Charge from the rubbed glass tube travelled along the line and made a ball at the far end attract brass leaf and feathers. In the end they sent it along about 800 feet of thread, roughly 240 metres, longer than two football pitches.","Gray had discovered two kinds of materials. Some, like metal wire, damp thread and the human body, let electricity flow along them. Others, like silk, glass, resin and hair, stopped it. Today we call these conductors and insulators. A conductor is a material electricity flows through easily, like copper. An insulator blocks it, like rubber, glass or plastic. Every electric cable in your home works on Gray's discovery: a copper conductor inside, wrapped in a plastic insulator to keep the electricity where it belongs.","Gray made other clever discoveries. He found that a charged object could make a nearby object electric without even touching it, an effect now called electrostatic induction. He also compared a solid cube of oak with a hollow one of the same size and found that both carried electricity equally well. That suggested electric charge sits on the outside surface of an object rather than soaking all the way through it, an idea later scientists confirmed.","Gray's most famous demonstration became a sensation. A boy was hung horizontally in the air on silk cords, like a human hammock. A charged glass tube was brought near his feet, and suddenly his hands and face could attract scraps of paper, chaff and brass leaf from a table below. The 'flying boy' showed that the human body could carry electricity, and that the silk cords kept it from escaping to the ground. Versions of the trick were repeated in lecture halls across Europe for decades.","The Royal Society awarded Gray its Copley Medal in 1731 and again in 1732, and in 1732 welcomed him as an honorary member. Yet he remained poor, and when he died in 1736 he was probably buried in a section of a London cemetery set aside for the poor, in a spot no one can now point to. Few people today know his name, but every wire in the world carries a little bit of his legacy."]},{"heading":"Du Fay Finds Two Kinds of Electricity","paragraphs":["Across the English Channel, a French scientist read about Gray's experiments with excitement. Charles François de Cisternay du Fay was a chemist and member of the French Academy of Sciences, and from 1732 he was also in charge of the Jardin du Roi, the royal botanical garden in Paris. Starting in 1732 he repeated Gray's work and then went further.","Du Fay showed that almost anything could be electrified if it was kept insulated, including metals, liquids and living animals. Metals had seemed not to work only because their charge leaked straight away through the experimenter's hand. He also proved wrong an idea that the colour of an object mattered.","Then he made his most important discovery. He charged a glass tube and used it to make a tiny flake of gold leaf float away, because the gold leaf, now charged by the glass, was pushed away from it. He expected that a rubbed piece of resin would push the gold leaf away too. Instead, the resin pulled it in. Somehow the gold leaf charged by glass was attracted by charged resin. After many more tests, du Fay announced in 1733 that there were two different kinds of electricity. He called them vitreous, from the Latin word for glass, and resinous, from resin.","His rule was simple and powerful: objects with the same kind of electricity push each other apart, and objects with different kinds pull together. Two vitreous objects repel. Two resinous objects repel. A vitreous object and a resinous object attract. Du Fay imagined electricity as two invisible fluids. His paper reached English readers in 1734 through the Royal Society's journal, Philosophical Transactions.","We still use du Fay's rule today, but with different names. About fifteen years later, the American Benjamin Franklin suggested that there was really just one electric fluid, and that objects had either too much of it or too little. He called these states positive and negative. Du Fay's vitreous electricity became positive charge, and resinous electricity became negative. Today we explain it with electrons: rubbing moves tiny negatively charged electrons from one material to another, leaving one side with extra and the other short. Du Fay died of smallpox in Paris in 1739, aged about 40, but his two kinds of electricity remain at the heart of physics.","By the 1740s, electricity had become the most exciting science in Europe. Experimenters could make it with spinning machines, send it along threads, and sort it into two kinds. But it always escaped, leaking into the air or the ground within moments. They began to wonder: could electricity be caught and kept, the way you keep water in a bottle? In a town in Germany and a university city in the Netherlands, two men were about to try, using a jar of water, and one of them was about to get the nastiest surprise of his life."]}],"deepDive":"Why does rubbing make charge? The effect Gilbert studied is now called the triboelectric effect, from the Greek tribein, to rub. When two different materials touch, electrons can move from one surface to the other, because different materials hold onto their electrons with different strengths. Pull them apart and one is left with extra electrons (negative) and the other with a shortage (positive). Rubbing simply makes many more points of contact. Scientists list materials in a triboelectric series: rub glass with silk and the glass ends up positive, matching du Fay's vitreous electricity; rub amber or many resins and plastics with fur or wool and they end up negative, matching resinous electricity. Surprisingly, physicists still argue about some details, such as whether ions or tiny fragments of material also move, and why even two pieces of the same material can charge each other. Gilbert's observation that damp air spoils the effect also has a clear explanation: a thin film of water on surfaces lets charge leak away, which is why you get more static shocks in dry winter weather. Historians debate credit too. The Thales story rests on writers centuries later, so it is best treated as tradition. Gilbert separated electricity from magnetism and coined electricus, but he thought of electrics as giving off an effluvium, a kind of invisible vapour, rather than as charged. Guericke's sulphur globe is often called the first electrical machine, yet he saw it as a model of cosmic forces. Gray and Wheler's long-distance experiments are well documented in the Royal Society's Philosophical Transactions. Du Fay's two-fluid theory and Franklin's one-fluid theory competed for decades. In a sense both captured part of the truth: there are two kinds of charge, as du Fay said, but in metals only one kind, the electrons, actually moves, a little like Franklin's single fluid.","funFact":"The strongest kind of electric eel can produce a shock of up to about 860 volts, more than three times the voltage of a British wall socket.","check":{"q":"What did Stephen Gray use to hold up his long thread so the electricity would not escape?","a":"Silk threads","wrong":["Copper wires","Wet string","Iron chains"]},"sources":[{"title":"History of electromagnetic theory","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_electromagnetic_theory"},{"title":"William Gilbert (physicist)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Gilbert_(physicist)"},{"title":"Stephen Gray (scientist)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_Gray_(scientist)"},{"title":"Charles François de Cisternay du Fay","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Fran%C3%A7ois_de_Cisternay_du_Fay"},{"title":"Otto von Guericke","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_von_Guericke"},{"title":"Thales of Miletus","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thales_of_Miletus"},{"title":"Electric ray","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_ray"},{"title":"Scribonius Largus","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scribonius_Largus"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/02-1.jpg","alt":"Title page of William Gilbert's De Magnete, printed in London in 1600 (MDC).","file":"Title-Page of Dr. W. Gilbert, \"De Magnete\" Wellcome L0016390.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Title-Page_of_Dr._W._Gilbert,_%22De_Magnete%22_Wellcome_L0016390.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/02-2.jpg","alt":"Otto von Guericke's sulphur globe, 1672: turned in a wooden frame and held up on a rod to show its electric pull.","file":"Guericke Schwefelkugel.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guericke_Schwefelkugel.jpg","author":"Otto von Guericke, Otto von Guericke Gesellschaft Magdeburg","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/02-3.jpg","alt":"\"L'Électrisée\" (about 1750): a lady standing on an insulating stand is charged by a spinning-globe electrical machine.","file":"Elektrisiermaschine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elektrisiermaschine.jpg","author":"The original uploader was Rainer Zenz at German Wikipedia.","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3799},{"n":3,"title":"Bottled Lightning and Benjamin Franklin","when":"1745 – 1760s","summary":"People found a way to trap electricity inside a glass jar, and it gave a shock strong enough to knock you off your feet. In America, Benjamin Franklin wondered if lightning was the same thing as these sparks. Brave experimenters with tall iron rods and a kite proved he was right, and the lightning rod was born.","sections":[{"heading":"The Jar That Bit Back","paragraphs":["By the 1740s, electricity was the most exciting show in Europe. Experimenters had friction machines: spinning globes of glass that they rubbed with their hands or a leather pad until crackling sparks leapt off. But there was one big problem. The electricity never stayed put. As soon as you stopped turning the machine, the charge leaked away into the damp air, and the fun was over. Could anyone catch it and keep it, the way you might catch rainwater in a barrel?","On 11 October 1745, a German churchman named Ewald Georg von Kleist tried exactly that. Von Kleist lived in Pomerania, on the cold Baltic coast of northern Europe. He took a small medicine bottle, poured in some alcohol, and pushed an iron nail through the cork so that it dipped into the liquid. Then he held the bottle in one hand and touched the nail to his electrical machine, hoping the electricity would flow down the nail and collect inside. When he reached over with his other hand and touched the nail, he got a shock so strong that it jolted his arms and shoulders. Something really had been stored inside that little bottle.","Von Kleist wrote letters about his discovery, but his descriptions were not very clear, and other people who tried to copy him often failed. The reason, as it turned out, was surprisingly simple. The trick only worked if the person holding the bottle was touching the outside of the glass with their bare hand. The hand was secretly part of the experiment.","A few months later, the same thing happened in the Dutch university town of Leiden. Pieter van Musschenbroek was a well-known professor of physics there, and he worked with a lawyer called Andreas Cunaeus who liked doing experiments as a hobby. Cunaeus held a glass of water in his hand while it was being charged from a machine, then touched the wire with his other hand. He received a terrific shock. Musschenbroek tried it himself and described the experience in a letter to the French scientist René Réaumur, dated 20 January 1746. He reportedly wrote that the blow struck his arm, shoulder and chest so hard that he thought for a moment it was all over for him, and that he would not try it again even for the whole kingdom of France.","Of course, once news of a painful and frightening experiment got out, everybody wanted to try it. The device spread across Europe within months and became known as the Leyden jar, after the old spelling of Leiden. Because von Kleist and Musschenbroek found it independently, historians today give credit to both of them, even though only Leiden ended up with its name on the jar.","So how did the jar work? Over the next few years experimenters improved it. The water was replaced by a lining of metal foil glued to the inside of the glass, and a second layer of foil was wrapped around the outside. The foil stopped a little short of the rim so that sparks could not jump over the top. A metal rod poked down through a stopper made of wood or cork and touched the inner foil. Glass is an insulator, which means electricity cannot pass through it. So when charge was pumped onto the inner foil, it could not escape through the glass. Instead it pulled an opposite charge onto the outer foil, which was connected to the ground through the hand or a chain. The two layers of charge sat there, face to face, held apart by the glass and attracting each other across it.","The moment someone made a path between the inner and outer foil, perhaps a wire, or a finger on the rod and a hand on the outside, all that stored charge rushed through the gap at once. That sudden rush is what gave the shock. Today we call a device like this a capacitor, and tiny capacitors are hidden inside almost every phone, computer and television. A typical Leyden jar about the size of a pint glass could be charged to tens of thousands of volts, which is why its spark could crack loudly and jump across a gap. It held only a small amount of energy, so a single jar usually did not seriously hurt anyone, but it certainly made people jump."]},{"heading":"Shocking Shows for Kings and Monks","paragraphs":["In an age before cinema, a science demonstration could be as thrilling as a magic show, and no one put on a better electrical show than a French priest and teacher called Jean-Antoine Nollet, usually known as the Abbé Nollet. Abbé was a title for a churchman, but Nollet spent most of his life lecturing about physics, building beautiful instruments and writing books such as his six-volume Lessons in Experimental Physics.","Imagine the scene at the royal palace in 1746. King Louis XV of France is watching. In front of him, about 180 of his guards stand in a long line, each holding the hand of the next man. Nollet charges a Leyden jar. The soldier at one end touches the outside of the jar, and the soldier at the other end touches the rod. In an instant, the whole line of men leaps into the air at the same moment, as if they had all been pinched at once. The king was delighted.","Nollet then did something even bigger. At a monastery of Carthusian monks, he arranged around 200 monks in a huge circle, roughly a mile (about 1.6 kilometres) around, with lengths of iron wire joining each person to the next. When the jar was discharged, every monk jumped at almost exactly the same time. The monks might not have enjoyed it, but the experiment taught something important. Nobody could see any delay between the first monk and the last. Electricity, it seemed, travelled along a line of people and wires incredibly fast, much faster than any sound or messenger.","These shows look silly to us now, but they mattered. They made thousands of ordinary people curious about electricity. They also spread knowledge: once audiences had seen a Leyden jar, some went home and built their own. One of the people who caught the electrical bug in this way lived thousands of kilometres from Paris, in a busy port town in the American colonies."]},{"heading":"A Printer in Philadelphia","paragraphs":["Benjamin Franklin was born in Boston in 1706, the fifteenth of seventeen children of a candle and soap maker. He left school at about ten, trained as a printer, and ran away to Philadelphia as a teenager. By his forties he had become one of the most successful men in town. He printed a newspaper, the Pennsylvania Gazette, and a hugely popular yearly booklet called Poor Richard's Almanack, full of weather forecasts, calendars and witty sayings. By 1748 he was rich enough to hand over the day-to-day running of his printing business and spend his time on what he loved most: asking questions about nature.","Franklin had seen electrical tricks performed in the 1740s by a travelling lecturer named Archibald Spencer. Soon afterwards, his friend Peter Collinson, a merchant in London who loved science, sent the Library Company of Philadelphia a glass tube for making electricity, along with news of the latest European experiments. Franklin was hooked. He and a small group of friends, including a Baptist minister and teacher named Ebenezer Kinnersley, rubbed tubes, charged jars and drew sparks for months on end.","Franklin did not just copy what Europeans had done. He came up with a new way of thinking about electricity. Earlier, Charles du Fay had said there were two kinds of electricity, vitreous (from glass) and resinous (from amber and resin). Franklin suggested instead that there was a single invisible electrical fluid in everything. An object with more than its normal share of this fluid, he said, was charged plus, or positive. An object with less than its normal share was minus, or negative. When a positive and a negative object touched, the fluid flowed from one to the other until the amounts evened out. Rubbing did not create electricity out of nothing; it only moved it from one place to another. This idea, that electric charge is never created or destroyed but only moved around, is now called the conservation of charge, and it is still one of the basic rules of physics.","Franklin's theory was not right in every detail. Scientists now know that it is tiny negatively charged particles called electrons that usually move, not a positive fluid. But his words were so useful that we still use them every day. Positive, negative, charge, plus and minus, all used in this electrical sense, come from Franklin and his letters. He also borrowed a word from the army. A battery was a row of cannons all fired together. Around 1748 Franklin built a row of charged glass panes, eleven of them, each sandwiched between thin sheets of lead, connected so they would all discharge at once. In a 1749 letter he called it an electrical battery. It was a set of capacitors rather than a battery like the one in your torch, but the name stuck and later moved on to the chemical batteries you will meet in Part 4.","Franklin was also not afraid to laugh at himself. In 1749 he planned a summer party by the river where a turkey would be killed by an electric shock and roasted on an electrically turned spit, with the guests drinking toasts from electrified glasses. While preparing an experiment on a turkey, he accidentally passed the charge of two large jars through his own body. In a letter he described a bright flash and a loud crack, and said his arms and the back of his neck felt numb until the next morning. A joke often linked to the story, though nobody is sure he really said it, is that he meant to kill a turkey but nearly killed a goose instead, the goose being himself. It was a reminder that even bottled electricity deserved respect.","Franklin sent long letters describing all of this to Peter Collinson in London. Collinson shared them with the Royal Society, Britain's leading science club, and in 1751 they were printed as a little book called Experiments and Observations on Electricity. It was soon translated into French, then other languages. A printer from a colonial town far from Europe's great universities was suddenly one of the most talked-about scientists in the world."]},{"heading":"Is Lightning Just a Giant Spark?","paragraphs":["For thousands of years, people had explained lightning in many ways: as the weapon of a sky god such as Zeus or Thor, as a punishment, or as some kind of exploding vapour in the air. By the 1740s, several European experimenters had noticed that the crack and flash of a Leyden jar looked a lot like a tiny thunderstorm. Some even wrote that lightning might be electrical. But nobody had tested the idea. It was just a guess.","Franklin decided to turn the guess into a real scientific question. In his notebook in November 1749, he wrote down a list of ways in which electricity and lightning seemed to agree. Both gave off light of a similar colour. Both travelled in crooked, zigzag paths. Both moved incredibly fast. Both were carried along by metals. Both made a crack or a bang. Both could set things on fire, melt metal and kill animals. Both left behind a sharp, sulphur-like smell. Then he added the most important line: since they agreed in all these ways, why not try an experiment to find out?","Franklin had already noticed something else in his experiments. A sharp metal point, such as a needle, could quietly draw the charge off a charged object from quite a distance away, without a loud spark. A blunt, rounded object did not do this nearly as well. If clouds were full of electricity, he wondered, could a tall, pointed iron rod draw some of that charge down where it could be tested?","In 1750 he described an experiment to do exactly that. He imagined a little hut, like a soldier's sentry box, built on top of a tall tower or church steeple. Out of the top of the box would stick a long, pointed iron rod, perhaps 6 to 9 metres tall, standing on an insulating stand so its charge could not drain away into the building. A person inside the box would hold a wire with a wax handle near the rod while a storm cloud passed overhead. If sparks jumped from the rod to the wire, that would prove that the cloud was electrified.","Thomas-François Dalibard was a French naturalist who studied plants. At the suggestion of the famous French scientist the Comte de Buffon, he translated Franklin's book of electrical letters into French. Reading Franklin's sentry-box idea, Dalibard decided to try it himself, but without bothering about a tower.","In the village of Marly-la-Ville, north of Paris, he set up an iron rod about 40 feet (12 metres) tall, pointed at the top. To stop the electricity from escaping into the ground, the bottom of the rod was kept off the earth on an insulating stand that reportedly included wine bottles, since glass is a good insulator. A small hut protected the base from the rain. Dalibard could not wait beside it all spring, so he left a helper, a former soldier, with instructions on what to do if a thunderstorm arrived.","On the afternoon of 10 May 1752, a storm cloud passed over Marly-la-Ville. The helper brought an insulated wire near the rod and saw sparks crackling across the gap, with the same blue light and sharp smell as sparks from a Leyden jar. Neighbours, including the village priest, rushed over to see. Dalibard reported the result to the French Academy of Sciences just a few days later. For the first time, someone had pulled electricity out of a storm cloud and shown that it behaved exactly like the electricity made by rubbing glass.","Within days the experiment was repeated by others in and around Paris, and King Louis XV himself was shown the effect. News raced across Europe. Dalibard was always clear that the idea came from Franklin, and so the American printer got the fame. But it is fair to remember that the first proof was carried out by a Frenchman in a quiet village, about a month before Franklin's own famous test."]},{"heading":"A Kite in a Storm","paragraphs":["Back in Philadelphia, Franklin was still waiting for a tall steeple. Then he had a clever idea. Why not use a kite? A kite could climb far higher than any building in town. According to the most detailed account, it was June 1752, and Franklin probably had not yet heard about Dalibard's success, because news took weeks to cross the ocean by ship.","Franklin built his kite out of a large silk handkerchief stretched over two crossed sticks of cedar. Silk was better than paper because it would not tear apart in the wind and rain. At the top he fixed a sharp, pointed wire, sticking up about a foot above the wood, to draw charge from the air. The kite string was made of hemp, which becomes a fairly good conductor when wet. Near the bottom, where Franklin held it, he tied a metal key, and below the key he attached a ribbon of silk. Dry silk is an insulator, so it kept the electricity from flowing into his hand. He also brought along a Leyden jar.","With his son William, then a young man in his early twenties, Franklin went out to a field as a storm approached. To keep the silk ribbon dry, they stood inside the doorway of a shed. For a long time nothing happened, and Franklin later admitted he was starting to feel disappointed. Then he noticed that the loose threads on the hemp string were standing up and pushing away from each other, just as hairs do on an electrified head. He moved his knuckle toward the key and felt a small spark jump across. As the rain soaked the string, the effect grew stronger, and he was able to collect charge from the key into his Leyden jar. Later he used that jar of cloud electricity to do the same tricks he did with electricity from a friction machine. It behaved exactly the same.","Here is something very important, which many books and cartoons get wrong. Franklin's kite was not struck by lightning. If a real bolt of lightning had hit the kite, it would almost certainly have killed him, as it would anyone holding the string. What actually happened was gentler. The air under a thundercloud is strongly electrified, and the pointed wire and wet string slowly gathered some of that charge from the air and the cloud, the same way his iron points drew charge from objects in his lab.","Historians also point out that the kite story has some mysteries. Franklin did not rush to announce it. His first description was a short letter printed in his Pennsylvania Gazette on 19 October 1752, which explained how to make such a kite and what it would do, but did not actually say when or where he had flown one. The fuller story, with the June date, the shed and William, appeared in 1767 in a history of electricity written by his English friend Joseph Priestley, who almost certainly got the details from Franklin himself. Because of this, a few historians have wondered whether some details were remembered wrongly or polished later. Most accept that the experiment really happened in some form, and many other people soon flew electrical kites of their own. In 1753 the Royal Society in London gave Franklin its top science prize, the Copley Medal, for his electrical work."]},{"heading":"Taming the Thunderbolt: The Lightning Rod","paragraphs":["Franklin was always practical. Once he knew that lightning was electricity, he asked: can we protect people from it? Lightning was a terrible danger in the 1700s. Wooden houses and barns burned down. Church steeples, the tallest buildings in most towns, were struck again and again, and gunpowder stores sometimes exploded when lightning hit them.","His answer was the lightning rod. It is an iron rod, pointed at the top, fixed to the highest part of a building and sticking up a little above the roof. A thick wire or metal strip runs from the rod down the outside of the building and deep into the moist ground. Franklin's first hope was that the sharp point would quietly leak charge into the air and stop lightning from forming at all. That part did not quite work out. But the rod did something even more useful. If lightning did strike, it hit the rod and was carried safely down the wire into the earth, instead of tearing through wooden beams, plaster and people inside. Metal is a much easier path for electricity than wood or brick, so the lightning follows the metal.","Franklin did not keep the idea secret or try to make money from it. He published simple instructions for making a lightning rod in Poor Richard's Almanack for the year 1753, which was cheap and read in thousands of homes. Rods were put up on buildings in Philadelphia, including the Academy of Philadelphia and the Pennsylvania State House, the building now called Independence Hall. Franklin also put a rod on his own house, with a clever twist. The wire from the rod passed near a pair of small bells inside, with a little metal ball hanging between them. When a charged storm cloud passed overhead, the ball swung back and forth and rang the bells, so Franklin could hurry over and do experiments. His wife Deborah was reportedly not a big fan of the bells.","Lightning rods spread across America and Europe, though not without arguments. Others argued about whether the top of the rod should be sharp or blunt; British scientists tended to favour a rounded end, while Franklin preferred a point. A modern study in 2000 found that moderately blunt tips actually work slightly better, and many new rods today are rounded. In what is now the Czech Republic, a priest called Prokop Diviš put up his own grounded rod in 1754, and historians still discuss how much he knew of Franklin's work.","Lightning rods are still used today, on churches, skyscrapers, towers and ships. And they show why one common saying is wrong. Lightning very often strikes the same place twice, especially tall buildings. The Empire State Building in New York is hit many times each year, and its lightning protection carries the charge safely to the ground every time."]},{"heading":"A Deadly Lesson in St Petersburg","paragraphs":["Collecting electricity from storms was a real scientific breakthrough, but it was also dangerous, and not everyone was as careful or as lucky as Franklin. Far to the east, in the Russian capital of St Petersburg, a physicist called Georg Wilhelm Richmann was studying lightning too. Richmann was a Baltic German scientist and a member of the St Petersburg Academy of Sciences. He had built a device he called an electrical gnomon, a kind of early meter that measured how strongly something was charged by how far a thread was pushed away from a metal rod. His friend and fellow scientist Mikhail Lomonosov was doing similar experiments.","On 6 August 1753 (26 July in the old Russian calendar), According to the usual account, Richmann was at a meeting of the Academy when he heard thunder. He hurried home with an engraver, usually named as Ivan Sokolov, who was going to make pictures of his work. At home he had set up an iron rod on the roof, connected by a wire to his measuring device indoors, so he could study the charge of passing storms.","As Richmann leaned toward his instrument, a large spark, which witnesses described as a ball of bluish fire, jumped from the apparatus to his forehead. There was a bang like a small cannon. Richmann fell dead. Sokolov was knocked unconscious, the door frame of the room was split, and the door was torn from its hinges. Doctors found a red spot on Richmann's forehead and saw that one of his shoes had been burst open. Many scientists think he was killed by ball lightning, a rare and still poorly understood glowing ball that sometimes appears during storms. His death is often described as the first documented case of ball lightning, and he is often called the first person to be killed by an electrical experiment.","Richmann's death shocked scientists across Europe. It showed that his indoor rod was not grounded and had brought the storm's electricity into the room rather than safely into the earth. The lesson was clear: lightning is powerful and dangerous, and the only safe rod is one that is properly connected to the ground and kept away from people. That is still true. Nobody should ever fly a kite near a storm or try to copy these old experiments.","Still, all the electricity of the 1700s had one big limitation. It came in sudden bursts. A Leyden jar discharged in a fraction of a second, with a crack and a flash, and then it was empty. A friction machine could only make small amounts, and only as long as someone kept turning the handle. Nobody could make electricity flow steadily, hour after hour, like water from a tap. You could not run anything on it, light a room with it, or send a constant signal down a wire.","The answer would come from a completely unexpected place. In the 1780s, in the old Italian university city of Bologna, a doctor who studied the bodies of animals was cutting up frogs on a table near an electrical machine. One day, a dead frog's leg suddenly twitched, as if it had come back to life. That strange little kick would start an argument between two brilliant Italians, and lead to the invention that finally made electricity flow."]}],"deepDive":"A Leyden jar is a capacitor: two conductors separated by an insulator (the dielectric). Its capacitance C depends on the foil area, the thickness of the glass and the glass's ability to be polarised; a typical pint-sized jar holds about 1 nanofarad. The stored energy is E = ½CV², so a 1-nanofarad jar charged to 30,000 volts stores about 0.45 joules, less than the energy needed to lift an apple half a metre. That is why one jar gave a painful jolt but rarely did lasting harm. Franklin's 'battery' of jars or glass panes connected together multiplied the stored charge, and big batteries of jars could kill small animals, which is why experimenters were warned to treat them with caution. Lightning is in another league entirely: a typical bolt carries tens of thousands of amperes for a fraction of a second, with peak temperatures of roughly 30,000 °C. Franklin's insight about points also holds up. Charge on a conductor crowds onto sharp curves, creating strong electric fields that ionise the surrounding air and allow charge to leak away quietly, the same 'corona' effect that makes St Elmo's fire glow on ships' masts. Historians debate several aspects of this story. Franklin's single-fluid theory was competing with two-fluid theories, and in a sense both were partly right: in metals only negative electrons move, but in salty water and gases both positive and negative particles carry current. The kite experiment is the other big debate. Because Franklin's October 1752 description did not say he had performed it, and Priestley's detailed account came fifteen years later, a few writers have suggested Franklin may have exaggerated or never flown the kite. Most historians reject that strong claim, noting that Franklin had a reputation for careful reporting and that others quickly repeated kite experiments successfully, including the French magistrate Jacques de Romas, who claimed to have had a similar idea independently. What nobody doubts is that Dalibard's rod experiment at Marly-la-Ville on 10 May 1752 came first, following Franklin's plan.","funFact":"Benjamin Franklin hung a pair of bells in his house that rang by themselves whenever an electrified storm cloud passed over his lightning rod.","check":{"q":"Who was the first to prove that lightning is electrical, in May 1752, by following Franklin's plan?","a":"Thomas-François Dalibard, with an iron rod at Marly-la-Ville in France","wrong":["Benjamin Franklin, by having his kite struck by lightning","Abbé Nollet, by shocking 180 royal guards","Georg Wilhelm Richmann, in St Petersburg"]},"sources":[{"title":"Leyden jar","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leyden_jar"},{"title":"Kite experiment","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kite_experiment"},{"title":"Benjamin Franklin and the Kite Experiment","publisher":"The Franklin Institute","url":"https://www.fi.edu/en/science-and-education/benjamin-franklin/kite-key-experiment"},{"title":"Benjamin Franklin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_Franklin"},{"title":"Thomas-François Dalibard","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas-Fran%C3%A7ois_Dalibard"},{"title":"Jean-Antoine Nollet","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Antoine_Nollet"},{"title":"Lightning rod","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightning_rod"},{"title":"Georg Wilhelm Richmann","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georg_Wilhelm_Richmann"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/03-1.jpg","alt":"A battery of four Leyden jars with metal-coated glass and brass rods, at Museum Boerhaave in Leiden.","file":"Museum Boerhaave - Leiden Jars.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museum_Boerhaave_-_Leiden_Jars.jpg","author":"Prof. Jos van den Broek","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/03-2.jpg","alt":"Currier & Ives print (1876) of Benjamin Franklin and his son flying a kite in a thunderstorm, June 1752.","file":"Benjamin Franklin Lightning Experiment 1752.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_Franklin_Lightning_Experiment_1752.jpg","author":"Currier & Ives, New York","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/03-3.jpg","alt":"19th-century engraving of Georg Richmann thrown back by a spark from his lightning rod apparatus in 1753.","file":"Richmanns Tod 1753.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richmanns_Tod_1753.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4329},{"n":4,"title":"Frog Legs and the First Battery","when":"1780 – 1801","summary":"An Italian doctor named Luigi Galvani saw dead frog legs kick when they were touched by metal, and he thought animals were full of electricity. His friend and rival Alessandro Volta thought the metals were the real secret, and to prove it he stacked up coins of zinc and copper with salty cardboard in between. That stack was the world's first battery, and it made electricity flow steadily for the very first time.","sections":[{"heading":"A Twitch in Bologna","paragraphs":["Bologna, in northern Italy, is home to one of the oldest universities in the world, founded in the 1000s. In the 1700s its medical students learned anatomy, the study of how bodies are built, in a beautiful wooden lecture theatre where professors cut open bodies in front of rows of watching students. One of the people who taught there was a quiet, serious doctor named Luigi Galvani.","Galvani was born in Bologna in 1737 and spent almost his whole life there. He studied medicine and philosophy at the university, graduating in 1759, and later became a lecturer and then a professor. In 1782 he was made Professor of Obstetrics, the medicine of helping babies be born. In 1762 he married Lucia Galeazzi, the daughter of one of his teachers, and she was a scientist in her own right who helped him in the laboratory. Some historians think she may have been the first person to notice the strange effect that made her husband famous, although nobody can be sure.","Galvani was especially interested in how muscles and nerves work. How does a thought in your brain make your leg kick? In the 1700s nobody knew. Some scientists thought nerves were hollow tubes that carried a special fluid, a bit like water pipes. Others wondered whether electricity, the exciting new force of the century, had something to do with it. Galvani decided to find out using frogs, which were easy to catch in the marshes and canals around Bologna and whose leg muscles keep twitching for a while after the frog has died.","From around 1780 Galvani spent years doing experiments with frog legs, often working on his lab bench near an electrical friction machine like the ones described in Part 3. In his own account, one of his assistants touched the nerve inside a frog's leg with the tip of a metal scalpel, just as a spark was being drawn from the electrical machine across the room. Suddenly, the leg muscles jerked violently, as if the frog had come back to life. Another person in the room noticed that the twitches happened exactly when the machine sparked.","This was very strange. The frog was not connected to the machine by any wire. Yet the spark somewhere else in the room seemed to reach out and make the dead leg move. Galvani was fascinated. He repeated the experiment again and again, changing one thing at a time, which is exactly what a careful scientist should do."]},{"heading":"Thunderstorms, Brass Hooks and Animal Electricity","paragraphs":["If a spark from a machine could make a frog leg twitch, Galvani wondered, could lightning do it too? He had read about Franklin's experiments with lightning rods. So he set up an iron wire on the roof of his house, like a lightning rod, and ran it down to some frog legs, with another wire leading to the water of a well. When storms came, the legs twitched each time lightning flashed in the sky. Electricity from the atmosphere seemed to have the same effect as electricity from his machine.","Then came the most puzzling result of all. Galvani had hung some prepared frog legs on brass hooks, pushed through the spinal cord, and hung the hooks on an iron railing outside, probably to wait for a storm. He noticed that the legs sometimes twitched even when the sky was perfectly calm and there was no lightning anywhere. He tried it indoors too. If he laid a frog on an iron plate and pressed the brass hook against the iron, the legs kicked. No machine, no storm, no Leyden jar. Just a dead frog, a piece of brass and a piece of iron.","Galvani went further. He made a curved bridge out of two different metals joined together, for example copper and zinc, or iron and silver. When he touched one end of this metal arc to the frog's nerve and the other end to its leg muscle, the leg twitched every single time. With an arc made of only one kind of metal, the twitching was much weaker or did not happen at all.","Where was this electricity coming from? Galvani came to a bold conclusion. He believed it came from inside the frog itself. Living animals, he suggested, produced their own special electricity in their brains, which was carried along the nerves and stored in the muscles, a bit like a tiny Leyden jar inside every muscle with charge on the inside and the outside. The metal arc, he thought, simply gave that stored animal electricity a path to flow along, and when it flowed, the muscle contracted. He called it animal electricity.","After about eleven years of work, in 1791 Galvani published his findings in Latin in a long essay whose title means Commentary on the Effects of Electricity on Muscular Motion. It caused a sensation. Scientists all over Europe started catching frogs and connecting their legs to pieces of metal. Some people even hoped that animal electricity might one day be used to cure paralysis or bring the dead back to life. Over the next few years, Galvani's name became so famous that the word galvanism was used for this kind of electricity, and today we still say a crowd is galvanised when it is suddenly jolted into action."]},{"heading":"Volta Has Doubts","paragraphs":["One of the scientists who read Galvani's essay with great excitement was Alessandro Volta, professor of physics at the University of Pavia, about 200 kilometres away from Bologna. Volta was born in 1745 in Como, a lakeside town near the Alps, into a noble but not very wealthy family. As a boy he was slow to start talking, and his family reportedly worried about him. But he grew into a brilliant, lively and curious man who loved electricity from his teenage years and wrote to famous scientists about it before he was twenty.","By 1791 Volta was already well known. In 1775 he had improved a device called the electrophorus, a simple tool for making and transferring static charge over and over again. In 1776, while boating on the marshy edge of Lake Maggiore, he noticed bubbles rising from the mud, collected them and found the gas would burn. It was what we now call methane, and by 1778 he had isolated it. He became professor at Pavia in 1779 and was a popular teacher. He also built extremely sensitive instruments for detecting tiny amounts of electric charge, which would turn out to be very important.","At first Volta was a fan of Galvani's animal electricity. He repeated the frog experiments and got the same twitches. But as he went on, something bothered him. Why did the experiment work so much better when the arc was made of two different metals? If the electricity was coming from the frog, the type of metal should not matter much. Yet it mattered a great deal.","Volta tried something clever and a little odd. He put a piece of one metal, such as tin foil, on the tip of his tongue, and a piece of a different metal, such as a silver coin, further back on his tongue, and touched the two metals together. He tasted a sour, sharp taste, which you might notice if you ever accidentally bite on foil with a metal filling. When he touched two metals to his eye in a similar way, he saw a flash of light. No frog was involved at all. To Volta this suggested that the electricity was being made where the two different metals met, and that the frog's leg was simply acting as a very sensitive detector, like a living electricity meter, twitching whenever even a tiny current passed through it.","From 1792 Volta began writing papers arguing that the important thing was the difference between the metals, not some special electricity inside animals. As he put it, it was the diversity of the metals that did it. He even ranked metals in order, finding that some pairs, like zinc and silver, gave a much stronger effect than others. This list was an early form of what chemists now call the electrochemical series."]},{"heading":"A Friendly but Serious Argument","paragraphs":["Volta and Galvani disagreed, but it was not a nasty fight. Volta respected Galvani, and it was Volta who named the effect galvanism in his honour. Both men were trying to find the truth, and each new experiment from one side pushed the other side to think harder. This is how science is supposed to work: an idea is tested, someone challenges it, and better experiments are designed to settle the question.","Galvani and his supporters did not give up. His nephew Giovanni Aldini, also a scientist, and another researcher named Eusebio Valli carried out new experiments to defend animal electricity. In the 1790s they showed that a frog leg could twitch even with no metal at all, for example when the cut end of a nerve was made to touch the frog's own muscle. If there was no metal, then the electricity must be coming from the animal. Volta replied that even different types of wet tissue might act like different conductors. The two sides wrote papers, letters and replies, and by 1798 their positions were too far apart to agree.","Sadly, Galvani's last years were unhappy. His wife Lucia died in 1790. Then, after the French army under Napoleon Bonaparte swept into northern Italy, Bologna became part of a new state called the Cisalpine Republic. All university professors were ordered to swear an oath of loyalty to it. Galvani refused, because he felt it went against his beliefs. As a result he lost his post as professor and his income. He went to live in his brother's house, and died in Bologna on 4 December 1798, while his nephew Giovanni Aldini and other friends were still trying to win back his post for him. He never saw the invention that his frogs had inspired.","So who was right? The surprising answer is: both of them, in different ways. Volta was right that two different metals touching a wet material create an electric current, and that this current was what made the frog legs kick in most of Galvani's metal experiments. But Galvani was right that living things use electricity too. We now know that every nerve in your body sends messages as tiny electrical pulses, made by charged particles moving in and out of the nerve cells. Your heart beats because of electrical signals, which is why doctors can record them with a machine called an electrocardiogram, or ECG. Some fish, such as electric eels and torpedo rays, can even produce shocks strong enough to stun prey. Galvani is often called the father of electrophysiology, the science of electricity in living bodies."]},{"heading":"Building the Pile","paragraphs":["To prove his point beyond doubt, Volta needed to show that two metals and something wet could make electricity with no animal anywhere in sight. And if one pair of metals made a little electricity, he reasoned, maybe many pairs connected together would make a lot more.","Volta tried several designs. In one, he set up a row of cups or wine glasses filled with salty water. In each glass he dipped a strip of zinc and a strip of copper or silver. Then he connected the zinc of the first glass to the copper of the next glass with a bent metal strip, and so on all the way along the row. Arranged in a circle, this was later called a crown of cups. The more cups he added, the stronger the effect.","His most famous design was even simpler. He took small round discs of zinc and discs of copper or silver, each about the size of a coin, and discs of cardboard or cloth that had been soaked in salt water or brine. He stacked them in a pattern: zinc, copper, wet cardboard, then zinc, copper, wet cardboard, again and again, until he had a column of twenty, forty or even sixty pairs. Glass rods on the sides held the pile upright. In Italian and French this stack was called a pila, meaning a pile or heap, and so it became known in English as the voltaic pile.","When Volta touched one wire to the top of the pile and another to the bottom, he felt a shock, saw a spark and could make a frog leg twitch, just like a weak Leyden jar. But there was an enormous difference. A Leyden jar gave a single snap and was empty. The pile kept on giving. Touch it again a moment later and the shock came again. Connect the two ends with a wire and electricity flowed through it continuously, minute after minute, until the metal was eaten away or the cardboard dried out. For the first time in history, a human being had made a steady electric current.","Volta compared his invention to the electric organ of the torpedo ray, a fish whose body contains stacks of special cells that let it give shocks. In fact he called his pile an artificial electric organ. It is a lovely irony: an animal helped inspire the very device that Volta used to argue against animal electricity."]},{"heading":"What Was Really Happening Inside","paragraphs":["Volta believed that the electricity was made simply by the two different metals touching each other, and that the wet cardboard just helped connect each pair to the next. He was a superb experimenter, but on this point he was mistaken. Over the next few years other scientists, especially the English chemist Humphry Davy, whom you will meet in Part 5, showed that the real source of the energy was a chemical reaction.","Here is what happens, in simple terms. Every material is made of atoms, and atoms contain tiny negatively charged particles called electrons. Zinc is a metal that gives up electrons fairly easily. When zinc touches salty water, some zinc atoms dissolve into the water, and each one leaves two electrons behind in the zinc metal. Those extra electrons build up on the zinc and push to get away. If you connect the zinc to the copper with a wire, the electrons rush along the wire toward the copper. At the copper surface they are picked up by particles in the salty water, and tiny bubbles of hydrogen gas form. As long as there is zinc left to dissolve and water to react, the flow keeps going. That flow of electrons through the wire is an electric current.","One zinc, copper and brine sandwich, called a cell, gives a push of a little less than one volt. That is not much; you would hardly feel it. But when cells are stacked one on top of another, their pushes add together. Forty cells give roughly thirty volts, enough to feel a definite tingle. This is exactly what happens in many devices today when several batteries are lined up end to end inside a toy or a torch. In fact the word battery, which Franklin first used for a row of Leyden jars, soon moved over to mean Volta's kind of chemical electricity maker, and has meant that ever since.","Notice that the battery does not make electric charge out of nothing, and the electricity is not used up as it flows around a circuit. The battery acts like a pump, using the energy stored in the chemicals to push charge around and around the loop of wire. When the zinc is mostly eaten away, the pump stops. That is why batteries go flat."]},{"heading":"A Letter to London and Bubbles in a Tube","paragraphs":["Volta wanted the most important scientists in the world to hear about his invention. On 20 March 1800 he wrote a letter in French to Sir Joseph Banks, the President of the Royal Society in London, describing the pile and the crown of cups in detail. It was a difficult time to send mail: Britain and France were at war, and much of Italy was a battleground. Volta sent the first part of the letter ahead, and the rest followed later. Banks read it with interest and showed it to friends before it was officially read to the Society and published later that year in the Society's journal, Philosophical Transactions, under a title meaning On the Electricity Excited by the Mere Contact of Conducting Substances of Different Kinds.","One of the people who heard about the letter early was Anthony Carlisle, a surgeon at Westminster Hospital in London. Carlisle was friends with William Nicholson, a remarkable man who had been a sailor, a writer, a translator, an inventor and a teacher, and who ran a monthly science magazine known as Nicholson's Journal. In late April and May 1800, the two men built their own pile at Nicholson's home in Soho, London. According to their report, it was made of 17 silver half-crown coins, an equal number of zinc discs and pieces of pasteboard soaked in salt water.","To make a good connection to the top of the pile, they put a drop of water on the metal plate and dipped a wire into it. To their surprise, little bubbles of gas began to form around the wire. Curious, they set up a proper test. They filled a small glass tube with water, closed both ends with corks, and pushed a brass wire through each cork so that the ends of the wires were close together inside the water but not touching. Then they connected the wires to the two ends of the pile.","Streams of tiny bubbles rose from one of the wires. When they tested the gas, it was hydrogen, the lightest gas of all. The other brass wire did not bubble much; instead it turned dark and tarnished, because the oxygen being produced was combining with the brass. When the experiment was later repeated with wires of platinum, a metal that does not easily react, both gases appeared as bubbles: hydrogen at one wire and oxygen at the other. And there was always about twice as much hydrogen as oxygen, which matches the recipe of water, H2O: two hydrogen atoms for every oxygen atom.","Nicholson and Carlisle had used electricity to split water into its two elements. This process is called electrolysis, from Greek words meaning breaking apart with electricity. It happened within weeks of the pile being described, and they published their results in Nicholson's Journal in the summer of 1800. Around the same time, a young German scientist, Johann Wilhelm Ritter, was doing similar experiments. Earlier, in 1789, two Dutch scientists, Adriaan Paets van Troostwijk and Jan Rudolph Deiman, had managed to split water using sparks from an electrical machine, but that method was slow and difficult. The voltaic pile made it easy. Suddenly chemists had a powerful new tool for pulling substances apart, and they rushed to use it on everything they could find."]},{"heading":"Volta Meets Napoleon","paragraphs":["News of the pile spread across Europe at astonishing speed. In September 1801 Volta travelled to Paris with his fellow scientist Luigi Brugnatelli. Paris was then the capital of science in Europe, and France was ruled by a young general who had made himself First Consul: Napoleon Bonaparte. Napoleon was fascinated by science and liked to be seen as its champion.","On 7 November 1801, Volta demonstrated his pile at a meeting of the Institut de France, the country's great scientific academy, with Napoleon himself in the audience. Volta showed how the pile produced shocks and sparks and argued that the electricity of his pile was the same as ordinary electricity from a friction machine. Napoleon was so impressed that he proposed a special gold medal for Volta, which was awarded the following month, along with a gift of money and a pension for life. Napoleon also set up a prize to reward future discoveries in electricity and galvanism.","The honours kept coming. In 1810 Napoleon made Volta a count, a noble title. Volta continued teaching at the University of Pavia until 1819, then retired to his home near Como. He died there on 5 March 1827 at the age of 82. In 1881, at an International Electrical Congress held in Paris, scientists from around the world agreed on official names for the units of electricity, and the unit of electrical push was named the volt in his honour. Every time you read 1.5 V on a battery or 230 V on a plug, you are reading Volta's name. In Como, a beautiful round museum on the lakeshore called the Tempio Voltiano displays his instruments today.","Why does all this matter so much? Before 1800, electricity came only in quick sparks and shocks. It was a curiosity, a party trick, a dangerous force pulled from the sky. After 1800, scientists could make electricity flow steadily for hours through a wire, and they could control how much flowed by adding or removing cells. This steady flow, the electric current, is the kind of electricity that would later run telegraphs, motors, light bulbs, phones and computers. Without a source of steady current, none of the inventions in the rest of this Deep Dive could have happened.","In the months after Volta's letter, laboratories all over Europe built piles of their own, some with hundreds of pairs of plates. And in London, at a new scientific institution on Albemarle Street, a brilliant young chemist was planning to build the biggest battery anyone had ever seen, with thousands of plates, to see just what this new current could do."]}],"deepDive":"The voltaic pile is an electrochemical cell repeated many times in series. In a zinc and copper cell with salt water as the electrolyte, zinc is oxidised at the negative electrode (Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻), releasing electrons into the external circuit. At the copper, those electrons reduce hydrogen ions or water, producing hydrogen gas. The copper does not get used up; it acts mainly as a conductor and a surface where the reaction happens. Each cell produces somewhere around 0.7 to 1 volt depending on the exact conditions, and cells in series add their voltages, so a 60-pair pile might give roughly 40 to 50 volts. Early piles had serious practical problems. Hydrogen bubbles coating the copper reduced the current (a problem called polarisation), the brine-soaked cardboard dried out, and the weight of the stack squeezed liquid out of the lower discs. Later designs, such as the trough battery and John Frederic Daniell's cell of 1836, were invented to give steadier, longer-lasting current. Historians of science find the Galvani and Volta debate a classic example of how two partly correct theories can drive progress. Volta's 'contact theory' held that electromotive force arises at the junction of dissimilar metals, and he defended it for the rest of his life. The 'chemical theory', supported by Davy and later by Michael Faraday, held that chemical reactions supply the energy; it won, because contact alone would amount to a perpetual-motion machine, getting energy for free. Volta was nonetheless correct that dissimilar conductors are needed and that animals were not required. Galvani, meanwhile, was vindicated in the 1800s when Carlo Matteucci and Emil du Bois-Reymond measured real electrical currents in muscles and nerves. Volta's pile also gives a useful lesson in naming: his device was not named after its function but after its shape, and the French word for battery is still 'pile' today.","funFact":"Alessandro Volta discovered methane, the gas that makes up most of natural gas, by stirring up bubbles from the muddy edge of Lake Maggiore in 1776, years before he invented the battery.","check":{"q":"What did Volta stack up to make the first battery, the voltaic pile?","a":"Discs of zinc and copper (or silver) separated by cardboard or cloth soaked in salt water","wrong":["Leyden jars filled with mercury","Frog legs hung on brass hooks","Spinning balls of sulfur and glass"]},"sources":[{"title":"Luigi Galvani","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luigi_Galvani"},{"title":"Galvanism","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galvanism"},{"title":"Alessandro Volta","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alessandro_Volta"},{"title":"Alessandro Volta","publisher":"Wikipedia (Italian)","url":"https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alessandro_Volta"},{"title":"Voltaic pile","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voltaic_pile"},{"title":"William Nicholson (chemist)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Nicholson_(chemist)"},{"title":"Electrolysis of water","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrolysis_of_water"},{"title":"Volt","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volt"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/04-1.jpg","alt":"Brass metal arcs once used by Luigi Galvani in his experiments on \"animal electricity\".","file":"Apparatus used by Galvani Wellcome L0011376.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apparatus_used_by_Galvani_Wellcome_L0011376.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/04-2.jpg","alt":"One of Alessandro Volta's piles: a stack of metal discs between rods, at the Tempio Voltiano in Como, Italy.","file":"VoltaBattery.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VoltaBattery.JPG","author":"GuidoB","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/04-3.jpg","alt":"Alessandro Volta shows his pile to Napoleon in Paris in 1801 (Le Petit Journal illustration, 1901).","file":"Volta présente son invention à Napoléon (crop to match).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volta_pr%C3%A9sente_son_invention_%C3%A0_Napol%C3%A9on_(crop_to_match).jpg","author":"Le Petit Journal","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3869},{"n":5,"title":"Davy's Dazzling Arc and Ørsted's Needle","when":"1800 – 1827","summary":"A daring chemist in London used giant batteries to pull brand-new metals out of ordinary stuff and to make a light brighter than anything people had seen. Then a teacher in Denmark watched a compass needle swing beside a wire, and the world learned that electricity and magnetism are secret partners. A young bookbinder named Michael Faraday was watching it all, and he built the very first electric motor.","sections":[{"heading":"The Showman Chemist of Albemarle Street","paragraphs":["Imagine pushing your way into a packed lecture hall in London in the early 1800s. Ladies in feathered hats, gentlemen in tall coats, poets, doctors and curious students squeeze onto curved wooden benches that rise steeply around a long table. On the table sit bottles, glass tubes, metal rods and rows of strange troughs full of liquid. A young man with curly hair and a quick, bright voice steps forward. Then there is a flash, a puff of smoke, and the audience gasps. This is the Royal Institution on Albemarle Street, and the man is Humphry Davy, the most exciting scientist in Britain.","Davy was born in 1778 in Penzance, a fishing town at the far south-western tip of Cornwall. He did not go to university. As a teenager he was apprenticed to a local surgeon and apothecary, a kind of chemist who mixed medicines, and he taught himself chemistry from books. He was also a keen poet and fisherman. In his early twenties he worked in Bristol, testing the effects of gases, including laughing gas, by breathing them himself, which was brave and also quite dangerous.","In 1801 the Royal Institution, a young organisation set up in 1799 to spread science and useful inventions, hired Davy as an assistant lecturer in chemistry and director of its laboratory, for 100 pounds a year. He gave his first lecture, on the new subject called galvanism, on 25 April 1801. Galvanism meant electricity made by chemical means, the kind discovered by Luigi Galvani and Alessandro Volta that you met in Part 4. By June 1802, aged just 23, Davy was promoted to full lecturer. His talks became so popular that they were one of the fashionable things to do in London.","What made Davy different from most chemists was one tool: the battery. Only a year after Volta announced his pile in 1800, Davy realised that a steady electric current was not just a curiosity. It was a force that could pull chemicals apart. He began building bigger and bigger batteries in the cellar of the Royal Institution, stacking hundreds and eventually thousands of metal plates in troughs of acid. In 1802 his battery was powerful enough to make a thin strip of platinum glow white-hot, an early hint of the electric light that later inventors would chase for decades. But Davy was mostly interested in something else: finding out what the world is made of."]},{"heading":"Prying Metals Out of Rocks and Ashes","paragraphs":["In 1800, chemists already knew many elements, the basic substances that cannot be broken down into anything simpler, such as gold, iron, sulfur and oxygen. But some common materials were puzzling. Potash, a white substance made by burning wood or plants and soaking the ashes in water, and soda, a similar substance from certain plants and minerals, were used for making soap and glass. Many chemists suspected that potash and soda contained unknown metals, but no fire, acid or chemical trick could split them.","Davy guessed that the atoms in these substances were held together by electrical attraction, so perhaps an even stronger electrical push could break them apart. This process of splitting a substance by passing an electric current through it is called electrolysis. Nicholson and Carlisle had already used it to split water into hydrogen and oxygen in 1800. Davy wanted to go further.","In the autumn of 1807 he took a lump of slightly damp potash, melted it, and passed a strong current through it from his large battery. At the negative wire, tiny shiny beads appeared, looking like drops of mercury. Some of them burst into bright flames the moment they formed. Others tarnished almost instantly, coating themselves with a white crust. Davy had found a brand-new metal, and he named it potassium, after potash. It was the first metal ever isolated by electrolysis. His cousin and assistant Edmund Davy later recalled that Humphry was so delighted that he danced about the laboratory.","Potassium is a very strange metal. It is soft enough to cut with a butter knife, it is lighter than water, and it reacts so violently with water that it fizzes, skates across the surface and catches fire with a lilac-coloured flame. That is why it had never been seen before: in nature it is always locked up tightly in compounds. Soon afterwards, using the same method on caustic soda, Davy produced another soft, silvery metal that also reacted furiously with water. He called it sodium. It is the same sodium that, joined with chlorine, makes the salt on your dinner table.","Davy did not stop there. In 1808 he reported to the Royal Society that he had isolated barium, calcium, strontium and magnesium, using electrolysis together with mercury to help collect the metals. Calcium is the element in your bones and teeth and in chalk; magnesium is the metal that burns with a blinding white light in old camera flashes and fireworks. In 1809 he isolated boron. In 1810 he showed that the green gas chemists called oxymuriatic acid contained no oxygen at all and was really an element, which he named chlorine, from the Greek word for pale green.","Think about that for a moment. In just a few years, one man with a battery in a London cellar added around half a dozen new elements to the list of known substances. Electricity had become a kind of super-tool for chemistry, able to do what fire could not. Davy became a celebrity across Europe. In 1807 the French Institute even awarded him a prize that Napoleon had set up for work on galvanism, although Britain and France were at war at the time. Davy accepted, arguing that scientists of different countries were not at war with each other."]},{"heading":"A Bridge of Blinding Light","paragraphs":["Davy's huge batteries had another trick. When he connected two sticks of charcoal to the ends of his battery, touched their tips together and then slowly pulled them apart, the current did not stop. Instead, it leapt across the gap as a brilliant, dazzling band of light, so bright it hurt to look at. Because his charcoal rods were held sideways, the rising hot air bent the glowing band upward into a curve like a bridge. Davy called it an arch, and the name slowly changed to arc. This was the electric arc, and the lamp made from it would be called the arc lamp.","Exactly when Davy first showed the arc is a little uncertain, and different books give dates from 1802 to 1809. He showed an early version to the Royal Society around 1806, and his most famous display came around 1808 to 1810, after the Royal Institution raised money for an enormous new battery of about 2,000 pairs of plates. With it Davy could stretch the arc across a gap of about 10 centimetres, around 4 inches. People who saw it compared it to a small piece of the Sun brought indoors.","How does an arc work? The current is so strong that it heats the tips of the carbon rods until they glow at more than 3,000 degrees Celsius, hotter than molten lava by a long way. The air in the gap becomes so hot that it starts to carry current itself, glowing as it does so. Carbon from the rods gradually turns into vapour and burns away, so the gap slowly gets wider, and someone has to keep pushing the rods closer together or the light goes out.","Davy was not the only person to find the arc. In Russia, a physicist named Vasily Petrov built an enormous battery of his own and described an electric arc between pieces of charcoal in 1803, but his work was written in Russian and was hardly known in the rest of Europe for many years. This is a pattern you will see again and again in the story of electricity: good ideas often appear in more than one place at about the same time.","Could the arc light up homes? Not a chance, at least not then. First, it was far too bright: one arc was like hundreds of candles blazing at once, perfect for a giant hall but blinding in a sitting room. Second, it hissed, flickered and sputtered, and it gave off fumes. Third, and most importantly, it was hungry. Davy's 2,000-plate battery used up its zinc and acid quickly and cost a fortune to rebuild. In 1810 there were no power stations and no generators. The arc was a magnificent show, but for the next sixty years it remained mostly a laboratory wonder. You will see arc lights finally leave the lab and light up streets, lighthouses and even a football match in Part 7."]},{"heading":"The Bookbinder's Apprentice","paragraphs":["Among the crowds who came to hear Davy was a young man who could not normally have afforded a ticket. His name was Michael Faraday. He was born on 22 September 1791 in Newington Butts, then a village just south of London, into a poor family. His father was a blacksmith who was often ill, and the family sometimes did not have enough bread. Michael went to school only long enough to learn reading, writing and simple arithmetic.","At the age of 14 he became an apprentice to George Riebau, a bookbinder and bookseller on Blandford Street in London. An apprentice was a young worker who learned a trade by working for a master, usually for seven years. For Faraday this was the perfect job, because he was surrounded by books all day. He did not only stitch and glue them; he read them. He was especially gripped by an article on electricity in the Encyclopaedia Britannica and by a popular book called Conversations on Chemistry by Jane Marcet, which explained chemistry through friendly discussions. He began doing small experiments of his own with whatever he could afford, building a simple electrical machine from bottles and scraps.","In 1812, near the end of his apprenticeship, a customer of the shop named William Dance gave Faraday tickets to hear Humphry Davy's lectures at the Royal Institution. Faraday sat high up in the gallery and wrote careful notes. Afterwards he copied them out neatly, added drawings of the equipment, and, being a bookbinder, bound them into a handsome volume of about 300 pages. He sent this book to Davy along with a letter asking for any job in science, however humble.","Davy was impressed, but at first he had no job to give. Then fate stepped in, in a slightly alarming way. In 1812 Davy was injured in the eye by an explosion while working with a dangerous compound called nitrogen trichloride, and for a while he could not write. Faraday helped him with his notes. Soon afterwards, a laboratory assistant at the Royal Institution was dismissed after a fight, and there was a vacancy. On 1 March 1813 Faraday was hired as Chemical Assistant, with a small wage and two rooms at the top of the building.","Only a few months later, in October 1813, Davy set off on a long tour of Europe, and he took Faraday with him as his assistant. Although Britain and France were still at war, Napoleon's government gave Davy permission to travel. For about eighteen months the young bookbinder saw Paris, the Alps and Italy, and met some of the greatest scientists of the age, including the elderly Alessandro Volta himself. It was Faraday's university. By the time they returned to London in 1815, he had learned more than any classroom could have taught him, and he was ready for the most exciting decade in the history of electricity."]},{"heading":"A Needle That Would Not Sit Still","paragraphs":["Now we travel north, across the sea to Copenhagen, the capital of Denmark. Here lived Hans Christian Ørsted, born in 1777 in the small town of Rudkøbing, the son of a pharmacist. He became a professor at the University of Copenhagen and was famous for his lively lectures and his love of big ideas.","For a long time, scientists treated electricity and magnetism as two quite separate things. You may remember from Part 2 that William Gilbert worked hard in 1600 to separate the magnet's pull from the amber effect. Electricity made sparks and shocks; magnetism made compass needles point north. Yet there were hints of a connection. Sailors reported that lightning strikes sometimes reversed the needles of ships' compasses, and some thinkers, including Ørsted, believed that all the forces of nature were really different faces of one deep unity. From about 1818 Ørsted was actively searching for a link between electricity and magnetism.","In the spring of 1820 he saw what he had been looking for. When a current from a battery flowed through a wire placed near a compass, the needle swung away from north. When the current stopped, the needle swung back. There is a popular story that this happened by pure accident in the middle of a lecture, but historians who have studied Ørsted's own accounts treat that as a myth. He had been looking for exactly this kind of effect, even if the result puzzled him at first. The surprising part was the direction. The needle did not point toward or away from the wire, as a magnet would be pulled toward iron. It turned sideways.","Ørsted spent the following weeks repeating the experiment with stronger batteries and trying different arrangements. He put glass, wood, water and metal between the wire and the needle, and the effect still passed straight through them. In July 1820 he published his results in a short report written in Latin, the international language of scholars, and sent copies to scientists all over Europe. It was only a few pages long, but it changed physics forever. A little later he realised what the sideways swing meant: the current creates a magnetic influence that circles around the wire, like rings around a finger.","You can picture it this way. Hold a pencil upright and imagine invisible hoops circling it at every height. If a current flows up the pencil, a tiny compass placed anywhere around it will point along those hoops. Reverse the current, and every compass flips to point the other way round. Electricity flowing in a straight line makes magnetism that goes in circles. That single fact is the starting point for electric motors, loudspeakers, doorbells, MRI scanners and electric cars.","The news spread like wildfire. Within months, scientists in Geneva, Paris, London and Berlin were all repeating the experiment. The Royal Society in London gave Ørsted its highest honour, the Copley Medal, in 1820. Today a unit of magnetic field strength used in older textbooks, the oersted, is named after him."]},{"heading":"Ampère's Racing Mind","paragraphs":["In September 1820 the French astronomer François Arago showed Ørsted's experiment to the Academy of Sciences in Paris. In the audience sat André-Marie Ampère, a mathematics professor born in Lyon in 1775. Ampère had had a hard life. He learned mostly from his father's library at home, and when he was a teenager his father was executed by guillotine during the French Revolution, in 1793. Ampère was known for being absent-minded and deeply thoughtful, and when he saw the swinging needle, his mind took off.","Within weeks he had made his own discoveries and presented them to the Academy. His big idea was this: if a current makes magnetism, then two wires carrying currents should push or pull on each other, even with no magnets around at all. He tested it, and he was right. Two parallel wires with currents flowing the same way attract each other. If the currents flow in opposite directions, the wires push apart. Electricity in motion behaves like magnetism.","Ampère went further. He wound wire into a long coil, which he named a solenoid, from a Greek word meaning pipe-shaped. When current flowed through it, the coil behaved just like a bar magnet, with a north end and a south end. Ampère suggested something bold: perhaps ordinary magnets are magnetic because tiny electric currents are circling inside them. He could not prove it, because nobody yet knew about electrons, but modern physics shows he was on to something important.","Most of all, Ampère turned the effect into mathematics. He worked out formulas that predicted exactly how strong the force between two currents would be, depending on how big the currents were, how far apart the wires were, and the angle between them. He called this new science electrodynamics, and he gathered it in a great memoir published in 1826. Around the same time, others found practical uses. In 1820 the German scientist Johann Schweigger wound wire many times around a compass to make the needle swing more strongly, creating an early galvanometer, an instrument for detecting and measuring current.","Decades later James Clerk Maxwell, whom you will meet in Part 6, called Ampère the Newton of electricity. In 1881 an international meeting of scientists named the unit of current the ampere, or amp for short, in his honour. Every time you read that a phone charger supplies 2 amps, you are using Ampère's name."]},{"heading":"The First Electric Motor","paragraphs":["Back in London, Michael Faraday had been promoted and was now doing his own research. In 1821 a friend, the editor of a science journal, asked him to write a review of everything that had been discovered about electricity and magnetism since Ørsted. Faraday, being Faraday, refused to simply copy what others said. He repeated the experiments himself, one after another.","Thinking about the circles of magnetic force around a wire, he had a brilliant idea. If the force goes round in circles, then a wire that is free to move should be pushed round and round a magnet, and never stop as long as the current flows. In early September 1821 he built a simple device to test it. He stood a bar magnet upright in a bowl and filled the bowl with mercury, a liquid metal that conducts electricity. From above, he hung a wire so that its lower end dipped into the mercury and could swing freely. Then he connected a battery so that current flowed down the wire and through the mercury.","The wire began to circle around the magnet, round and round, steadily, like a dancer twirling around a pole. In a second version, the wire was fixed and the magnet was free to move, and the magnet circled the wire. Faraday called it electromagnetic rotation. It was the first time anyone had turned electric current into continuous motion. In simple terms, it was the first electric motor. Faraday's brother-in-law later remembered him dancing around the apparatus with excitement.","It was only a toy at first. It could not lift anything or turn a wheel usefully, and it needed a dish of poisonous mercury. But it proved a principle: electricity could make things move. Every electric motor today, from the tiny one that vibrates a phone to the huge ones that drive high-speed trains, works by electric currents and magnets pushing on each other.","Sadly, the discovery also caused trouble. William Hyde Wollaston, a senior and respected scientist, had been trying to make a current-carrying wire spin on its own axis, and Davy had been involved too. When Faraday published his results without mentioning them, some people accused him of using Wollaston's ideas. Faraday was deeply upset and explained that his device worked in a different way. Wollaston himself seems to have accepted this, but the incident strained Faraday's friendship with his old master. When Faraday was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society in 1824, Davy, by then its president, is reported to have opposed it. The bookbinder had become a rival."]},{"heading":"Magnets You Can Switch On and Off","paragraphs":["Ampère had shown that a coil of wire carrying current acts like a magnet. An English inventor named William Sturgeon took the next step. Sturgeon had a tough start in life: he joined the army in 1802 and taught himself mathematics and science in his spare time. Later he became a lecturer at a military college at Addiscombe, near London.","In 1824 and 1825 Sturgeon wound bare copper wire around a horseshoe-shaped piece of iron, varnished so that the wire would not touch the metal directly. When he connected a battery, the iron became a powerful magnet. His electromagnet weighed only about 7 ounces, roughly 200 grams, yet it could lift about 9 pounds, around 4 kilograms, which is about twenty times its own weight. And here was the amazing part: when he disconnected the battery, the iron lost most of its magnetism and dropped its load. Here was a magnet you could switch on and off.","Across the Atlantic, in Albany, New York, a schoolteacher named Joseph Henry made electromagnets far stronger. Sturgeon's bare wire could only be wound in one loose layer, or the turns would touch and the current would take a short cut. Henry solved this by insulating the wire, wrapping it in silk, so he could wind layer upon layer, hundreds and then thousands of turns. Starting around 1830 his magnets became astonishing. One of them, built in the early 1830s, could hold up 2,063 pounds, about 936 kilograms, which is close to a tonne, the weight of a small car.","Henry also showed that the same electromagnet could be worked from a distance, by sending current through a long wire to make a bell ring or a lever click. In 1835 he invented the electromagnetic relay, a switch worked by a small electromagnet. These were exactly the building blocks of the electric telegraph that you will meet in Part 7, and electromagnets are still everywhere today, in scrapyard cranes, electric locks, speakers and hospital scanners.","Meanwhile in Germany, a schoolteacher in Cologne named Georg Simon Ohm was asking a quieter question: how much current flows through a wire, and why? He carefully measured currents through wires of different lengths and thicknesses. In 1827 he published a book called Die galvanische Kette, mathematisch bearbeitet, which means The Galvanic Circuit Investigated Mathematically. In it he showed that the current in a wire depends on the push driving it, which we now call voltage, divided by how much the wire holds the current back, which we call resistance. This is Ohm's law. You will see how it works inside a light bulb in Part 11.","At first, Ohm's work was coldly received. Some German scientists thought it was too mathematical and not proper experimental science, and Ohm left his teaching job disappointed. Recognition came slowly but surely: in 1841 the Royal Society in London gave him the Copley Medal, and the unit of resistance, the ohm, is named after him.","By 1827, then, the world had learned a huge amount in just a few years. A current could split chemicals, make dazzling light, swing a compass, attract another wire, spin a wire around a magnet and turn a lump of iron into a crane. Electricity could make magnetism. And Michael Faraday, scribbling in his notebook at the Royal Institution, could not stop thinking about one question. If electricity can make magnetism, can magnetism make electricity?"]}],"deepDive":"Why was Ørsted's result so shocking to physicists in 1820? Every force known before it, such as gravity and the pull between electric charges, acts along the straight line joining two objects. Ørsted's needle did something new: the force from the current pointed sideways, around the wire. Today we describe this with the idea of a magnetic field whose lines form closed circles around a current. The strength of that field falls off with distance, roughly in proportion to 1 divided by the distance from a long straight wire, which Jean-Baptiste Biot and Félix Savart measured in Paris in 1820. Ampère's achievement was to write the force between two current-carrying circuits as a mathematical law, and his work later became the basis of the official definition of the ampere: for most of the twentieth century, one ampere was defined as the current that, flowing in two thin parallel wires one metre apart, produces a force of 2 × 10^-7 newtons per metre of length between them. (Since 2019 the ampere has been defined instead by fixing the charge of a single electron.) Historians also debate how to share credit for the motor. Faraday's 1821 rotation device turned current into continuous motion, but it was weak and needed mercury. In 1822 Peter Barlow built a spinning star-shaped wheel dipping into mercury, and in 1832 William Sturgeon showed a motor with a commutator, a rotating switch that reverses the current at just the right moment so the motor keeps turning in one direction. Practical motors grew from all these steps. Davy's arc raises another debate: he is often named its discoverer, yet Vasily Petrov described a continuous arc in 1803 and the dates of Davy's own early demonstrations are uncertain. A sensible conclusion is that the arc was found independently by several experimenters once batteries became large enough. Finally, Ohm's law looks simple, V = I × R, but in 1827 it was hard to test, because batteries of the time gave voltages that drifted as they were used. Ohm's careful measurements were part of what made his result convincing in the end.","funFact":"Potassium, the metal Humphry Davy discovered with his battery in 1807, is so soft you could cut it with a butter knife, and it reacts so strongly with water that it bursts into a lilac-coloured flame.","check":{"q":"What did Hans Christian Ørsted see in 1820 that showed electricity and magnetism are linked?","a":"A compass needle swung when an electric current flowed through a nearby wire","wrong":["A frog's leg twitched when touched with two metals","A kite collected sparks from a storm cloud","A copper disc made electricity when it spun between magnets"]},"sources":[{"title":"Humphry Davy","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humphry_Davy"},{"title":"Arc lamp","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arc_lamp"},{"title":"Michael Faraday","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Faraday"},{"title":"Michael Faraday (biography)","publisher":"The IET Archives","url":"https://www.theiet.org/membership/library-and-archives/the-iet-archives/biographies/michael-faraday"},{"title":"Hans Christian Ørsted","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_Christian_%C3%98rsted"},{"title":"André-Marie Ampère","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9-Marie_Amp%C3%A8re"},{"title":"Electromagnet","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnet"},{"title":"Georg Ohm","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georg_Ohm"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/05-1.jpg","alt":"Sir Humphry Davy painted by Sir Thomas Lawrence, with his miners' safety lamp on the table beside him.","file":"Sir Humphry Davy, Bt by Sir Thomas Lawrence.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Humphry_Davy,_Bt_by_Sir_Thomas_Lawrence.jpg","author":"Thomas Lawrence","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/05-2.jpg","alt":"19th-century school apparatus for Ørsted's experiment: a current in the wire swings the compass needle.","file":"Oersted experiment.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oersted_experiment.png","author":"Agustin Privat-Deschanel","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/05-3.jpg","alt":"Faraday's 1821 drawing of electromagnetic rotation: a wire and a magnet circling in cups of mercury.","file":"Faraday magnetic rotation.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faraday_magnetic_rotation.jpg","author":"Michael Faraday","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4174},{"n":6,"title":"Faraday's Magic Ring","when":"1831 – 1880s","summary":"Michael Faraday wrapped two coils of wire around an iron ring and discovered how to make electricity from magnets. Then he spun a copper disc between magnet poles and built the very first generator. His big idea of invisible force fields helped later scientists discover radio waves, and he loved sharing science with children at Christmas.","sections":[{"heading":"Ten Years of Wondering","paragraphs":["At the end of Part 5 we left Michael Faraday with a question buzzing in his head. Ørsted had shown in 1820 that an electric current makes magnetism. Nature often seems to work in pairs, so surely the reverse should be true too. Surely a magnet could make an electric current. Faraday is often said to have written a reminder to himself in his notebook in 1822: convert magnetism into electricity.","It sounds simple, but it was not. Faraday and many other scientists tried. They put strong magnets next to coils of wire and watched the needles of their meters. Nothing happened. They wound wires around magnets and waited. Nothing. A magnet sitting still next to a wire produced no current at all, however long you left it. In Paris, François Arago noticed in 1824 that a spinning copper disc could drag a hanging magnet round with it, a strange effect that no one could explain. The answer was hiding right there, but nobody yet saw it.","Faraday was busy with many other things during these years. In 1825 he discovered benzene, a chemical that is now used to make plastics, dyes and medicines. He spent years trying to make better glass for telescopes. He became director of the laboratory at the Royal Institution in 1825, and he started the Friday Evening Discourses, talks for the public that still take place today. He also married Sarah Barnard in 1821, and they lived in rooms at the top of the Royal Institution building for decades. They never had children of their own, but they helped raise a niece and loved having young visitors.","In 1829 Humphry Davy died in Geneva, aged only 50. With his old master gone, and his reputation now secure, Faraday felt free to return to the big question. In the summer of 1831 he went back to it with full force."]},{"heading":"29 August 1831: The Magic Ring","paragraphs":["Imagine the basement laboratory of the Royal Institution on a late summer day in 1831. It is cool and a little damp, lit by daylight from high windows and the glow of a furnace. On the bench lies a ring of soft iron, about 15 centimetres, or 6 inches, across and as thick as a finger. Faraday has spent days wrapping it with copper wire. Because there is no plastic in 1831, he insulates the wire by winding cotton thread and calico between the layers.","He has wound two separate coils on opposite sides of the ring. He calls one side A and the other side B. The two coils do not touch each other at all. They are connected only through the iron. Coil B is joined by a long wire to a galvanometer, a needle that swings when current flows. The wire is long enough that the battery's own magnetism will not disturb the needle. Coil A can be connected to a battery.","On 29 August 1831, Faraday connected coil A to the battery. At that instant, the needle connected to coil B gave a sudden kick, swung, and then settled back to zero. Current was flowing steadily through coil A, the iron ring was now a magnet, but coil B produced nothing more. Then he disconnected the battery. Again the needle kicked, but this time in the opposite direction, and settled back.","Faraday realised what was happening. A steady magnet did nothing. But at the moment the magnetism was changing, growing as the current switched on or vanishing as it switched off, a brief current was pushed through the second coil. He described it as a wave of electricity. The secret was not magnetism. It was changing magnetism. That is why everyone had failed for ten years: they had been waiting for a still magnet to do something, and a still magnet never will.","Today we call this effect electromagnetic induction: making an electric current by changing the magnetism passing through a coil, or by moving a wire and a magnet past each other. Faraday's ring was, in fact, the first transformer, a device with two coils on an iron core. In Part 13 you will see how transformers like this became one of the most important machines in the whole electrical world. Faraday's original ring still survives and is displayed at the Royal Institution in London."]},{"heading":"A Magnet Plunged into a Coil","paragraphs":["Faraday did not rush to announce his discovery. Over the next few weeks he tested it in every way he could think of, because he wanted to be absolutely sure. If changing magnetism was the key, he reasoned, then he did not need a battery at all. He could make the magnetism change simply by moving a magnet.","In the autumn of 1831 he wound a long coil of wire around a hollow paper tube and connected it to his galvanometer. Then he took an ordinary bar magnet and plunged it quickly into the tube. The needle swung. He pulled the magnet out again. The needle swung the other way. When the magnet sat still inside the coil, nothing happened. Push it in, current one way. Pull it out, current the other way. The faster he moved the magnet, the bigger the kick.","For the first time in history, someone had made electricity from motion and a magnet, without any battery or chemicals. Think about how important this is. A battery makes electricity from chemicals, and when the chemicals are used up, it stops. But motion can come from almost anywhere: a turning water wheel, a steam engine, a windmill, a falling river, a person turning a handle. If you can turn motion into electricity, you can make as much electricity as you have motion to spare.","You can try a gentle version of this yourself with a bicycle dynamo, the small generator that rubs against a tyre to power a bike lamp. Inside is a magnet that spins next to a coil of wire. When the bike stands still, the lamp is dark. Pedal slowly and it glows dimly. Pedal fast and it shines brightly, because the magnetism through the coil is changing faster. Wind-up torches and some hand-cranked radios work in exactly the same way, and so does the generator in a car that keeps its battery charged. Each one is a direct descendant of the bar magnet Faraday pushed into a paper tube in 1831.","There is one more important rule, worked out by the Russian-German physicist Heinrich Lenz in 1834. The current that is induced always flows in a direction that tries to resist the change causing it. That means you have to push a little harder to move the magnet when current is flowing. Nothing is free: the energy in the electricity comes from the work your muscles, or a steam engine, put in. This is why big generators need huge engines or turbines to turn them.","In late November 1831 Faraday read the first of his famous papers on the subject to the Royal Society, as part of a long series he called Experimental Researches in Electricity. He kept careful numbered notes of his experiments for decades, and his laboratory diary, thousands of paragraphs long, is still kept at the Royal Institution."]},{"heading":"The Spinning Copper Disc","paragraphs":["Plunging a magnet in and out of a coil makes only short bursts of current. Faraday wanted something better: a machine that made electricity continuously, for as long as you kept it moving. In the autumn of 1831 he found one, and his notebook entry of 28 October describes the experiment.","He took a flat disc of copper and mounted it on an axle so it could spin like a wheel. He placed it between the poles of a large, powerful horseshoe magnet, so that the edge of the disc passed through the strong magnetism between the poles. Then he pressed one sliding contact against the axle in the middle and another against the rim of the disc, and joined both to his galvanometer with wires. When he turned a handle and the disc spun, the needle moved and stayed moved. As long as the disc kept turning, a steady current flowed.","Why does it work? Think of the copper disc as millions of thin spokes running from the centre to the edge. As each spoke sweeps through the magnetism, the charges inside it are pushed along the spoke, outward or inward depending on the direction of spin. So there is always a push between the centre of the disc and its rim, and if you connect them with a wire, current flows around the circuit. Turn the disc the other way and the current reverses.","This Faraday disc was the first electric generator, also called a dynamo: a machine that turns motion into electricity. It was not very good. The voltage it made was tiny, much less than a single modern torch battery, and some of the current swirled around uselessly inside the disc itself, warming it up instead of flowing out through the wires. It was never used as a practical power source. But it showed, beyond any doubt, that a turning machine could produce a steady supply of electricity.","Every big power station in the world today, whether it burns gas, splits atoms or catches falling water, contains generators that work by the principle Faraday discovered in 1831: a magnet and coils of wire moving past each other. Only solar panels and batteries make electricity in a really different way. When you switch on a light at home, there is a good chance a giant spinning descendant of Faraday's little disc is turning somewhere for you."]},{"heading":"Meanwhile, in Albany: Joseph Henry","paragraphs":["Faraday was not alone. Remember Joseph Henry, the American teacher from Part 5 who built electromagnets that could lift nearly a tonne? He was born in Albany, New York in 1797 into a poor family, worked for a while as a watchmaker's apprentice and even acted in a theatre group, before a book of popular science lectures turned his attention to science. He became professor of mathematics and natural philosophy at the Albany Academy, a school for boys, in 1826.","Henry had a problem Faraday did not have: time. He taught for many hours a day, and the only period he could use the school's large hall for experiments was during the summer holidays. Yet working with his powerful electromagnets, Henry noticed the same effect Faraday found. When the current in one coil was switched on or off, a current appeared briefly in another coil nearby. Some historians think he may have observed induction around 1831, possibly even before Faraday, though his notes are incomplete and the exact date is uncertain.","But Henry was slow to publish. He wanted to finish his experiments properly, and he was far from the main scientific journals of Europe. When he read about Faraday's results in 1832, he quickly wrote up his own findings and published them in the American Journal of Science. Henry also discovered something Faraday had not yet studied in detail: self-induction, the way a single coil resists sudden changes in its own current. When you break the circuit of a big coil, the collapsing magnetism can produce a big spark. Faraday published his own work on this a little later.","So who discovered electromagnetic induction? Faraday published first and explored it far more thoroughly, so he receives the main credit. But it is fair to say that Henry discovered it independently, at about the same time, on the other side of an ocean. Science honours both men: the unit of capacitance, the farad, is named after Faraday, and the unit of inductance, the henry, is named after Henry.","Henry went on to a remarkable career. In 1832 he became a professor at the College of New Jersey, now Princeton University, and in 1846 he became the first Secretary of the brand-new Smithsonian Institution in Washington, D.C., a post he held until his death in 1878. He helped turn the Smithsonian into one of the most important science institutions in the United States."]},{"heading":"Pixii's Hand-Cranked Machine","paragraphs":["News of Faraday's discovery reached Paris quickly, and an instrument maker there named Hippolyte Pixii, only in his early twenties, set to work. His family made scientific instruments, and in 1832 he built one of the first machines designed to produce electricity from motion in a useful way.","Pixii's machine turned Faraday's idea around. Instead of moving a wire past a still magnet, it spun the magnet. A horseshoe-shaped permanent magnet was mounted on an upright axle, and a hand crank with gears turned it. Just above the magnet's poles sat two coils of wire wound on iron cores. As the magnet spun, its north and south poles took turns sweeping past the coils, and each pass produced a pulse of current.","Pixii noticed something interesting: the current reversed direction every time a north pole passed the coils after a south pole. In other words, his machine naturally produced alternating current, electricity that switches direction back and forth. This is what spinning magnets do. We will come back to that fact in a big way in Part 13 and Part 14, when alternating current becomes the centre of a great industrial battle.","In 1832, though, scientists wanted current that flowed one way, like the current from a battery, because that was what they used for electrolysis and experiments. So, on the suggestion of André-Marie Ampère, Pixii added a commutator, a clever rotating switch on the axle that flipped the connections every half turn. The output then always flowed in the same direction, though it came in bumpy pulses rather than smoothly.","Pixii's machine is called a magneto, because it used permanent magnets. It was still small and turned by hand, but it was a true ancestor of the dynamos that would light cities later in the century. A reproduction of it is displayed at the Ampère Museum near Lyon in France. Over the next forty years, inventors in many countries improved these machines step by step, adding more coils, using electromagnets instead of permanent magnets and driving them with steam engines, until, as you will see in Part 7, they could finally make enough electricity to power arc lights and industry."]},{"heading":"Invisible Lines of Force","paragraphs":["Faraday had very little mathematics. He had left school early and never studied algebra or calculus. So instead of equations, he thought in pictures. Scatter iron filings on a sheet of paper over a magnet and tap the paper gently. The filings arrange themselves into curving lines that loop from one pole to the other. Faraday believed these lines of force were not just a pretty pattern. He thought they were real, filling the space around every magnet and every current, like invisible stretched rubber bands.","This was a radical idea. Most scientists of the time thought of forces as acting directly across empty space between objects, the way Newton described gravity. Faraday instead imagined space itself filled with something, what we now call a field. In his picture, induction happened whenever a wire cut across lines of magnetic force, or when the lines passing through a coil changed in number. It was a simple, beautiful way of seeing it, and it gave correct predictions.","In 1845 Faraday made another stunning discovery. He passed a beam of light through a special heavy glass placed in a strong magnetic field, and found that the magnetism twisted the direction in which the light waves vibrated. This is now called the Faraday effect. It was the first experimental evidence that light and magnetism are connected. Faraday began to wonder whether light itself might be a kind of vibration of his lines of force.","The person who turned Faraday's pictures into mathematics was a young Scottish physicist, James Clerk Maxwell, born in Edinburgh in 1831, the very year of Faraday's ring. Maxwell admired Faraday deeply and often attended lectures at the Royal Institution. In papers published in 1861 and 1862 he built a model of Faraday's lines of force, and he calculated how fast a disturbance in electric and magnetic fields would travel. The answer came out at almost exactly the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometres per second. Maxwell wrote that we can scarcely avoid the conclusion that light is made of waves in the same medium that causes electric and magnetic effects.","In 1865 Maxwell published A Dynamical Theory of the Electromagnetic Field, setting out his theory fully, and in 1873 his great book, A Treatise on Electricity and Magnetism. His equations showed that light is an electromagnetic wave, and they also predicted that there should be other electromagnetic waves, with wavelengths much longer or shorter than visible light. Maxwell died in 1879, aged only 48, before anyone could prove him right.","The proof came from Germany. Between 1886 and 1888, at the technical university in Karlsruhe, Heinrich Hertz built a transmitter with two metal rods about a metre long and a small gap between them. A high-voltage coil made sparks leap across the gap, and those sparks sent out invisible waves. Across the room, a simple loop of wire with its own tiny gap acted as a receiver; when the waves arrived, a faint spark jumped in it. Hertz measured waves a few metres long and showed they travelled at the speed of light and could be reflected, just as Maxwell had predicted. He had made the first radio waves on purpose. Asked what his discovery was good for, Hertz is reported to have said that it was of no use whatsoever. Within a few years, others used those same waves to send wireless messages, and today they carry radio, television, Wi-Fi and mobile phone signals. The unit of frequency, the hertz, is named after him."]},{"heading":"Plain Mr Faraday","paragraphs":["What was Michael Faraday like as a person? By all accounts he was modest, gentle and deeply religious. He belonged to a small Christian group called the Sandemanians, who believed in living simply and humbly, and he served as a deacon and elder in their church. He refused to get rich from his discoveries and did a great deal of unpaid work for the public, such as advising on lighthouses and safety in coal mines.","He received many honours, but he turned down a knighthood. He wanted to remain, as he put it, plain Mr Faraday to the end. He also twice declined the offer to become president of the Royal Society. Despite having no university education, he became one of the most respected scientists in the world.","Faraday also cared about explaining science to everyone, especially young people. In 1825 the Royal Institution began a series of Christmas Lectures for young audiences, a tradition Faraday championed. He gave them himself nineteen times between 1827 and 1860, more than anyone else in their history. His most famous series, in 1848, was called The Chemical History of a Candle. Using nothing more than a candle flame, he explained combustion, air, water and much of chemistry, and the lectures became a book that is still in print. The Christmas Lectures continue today, have been shown on British television for decades, and have stopped only during the Second World War.","In 1836 he showed another famous effect. He built a room-sized box covered in metal foil, stepped inside, and had powerful sparks discharged onto the outside. Inside, his instruments detected no charge at all: electricity stays on the outside of a closed metal shell. This Faraday cage is why people are fairly safe inside a car or aeroplane struck by lightning, and why a microwave oven keeps its waves inside.","As Faraday grew older, his memory began to fail. In 1848 Queen Victoria and Prince Albert gave him the use of a house at Hampton Court, near London, where he moved fully in 1858. He ended his service at the Royal Institution in 1865 after more than fifty years there, and he died at Hampton Court on 25 August 1867, aged 75. He was buried simply, in a Sandemanian plot in Highgate Cemetery in London.","Faraday's discoveries are everywhere. The electric motor, the generator, the transformer, the idea of the field that led to radio: all of them trace back to that unschooled bookbinder's apprentice with his coils of wire and his notebook. And the most important of them was the discovery of 29 August 1831. Before it, electricity came only from rubbing machines and expensive batteries that soon ran down. After it, electricity could be made from motion, any motion at all. The key was in the lock. Within a few decades, people would build machines to make electricity on a giant scale, and set it to work."]}],"deepDive":"Faraday stated his discovery in words, but it can be written today as Faraday's law of induction: the voltage induced in a loop of wire equals the rate at which the magnetic flux through it changes. Magnetic flux is roughly the strength of the magnetic field multiplied by the area it passes through. So there are three ways to induce a voltage: change the field strength (as when coil A on the ring was switched on), move the magnet or the coil (the bar magnet in the tube), or change the area or angle (a coil spinning in a field, as in every modern generator). If the coil has N turns, the voltage is N times bigger, which is why generators and transformers use many turns of wire. Lenz's law, from 1834, adds the minus sign: the induced current always opposes the change that caused it. This is the law of conservation of energy in disguise; if the induced current helped the change instead, you could build a machine that sped itself up forever, which is impossible. Historians still discuss the question of priority with Joseph Henry. Henry's notebooks from Albany are incomplete, and he did not publish until 1832, after Faraday's results were known, so his exact date is uncertain; most historians conclude that he found induction independently but cannot be shown to have found it first. Faraday's insistence on fields was also controversial. Many leading physicists of the 1830s and 1840s preferred action at a distance, and some of them did not take lines of force seriously until Maxwell's equations made them precise. In Maxwell's theory, a changing magnetic field produces an electric field, and a changing electric field produces a magnetic field; the two can keep regenerating each other and travel through empty space as a wave at a speed set by two measured electrical constants, about 3 × 10^8 metres per second. That number matching the measured speed of light was one of the great surprises of nineteenth-century science, and Hertz's experiments confirmed it. Einstein, who kept a picture of Faraday on his study wall, built his special theory of relativity in 1905 partly on puzzles from electromagnetic induction.","funFact":"Faraday's 1831 iron ring, the first transformer, still exists and is on display at the Royal Institution in London, close to the basement laboratory where he made it.","check":{"q":"What was the secret Faraday discovered about making electricity with magnetism?","a":"The magnetism had to be changing or moving; a still magnet makes no current","wrong":["The magnet had to be made of copper","The magnet had to be heated until it glowed","The magnet had to touch the wire directly"]},"sources":[{"title":"Electromagnetic induction","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_induction"},{"title":"Michael Faraday (biography)","publisher":"The IET Archives","url":"https://www.theiet.org/membership/library-and-archives/the-iet-archives/biographies/michael-faraday"},{"title":"Michael Faraday","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Faraday"},{"title":"Homopolar generator","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homopolar_generator"},{"title":"Joseph Henry","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Henry"},{"title":"Hippolyte Pixii","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hippolyte_Pixii"},{"title":"James Clerk Maxwell","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Clerk_Maxwell"},{"title":"Heinrich Hertz","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Hertz"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/06-1.jpg","alt":"Faraday's iron ring wrapped in coils of wire, which he used in 1831 to discover electromagnetic induction.","file":"Faraday ring transformer.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faraday_ring_transformer.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/06-2.jpg","alt":"Old engraving of Faraday's disc: a hand-cranked copper disc (D) spins past a magnet to make electric current.","file":"Faraday disk generator.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faraday_disk_generator.jpg","author":"Émile Alglave","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/06-3.jpg","alt":"Engraving of Pixii's machine: turning a crank spins a horseshoe magnet under two wire coils to make electricity.","file":"Pixii's magneto-electric machine. Wellcome M0017722.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pixii%27s_magneto-electric_machine._Wellcome_M0017722.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"}],"words":3788},{"n":7,"title":"Electricity Goes to Work","when":"1837 – 1879","summary":"Electricity stopped being a science trick and got a job! It sent messages along wires and under the ocean, coated spoons in shiny silver, and lit up whole streets with blazing arc lamps. But those lamps were far too bright for anyone's bedroom.","sections":[{"heading":"A Wire That Talks","paragraphs":["By the 1830s, scientists had batteries that made a steady current, electromagnets that jumped to life when current flowed, and Faraday's discovery that motion could make electricity. Those were wonderful discoveries, but almost all of them lived inside laboratories and lecture halls. Ordinary people had never used electricity for anything at all. That was about to change, and the first job electricity was given was not lighting. It was talking.","Electricity, though, moves along a wire almost instantly. The electrons themselves only drift slowly, but the electrical push travels down the wire at an enormous speed, so switching on a current at one end affects the other end, many kilometres away, in a tiny fraction of a second. Inventors realised that if they could make that current do something visible at the far end, they could send messages faster than anything humans had ever known. A machine that does this is called a telegraph, from Greek words meaning 'far writing'.","Many people experimented with electric telegraphs, in Germany, Russia, Britain and America. The first one to go into real commercial service was built by two Englishmen with very different personalities. William Fothergill Cooke was a restless young businessman who had seen a telegraph demonstration in Germany and wanted to make money from the idea. Charles Wheatstone was a quiet, brilliant professor at King's College London who knew the science deeply. Together they patented their system in May 1837.","Their telegraph used needles, a bit like compass needles. Each needle sat inside a coil of wire. When current flowed one way through the coil, the needle tipped left; when it flowed the other way, it tipped right. Their first commercial design had five needles arranged across a diamond-shaped board printed with letters. By making two needles tip at the same time, the operator made them point together at one letter on the board. Five needles could only point to twenty letters, so a few letters such as C, J, Q, U, X and Z were left out, and people simply spelled around them.","Railways were the first big customers. Trains were new, fast and sometimes dangerous, and railway managers badly needed to know where each train was. In 1838 a five-needle line opened beside the Great Western Railway tracks between Paddington station in London and West Drayton, about 13 miles (21 kilometres) away. Its wires had trouble with their insulation, so later versions used two needles and then just one, reading a code made of left and right swings. Single-needle telegraphs stayed in use on some British railways until the 1930s.","What really made the public notice was a crime. On New Year's Day 1845 a man named John Tawell was suspected of a murder in Slough, west of London. He jumped on a train to London, believing nobody could get there ahead of him. But a telegraph message from Slough raced him to Paddington with his description, and police were waiting when he stepped off the train. Newspapers loved the story. Suddenly everyone understood that the telegraph was faster than any train. The same year, the Electric Telegraph Company was formed to build lines across Britain."]},{"heading":"Dots, Dashes and 'What Hath God Wrought'","paragraphs":["Across the Atlantic, the most famous telegraph story belongs to a man who was not a scientist at all. Samuel Morse was a painter. He had studied art in London, painted portraits of important Americans, and in 1826 helped found the National Academy of Design in New York. Sadly, in 1825 his young wife Lucretia died while he was away from home working, and some of his biographers suggest the slowness of the news hurt him deeply. Whether or not that was his reason, Morse became fascinated by the idea of instant messages.","In 1832, sailing home from Europe, Morse got talking with a Boston scientist named Charles Thomas Jackson, who described experiments with electromagnets. Morse began sketching a telegraph in his notebook during the voyage. Turning those sketches into a working machine took years and lots of help. Leonard Gale, a chemistry teacher at New York University, showed him how to make the signal travel much farther by using better batteries and relays. A relay is a small electromagnetic switch: a weak current arriving from far away flips the switch, which then turns on a fresh, strong current from a local battery. Joseph Henry in America and Edward Davy in Britain had both worked out relays in the 1830s. With relays, a message could be passed along a chain, refreshed at each step, like runners in a relay race handing on a baton.","Morse's other key helper was a young man named Alfred Vail. Vail was skilled with machines, and his family's ironworks in New Jersey helped pay for the work. Vail built much of the apparatus, including the 'register', which pressed marks onto a moving strip of paper tape, and he worked closely with Morse on the famous code.","Morse code turns every letter into a short pattern of short signals (dots) and long signals (dashes). The letter E, the most common letter in English, is just a single dot. T is a single dash. Rarer letters get longer patterns. The idea is the same one used in clever computer coding today: give the most common things the shortest codes, and your messages get shorter. Unlike Cooke and Wheatstone's needles, Morse's system needed just one wire (the earth itself could complete the circuit), which made it much cheaper to build.","Morse spent years trying to persuade people his idea was worth paying for. Finally, in 1843, the United States Congress set aside 30,000 dollars to build an experimental line about 38 miles (around 61 kilometres) long, from Washington, D.C. to Baltimore, Maryland. Workers first tried burying the wire in pipes, but the insulation failed, so they strung it on wooden poles instead. That is why, for the next hundred years, rows of telegraph poles marched beside roads and railway lines all over the world.","On 24 May 1844, Morse sat in a room in the United States Capitol building in Washington and tapped out a message to Vail, waiting at a railway depot in Baltimore. The words were chosen by Annie Ellsworth, the young daughter of a friend who had helped get the money through Congress. She picked a line from the Bible, from the Book of Numbers: 'What hath God wrought', meaning roughly 'What amazing things God has made happen'. Vail received it and sent it straight back. The message had crossed 38 miles in a moment."]},{"heading":"A Cable Under the Ocean","paragraphs":["Land telegraphs were one thing. Laying a wire across the bottom of the Atlantic Ocean, through water up to several kilometres deep, was another thing entirely. Yet an American businessman named Cyrus Field became determined to do it. In 1856 the Atlantic Telegraph Company was formed, with Field as one of its leaders, and it ordered about 2,500 nautical miles (some 4,600 kilometres) of cable.","An undersea cable is a copper wire coated in insulation and protected by an outer layer of iron wires. In those days the insulation was gutta-percha, a rubbery substance made from the sap of trees from South-East Asia. It was one of the few materials that stayed waterproof and did not rot in seawater for years. The cable was so heavy that it took huge ships to carry it, and it often snapped as it was being paid out over the stern, sinking to the sea floor and taking months of money and hard work with it.","After several failures, the cable was finally connected in August 1858, running from Valentia Island in the far west of Ireland to Newfoundland. On 16 August the first official message was sent, and Queen Victoria exchanged greetings with the American President, James Buchanan. There were celebrations with fireworks and church bells in New York. But the signal was horribly weak and blurred. Sending even a single letter took about two minutes. Queen Victoria's message of 98 words took a long time to get through: accounts differ, but at least over an hour, and the copy sent back took far longer.","Then things got worse. The company's chief electrician, Wildman Whitehouse, believed the answer was to push harder, and he applied very high voltages, up to about 2,000 volts, to force signals through. The high voltage damaged the insulation, and in September 1858, only about three weeks after it had begun working, the cable fell silent forever. Whitehouse was dismissed.","Meanwhile a young Scottish physicist named William Thomson, who later became Lord Kelvin, had a much better approach. Instead of shouting louder, he built a better ear. His mirror galvanometer was an extremely sensitive detector: a tiny magnet with a small mirror attached hung by a silk thread inside a coil. A beam of light bounced off the mirror onto a scale, so even the faintest current made the spot of light swing visibly across the room. It worked with gentle currents that did not harm the cable.","The American Civil War and money troubles delayed another try for years. When it came, it used the most extraordinary ship of the age: the Great Eastern, designed by the engineer Isambard Kingdom Brunel. She was so gigantic that no ordinary harbour could easily handle her, but she was perfect for carrying an entire ocean's worth of cable in one go. In 1865 the cable snapped after more than 1,000 nautical miles had been laid, and its end was lost in deep water. The crew tried to fish it up with grappling hooks and failed.","In July 1866 they tried again with a new and better cable, and this time it worked. On 27 July 1866 the Great Eastern reached Heart's Content in Newfoundland, and the cable was put into service the same day. Even more amazingly, the ship then went back out, found the broken 1865 cable lying on the sea bed, hauled it up, joined it to new cable, and completed a second line in September. The new cables could carry about 8 words a minute, about 80 times faster than the 1858 one. News that once took ten days to cross the Atlantic by ship now took minutes. It was one of the first true steps towards the connected world we live in today."]},{"heading":"Silver Spoons from a Battery","paragraphs":["While telegraph wires were stretching across countries, electricity found a second job in a very different place: the workshops of Birmingham, in the middle of England, a city famous for making buttons, buckles, jewellery, cutlery and thousands of other metal goods.","Here is the trick. Remember how Nicholson and Carlisle used Volta's pile to split water, and how Humphry Davy used electricity to pull new metals out of chemical compounds? Electricity can push metal atoms around inside a liquid. Dissolve some silver in a suitable liquid, and the liquid fills up with silver atoms that have lost a little electric charge. These charged atoms are called ions. Hang a plain metal spoon in the liquid, connect it to the negative side of a battery, connect a bar of pure silver to the positive side, and switch on. The silver ions drift to the spoon, pick up electrons, and settle on it as a smooth, thin layer of real silver. Meanwhile, the silver bar slowly dissolves, topping up the liquid. This is electroplating.","The first person to do it was an Italian chemist, Luigi Brugnatelli, a friend of Volta's, who gold-plated objects using a voltaic pile in 1805. But his work was mostly ignored, and for about thirty years nobody turned it into a business. In the late 1830s, scientists in Britain and Russia independently worked out how to copy objects using deposited copper. In Russia, Moritz Hermann von Jacobi (known in Russian as Boris Jacobi) used the method, called electrotyping, to make exact copies of printing plates and even sculptures.","The breakthrough for silver and gold came from a Birmingham surgeon named John Wright, who discovered that a solution containing a chemical called potassium cyanide worked beautifully for plating these precious metals. Cyanide is deadly poisonous, so this was serious, careful work for trained people only. Wright's associates, the cousins George and Henry Elkington, took out patents in 1840 and built a great factory in Birmingham.","There is a hidden link to the rest of our story here. Plating needed a lot of steady current, and batteries were expensive to run because they used up zinc and acid. So in 1844 the Elkingtons began using a generator, a machine built by John Woolrich that turned the motion of a steam engine into electricity using Faraday's induction. It is often described as the first electrical generator used in a real industry. Electroplating proved that there was money to be made from electricity, and it gave engineers a reason to build bigger and better generators. By the 1850s, plating with nickel, brass, tin and zinc had all been developed, and today electroplating is still used on everything from car parts to the tiny gold contacts inside your phone."]},{"heading":"Machines That Feed Themselves","paragraphs":["Faraday's spinning copper disc had shown that motion could make electricity, and Hippolyte Pixii's hand-cranked machine had added a clever part called a commutator. A spinning coil in a magnetic field naturally produces a current that flips back and forth. The commutator is a rotating switch on the shaft that reverses the connections at just the right moment, so the current coming out always flows the same way. That kind of machine, making direct current, is called a dynamo.","But early generators had a weakness. Their magnetic fields came from permanent magnets, the same kind of magnet you might stick on a fridge, only bigger. Permanent magnets are not very strong compared with a good electromagnet, so the machines could only make modest amounts of power. Building bigger ones meant stacking up heavier and heavier magnets.","Why not use electromagnets instead? The difficulty was that electromagnets need current to work, and getting that current from a separate battery was costly. Then several inventors noticed a wonderful loophole. The iron cores of the electromagnets keep a tiny bit of magnetism even when switched off. So when the machine starts spinning, that weak leftover magnetism makes a weak current. If you feed that current back into the electromagnets, they get a bit stronger, which makes a bit more current, which makes the magnets stronger still. Round and round it goes, and in a few seconds the machine has built itself up to full power. This is called self-excitation, and a machine that works this way 'feeds itself'.","As so often in this story, the idea came to several people at almost the same time. In Hungary, a monk and physics teacher named Ányos Jedlik had worked out the principle around 1856 but never patented it. In Britain, Henry Wilde described a powerful dynamo design in 1866, and Samuel Alfred Varley filed a patent on 24 December 1866. In Germany, Werner von Siemens, an engineer who had already built a big telegraph business, described his 'dynamo-electric' machine in a report to the Berlin Academy of Sciences early in 1867. On 17 January 1867, papers by Siemens and by Charles Wheatstone, the telegraph pioneer, were presented at the Royal Society in London. It is fairest to say the self-exciting dynamo had several inventors.","The next step came from a Belgian carpenter's son named Zénobe Gramme, who worked as a model-maker in Paris. Building on an earlier ring-shaped design by the Italian Antonio Pacinotti from about 1860, Gramme wound many coils of wire around a spinning iron ring, each connected to its own segment of the commutator. With many coils taking turns, the output was smooth and nearly steady, not jerky. He demonstrated his machine to the Paris Academy of Sciences in 1871, and the Gramme machine became the first generator to make power on a commercial scale for industry. It was used for electroplating, for arc lights and in early factories.","Then, at an exhibition in Vienna in 1873, something unexpected happened. Gramme's business partner, Hippolyte Fontaine, connected the wires of one Gramme machine to a second one that was already running. To everyone's surprise, the second machine's shaft began to spin by itself. A dynamo run backwards is an electric motor! Faraday had shown this connection decades earlier, but now it worked at a size that could drive real machinery. The same machine could turn motion into electricity and electricity back into motion, which is still true of electric car motors today."]},{"heading":"Arc Lights Leave the Lab","paragraphs":["Now there was finally a cheap, powerful source of electricity: a steam engine driving a dynamo. And people already knew one way to turn electricity into light. Back in the early 1800s Humphry Davy had dazzled audiences at the Royal Institution with a brilliant arc of light leaping between two pieces of charcoal. The problem then had been that it gobbled up the power of enormous batteries. With dynamos, the arc light could finally go out into the world.","Here is how an arc lamp works. Two carbon rods touch, and current flows through them. Then they are pulled a few millimetres apart. The current keeps jumping across the gap through the hot air, which becomes a glowing, electrified gas. The tips of the rods reach several thousand degrees Celsius and glow an intense blue-white. One arc lamp could give off as much light as hundreds of candles, and big ones far more.","But there was a catch: the carbon rods slowly burn away, and as they shorten, the gap grows until the arc goes out. Early arc lamps needed a clever clockwork or electromagnetic gadget, called a regulator, to keep nudging the rods closer together. Regulators were fiddly and expensive.","A young Russian engineer named Pavel Yablochkov came up with a beautifully simple solution in 1876. Instead of pointing the two rods at each other, he stood them side by side, like two candles, separated by a thin strip of material that does not conduct, such as kaolin (a white clay) or plaster of Paris. The arc burned across the tips at the top, and as the rods burned down, the strip between them crumbled away at the same speed, so the gap always stayed the right size. No regulator needed! People called it the 'electric candle'.","There was one more clever detail. With direct current, one rod burned away about twice as fast as the other, which ruined the trick. So Yablochkov ran his candles on alternating current, which flips back and forth, making both rods wear down evenly. That made his candles some of the first popular users of AC, years before the War of the Currents you will read about later. Each candle lasted about two hours and then had to be replaced, so lamp holders often held several candles that switched over one after another.","In 1877 Yablochkov candles lit a hall of a great Paris department store, the Louvre store, with about 80 lamps. Then, for the great Paris Exposition (world's fair) of 1878, they lit the Avenue de l'Opéra and the square in front of the Opera House, part of a display of about 64 lamps glowing in white glass globes, each holding several candles. Visitors had never seen a street at night look so bright. That December, electric candles were installed along the Victoria Embankment beside the River Thames in London. They spread to cities from Rio de Janeiro to Calcutta, and at their peak French factories turned out about 8,000 candles a day.","In America, Charles F. Brush of Cleveland, Ohio, took a different path. Brush, a young chemist and engineer, began designing his own dynamo in 1876, and in 1878 tests by the Franklin Institute in Philadelphia judged his machine better than its rivals. He also built a reliable arc lamp with an automatic regulator, and arranged many lamps in a row, one after another, on a single circuit. On the evening of 29 April 1879, Brush's arc lamps lit up the Public Square in the middle of Cleveland, powered by a generator nearby. Crowds came out to stare at the brilliant light. Within a couple of years Brush systems were glowing in New York, where they lit part of Broadway, and in Boston, Philadelphia and other cities. In San Francisco in 1879, a company bought Brush generators and began selling electric light from a central plant to many different customers, one of the very first electric utilities in the world."]},{"heading":"Football by Arc Light","paragraphs":["Imagine a chilly autumn evening, 14 October 1878, at the Bramall Lane ground. In those days, football matches had to finish before it got dark, which in a northern English winter could mean mid-afternoon. But on this night, a game kicked off after sunset. Arc lamps had been raised high on wooden towers around the pitch, and dynamos, driven by steam engines, supplied the current. The players, most of them from the Sheffield club called The Wednesday (today's Sheffield Wednesday), were split into two teams, the Reds and the Blues.","More than 20,000 people came to watch, an enormous crowd for the time. Many of them had come as much to see the lights as the football. Reports say the glare was dazzling, and players sometimes squinted or were blinded for a moment when they looked towards a lamp. The dynamos and the steam engines that drove them were not always reliable, and there was no electricity grid to fall back on if something went wrong.","Bramall Lane is now counted as the first stadium to host floodlit association football, and other experimental floodlit matches followed in different towns that winter. But it took many decades before floodlights became normal. For years, the football authorities were suspicious of night games, and it was not until the 1950s that floodlit matches became common in English football. Still, that evening in 1878 was a preview of the future: a world where electricity could keep sport, work and fun going long after the sun had set."]},{"heading":"Too Bright for the Living Room","paragraphs":["So by 1879, electricity was sending messages across oceans, plating cutlery, spinning motors and lighting streets, squares, shops and football pitches. Why, then, did nearly every home in the world still light its rooms with candles, oil, kerosene or gas?","The answer is that arc lights were almost the perfect light for the wrong job. Imagine putting a lamp as bright as hundreds of candles on your bedroom ceiling. You would not be able to look anywhere near it. Arc lamps also hissed, flickered and buzzed. They gave off strong ultraviolet light, the invisible kind that causes sunburn, and fumes, including some carbon monoxide. Their white-hot carbon tips could spit sparks, a real fire risk in a house full of curtains, wood and paper. Their carbons needed replacing every few hours.","What homes needed was something completely different: lots of small, gentle, steady lights, maybe as bright as ten or twenty candles each, which could each be switched on and off separately without affecting any of the others. Inventors called this challenge 'subdividing the light', meaning splitting one huge electric light into many little ones. Some respected experts thought it might be impossible. The British engineer William Preece, a leading figure at the Post Office telegraph service, was among those who doubted it could be done in a practical, affordable way.","There was an obvious alternative to the arc. Instead of making electricity leap across a gap, why not send it through a thin strip or wire that would get so hot it would glow, like the element of a toaster but brighter? It sounds simple. It was not. For decades, every glowing wire anyone tried either burned up in seconds, melted, darkened its glass, or cost far too much to make.","And here is the surprise. The answer, a small, gentle glowing wire sealed inside a glass bulb, was not a brand-new idea in 1879. Inventors had been chasing it for about forty years already. In the next part, we go back to meet the patient, often forgotten people who ran that long race."]}],"deepDive":"Why was the 1858 Atlantic cable so slow, even before it was damaged? The answer is a property called capacitance. A long cable is really a copper wire separated from the surrounding seawater by a thin layer of insulation, and that makes it act like a gigantic, stretched-out Leyden jar. Each pulse of current has to charge up the cable before the far end feels much, and then the charge has to drain away again. Sharp on-off signals become smeared, rounded humps, and if you send pulses too quickly they blur into one another. In the 1850s William Thomson worked out what is often called his 'law of squares': the delay in a cable grows roughly with the square of its length. Double the length and the signals become about four times sluggish. That meant a transatlantic cable needed a thick copper core, good insulation and very sensitive receivers, not brute-force voltage. Wildman Whitehouse, who favoured high voltage, clashed with Thomson, and the failure of the 1858 cable after about three weeks helped settle the argument in Thomson's favour. A government inquiry afterwards studied what had gone wrong, and the lessons shaped the much more successful 1866 cable. Historians also argue about credit for the dynamo. Ányos Jedlik described self-excitation around 1856 but did not publish widely or patent it; Wilde, Varley, Siemens and Wheatstone all reached the idea in 1866–67, and national pride has sometimes coloured who gets named first. Most historians treat it as a case of simultaneous invention: once permanent-magnet generators, electromagnets and the commutator all existed, the next step was waiting to be noticed. A similar debate surrounds the arc lamp: Yablochkov, Brush, Werner von Siemens, and the French makers of regulator lamps such as Serrin all built successful systems, and which counts as first depends on whether you mean first demonstration, first patent or first paying customers. Finally, the numbers show why arc lamps lost the home market. A big arc lamp might use hundreds or even thousands of watts, which suited a street corner but would have flooded a parlour. Engineers measured light in 'candlepower', and arc lamps were rated in hundreds or thousands of candles. The challenge set by 1879 was not making light, but making small amounts of it, cheaply and safely, in many separate places at once.","funFact":"When the first Atlantic cable worked in 1858, sending one single letter of the alphabet across the ocean took about two minutes.","check":{"q":"Why did Pavel Yablochkov run his 'electric candles' on alternating current?","a":"So that both carbon rods burned down evenly","wrong":["Because alternating current was cheaper to send under the ocean","So that the candles would change colour","Because direct current had not been discovered yet"]},"sources":[{"title":"Cooke and Wheatstone telegraph","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooke_and_Wheatstone_telegraph"},{"title":"Samuel Morse","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Morse"},{"title":"Transatlantic telegraph cable","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transatlantic_telegraph_cable"},{"title":"Electroplating","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electroplating"},{"title":"Dynamo","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamo"},{"title":"Gramme machine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gramme_machine"},{"title":"Yablochkov candle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yablochkov_candle"},{"title":"Arc lamp","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arc_lamp"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/07-1.jpg","alt":"Cooke and Wheatstone's 1837 five-needle telegraph at the Science Museum, London; the needles point to letters on the diamond board.","file":"Wheatstone-Cooke five-needle telegraph (1837), Science Museum, London (141904071).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wheatstone-Cooke_five-needle_telegraph_(1837),_Science_Museum,_London_(141904071).jpg","author":"chailey","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/07-2.jpg","alt":"Painting of the giant steamship Great Eastern on stormy seas while laying the first successful Atlantic telegraph cable (1866).","file":"The steamship Great Eastern laying the first successful Atlantic cable RMG BHC3380.tiff","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_steamship_Great_Eastern_laying_the_first_successful_Atlantic_cable_RMG_BHC3380.tiff","author":"Henry Clifford","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/07-3.jpg","alt":"A real Gramme dynamo (No. 14), built in 1873 by Mignon and Rouart in Paris.","file":"Machine Gramme.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Machine_Gramme.jpg","author":"Icone5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":4392},{"n":8,"title":"The Long Race to a Gentle Glow","when":"1802 – 1879","summary":"For almost eighty years, inventors tried to make a tiny wire glow inside a glass bulb. Their wires kept burning up, melting or turning the glass black. Bit by bit, about twenty different people, from Scotland to Russia to Canada, inched closer to a light bulb that really worked.","sections":[{"heading":"Why Hot Things Glow","paragraphs":["Have you ever watched the rings of an electric cooker, or the bars of an old electric heater, warm up? First nothing seems to happen. Then a dull red glow appears. If the metal could get hotter still, it would turn orange, then yellow, and finally a brilliant white. Blacksmiths have known this for thousands of years: they judged the temperature of a horseshoe in the forge just by its colour.","Glowing because something is very hot has a scientific name: incandescence. It is the same reason the Sun shines, a candle flame glows yellow, and a fire's embers turn orange. Everything warm gives off some energy as invisible infrared rays (which is why you can feel the heat of a radiator from across a room). Make something hot enough, and some of that energy comes out as visible light too. The hotter it gets, the more light it gives, and the whiter that light looks.","Now remember what we learned about electric current. When current is pushed through a wire, the wire resists it a little, and that resistance turns some of the electrical energy into heat. Thick copper wires barely warm up. But a very thin wire, or one made of a material that resists current strongly, can get very hot indeed. If you could make such a wire hot enough, it would glow. That is the simple idea behind the incandescent light bulb: heat a thin strip, called a filament, with electricity until it shines.","People noticed this early. In 1761 the American experimenter Ebenezer Kinnersley, a friend of Benjamin Franklin, used electricity from Leyden jars to make a wire glow red-hot. And in 1802, just two years after Volta's pile, Humphry Davy at the Royal Institution in London passed current from a large battery through a thin strip of platinum. The strip glowed. Davy chose platinum because it melts only at a very high temperature, about 1,770 degrees Celsius, and does not rust or tarnish. The light was weak and did not last, and Davy soon turned his attention to the much brighter carbon arc. But he had shown the principle: electricity could make a solid strip glow without any flame.","So why did it take nearly eighty more years to turn that glowing strip into a bulb you could buy? To understand the long race, we first need to meet the three enemies that every filament-maker had to defeat."]},{"heading":"Three Enemies of the Filament","paragraphs":["The first enemy was oxygen. About a fifth of the air around us is oxygen, and oxygen loves to combine with hot materials. That is what burning is. Heat a carbon thread in ordinary air and it simply catches fire and turns to ash in moments. Heat a metal wire and oxygen attacks its surface, making it crumble. A filament had to glow at well over 1,000 degrees Celsius to give useful light, and at those temperatures almost anything in air is destroyed quickly. The answer was to take the air away, sealing the filament inside glass and removing the air to make a vacuum, or replacing the air with a gas that does not react, such as nitrogen. But making a really good vacuum turned out to be one of the hardest parts of the whole problem.","The second enemy was heat itself. To give a good white light, a filament has to be extremely hot, and most metals melt before they get hot enough. Platinum could survive higher temperatures than most, but even platinum lamps had to be run carefully. A little too much current and the filament melted, breaking the circuit. Carbon was another favourite. It does not melt at ordinary pressures at all. Instead, at around 3,600 degrees Celsius it turns straight from solid to vapour, so it can be heated far more than platinum. But carbon had its own problems: it was brittle, hard to shape into thin threads, and in a poor vacuum it slowly turned to vapour even below that temperature.","The third enemy was blackening. When carbon or metal atoms escaped from the hot filament, they flew across the bulb and stuck to the cool glass, forming a dark film like soot on a chimney. The bulb got dimmer and dimmer, and the filament got thinner and thinner until it snapped. Hidden moisture and leftover air trapped inside the bulb, or soaked into the filament, made this worse, because when the lamp was switched on, the water was split, and the oxygen released attacked the filament.","And beyond these three enemies stood a fourth problem that had nothing to do with the bulb: where would the electricity come from? Before about 1870, most electricity came from batteries, which ate up expensive zinc and acid. Running even one lamp all evening from batteries cost far more than burning gas or oil. Until the dynamos of the 1860s and 1870s made cheap current possible, an electric lamp was a beautiful experiment that almost nobody could afford to use."]},{"heading":"Platinum Under Glass: The First Lamp Patents","paragraphs":["Even so, determined inventors kept trying. In 1835, a schoolteacher in Dundee, Scotland, named James Bowman Lindsay showed a steady electric light at a public meeting and said he could read a book by it from about a foot and a half away. He never developed it into a product, and nobody is quite sure exactly how his lamp was made, but he was clearly thinking about lighting rooms with electricity decades ahead of most people. In 1838 a Belgian lithographer, Marcellin Jobard, suggested and experimented with a carbon filament glowing in a vacuum.","One of the earliest lamps we know well was made in about 1840 by Warren de la Rue, an English scientist born in Guernsey in 1815. De la Rue was a remarkable person. His family ran a famous London printing and stationery company, which printed playing cards and later stamps and banknotes, and he worked in the business. In his spare time he was a keen astronomer who became a pioneer of photographing the Moon and the Sun, and he photographed the total solar eclipse of 1860.","De la Rue's lamp was simple and clever. He coiled a thin platinum wire and sealed it inside a glass tube from which he had pumped out as much air as he could. Coiling the wire packed more glowing metal into a small space. Taking away the air meant there was less gas to carry heat away from the wire and less to react with it. When current flowed, the coil glowed. It worked! But platinum was, and still is, extremely expensive, far too costly for lamps that might be sold by the thousand. And the vacuum pumps of the time left plenty of air behind. De la Rue's lamp stayed a laboratory curiosity.","The first person to get a patent for an incandescent lamp was Frederick de Moleyns, in Britain, in 1841. A patent is an official document from a government that says an invention belongs to the person who registered it, so nobody else can copy and sell it for a number of years without permission. De Moleyns' lamp used platinum wires in a glass globe emptied of air, and he also used powdered carbon (charcoal), which was fed in to glow where the wires met. His bulbs are said to have quickly blackened as the carbon was thrown onto the glass, a sign of the third enemy at work.","Notice how many of these early attempts used either platinum or carbon. Inventors had worked out that they needed a material that could stand terrible heat. Platinum was reliable but too costly and melted too easily. Carbon was cheap and could take even more heat, but it burned in air and blackened the glass in a poor vacuum. For the next forty years, the race swung back and forth between these two materials, with carbon slowly taking the lead."]},{"heading":"Carbon Takes the Stage","paragraphs":["In 1845 a young American from Cincinnati, Ohio, named John Wellington Starr took the carbon idea further. Instead of platinum, Starr used a small stick of carbon. To keep the oxygen away, he placed it in a vacuum made with mercury: a glass tube filled with mercury was turned upside down, and as the heavy mercury sank, it left an empty space at the top, called a Torricellian vacuum after the Italian scientist who first made one. Starr's carbon glowed brightly in this space. He travelled to England with his lamp, and a British patent for it was taken out in 1845, in the name of his backer, Edward King.","Sadly, Starr's story ended early. He died in November 1846, when he was only about twenty-four years old, and his lamps were never produced for sale. Yet his work did not vanish. Joseph Swan, a young chemist in the north of England whom you will meet properly in the next part, is often said to have been inspired as a young man by electric lamps demonstrated in the 1840s, possibly including Starr's. Ideas in science often travel like this, from one person's unfinished work into somebody else's notebook.","Other inventors kept experimenting during the 1850s. In 1851 the famous French stage magician Jean Eugène Robert-Houdin, who loved gadgets as much as tricks, showed off incandescent lamps on his estate near Blois in France. In 1859 an American inventor, Moses Farmer, lit a room in his house in Salem, Massachusetts with platinum lamps. Years later, a young Thomas Edison saw one of Farmer's lamps and asked him for advice. None of these lamps lasted long or cost little enough to sell, but each one taught inventors something.","You may sometimes read that a German-American watchmaker named Heinrich Göbel made a working bamboo-filament bulb in New York in the 1850s. That claim only appeared in 1893, during lawsuits about light bulb patents, and the courts did not believe it. Historians who have studied the evidence since have concluded the story is not supported, so it is best treated as a legend rather than a real invention."]},{"heading":"From St Petersburg to Toronto","paragraphs":["By the early 1870s, the race had spread across the world. In Russia, a young engineer named Alexander Lodygin was working on electric light. Lodygin had been born in 1847 in the Tambov region, south-east of Moscow, and trained as an army officer before working at a weapons factory in Tula. He was an inventive dreamer who also designed a diving apparatus and an electric flying machine. But his most important work was his lamp.","Lodygin's lamp used a thin rod of carbon inside a sealed glass container. To protect the carbon, he tried both pumping out the air and filling the container with nitrogen, a gas that does not burn. He applied for a Russian patent in 1872 and showed his lamps at the St Petersburg Institute of Technology in 1873. In 1873 and 1874 his lamps were tried out in experiments in St Petersburg, including lighting in the city's streets and buildings. Imagine walking past at night and seeing a few small, steady white lights in a city lit by flickering gas jets!","In 1874 Lodygin was granted his Russian patent, and the Petersburg Academy of Sciences awarded him its Lomonosov Prize, an important Russian science award. He also took out patents in several other countries, including Austria, Britain, France and Belgium, and founded a company to make his lamps. But his carbon rods still did not last long enough, and the company struggled. Later, Lodygin moved to France and the United States, where he worked on lamps with filaments made of metals with very high melting points, including tungsten, which would become enormously important in the next century. He returned to Russia for a time and finally died in Brooklyn, New York, in 1923. Russians rightly remember him as one of the inventors of the light bulb.","Meanwhile, in Toronto, Canada, two men were having the same kind of idea. Henry Woodward, born in 1832, was the inventive one of the pair, and his partner Mathew Evans was a hotel keeper. In July 1874 they filed a Canadian patent for a lamp in which a piece of carbon glowed inside a glass container filled with nitrogen. The patent was granted in August 1874. They later obtained a United States patent as well.","Woodward and Evans had a working idea but very little money. They tried to find investors to help them build a business and could not. So in 1879 they sold the rights to their American patent to Thomas Edison, whose company wanted to be sure that nobody could block its own lamp in court. To this day, Canada honours Woodward and Evans as inventors of the light bulb. Like Lodygin, they reached the starting line of the real race but were held back by the same problems as everyone else: carbon that did not last, poor vacuums, and the high cost of electricity."]},{"heading":"The Hidden Hero: Sprengel's Mercury Pump","paragraphs":["If you want to know who helped make the light bulb possible, here is a name you may never have heard: Hermann Sprengel. He was not trying to make lamps at all. He was a chemist, born in Hanover in Germany in 1834, who moved to England and worked in London laboratories. And in 1865 he invented a new kind of vacuum pump.","Older air pumps worked a bit like a bicycle pump in reverse, with pistons and valves sucking air out of a container. They could remove most of the air, but tiny leaks around the valves always let some back in, so they could never get the last traces. Sprengel came up with a completely different method that used falling drops of mercury, the heavy liquid metal.","Picture a tall, narrow glass tube standing upright. At the top, the tube is connected sideways to the container you want to empty, such as a lamp bulb. Mercury is allowed to drip, one drop at a time, down the tube. Each falling drop fills the tube like a tiny piston, and between one drop and the next, a little bubble of air from the bulb gets trapped. The drops carry the trapped bubbles down and out at the bottom. Drop after drop, bubble after bubble, the air is slowly carried away. The mercury is collected and poured back in at the top to be used again.","It was slow. Sprengel's early pump took about twenty minutes to empty a container of half a litre. But it had no leaky valves, and it could keep working until almost every last bit of air had gone. It produced a better vacuum than any machine before it, with the pressure inside falling to around a hundred-millionth of normal air pressure. Later inventors improved it further, and the English scientist William Crookes connected several Sprengel pumps in series to make the very empty tubes he used to study glowing electric discharges.","Here is why it mattered so much. With a really good vacuum, there was almost no oxygen left to attack a carbon filament, and far less gas to carry away the escaping carbon. The second and third enemies of the filament had suddenly become much weaker. By the late 1870s, inventors who used Sprengel-type pumps, and who learned to heat their filaments while pumping to drive out hidden gases, could keep a carbon filament glowing for hours, not minutes. The two most famous light bulb makers, Joseph Swan in England and Thomas Edison in America, both relied on mercury pumps of this kind to empty their bulbs. A great invention is often built on top of a quiet invention that most people never hear about.","At almost the same moment, the fourth problem was being solved too. As you saw in the last part, the self-exciting dynamos of Siemens, Wheatstone and others, and the smooth Gramme machine of 1871, had made electricity much cheaper to produce. By 1878 the pieces of the puzzle were finally on the table: good vacuums, cheap current from dynamos, and decades of experience with carbon."]},{"heading":"Building a Lamp by Hand","paragraphs":["It is easy to say 'put a filament in a glass bulb and pump out the air', but think about what that really meant in a workshop of the 1870s. There were no factories making bulbs, no ready-made parts and no instruction books. Every lamp was made by hand, and every step was a puzzle of its own.","First came the glass. A glassblower heated a tube of glass in a hot flame until it was soft and glowing, then blew and turned it to shape a bulb, leaving a narrow neck. Glass is an excellent insulator, so it does not let current leak away, and it is clear, so light shines straight through. But it is also fragile, and it cracks if one part is heated much faster than another.","Next, the current had to get inside. That sounds simple until you remember that the bulb had to stay perfectly airtight. Two wires had to pass right through the hot glass and be sealed into it. Here inventors hit a surprising problem. When things warm up, they expand, and when they cool, they shrink. Most metals expand and shrink by a different amount from glass, so as a sealed lamp cooled, the metal pulled away from the glass, leaving tiny cracks that let air creep back in. Platinum, by lucky chance, expands and shrinks by almost exactly the same amount as ordinary glass. So even inventors who used carbon filaments usually used short platinum wires to carry the current through the glass. This is one reason platinum stayed in the story long after it was abandoned as a filament.","Then the filament had to be attached to those lead-in wires without breaking, because carbon threads and rods snapped easily. Inventors tried clamps, tiny screws, and pastes made of carbon to make a good electrical joint. A bad joint got hot, glowed in the wrong place, and failed.","Finally the bulb was connected to the pump. As the air was slowly removed, experimenters learned to switch the lamp on gently so the filament warmed up while the pump was still running. Heat drove out gases hidden inside the carbon and on the glass, and the pump carried them away. Only then was the narrow neck melted shut in a flame, sealing the vacuum in for good.","After all that, the lamp had to be tested. How bright was it? Inventors compared it with a standard candle, measuring its brightness in 'candlepower', and they timed how many minutes or hours it lasted before the filament broke or the glass went black. Most early lamps failed these tests quickly, and the inventor went back to the bench to try again. It is no wonder the race took so long."]},{"heading":"A Bulb With Many Parents","paragraphs":["So who invented the light bulb? If you ask most people, they will say one name. But as you have seen, the true answer is long and crowded. Historians Robert Friedel and Paul Israel, who studied the history carefully, listed more than twenty people who built incandescent lamps before Joseph Swan and Thomas Edison made theirs. There were English, Scottish, French, Belgian, Russian, American, Canadian and other inventors, working over almost eighty years, from Davy's glowing platinum strip in 1802 to the late 1870s.","Each of them added something. Davy showed that a solid strip could glow. De la Rue showed the value of a vacuum and of coiling the wire. De Moleyns got the first patent. Starr and Jobard pushed carbon forward. Lodygin, Woodward and Evans tried nitrogen gas and took out patents in several countries. Sprengel, without meaning to, gave everyone a far better vacuum. And the dynamo builders made the current cheap. It is a bit like a relay race in which most of the runners are forgotten, even though without them nobody would have reached the finishing line.","Why did all these earlier lamps fail to become everyday products? Partly because the materials were not yet good enough, and partly because the technology around them did not exist. There was no electricity supply in homes, no meters, no sockets and no switches. A light bulb on its own is like a mobile phone in a world with no phone network: clever, but not very useful. The inventors who finally won would be the ones who solved the lamp and, at the same time, thought about the whole system needed to power it.","By the autumn of 1878, the race had reached its final lap. Newspapers were reporting on electric light almost every week. Gas company shares even wobbled on the stock markets as investors wondered whether electricity was about to put gas lighting out of business. And two men, working thousands of kilometres apart, were closing in on the prize at exactly the same time. One was a quiet, careful chemist in Newcastle upon Tyne, in the north of England, who had first tried carbon filaments nearly thirty years before. The other was a famous American inventor in New Jersey, with a team of skilled helpers and a habit of making bold promises to reporters. In the next part, we travel to Newcastle."]}],"deepDive":"Why is a hotter filament so much better? The physics of incandescence says that the total energy given off by a hot object rises extremely fast with temperature, roughly with the fourth power of its absolute temperature (the Stefan–Boltzmann law). At the same time, the peak of its glow shifts from invisible infrared towards visible light as it heats up (Wien's law). A filament at about 1,000 degrees Celsius glows dull red and sends out almost all of its energy as heat. At around 2,000 degrees, a larger share comes out as visible light, though still only a small fraction. This is why platinum, which melts at about 1,770 degrees Celsius, was a dead end: it simply could not run hot enough to be efficient. Carbon, which survives to around 3,600 degrees in a good vacuum, could in principle be pushed much harder, which is why most serious inventors after 1845 bet on it. The other key number was resistance. Early experimenters often used thick carbon rods with low resistance. A low-resistance lamp needs a large current to get hot, and large currents need thick, expensive copper wires to avoid wasting power as heat in the wiring. Historians emphasise that one key step at the end of the 1870s was the realisation that a high-resistance filament, thin and long, would let many lamps run side by side from one supply using much less copper. Robert Friedel and Paul Israel argue that the first truly practical lamp came from combining several factors at once: an effective carbon filament, a high vacuum, high resistance, and a design that suited a complete distribution system. Historians also debate how much credit each early inventor deserves. Patents are not proof that a lamp worked well, and some inventors' claims, such as Heinrich Göbel's, were made long after the event and have been rejected. Others, like Lodygin and Woodward and Evans, have strong documentation but never achieved commercial success. National pride plays a part too: Russian, Canadian, British and American accounts each tend to highlight their own pioneers. A fair summary is that the incandescent lamp was a cumulative invention, built step by step, and that the vacuum pump was as important as any filament.","funFact":"The Sprengel pump that helped make light bulbs possible worked by letting drops of liquid mercury fall down a glass tube, each drop carrying a tiny bubble of air away with it.","check":{"q":"Why was Hermann Sprengel's 1865 mercury pump so important for light bulbs?","a":"It made a much better vacuum, so filaments were not burned up by oxygen","wrong":["It made the glass of the bulb stronger","It produced the electricity that lit the bulbs","It turned platinum into cheap carbon"]},"sources":[{"title":"Incandescent light bulb","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incandescent_light_bulb"},{"title":"Sprengel pump","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sprengel_pump"},{"title":"Alexander Lodygin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Lodygin"},{"title":"Henry Woodward (inventor)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Woodward_(inventor)"},{"title":"Warren De la Rue","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warren_De_la_Rue"},{"title":"John Wellington Starr","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Wellington_Starr"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/08-1.jpg","alt":"Drawing of a Sprengel pump: mercury dripping down the tall glass tube sucks the air out of the globe R.","file":"Sprengel vacuum pump.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sprengel_vacuum_pump.png","author":"Augustin Privat Deschanel","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/08-2.jpg","alt":"Alexander Lodygin's early electric lamp with his patent papers, shown at the Polytechnical Museum in Moscow.","file":"Lodygin 1872.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lodygin_1872.jpg","author":"Allesmüller","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/08-3.jpg","alt":"Figure 3 from Henry Woodward's US patent 181,613 for an electric light: a glass globe holding a glowing carbon piece.","file":"Woodward light bulb.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woodward_light_bulb.JPG","author":"Henry Woodward","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3882},{"n":9,"title":"Joseph Swan Lights Up Newcastle","when":"1850 – 1883","summary":"Joseph Swan was a chemist in Newcastle, England, who spent years baking strips of paper and thread into carbon and sealing them in glass. In 1879 he showed hundreds of people a glowing lamp with no flame at all. Soon his bulbs lit his own house, a castle-like mansion powered by lakes, and a whole London theatre.","sections":[{"heading":"A Chemist's Boy from Sunderland","paragraphs":["On 31 October 1828, in a house called Pallion Hall near the River Wear in Sunderland, in the north-east of England, a baby named Joseph Wilson Swan was born. Sunderland was a town of shipyards, coal and busy quays. Smoke from chimneys and ships hung over the river, and at night the streets were lit by flickering gas lamps and the windows of homes by candles and oil. Nobody in that town, and nobody anywhere in the world, had ever seen a room lit by a glowing electric bulb. Before Joseph Swan died, millions of people would have.","Young Joseph was curious about everything. He liked machines, he liked chemistry, and he loved reading about the experiments of scientists such as Humphry Davy and Michael Faraday, whose discoveries you met in earlier parts of this story. When he was a teenager he was apprenticed for six years to a firm of pharmacists in Sunderland called Hudson and Osbaldiston. An apprentice was a young worker who learned a trade by helping an expert, a bit like a very long work-experience placement. In a pharmacy, Swan learned how to weigh chemicals carefully, mix solutions, heat substances in flasks and keep a tidy, safe workbench. These were exactly the skills an inventor would need later.","In 1846 Swan moved a few miles north to Newcastle upon Tyne to join the chemist's business of his brother-in-law, John Mawson. Mawson's shop is said to have been founded in 1828, the same year Swan was born. Swan soon became a partner, and the firm later traded under the name Mawson, Swan and Morgan. It sold medicines, chemicals and, increasingly, supplies for the exciting new hobby of photography.","Newcastle in the middle of the 1800s was one of the most inventive cities on Earth. Coal from the surrounding mines was shipped down the River Tyne to London and around the world. George and Robert Stephenson had built famous locomotives nearby. The engineer William Armstrong ran enormous works on the riverbank that made cranes, hydraulic machines and, later, ships and guns. The city also had a remarkable club for curious minds: the Literary and Philosophical Society of Newcastle upon Tyne, usually called the Lit and Phil, founded in 1793 as a 'conversation club'. Its members met in a handsome library on Westgate Road to hear lectures and argue about the latest science. Armstrong was its president for forty years, from 1860 to 1900, and Swan was an enthusiastic member.","So picture the scene: a young chemist with steady hands, a shop full of chemicals, a city full of engineers and a society full of people who loved new ideas. It was the perfect place for someone to take on one of the hardest puzzles of the age: how to make electricity give a gentle, steady light that could be used inside a home."]},{"heading":"Paper in a Glass Bubble","paragraphs":["Swan started working on electric light in about 1850, when he was only around 22 years old. He knew the basic idea that you read about in Part 8: if you push enough electric current through a thin strip of material, it heats up, and if it gets hot enough it glows. This glowing-from-heat is called incandescence. The challenge was finding a strip, which inventors called a filament, that could glow brightly for hours without melting, breaking or burning away.","Swan chose carbon. Carbon is the black stuff in soot, charcoal and pencil 'lead' (which is really graphite, a form of carbon). It has a huge advantage: it does not melt at ordinary furnace temperatures. Instead, if it gets extremely hot it turns straight into vapour, and that only happens at temperatures well above 3,000 degrees Celsius. That meant a carbon filament could, in theory, glow white-hot without dripping like a metal wire might.","But how do you make a thin strip of carbon? Swan's trick was to start with something that was already thin and flexible, such as paper or card, and bake it. He cut strips of paper, packed them in powdered charcoal inside a closed container so that air could not get at them, and heated them in a fire. The heat drove off everything in the paper except the carbon, leaving a fragile black skeleton of the original strip. This process is called carbonising. If you have ever seen a piece of toast that was left far too long under the grill and turned black right through, you have seen a rough kind of carbonising.","There was a big catch. Carbon burns in air. If you heat a carbon strip to glowing in an ordinary room, the oxygen in the air reacts with it and it burns away in moments, just like a piece of charcoal on a barbecue. So Swan sealed his carbon strips inside glass bulbs and pumped out as much air as he could, trying to create a vacuum, an empty space with almost no gas inside.","By about 1860 Swan had a lamp that actually worked. It glowed. But it did not glow for long. The pumps of the day could not remove enough air, so the few oxygen molecules left inside slowly attacked the filament. The batteries that supplied the electricity were expensive, weak and messy, and there were not yet any good dynamos to make current from motion. Swan's lamps were short-lived and inefficient, and he could not see a way to make them practical.","So he put the problem aside, though he never really forgot it. For the next fifteen years he poured his energy into photography instead, where he made important inventions that we will come back to at the end of this part. It is worth remembering that this was not giving up. Sometimes an idea is simply ahead of the tools needed to make it work, and a wise inventor waits until the tools catch up."]},{"heading":"Better Pumps and a Night in Newcastle","paragraphs":["The tool that finally caught up was the vacuum pump. In 1865 the German-born chemist Hermann Sprengel, working in London, invented a pump that used falling drops of mercury to trap tiny pockets of air and carry them away, little by little, out of a glass vessel. You read about it in Part 8. Sprengel's pump was slow, but it could make a far emptier space than older piston pumps. (According to some accounts, Swan himself had described a mercury pump design to the British Association in 1863, a couple of years before Sprengel's work, but it was the Sprengel pump that became widely used.)","In about 1875 Swan returned to his old dream. This time he had an important helper: Charles Stearn, a skilled experimenter with vacuum pumps. Together they tackled the problem that had always killed early lamps. Even in a good vacuum, a new carbon filament would give off gases trapped inside it when it first heated up, and soot-like carbon would drift off and coat the inside of the glass. The bulb went black and dim, and the filament wore away.","Swan and Stearn worked out a clever answer. They kept pumping air out of the bulb while current was already flowing through the filament and making it hot. The heat drove out the hidden gases, and the pump carried them away before the bulb was sealed. The result was a cleaner vacuum and a filament that did not ruin its own glass so quickly. Swan later received a British patent for this processing method in 1880.","Now the story arrives at a famous evening. On 18 December 1878 Swan gave a talk to the Newcastle Chemical Society and showed a carbon lamp glowing inside its glass bulb. This was his first public demonstration of the new lamp. According to accounts of the meeting, the demonstration did not go perfectly: the lamp was given too much current and failed. Even so, the idea had been shown in public, and Swan repeated the lecture successfully on 17 January 1879.","Then came the big show. On 3 February 1879 Swan spoke at the Lit and Phil in Newcastle, with his friend William Armstrong in the chair. More than 700 people squeezed in to listen. Imagine the hall, normally lit by gas, with its rows of eager faces, and the quiet moment when Swan connected his apparatus and a small, steady, smokeless glow appeared inside a glass bubble. There was no flame, no smell and no flicker. For many people in that room, it was the first electric light they had ever seen that was not a blinding, sputtering arc lamp.","It is important to be fair here. The lamps Swan showed in 1878 and 1879 were not yet a finished product. Their filaments were fairly thick carbon rods, and they did not last very long. They proved the idea, but they had a hidden weakness that you will meet in the next section. Still, these Newcastle demonstrations came months before Thomas Edison's team in America had its first long-lasting carbon lamp, which is why historians say Swan and Edison were racing side by side, not one behind the other."]},{"heading":"Thick Rods, Thin Threads","paragraphs":["To understand Swan's hidden weakness, we need a quick bit of electrical science. Every material holds back electric current a little. This holding-back is called resistance, measured in ohms. A thick, short piece of carbon has low resistance: current flows through it easily. A long, very thin thread of carbon has high resistance: current has to squeeze through it.","Swan's early rods were fairly thick, so they had low resistance. To make a low-resistance filament glow white-hot, you need to push a large current through it. Large currents cause a problem in the wires that bring the electricity to the lamp. Wires heat up and waste energy when they carry big currents, and the waste grows very fast: double the current and you get about four times as much heating in the wire. To carry big currents without huge losses, you need very thick copper cables, and copper is expensive. Lighting a whole street of houses this way would have meant laying a fortune in copper under the pavement.","There was a second issue. Low-resistance lamps worked best when they were connected in series, one after another in a single loop, like beads on a necklace. The trouble with a series loop is that if one lamp breaks, the loop is broken and every lamp goes out, and you cannot easily switch one room off without affecting the others. For homes, it is much better to connect lamps in parallel, where each lamp has its own path from the main wires, so each can be switched on or off by itself. Parallel lighting on a big scale works best with high-resistance lamps that each take only a small current. This is the very idea that Edison's team concentrated on, as you will see in Part 10.","Swan realised his filament needed to be thinner and tougher. His answer came from chemistry. He took ordinary cotton thread and soaked it in sulfuric acid, which changed the surface of the cotton into a smooth, tough, almost horn-like material. This was called 'parchmentised' thread, because a similar acid treatment was used to make strong parchment paper. When the treated thread was carbonised, it gave a thin, even, springy carbon filament that was far better than his old rods. Swan received British patent number 4933 for this on 27 November 1880.","He kept improving. In 1881 Swan patented a process for squeezing a syrupy form of cellulose, called nitrocellulose, through tiny holes to make long, perfectly even threads. Cellulose is the natural material that makes up plant fibres like cotton and wood. These man-made threads could be carbonised into very uniform filaments, and over the next decades squirted-cellulose filaments became the standard way to make carbon lamps. Interestingly, the same idea of pushing a liquid through tiny holes to make fibres was later taken up by the textile industry, helping lead to artificial silks. Swan's lamp research had accidentally helped invent a new kind of thread for clothes.","By 20 October 1880 Swan's improved lamps were good enough to light the lecture room of the Lit and Phil during a talk, and the society proudly describes its building as the first public building lit this way. The gentle glow had moved out of the laboratory and into a room full of ordinary people."]},{"heading":"Underhill and the Wizard's Castle","paragraphs":["Swan did much of his experimenting at home, in the conservatory of his house called Underhill, at Low Fell in Gateshead, just across the River Tyne from Newcastle. A conservatory is a glass room attached to a house, usually full of plants, and Swan's was full of glassware, batteries and wires instead. Underhill is often described as the first house in the world to be lit by working electric light bulbs. Today a blue plaque marks the spot. Imagine being one of Swan's children, walking into a room in the evening and seeing it filled with steady light from little glass globes, while every other house in the street still glowed orange with gas and candles.","The next house to shine belonged to Swan's friend William Armstrong. Armstrong was a lawyer who had become one of Britain's greatest engineers. He had built a country house called Cragside, perched on a rocky hillside above the Debdon Burn near Rothbury in Northumberland. Armstrong loved water power. He built dams on his estate to create five new lakes, and used the falling water to drive machines.","In 1870 Armstrong had a water-powered engine on the estate drive a Siemens dynamo, and by 1878 this was lighting an arc lamp in Cragside's picture gallery. This made Cragside a pioneer of hydroelectric power: electricity made from moving water. But as you read in Part 7, an arc lamp is a harsh, dazzling thing, more suited to a street than a room full of paintings.","In 1880 Swan personally supervised the fitting of his incandescent lamps at Cragside. The gallery got twelve overhead lamps, with eight more that could be switched on using current borrowed from the dining room lamps when nobody was eating. Swan called it 'the first proper installation' of his lighting. It was the first private home, other than Swan's own, to be lit by his bulbs, and it was powered not by coal but by water from Armstrong's lakes, sent through a generator in a building later known as the Burnfoot Power House. Today Cragside is looked after by the National Trust, and a modern water turbine installed in 2014 once again makes some of the house's electricity.","Swan lamps soon spread far beyond Northumberland. In June 1881 the passenger steamship City of Richmond was fitted with them, and the Royal Navy started using them too, with the warship HMS Inflexible among the first. On a ship, a flame-free light was a huge safety improvement. Swan also helped develop electric safety lamps for coal miners, shown in Newcastle in 1881. In mines, an open flame could set off an explosion of the gas called firedamp, so a light with its glowing part sealed inside glass was a very attractive idea. In 1881 Swan founded the Swan Electric Light Company to make lamps in large numbers, and later that year his lamps were shown at the great International Exposition of Electricity in Paris, where he won a French honour, the Legion of Honour."]},{"heading":"The Savoy: A Theatre Without Gas","paragraphs":["The most famous early Swan installation was in London. Richard D'Oyly Carte was a theatre producer who presented the comic operas of W. S. Gilbert and Arthur Sullivan, such as The Pirates of Penzance. Their shows were so popular that Carte built a brand-new theatre for them beside the Strand in London, called the Savoy. It opened on 10 October 1881 with Gilbert and Sullivan's opera Patience, which moved there from another theatre where it had been running since April.","Victorian theatres were lit by hundreds of gas jets. They were hot, they used up oxygen, and they left the air stuffy and smelly. Worse, theatres were full of painted scenery, curtains and costumes, and gas-lit theatre fires were a terrible danger in the 1800s. Carte decided to do something nobody had done before: light an entire public building with electricity. He argued that electric lamps did not make the air hot and stuffy like gas, and that they 'consume no oxygen'.","Swan supplied about 1,200 of his lamps. Electricity came from a 120-horsepower steam-driven generator set up on open land near the theatre. At first the generator was not big enough for everything, so the auditorium and front of house were lit electrically while the stage was still lit by gas. On 28 December 1881 the stage was switched over too, and from then on the Savoy was lit entirely by electricity, making it the first public building in the world to be so. Gas lights were kept as a backup, but they were rarely needed.","Many people in the audience were nervous. Wasn't electricity the stuff of lightning? Might the lamps explode or start a fire? Carte answered with a piece of showmanship. On stage, in front of the audience, he took a glowing lamp and broke it. The light went out, and nothing caught fire. A gas jet would never have been so harmless. The message was clear: this new light was safe.","Newspapers were impressed. The Times judged that the electric light was clearly better than gaslight for seeing the stage. Audiences could see the actors' faces more clearly, the colours of costumes looked truer, and the hall stayed cooler. Today a plaque at the Savoy remembers Swan's achievement. A London theatre audience laughing at a comic opera might not seem like a turning point in history, but for many wealthy Londoners the Savoy was the place where they first saw electric light behave like a friendly servant instead of a dangerous wild animal."]},{"heading":"Rivals Become Partners","paragraphs":["While Swan was lighting Newcastle, Gateshead and London, Thomas Edison's team in New Jersey was racing toward the same goal. Edison had boldly told reporters he would solve electric lighting, and in January 1880 he received his American patent for a carbon-filament lamp. Both men developed their lamps independently, and both patented them around 1880. Each had good reason to think the bulb was his.","When Edison's company began selling lamps in Britain, a clash was almost certain. Swan held strong British patents, and he had shown working carbon lamps in public in Newcastle before Edison's famous demonstrations. Edison held patents too. Accounts differ about exactly who sued whom and in what order, but it is clear that both sides started legal action over their patents, and that a long fight in court would have been costly and uncertain for both. Swan's early public demonstrations made it very hard for Edison to claim that the whole invention was his alone in Britain.","So, instead of fighting to the end, the two sides made a deal. In 1883 the Swan and Edison businesses in Britain merged to form the Edison and Swan United Electric Light Company. People quickly shortened the long name to 'Ediswan', and Ediswan lamps became famous across the British Empire. The company made its lamps at a factory at Ponders End in north London, and later it made radio valves there too: glass tubes that grew out of light bulb technology and powered early radios.","The story of Swan and Edison is a good lesson in how invention really works. It was not one lonely genius suddenly shouting 'Eureka!' It was a crowd of people across several countries, building on Davy, de Moleyns, Sprengel and many others, and sometimes arriving at similar answers at almost the same time. In Britain, Swan's name is often given first place. In America, Edison's is. A fair historian gives both their due: Swan for his early, practical carbon lamps and lovely lighting installations, and Edison's team for a long-lasting lamp and a whole electrical system to run it."]},{"heading":"Pictures, Honours and a Long Life","paragraphs":["Swan's light bulb was not his only great invention. During the years when he had put electric light aside, he became one of the most important figures in early photography. In 1864 he patented the carbon process, a way of making photographic prints using a pigment such as carbon in gelatine. Carbon prints did not fade the way many early photographs did, and Swan sold his patents for the process to the Autotype Company in 1868.","He also noticed that gently heating a type of light-sensitive emulsion made with silver bromide made it far more sensitive to light. This helped lead to faster 'dry plates', which photographers could buy ready-made instead of having to coat glass plates with wet chemicals moments before taking a picture. Then, in 1879, the same year as his famous light bulb lecture, he patented bromide paper. This was paper coated with a silver bromide emulsion, and it became the standard paper for printing black-and-white photographs for many decades. If you have ever seen an old family photo from the 1900s, there is a good chance it was printed on a descendant of Swan's bromide paper.","Swan's family life had sadness as well as joy. He married Frances White, known as Fanny, in 1862, and after her death in 1868 he married her younger sister, Hannah, in 1871. He had several children. His daughter Frances Isobel became a sculptor. Her son, Christopher Morcom, was a close school friend of the future computer pioneer Alan Turing.","Honours piled up. Swan was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society, Britain's leading science academy, in 1894. He became president of the Institution of Electrical Engineers in 1898, and in 1903 he was the first president of the Faraday Society, named after the hero whose lectures he had read about as a boy. In 1904 King Edward VII knighted him, so he became Sir Joseph Swan, and in the same year the Royal Society awarded him its Hughes Medal. He died at his home in Warlingham, Surrey, on 27 May 1914, aged 85, having lived from the age of candles and gas into an age when electric light was spreading through cities across the world.","But to see how electric light became an entire industry, we have to cross the Atlantic Ocean. In a small village in New Jersey, in a long white wooden building full of bottles, wires and machines, a famous inventor had promised the newspapers that he would beat everyone to a cheap, practical electric light. His name was Thomas Edison, and his team was working day and night."]}],"deepDive":"Why does Swan's name come first in Britain and Edison's in America? Part of the answer is national pride, but part is genuinely technical. Historians usually separate three things: who first made a carbon lamp glow in a vacuum, who first made one that lasted long enough to sell, and who first built a system to supply many lamps cheaply. Swan had a glowing carbonised-paper lamp by about 1860, decades before Edison, but it was not practical. His December 1878 and February 1879 demonstrations came before Edison's successful carbon-filament test of 22 October 1879, which is why British courts took his priority seriously. However, Swan's early lamps used comparatively thick carbon rods with low resistance. The power P drawn by a lamp equals V times I (voltage times current). For the same light, a low-resistance lamp needs a large current at a low voltage, and the power wasted in the supply wires is I squared times R, so doubling the current roughly quadruples the loss. That forces either very thick copper mains or lamps wired in series, where one failure darkens the whole chain. Edison's team, guided by Francis Upton's calculations, deliberately chased a high-resistance filament (around 100 ohms or more when hot) so lamps could be wired in parallel at about 100 volts with modest currents. Swan's parchmentised cotton thread (patent 1880) and his squirted nitrocellulose filaments (patented 1881) moved his lamp toward that ideal. The squirted-cellulose method was so good that it later became the standard way of making carbon filaments, and the same principle fed into the artificial-fibre industry. Another debate concerns vacuum technology: Swan is said to have described a mercury pump in 1863, but the Sprengel pump of 1865 was the one that spread through laboratories, and Swan's later success depended on Charles Stearn's skill with it, especially the trick of heating the filament while pumping to drive out trapped gases. The 1883 Ediswan merger was a business compromise rather than a court verdict on who invented the bulb. The fairest summary is that the practical incandescent lamp was a shared, overlapping achievement, and Swan's Newcastle work was an essential part of it.","funFact":"When Richard D'Oyly Carte wanted to prove his new electric lights were safe, he broke a glowing bulb on the Savoy Theatre stage in front of the audience, and nothing caught fire.","check":{"q":"Which London theatre, opened in 1881, became the first public building in the world lit entirely by electricity using Swan lamps?","a":"The Savoy Theatre","wrong":["The Globe Theatre","The Royal Albert Hall","The Palladium"]},"sources":[{"title":"Joseph Swan","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Swan"},{"title":"Savoy Theatre","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Savoy_Theatre"},{"title":"Cragside","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cragside"},{"title":"Incandescent light bulb","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incandescent_light_bulb"},{"title":"Literary and Philosophical Society of Newcastle upon Tyne","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Literary_and_Philosophical_Society_of_Newcastle_upon_Tyne"},{"title":"Edison and Swan Electric Light Company","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edison_and_Swan_Electric_Light_Company"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/09-1.jpg","alt":"Sir Joseph Wilson Swan, an old man with a long white beard, at his laboratory bench in 1910.","file":"Joseph Wilson Swan 2.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Wilson_Swan_2.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/09-2.jpg","alt":"Old drawings of three early carbon-filament lamps: Edison's, Maxim's and, on the right, Swan's with its looped filament.","file":"EdisonMaximSwan.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EdisonMaximSwan.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/09-3.jpg","alt":"Cragside in Northumberland, England, the first house lit by hydroelectric power and Swan's light bulbs.","file":"Cragside1.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cragside1.jpg","author":"Glen Bowman from Newcastle, England","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4109},{"n":10,"title":"Edison's Invention Factory","when":"1876 – 1892","summary":"Thomas Edison built a laboratory in the New Jersey countryside where a whole team invented things together. They baked thread, paper and thousands of plants into carbon until they found filaments that glowed for hours, then for months. Then they invented everything else a light bulb needs: switches, fuses, meters, wires and generators.","sections":[{"heading":"The Wizard Moves to the Countryside","paragraphs":["Thomas Alva Edison was born on 11 February 1847 in Milan, Ohio, the youngest of seven children. When he was seven his family moved to Port Huron, Michigan. He went to school for only a few months. His mother taught him to read, write and do arithmetic, and after that he taught himself, devouring books on science and machines. At about thirteen he was selling newspapers and sweets to passengers on a railway line running to Detroit, and by sixteen he was working full-time as a telegraph operator, tapping out and decoding messages in Morse code. The telegraph, which you met in Part 7, taught him a huge amount about batteries, wires, magnets and circuits.","Edison began inventing improvements to the telegraph, and he was good at it. His first patent, granted in 1869, was for an electric vote recorder. In the early 1870s he ran workshops in Newark, New Jersey, and sold an important telegraph invention, the quadruplex system, which could send four messages along one wire at the same time. The money from that sale helped pay for his boldest idea yet.","In 1876 Edison sold his Newark businesses and moved his family and his team to Menlo Park, a quiet patch of New Jersey countryside about 25 miles (40 kilometres) southwest of New York City. The place was a failed housing development with a railway station and a few houses. There he built a white, two-storey wooden building that cost about 2,500 dollars and was finished on 25 March 1876. On the ground floor were a machine shop full of precision tools and an area for chemistry and scientific instruments. Upstairs was a long, bright laboratory lined with shelves of bottles and jars.","What made Menlo Park special was not one building but one idea: a place built specially for inventing, with tools, materials and skilled workers all ready for whatever project came next. It is often described as the first industrial research laboratory, a model for later research centres such as Bell Laboratories. Edison reportedly boasted that his team would turn out small inventions every few weeks and a big one every few months. People nicknamed it the 'invention factory'. By spring 1878 the staff had grown to about 25 men, including experimenters, laboratory assistants, machinists and a patternmaker who carved the wooden shapes used to cast metal parts.","The invention that made Edison world-famous was the phonograph. In early December 1877 his machinist John Kruesi built the first one from Edison's sketch: a metal cylinder wrapped in tinfoil, turned by a handle, with a needle that cut a groove as someone spoke into a horn. Turn the handle again, and the needle traced the groove and played the voice back. On 7 December 1877 Edison showed it at the office of the magazine Scientific American in New York, and in April 1878 he demonstrated it to President Rutherford B. Hayes. A machine that could capture a human voice seemed almost like magic, and the newspapers began calling Edison 'the Wizard of Menlo Park'. Later in life, Edison said he disliked being called a genius."]},{"heading":"A Team, Not a Lone Genius","paragraphs":["Edison's name is on the patents, but the Menlo Park lab was a team, and the light bulb was team invention. Let's meet some of the people who worked in that long upstairs room.","Charles Batchelor was born on Christmas Day 1845 and grew up in Manchester, England, where he trained as a draftsman and machinist. He met Edison in Newark in 1870, and by 1873 Edison was calling him his 'chief experimental assistant'. Batchelor was patient, careful and good with his hands. He and Edison worked so closely that some of their notebooks were jointly signed 'E and B'. During the light bulb work, Batchelor carried out many of the delicate filament experiments himself, carbonising materials and mounting them in bulbs.","John Kruesi was born in Switzerland in 1843 and trained as a locksmith before moving to the United States in 1870. Edison hired him in 1872, and at Menlo Park he became head machinist. Edison would scribble a rough sketch, and Kruesi would turn it into a working machine. His work was so precise that historians say when a device failed, it was usually because the idea was wrong, not because it was badly built. Kruesi built the first phonograph and many parts of the lighting system, including generators.","Francis Upton was the team's mathematician. He had studied at Bowdoin College and Princeton University, where he earned a doctorate, and then spent a year in Berlin studying under the famous physicist Hermann von Helmholtz. Edison hired him in late 1878, first to search through all the earlier patents and scientific papers on electric lighting, and then as a mathematical and experimental assistant. Edison had brilliant instincts, but Upton could turn those instincts into equations, working out how thick the wires should be and how much power a system would need. Because of his university learning and his piano playing, Edison nicknamed him 'Culture'.","In 1879 the team gained a skilled German glassblower named Ludwig Boehm (also spelled Böhm). Every experimental bulb had to be shaped by hand from hot glass, with the filament's wire leads sealed tightly into it so no air could leak in. Boehm made bulbs and helped build better vacuum pumps, which, as you saw with Joseph Swan in Part 9, were essential to keeping a filament alive.","There were many others too, such as the machinist John Ott and his brother Fred Ott, who went on to work for Edison for fifty-seven years. Imagine the lab late at night: gas lamps hissing, a vacuum pump dripping mercury drop by drop, Batchelor peering at a glowing thread through a smoked glass, Upton scribbling sums, Kruesi's lathe whirring downstairs, and Edison wandering between benches asking questions. That noisy, crowded room is the real picture of how the light bulb was invented."]},{"heading":"A Bold Promise and a Clever Idea","paragraphs":["Edison began serious electric light experiments in the late summer of 1878. On 8 September 1878 he travelled with Charles Batchelor, the physics professor George Barker and the chemist Charles Chandler to the workshop of William Wallace in Ansonia, Connecticut. Wallace and Moses Farmer had built a dynamo that powered a set of arc lights. Edison was fascinated. He came away convinced that he could do something better: divide that blazing arc light into many small, gentle lights for homes, the challenge called 'subdividing the light' that you met in Part 7.","Within days Edison announced to newspaper reporters that he had solved the problem of electric lighting. He had not, not yet. It was a bold, even reckless, promise, but it worked as publicity. On 16 October 1878 a group of wealthy investors, many connected with the Western Union telegraph company and the banking firm Drexel, Morgan and Company, founded the Edison Electric Light Company to pay for the research. Edison now had money, but he also had to deliver.","His first lamps used platinum, a metal that melts at a very high temperature, about 1,770 degrees Celsius. Platinum filaments could glow, but they tended to melt or fail if they got slightly too hot, and Edison's team built clever switches to cut the current when a filament overheated. Even more seriously, platinum was extremely expensive. A lighting system for a whole city made with platinum would have cost a fortune.","While the team struggled with materials, Edison and Upton thought hard about the system as a whole. Remember the problem Swan faced: low-resistance lamps need big currents, and big currents need thick, expensive copper wires. Edison wanted his lamps arranged in parallel, so that each lamp had its own path from the main wires and could be switched on and off by itself, just like a gas lamp. With lamps in parallel, every lamp you add draws extra current from the main wires.","Here is where Upton's maths mattered. The power used by a lamp equals voltage multiplied by current. If each lamp had a high resistance, it would take only a small current at a sensible voltage, around 100 volts. Small currents meant much less energy wasted as heat in the wires, so the copper cables could be far thinner and cheaper. The team's calculations showed that a high-resistance filament could cut the copper needed to a small fraction of what low-resistance lamps would require. Historians count this as one of Edison's key insights: he designed the lamp to fit the system, instead of designing the system to fit the lamp.","A high-resistance filament had to be long and very thin, like a hair. Making something that thin glow white-hot for hundreds of hours inside a glass bulb was the real problem. The team needed better vacuum pumps, better glass sealing, and the right material. They began to look closely at carbon again."]},{"heading":"Thirteen Hours of Cotton Thread","paragraphs":["Carbon had been tried for decades, as you saw in Part 8, but now the Menlo Park team had something earlier inventors had lacked: excellent vacuum pumps, a skilled glassblower and a determination to test, test and test again. Batchelor carbonised all sorts of things: paper, card, wood shavings, fishing line, and plain cotton sewing thread. Carbonising thin thread was maddeningly difficult. The fragile black fibres snapped when someone breathed too hard, and many broke while being clamped to their wire leads.","On 22 October 1879 the team tried a lamp with a filament of carbonised cotton thread, sealed in a bulb pumped down to a very good vacuum. It began to glow. Hours passed, and it was still glowing. Lab records point to a life of roughly 13 to 15 hours; the US National Park Service, which looks after Edison's later laboratory, says it burned for about 13 and a half hours. That might not sound much, but every earlier attempt had lasted minutes or a few hours at most. It was the first successful experiment with a high-resistance carbon filament, and the team knew they had found the right path. You may read elsewhere that this first bulb burned for 40 hours, but the laboratory evidence supports the shorter figure.","Edison moved quickly to protect the idea. He signed his patent application on 1 November 1879, and it was filed at the US Patent Office on 4 November 1879. The patent, number 223,898, was granted on 27 January 1880. It described an electric lamp with a carbon filament of high resistance, made from materials such as cotton or paper, inside a glass bulb with a nearly perfect vacuum.","Over the next weeks the team improved the design. They found that carbonised paper and card could be cut into neat horseshoe-shaped filaments that were easier to make in large numbers. More lamps were blown, pumped and sealed. The lab began to prepare something Edison loved: a big public show."]},{"heading":"New Year's Eve at Menlo Park","paragraphs":["News of the lamp leaked out. In late December 1879, newspaper stories about the new electric light sent waves of excitement across America. Edison decided to throw open the doors of Menlo Park and let the public see for themselves. The first public demonstration of his incandescent lighting system took place on 31 December 1879, New Year's Eve.","In the weeks before, John Kruesi oversaw the preparations, which were recorded carefully in an order book. Workers ran wires to the laboratory buildings and to nearby houses, and put up a line of poles carrying lamps along the road. When darkness fell, the little village glowed with dozens of steady electric lights, while the rest of rural New Jersey sat in the dark or lit by oil lamps.","Crowds came from New York, Newark and beyond, many of them arriving by train at the Menlo Park station. Imagine stepping off a cold train on a cold winter evening and seeing a row of glowing globes along a country road, then walking into a laboratory lit by lamps with no flame, no smoke and no smell. Visitors could watch lamps being switched on and off, one at a time, without affecting the others, which showed the power of the parallel system. Christie Street, the road beside the lab, became one of the first streets in the world lit by electric lamps.","Not everyone was convinced. Some experts, especially those connected to the gas industry, doubted that the lamps would last or that a whole city could ever be wired. They had a point: a lamp that lasted a few hundred hours, connected to a few generators in a village, was very different from a system lighting thousands of buildings. To win, Edison's team still needed a longer-lasting filament and a full set of machinery to make, measure and deliver electricity."]},{"heading":"The Great Bamboo Hunt","paragraphs":["The hunt for a better filament became one of the most famous searches in the history of invention. Edison's team tested thousands of materials; by some counts, more than 6,000 different plant materials were tried, from grasses and wood fibres to cork and coconut hair. Each one had to be cut, carbonised, mounted in a bulb, pumped and lit, then timed until it failed. It was slow, patient, repetitive work, the opposite of the 'flash of genius' story people like to tell.","In the summer of 1880 the team found a winner. That summer they began testing lamps with bamboo filaments. The long, straight fibres of bamboo turned into tough, even carbon threads when carbonised, and a particular kind of bamboo from Japan proved best of all. Bamboo filaments could last more than 1,200 hours, enough to light a home for many months of evenings. Bamboo became the standard filament in Edison lamps for years, and Edison even sent people travelling to search for the best bamboo in different countries. Later, squirted cellulose filaments, made by a method similar to Joseph Swan's, took over.","Meanwhile, the lamps had found their first paying customer. Henry Villard, a businessman who had invested in Edison's company, owned a new steamship called the Columbia. In spring 1880 Edison's team installed a complete lighting plant on board, with dynamos and lamps, before the ship sailed around South America to Oregon. It was the first commercial use of Edison's incandescent lamps, and the first marine incandescent lighting plant. On a wooden ship full of passengers, a light without flames was a very welcome thing.","It is worth pausing on one famous saying. You may have seen Edison quoted as saying that he had not failed, he had just found thousands of ways that would not work. Versions of this saying are everywhere, but nobody has found a reliable record of Edison saying those exact words, so it is best treated as a popular saying rather than a true quote. What the laboratory notebooks do show is the spirit behind it: careful, stubborn testing, with every failure written down and learned from."]},{"heading":"Rivals and the Remarkable Lewis Latimer","paragraphs":["Edison's team was not the only one in America chasing the electric lamp. One of the strongest rivals was Hiram Maxim, born in 1840 in Sangerville, Maine. In 1878 Maxim co-founded the United States Electric Lighting Company with the engineer Edward Weston. He installed what are described as the first electric lights in a New York City building, the Equitable Life Building on Broadway, and he held many patents on lamps and on ways of making carbon. Maxim argued bitterly with Edison over who deserved credit for the bulb, complaining that Edison simply understood patent law better. Another American inventor, William Sawyer, also claimed to have made carbon lamps first. In 1883 the US Patent Office ruled against part of Edison's patent because of Sawyer's earlier work, but in 1889 a judge upheld Edison's claim to the carbon filament. Maxim himself had moved to Britain in 1881, and he later became famous for a very different invention, an automatic machine gun.","Working at Maxim's company was one of the most remarkable people in this whole story. Lewis Howard Latimer was born in 1848 in Chelsea, Massachusetts. His parents, George and Rebecca Latimer, had escaped from slavery in Virginia in 1842, and George's capture in Boston became a famous court case that stirred the campaign against slavery in Massachusetts. Lewis grew up poor. At sixteen he joined the United States Navy and served in 1864 and 1865, near the end of the American Civil War.","After the war Latimer got a job as an office boy at a patent law firm in Boston. He watched the draftsmen making precise technical drawings for patents, taught himself to draw, and rose to become head draftsman. In 1876 he drew the patent drawings for Alexander Graham Bell's telephone. In 1879 he joined Maxim's United States Electric Lighting Company in Bridgeport, Connecticut.","There, Latimer tackled a nagging problem. Carbon filaments often cracked or warped while being baked, and many were wasted. Latimer developed a better way of heat-treating the filaments that reduced breakage and allowed them to be formed into neat shapes, such as the 'M'-shaped filaments used in Maxim lamps. His patent for a process of manufacturing carbons was granted on 17 January 1882. He also helped set up electric lighting systems in several cities, travelling to supervise installations.","In 1884 Latimer joined the Edison company as a draftsman and patent expert, helping to defend Edison's patents in court with his sharp eye for technical detail. In 1890 he published a book, Incandescent Electric Lighting, explaining how electric lighting systems worked. He stayed with the company after it became part of General Electric in 1892. In 1918 he became the first person of colour in the Edison Pioneers, a club of people who had worked with Edison in the early days. Latimer was also a poet and musician, taught English and drawing to immigrants, and worked for civil rights. He died in Flushing, New York, in 1928, and today he is honoured in the National Inventors Hall of Fame. His life is a reminder that the story of the light bulb includes many people whose names were left out of the textbooks for too long."]},{"heading":"Edison's Real Genius: A Whole System","paragraphs":["If Edison did not invent the first incandescent lamp, why is he so famous for it? The best answer is that his team built not just a bulb, but everything a bulb needs to be useful. A lamp on its own is like a single tap with no pipes, reservoir or water meter. Edison's team designed the whole waterworks of electricity.","First, they needed generators. Kruesi and the team designed dynamos that gave a steady voltage, so lamps would not flicker or brighten as others were switched on and off. One early design, with two tall upright magnets, earned the funny nickname 'Long-Waisted Mary Ann' (it is also remembered as 'Long-Legged Mary Ann'). Second, they needed safe wiring. Overhead wires could be torn down by storms, so Edison planned underground cables in protective tubes, and in 1881 Kruesi was put in charge of the Edison Electric Tube Company to make them.","Third, they needed small parts that we now take for granted: screw-in lamp sockets, switches, and fuses, thin pieces of metal that melt and break the circuit if too much current flows, preventing fires. Fourth, a company selling electricity needed to know how much each customer used, so the team developed meters; Upton helped work on them. And finally, they needed central power stations to feed it all, which you will visit in Part 12.","In 1881 Edison left Menlo Park to set up factories and offices in New York, where he could build and install this system. Over the following years his electric lighting work led to many Edison electric lighting companies being set up across America and around the world. In 1887 he opened a much bigger laboratory complex in West Orange, New Jersey, and in 1892 his companies merged with a rival to form General Electric. By the end of his life, on 18 October 1931, Edison held 1,093 US patents, more than almost anyone in history.","Menlo Park itself did not survive. The buildings fell into ruin after Edison left. But the car maker Henry Ford, a great admirer of Edison, rebuilt the laboratory at his museum village, Greenfield Village in Dearborn, Michigan, using exact measurements of the original foundations and some of the original materials. It was dedicated on 21 October 1929, fifty years after the October 1879 lamp test, at a celebration called Light's Golden Jubilee. President Herbert Hoover came, and the elderly Edison himself took part, recreating the moment his team's lamp first glowed.","We have now met the inventors: Davy, de Moleyns, Swan, Edison, Batchelor, Upton, Kruesi, Boehm, Maxim, Latimer and more. But what actually happens inside that little glass ball when you flick the switch? What is the electricity doing, and why does a thin thread glow when a thick wire stays cool? To find out, we need to shrink down to the size of an atom."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of technology, especially Thomas P. Hughes, argue that Edison's most important contribution was not the lamp itself but a 'system' designed as a whole. The economic logic is worth following. In a parallel circuit every lamp sees the same voltage, and the total current is the sum of the lamp currents. Copper losses in the mains are I squared times R, so a district of low-resistance lamps drawing large currents would need enormous conductors. By choosing a filament with a hot resistance of very roughly 100 ohms or more, and a supply of about 100 to 110 volts, each lamp drew only around one ampere or less. Francis Upton's calculations, applying Ohm's law and Joule heating to street-length conductors, showed this could cut the weight of copper dramatically, making central-station lighting affordable against gas. That is why Edison's notes return again and again to 'high resistance'. The timeline is also debated. Edison announced success in September 1878, more than a year before he had it, partly to attract investors; the first successful high-resistance carbon test came on 22 October 1879, with lab records indicating roughly 13 to 15 hours of life, not the 40 hours sometimes claimed. Patent 223,898 was challenged for years: in 1883 the Patent Office favoured William Sawyer and Albon Man on certain claims, while in 1889 a federal court upheld Edison's carbon-filament claim. Meanwhile Britain's Ediswan merger reflected Joseph Swan's strong prior position. Another debate concerns credit inside the lab. Notebooks show that Charles Batchelor did many of the filament experiments, Upton the mathematics, John Kruesi the machines and Ludwig Boehm the glass and pumps, and Edison's company also benefited from outsiders such as Lewis Latimer, whose 1882 filament-making patent came from his time at Hiram Maxim's rival firm. Finally, there is a famous scientific side-effect: while studying why bulbs blackened, Edison's team noticed that current could flow through the vacuum from the hot filament to a separate metal plate. This 'Edison effect' was not understood until the electron was discovered, but it later became the basis of the vacuum tubes that powered radio and early computers.","funFact":"The machinist John Kruesi, who built Edison's first phonograph in 1877 from a rough sketch, later helped run General Electric's huge works in Schenectady, New York.","check":{"q":"Why did Edison's team want a thin filament with high resistance?","a":"So many lamps could run side by side in parallel using small currents and thinner, cheaper copper wires","wrong":["So the bulb would glow blue instead of yellow","So the lamp would not need a glass bulb","So the lamp could run without any generator"]},"sources":[{"title":"Chronology 1871–1880","publisher":"Thomas A. Edison Papers, Rutgers University","url":"https://edison.rutgers.edu/life-of-edison/chronology/1871-1880"},{"title":"The Invention Factory","publisher":"Thomas A. Edison Papers, Rutgers University","url":"https://edison.rutgers.edu/life-of-edison/biographical-essays/the-invention-factory"},{"title":"A Big Bonanza: Edison's Electric Lighting System","publisher":"Thomas A. Edison Papers, Rutgers University","url":"https://edison.rutgers.edu/life-of-edison/biographical-essays/a-big-bonanza-edison-s-electric-lighting-system"},{"title":"Edison Biography","publisher":"US National Park Service, Thomas Edison National Historical Park","url":"https://www.nps.gov/edis/learn/historyculture/edison-biography.htm"},{"title":"The Electric Light System, Phonograph and Motion Pictures","publisher":"US National Park Service","url":"https://www.nps.gov/edis/learn/kidsyouth/the-electric-light-system-phonograph-motion-pictures.htm"},{"title":"Lewis Howard Latimer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewis_Howard_Latimer"},{"title":"Francis Robbins Upton","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Robbins_Upton"},{"title":"Incandescent light bulb","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incandescent_light_bulb"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/10-1.jpg","alt":"Edison's Menlo Park laboratory building, rebuilt at Greenfield Village in Dearborn, Michigan.","file":"Menlo Park Laboratory of Thomas Edison site of the Invention of the light bulb.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menlo_Park_Laboratory_of_Thomas_Edison_site_of_the_Invention_of_the_light_bulb.JPG","author":"Swampyank","license":"CC BY 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/10-2.jpg","alt":"An original 1879 Edison light bulb from Menlo Park, with its thin horseshoe-shaped carbon filament inside the glass.","file":"Edison Carbon Bulb.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edison_Carbon_Bulb.jpg","author":"Terren","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/10-3.jpg","alt":"Inventor and draftsman Lewis H. Latimer standing beside his seated wife, Mary.","file":"Mr. and Mrs. Lewis H. Latimer.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mr._and_Mrs._Lewis_H._Latimer.jpg","author":"Unknown photographer","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3823},{"n":11,"title":"Inside the Bulb: How It Works","when":"Science + 1897 – 1925","summary":"A light bulb is a tiny wire that gets so hot it glows white, hotter than lava. Billions of electrons push through it, bumping and jostling, and that bumping makes heat and light. In this part we shrink down to see how it all works, meet the metal that made bulbs last, and visit a fire station bulb that has been glowing for more than 120 years.","sections":[{"heading":"Shrinking Down Inside a Wire","paragraphs":["Edison, Swan and all the other lamp makers we have met so far had a strange problem. They could build a bulb that glowed, measure how bright it was, and work out how much copper wire they needed to feed it. But not one of them knew what electricity actually was. They talked about an 'electric fluid' that flowed through wires the way water flows through a hose. It was a useful picture, but nobody had ever seen the thing that was flowing.","The answer arrived in 1897, almost twenty years after the first good carbon lamps. In Cambridge, England, a physicist named Joseph John Thomson, known to everyone as J.J., was working at the Cavendish Laboratory. He was studying cathode rays, the mysterious glowing beams that appear inside a glass tube when most of the air is pumped out and a high voltage is applied across it. Scientists had argued for years about what these rays were. Some thought they were a kind of wave, like light. Others thought they were a stream of tiny particles.","Thomson found that the rays could be bent by magnets and also by electric charges, and he measured how much they bent. From those measurements he worked out that the rays were made of particles that carried negative charge and were incredibly light: more than a thousand times lighter than a hydrogen atom, the lightest atom of all. Even more surprising, the particles were exactly the same no matter which gas was in the tube or which metal the rays came from. On 30 April 1897 he announced his idea that atoms were not the smallest things in the universe after all. They contained much smaller pieces.","Thomson called these pieces 'corpuscles', an old word meaning tiny bodies. Other scientists preferred the name 'electron', which the Irish physicist George Johnstone Stoney had suggested a few years earlier for the smallest unit of electric charge, and that name stuck. Thomson won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1906, and today a plaque outside the old Cavendish Laboratory marks the discovery.","Here is the picture that finally made sense of every light bulb. Everything around you, including the copper in a wire, is made of atoms. Each atom has a heavy centre (the nucleus, which carries positive charge) surrounded by electrons, which carry negative charge. In most materials the electrons stay tightly attached to their own atoms. But in metals like copper, silver and aluminium, some electrons are only loosely held. They wander from atom to atom, forming a kind of electron 'sea' that fills the whole piece of metal. That is why metals are such good conductors: they are packed with charges that are free to move.","An electric current, then, is simply a flow of charge. In a metal wire, it is that sea of free electrons drifting along in one direction. (In salty water, inside a battery and in your own nerves, the moving charges are ions instead: atoms that have gained or lost electrons.)","One surprise often shocks people (not literally). You might think electrons race through a wire at the speed of light. They do not. In an ordinary household wire, the electrons drift along at roughly a millimetre per second, slower than a snail. Meanwhile they are also jiggling around randomly at enormous speeds, but that random jiggling goes nowhere in particular. So why does the light come on the instant you flip the switch? Because the wire is already completely full of electrons. When the switch closes, the electrical push travels along the wire almost as fast as light, and electrons all along the circuit start drifting at once, including the ones already sitting inside the bulb. It is like a garden hose that is already full of water: turn the tap, and water comes out of the far end immediately, even though no single drop has travelled the whole length.","Another common myth is that a bulb 'uses up' electricity. It does not use up the electrons at all. Every electron that flows into the bulb flows back out again, around the circuit and back to the power station or battery. What the bulb uses is energy. The electrons arrive carrying energy, given to them by the push of the power source, and inside the bulb that energy is turned into heat and light. The charges themselves just keep going round and round, like the chain on a bicycle, which carries the push of your legs to the wheel without ever being used up."]},{"heading":"Push, Flow and Squeeze: Volts, Amps and Ohms","paragraphs":["To understand what happens inside a bulb, you need three words that electricians use every day: voltage, current and resistance. They are easiest to picture with water in pipes, the same comparison the early lamp makers liked.","Imagine a water tank on a hill, with a pipe running down to a water wheel and back up again by a pump. Voltage is like the pressure in the pipe, the push that drives the water along. A tall tank with lots of pressure is like a high voltage. Voltage is measured in volts, named after Alessandro Volta, whose pile of metal discs we met in Part 4. A single AA battery gives about 1.5 volts. A car battery gives about 12 volts. The sockets in a British or European home give about 230 volts, and in North America about 120 volts.","Current is like the amount of water flowing past a point each second. In electricity, it is the amount of charge flowing past a point each second, and it is measured in amperes, or amps for short, named after André-Marie Ampère. One amp means a certain very large number of electrons (about six billion billion of them) passing a point every second.","Resistance is how hard it is for the current to get through. A wide, smooth pipe lets water gush through easily; a narrow pipe stuffed with gravel holds it back. In the same way, a thick copper wire has very low resistance, while a long, thin wire made of a less conductive metal has much more. Resistance is measured in ohms, named after the German schoolteacher and physicist Georg Simon Ohm.","In 1827, Ohm published the rule that links all three, now called Ohm's law. In simple words it says: current equals voltage divided by resistance. Double the push and you double the flow. Double the resistance and you halve the flow. Engineers write it as I = V / R, where I stands for current, V for voltage and R for resistance.","There is one more useful rule. The power used by something electrical, meaning how fast it turns electrical energy into other kinds of energy, equals voltage times current. Power is measured in watts, named after the Scottish steam engineer James Watt. A 60-watt bulb on a 120-volt American supply draws half an amp, because 120 times one-half equals 60. The same 60-watt bulb made for a 230-volt British supply draws only about a quarter of an amp.","Now we can see why Edison's team cared so much about high resistance, the idea we met in Part 10. If each lamp has high resistance, it needs only a small current to give its light. Small currents can travel through thinner, cheaper copper wires to reach hundreds of lamps at once. A working 60-watt bulb on a 120-volt supply has a resistance of about 240 ohms while it is glowing. Hundreds of such lamps wired side by side could share one system without needing copper cables as thick as tree trunks.","Here is a curious twist. The filament's resistance changes with temperature. When the metal is cold, its resistance is far lower, roughly a tenth or less of its value when glowing. So in the split second after you flick the switch, a surge of current rushes in before the filament heats up and its resistance climbs. That sudden jolt is one reason old-style bulbs most often failed right at the moment they were switched on, with a little flash and a 'tink'."]},{"heading":"Franklin's Backwards Guess","paragraphs":["If you look at a circuit diagram in a school science book, you will see little arrows showing current flowing out of the positive (+) end of a battery, around the circuit, and back into the negative (-) end. But we have just learned that in a metal wire the moving things are electrons, which carry negative charge. Negative charges are attracted towards positive, so the electrons actually drift the other way: out of the negative end and towards the positive end. So which is right?","The mix-up goes back more than 250 years, to Benjamin Franklin in Philadelphia (Part 3). Franklin pictured electricity as a single invisible fluid. An object with extra fluid he called 'positive', and an object short of fluid he called 'negative'. When a spark jumped, he imagined the fluid rushing from the positive object to the negative one. It was a clever guess, and it let him explain a huge number of experiments. But he had to choose which side to call 'extra', and he had no way of seeing what was really moving. He chose, and by bad luck, he chose the opposite of what electrons actually do.","By the time Thomson discovered the electron in 1897, scientists and engineers had spent well over a century drawing currents going from plus to minus. Thousands of books, rules and calculations were built on it. Switching everything around would have caused chaos. So they kept Franklin's direction and gave it a name: conventional current. The direction the electrons really move is called electron flow.","The good news is that for almost every calculation it makes no difference. A flow of negative charges one way has exactly the same effect on a light bulb, a motor or a heater as a flow of positive charges the other way. The bulb glows just as brightly either way. Engineers simply have to remember which picture they are using. And in some places, such as salty water or the inside of a battery, positive charges really do move, so conventional current is not always 'wrong' at all."]},{"heading":"Why a Thin Wire Glows White-Hot","paragraphs":["So what actually happens inside the glass? As electrons are pushed through the filament, they keep crashing into the vibrating atoms of the metal. Each collision passes on a little energy, making the atoms shake harder. Faster-shaking atoms are what we feel as heat. This heating effect was carefully measured by the English brewer and scientist James Prescott Joule around 1840 and 1841, and it is still called Joule heating. It is the same effect that warms the coils of a toaster, an electric hob or a hair dryer.","Joule found that the heat depends on the resistance and, even more strongly, on the current. Double the current through the same wire and you get about four times as much heat. In a lamp circuit, the thick copper wires in the walls have very low resistance, so very little heat is made there. Almost all the resistance in the circuit is packed into one tiny place: the filament. That is where nearly all the energy is turned into heat, so that is where the temperature soars.","The filament is also extremely thin, often thinner than a human hair, and in later bulbs it was wound into a tight coil. A thin wire has more resistance than a thick one, and it has very little material to soak up the heat, so it warms up within a fraction of a second. As it heats, it starts to glow. Anything hot enough gives off light: this is called incandescence, the same word that gives the 'incandescent bulb' its name.","The colour of the glow tells you how hot something is. At around 500 to 600 degrees Celsius, metal glows a dull, deep red, like the coils of an electric heater. Hotter, and it turns cherry red, then orange, then yellow. The filament of an ordinary household bulb runs at roughly 2,500 degrees Celsius, about twice as hot as the lava flowing from a volcano. At that temperature it glows a warm yellowish-white, the cosy colour many people still think of as 'proper' light.","Now you can see why the filament needs to live inside a sealed glass bubble. If air could reach a filament at 2,500 degrees, the oxygen in the air would attack it at once. A carbon filament would burn up like a match; a metal one would quickly oxidise (combine with oxygen) and fall apart. That is why Swan and Edison worked so hard on their vacuum pumps (Parts 8 to 10): with almost all the air removed, there was hardly any oxygen left to do damage.","Even in a vacuum, though, a filament slowly wears away. At such high temperatures, atoms on its surface gradually boil off, a process called evaporation. The escaping atoms fly across the bulb and stick to the cool glass, which is why old bulbs often have a grey or black smudge inside. As the filament loses material, it gets thinner in some spots. Thinner spots have more resistance, so they get hotter, so they lose material even faster. Eventually one spot becomes so thin that it snaps, and the bulb is dead.","Engineers found a clever trick to slow this down: instead of a vacuum, fill the bulb with a gas that does not react with the hot metal, such as argon or nitrogen. The gas atoms crowd around the filament and bounce many escaping atoms back towards it, so the filament evaporates more slowly and can run hotter and brighter. Swirling currents of hot gas carry the rest of the evaporated metal upward, which is why the dark smudge in a gas-filled bulb usually appears at the top instead of all over. Who worked this out, and with which metal, is the next part of our story."]},{"heading":"Tungsten: The Metal That Would Not Melt","paragraphs":["The first practical bulbs of the 1880s and 1890s used carbon filaments, made by baking threads of cotton, paper or bamboo until only carbon was left. Carbon was cheap and could survive high temperatures, but it had weaknesses. It evaporated fairly fast, blackening the glass, and if it ran too hot it failed quickly. So carbon bulbs had to run at a lower temperature, giving a dim, orange-yellow light and wasting a lot of energy. Inventors began searching for a better filament material, trying metals such as osmium and tantalum around the start of the twentieth century.","The winner was tungsten, a heavy grey metal whose name comes from Swedish words meaning 'heavy stone'. (Its chemical symbol is W, from its other name, wolfram.) Tungsten has the highest melting point of any metal: about 3,422 degrees Celsius. That meant it could run much hotter than carbon without falling apart, and a hotter filament gives a whiter, brighter light from the same amount of electricity.","In 1904 two inventors, the Hungarian Sándor (Alexander) Just and the Croatian Franjo Hanaman, received a Hungarian patent for a lamp with a tungsten filament. The Hungarian company that licensed their patent later took the name Tungsram, a mix of 'tungsten' and 'wolfram'. Their bulbs gave much more light per watt than carbon lamps. But there was a big problem. Tungsten made this way was terribly brittle. The filaments were made by pressing tungsten powder into fine threads and heating them, and the result could snap if you looked at it the wrong way. A bulb dropped on the floor, or even knocked during delivery, might be ruined.","The fix came from the research laboratory of General Electric in Schenectady, New York, the company that had grown partly out of Edison's businesses. There, a young engineer and physicist named William D. Coolidge set out to make tungsten bendable, or ductile, so it could be drawn into a long, tough wire like copper. Most experts thought it was impossible. Coolidge spent years experimenting, starting around 1906. His team found that if tungsten was pressed from powder, heated, and then hammered and drawn through dies again and again while hot, the metal's tiny crystals were stretched into long fibres, and it became strong and flexible.","By about 1910, Coolidge's process could make ductile tungsten wire, and from about 1911 General Electric was selling lamps with drawn tungsten filaments. These bulbs were tough enough to survive everyday life and far more efficient than carbon lamps.","The next great step came from another scientist in the same laboratory, the chemist Irving Langmuir, who joined General Electric in 1909. Langmuir was supposed to be studying why bulbs went black, but he was the kind of person who loved digging into basic science. He found that, with a perfectly clean bulb and the right gas, filling the glass with an unreactive gas instead of keeping a vacuum made the tungsten evaporate much more slowly. He also discovered that winding the filament into a tight coil helped a lot: the coil kept a thin layer of hot gas wrapped around itself, so less heat was carried away. Langmuir's gas-filled, coiled-filament lamp was developed around 1913, and it greatly improved how much light a bulb gave for its electricity.","Langmuir later won the 1932 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for his studies of what happens on the surfaces of materials, work that partly began inside light bulbs. With tungsten, inert gas and coils, the classic light bulb was essentially complete. The bulbs many grandparents grew up with in the 1950s worked in almost exactly the same way."]},{"heading":"A Heater That Happens to Glow","paragraphs":["For all its cleverness, the incandescent bulb has one enormous flaw. It is really a heater that happens to give off a bit of light. Of all the electrical energy going into an ordinary old-style bulb, about 90 percent or more comes out as heat, not visible light. Careful measurements put the share that becomes visible light at less than 5 percent for a typical household bulb, often only about 2 percent.","Where does all that heat go? Much of it is radiated as infrared, a kind of light with waves too long for our eyes to see but which we feel as warmth on our skin. A glowing filament gives off a broad rainbow of radiation, and at 2,500 degrees Celsius most of it falls in the invisible infrared part. Only a small slice lands in the narrow band our eyes can detect.","Scientists measure lighting efficiency in lumens per watt: how much light (in lumens) you get for each watt of power. A standard incandescent bulb gives roughly 13 to 16 lumens per watt. A modern white LED lamp, which we will meet in Part 18, gives around 100 lumens per watt or more. In other words, an LED can give the same light for about a sixth of the electricity, or less.","Why not just make the filament even hotter, so more of its glow lands in the visible range? But there is a cruel trade-off. The hotter the filament, the faster it evaporates and the sooner it breaks. Running a filament a little hotter makes it noticeably brighter and more efficient but can dramatically shorten its life. Running it cooler makes it last much longer but gives a dimmer, redder light for the same electricity. Every bulb designer had to choose a balance point between brightness, efficiency and lifespan."]},{"heading":"The 1,000-Hour Club and the Bulb That Won't Quit","paragraphs":["That trade-off between brightness and lifespan leads to one of the strangest stories in the history of the light bulb. On 15 January 1925, representatives of the world's biggest bulb companies set up a company in Geneva, Switzerland, called Phoebus S.A., named after a Greek god of light. Its members included Osram of Germany, Philips of the Netherlands, Associated Electrical Industries of Britain, Tungsram of Hungary, the Tokyo Electric company of Japan and an overseas branch of America's General Electric. It became known as the Phoebus cartel. A cartel is a group of companies that secretly or openly agree not to compete properly with each other.","The cartel divided up the world's markets, set how many bulbs each member could sell, and helped keep prices up. It also did something that still makes people angry today. It agreed that ordinary bulbs should have a standard life of 1,000 hours. Before this, some bulbs had been designed to last considerably longer, by some accounts up to 2,500 hours. Member companies sent sample bulbs to be tested, and a company whose bulbs lasted too long beyond the standard could be fined, with the fines rising the more hours its bulbs went over.","Was this a sneaky plan to make people buy more bulbs? Many historians and writers think so. The Phoebus cartel is often given as one of the first big examples of 'planned obsolescence', meaning products deliberately designed to wear out sooner than they need to. Internal company letters seem to show executives worried that long-lasting bulbs were bad for sales, and in 1949 a United States court ruled against General Electric in a case that included its lamp-life policies.","But the story is not completely one-sided. Because of the trade-off we just met, a shorter-life bulb can be brighter and more efficient on the same electricity. Some engineers argued that 1,000 hours was a sensible balance, and in 1951 a British government commission looked at the question and decided that the 1,000-hour standard was a reasonable compromise for customers at the time. Historians still debate how much the rule was about engineering and how much about profit. Most agree that profit played a large part. The cartel fell apart around the start of the Second World War in 1939 and 1940, when its members ended up on opposite sides of the conflict.","Meanwhile, in a little fire station in California, one bulb was quietly ignoring all the rules. The Centennial Light hangs from the ceiling of Fire Station 6 in Livermore, California. It was made by hand in the late 1890s by the Shelby Electric Company of Shelby, Ohio, using a design linked to a French engineer named Adolphe Chaillet. In 1901 it was given to the town's fire department, and it has been glowing almost continuously ever since, more than 120 years.","It has had a few short breaks. It was off for about a week in 1937 while the fire station was renovated. In 1976 it was carefully moved to its present station, and was without power for only 22 minutes. In May 2013 a webcam watching the bulb seemed to show that it had finally died, but an electrician found the fault was in the power supply, not the bulb. After nearly ten hours it was glowing again. Guinness World Records has listed it as the longest-lasting light bulb.","Why has it lasted so long? It was originally a 60-watt bulb, but today it glows very dimly, giving off about as much light as a 4-watt bulb, more like a nightlight than a lamp. It has a thick, hand-made carbon filament, and it is almost never switched off, so it avoids the stressful surge of current and the heating and cooling that wear filaments out. In other words, it lasts so long partly because it is dim and inefficient: a perfect, glowing example of the trade-off between brightness and life.","There is one thing even the Centennial Light cannot do without. A bulb, whether it is a fragile carbon thread from 1879, a tough tungsten coil from 1913 or a famous fire-station lamp, is completely useless on its own. It needs a steady stream of electricity pushed into it, every second it glows. And in 1880, when the first good bulbs were ready, almost no building on Earth had a supply. There were no power stations, no cables under the streets and no sockets in the walls. If the light bulb was going to change the world, someone would have to build an entirely new kind of business: one that made electricity in one place and sent it out to everyone else."]}],"deepDive":"Why does a hot filament give off so little visible light? The answer lies in what physicists call black-body radiation. Any hot object emits a spread of wavelengths, and the peak of that spread shifts to shorter wavelengths as the object gets hotter (Wien's displacement law). The Sun's surface, at about 5,500 degrees Celsius, peaks right in the visible range, which is no coincidence: our eyes evolved to use sunlight. A tungsten filament at roughly 2,500 to 2,800 degrees Celsius peaks in the near-infrared, at around one micrometre, so most of its output is invisible heat. Even in theory, a tungsten filament lamp could not exceed about 52 lumens per watt, compared with a theoretical maximum of around 300 or more for white light from an ideal source. Real household bulbs achieved about 13 to 16 lumens per watt, meaning under 5 percent of the electrical energy became visible light. The life-versus-efficiency trade-off is extremely steep. A widely used engineering rule of thumb says that running a lamp just 5 percent above its rated voltage gives a little more light but can cut its life roughly in half, while running it about 5 percent below its rated voltage can roughly double its life. That is why the Phoebus cartel debate is genuinely tricky. Critics point to internal correspondence and the fines for long-lived lamps as evidence that profit drove the 1,000-hour rule, and a US court in 1949 found General Electric's lamp practices broke antitrust law. Defenders note that a 2,500-hour bulb would have been noticeably dimmer for the same power, so customers would have paid more for electricity to get the same light; Britain's Monopolies Commission accepted this argument in 1951. Many historians conclude both things can be true: 1,000 hours was a defensible engineering choice, but the cartel enforced it with fines to protect sales rather than letting customers choose. Another subtle point is the filament's positive temperature coefficient. Tungsten's resistance rises roughly tenfold or more between room temperature and operating temperature, so a cold bulb draws a large inrush current for a few milliseconds. This makes incandescent lamps partly self-regulating once hot, but it also explains why so many failed at switch-on.","funFact":"The Centennial Light in Livermore, California, began life as a 60-watt bulb but after more than 120 years of glowing it now gives about as much light as a 4-watt nightlight.","check":{"q":"Why is tungsten such a good material for light bulb filaments?","a":"It has the highest melting point of any metal, so it can glow very hot without melting","wrong":["It is the cheapest and softest metal","It is the only metal that conducts electricity","It does not get hot when current flows through it"]},"sources":[{"title":"Incandescent light bulb","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incandescent_light_bulb"},{"title":"J. J. Thomson","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._J._Thomson"},{"title":"Electric current","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_current"},{"title":"Irving Langmuir","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irving_Langmuir"},{"title":"Phoebus cartel","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phoebus_cartel"},{"title":"Centennial Light","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centennial_Light"},{"title":"Tungsram","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tungsram"},{"title":"Joule heating","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joule_heating"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/11-1.jpg","alt":"Close-up of a switched-on light bulb: the thin glowing wire filament shines inside the clear glass globe.","file":"Close-up light bulb filament 2.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Close-up_light_bulb_filament_2.JPG","author":"domdomegg","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/11-2.jpg","alt":"The Centennial Light, glowing at Fire Station 6 in Livermore, California, where it has burned since 1901.","file":"Livermore Centennial Light Bulb.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Livermore_Centennial_Light_Bulb.jpg","author":"LPS.1","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/11-3.jpg","alt":"J. J. Thomson, the Cambridge physicist who discovered the electron, in a 1913 portrait printed for the journal Nature.","file":"J. J. Thomson (5227651).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J._J._Thomson_(5227651).jpg","author":"Emery Walker / After John Palmer Clarke","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4360},{"n":12,"title":"The First Power Stations","when":"1878 – 1890","summary":"A light bulb needs electricity, but in 1880 there were no power stations anywhere. In this part, a little English town uses a water wheel to light its streets, Edison's team digs up the roads of New York to lay cables, and a giant machine named after a circus elephant starts to hum. By the end, people could pay for electricity just like they paid for gas.","sections":[{"heading":"A Problem Nobody Had Solved Before","paragraphs":["Picture yourself in 1880 with a brand-new light bulb in your hand. It is a marvel. It glows without a flame, without smoke and without the smell of gas. There is just one problem: where are you going to plug it in? There are no sockets in your walls, no cables under your street and no power station in your town. In the whole world, nobody yet sells electricity to the public.","Up to this point, anyone who wanted electric light had to make their own. A factory owner, a theatre or a very rich person could buy a steam engine and a dynamo (a generator) and set up what was called an isolated plant: a private power station serving just one building. This is how Swan's lamps lit Lord Armstrong's house at Cragside, using a stream and a water turbine, and how many of Edison's first customers got their light. The banker J.P. Morgan had one installed at his house at 219 Madison Avenue in New York, and on 6 June 1882 it became one of the first private homes in America lit by electricity. A coal-fired steam engine drove two generators to light the house.","Isolated plants worked, but they were noisy, smelly, expensive and needed someone to look after them. Most families could never afford one. The big idea, which Edison's team had been planning since 1878, was a central station. Instead of every building making its own electricity, one large station would make it for a whole neighbourhood and send it out through wires, just as gas companies already made gas in one place and piped it to thousands of homes. Customers would simply pay for what they used.","It sounds obvious now, but in the early 1880s it was a gamble. Would the machines be reliable enough to run all night, every night? Could cables be laid safely under busy streets? How would you measure how much electricity each customer used? And could the whole thing ever make money while competing with cheap, familiar gas? Between 1881 and 1882, a handful of pioneers on both sides of the Atlantic tried to find out."]},{"heading":"Godalming: A Town Lit by a River","paragraphs":["One of the very first answers came not from a big city but from a small market town in Surrey, in the south of England, about 30 miles south-west of London. Godalming sits where the River Ock meets the River Wey, among old timber-framed houses and narrow streets. In the 1870s the price of gas had risen, and the town's leaders were unhappy about the cost of their gas streetlamps. They decided to try something new.","In 1881 a London firm called Calder and Barrett installed an electric generator at Westbrook Mill, a water mill on the River Wey. The generator was turned by two waterwheels, so the flowing river provided the power. Wires were strung overhead along Mill Street into the middle of the town. The system was simple and a bit of a mixture. There were a few powerful arc lights (the dazzling type we met in Part 7): three at the mill and four more in the town centre. A separate, gentler circuit fed 34 small incandescent lamps, some at the mill and the rest around the centre of town.","In the autumn of 1881, the people of Godalming saw their streets lit by electricity. A drawing published in the London magazine The Graphic in November 1881 showed the scene to readers across the country. Because the system supplied both public streetlights and some private customers, Godalming has a strong claim to be the first town in the world with a public electricity supply of this kind, though a few other places were experimenting with similar ideas at about the same time.","Not everyone was impressed. Some townspeople complained that the lights in the town centre were too dim, while the arc lights at the mill were so bright they were uncomfortable. The river was also not always reliable. Before the end of 1881 the generator had been moved to a site behind the White Hart pub and was being driven by a steam engine instead of the water wheels. In April 1882 the German company Siemens took over running the system.","Sadly, the experiment did not last. Electricity was still expensive, there were few customers and the equipment was primitive. In 1884, when the contract came up for renewal, Siemens chose not to bid again, and Godalming went back to gas lamps. Even so, the little town had shown that a public electricity supply was possible. It was a small, flickering first step, but an important one."]},{"heading":"Jumbo Comes to London","paragraphs":["Meanwhile, Edison's company was racing to show the world a complete system. Its first public central station did not open in America at all, but in London. An associate of Edison named Edward Hibberd Johnson was looking for a place to show off the system in Britain. He found the perfect spot: Holborn Viaduct, an elegant iron bridge built in the 1860s to carry a road over a valley in the City of London. Underneath the road were hollow passages, called culverts, where cables could be laid without digging up the street.","The station started up on 12 January 1882. Its heart was an enormous dynamo shipped from America. Edison's team nicknamed these giant generators 'Jumbo', after a famously huge African elephant that the showman P.T. Barnum had just bought from London Zoo for his circus. The name fitted. The machine weighed about 27 tonnes, as much as four or five real elephants. A coal-fired steam engine turned it, and it produced about 93 kilowatts of power (about 125 horsepower) at 110 volts.","At first, the Holborn Viaduct station lit 968 lamps, each about as bright as 16 candles, for street lighting along the viaduct from Holborn Circus to St Martin's Le Grand. Later it supplied about 3,000 lamps, including some for nearby buildings and private customers. For Londoners walking along the viaduct at night, it was a startling sight: steady, white electric light in a city still mostly lit by flickering gas.","But Holborn Viaduct was more of an advertisement than a business. The station ran at a heavy loss every year. It sat on land owned by the Crown, so it could not easily be expanded, and the business climate for British electricity companies was difficult (more on that below). In September 1886, after about four and a half years, it was shut down, and the streetlamps along the viaduct went back to gas. Still, it had proved that Edison's lamps, dynamos and wiring could work together as one system for years at a time."]},{"heading":"Pearl Street: Digging Up Manhattan","paragraphs":["Edison's real target was New York. On 17 December 1880 he set up the Edison Electric Illuminating Company of New York to build and run central stations in the city. Edison's team chose a district in lower Manhattan, at the southern tip of the island, which they called the First District. It was a patch of about one square mile, bounded by Spruce Street, the East River, Wall Street and Nassau Street. It was packed with banks, offices, newspapers and shops, exactly the kind of wealthy customers who might pay for the newest kind of light.","Before a single lamp could be lit, the team had to go street by street through the district counting customers. They noted how many gas jets each building used and how long they burned, so they could guess how much electricity people would need. Francis Upton, the mathematician we met in Part 10, worked on the calculations that showed how big the wires needed to be and how much the system would cost.","Then came the digging. Unlike the overhead telegraph wires and arc-light cables that already tangled above many American streets, Edison wanted his wires underground, where they would be safer and out of sight. Workers dug trenches along the streets and laid copper conductors inside iron pipes, packed with an insulating mixture of asphalt and oils to stop the electricity leaking out. It was slow, hard and expensive work.","For the power station itself, the company bought two old buildings at 255 and 257 Pearl Street, near the East River and close to the busy Fulton Fish Market. The site was about 15 metres wide and 30 metres deep. The buildings were strengthened to hold the heavy machinery. Meanwhile, the Edison Machine Works, a factory on Goerck Street in Manhattan, built the heavy generators, and other Edison factories made the lamps, sockets, switches and cables. Edison had to invent not just a product but a whole industry to supply it.","On the afternoon of Monday 4 September 1882, everything was finally ready. At Pearl Street, the stokers had the fires roaring under the boilers and steam engines turning the dynamos. A few streets away, in the offices of the bank Drexel, Morgan and Company on Wall Street, Edison waited with a group of businessmen. When the current was switched on, lamps in the offices began to glow.","The station's equipment was enormous for its day. It began with six large dynamos of the Jumbo type, each giving around 100 kilowatts, enough in total for roughly 600 kilowatts when all were running. Each dynamo was driven by its own steam engine. At first the company used fast-running engines made by the Porter-Allen company, but they proved unreliable with Edison's dynamos, so they were replaced with engines from another firm, Armington and Sims. The whole station burned coal, shovelled into the furnaces under the boilers.","On that first day the system was small. Pearl Street supplied about 400 lamps belonging to roughly 80 customers. (Different sources give slightly different numbers, such as 82 or 85.) Among the first were the Drexel, Morgan offices and the offices of The New York Times on Park Row. The Times wrote about the new light the very next day, describing a soft, steady glow that was much kinder to the eyes than gas.","The business grew steadily. By 1884, the Pearl Street station had 508 customers using 10,164 lamps. People who visited the district at night noticed the difference immediately. There were no flickering flames, no smell of gas, no blackened ceilings and far less risk of fire. Shopkeepers found that customers lingered in brightly lit stores, and office workers could keep going after dark without headaches from fumes.","Pearl Street was not perfect. Its machines had teething troubles, especially the first steam engines. On 2 January 1890, a fire broke out and destroyed all but one of the dynamos. The station was rebuilt and kept working until 1895, when bigger and more efficient power stations nearby took over its work. The one surviving dynamo is now preserved at Greenfield Village, part of The Henry Ford museum complex in Dearborn, Michigan. One of three working scale models of the station, built in 1929, can still be seen at the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History in Washington, D.C."]},{"heading":"Appleton: Water Power in Wisconsin","paragraphs":["Less than four weeks after Pearl Street started, another historic station switched on more than 1,000 kilometres away, in the town of Appleton, Wisconsin. Appleton sits on the Fox River, which drops steeply through the town, and its fast-flowing water already turned the wheels of paper mills and flour mills.","The idea came from Henry J. Rogers, president of the Appleton Paper and Pulp Company and also of the local gas company. The story goes that he heard about Edison's plans for New York while on a fishing trip, and decided Appleton could do the same thing with river power instead of coal. He ordered two small Edison generators of the 'K' type. The first was installed in his paper mill and driven by a water wheel turned by the Fox River.","On 30 September 1882, the generator began turning. It produced only about 12.5 kilowatts, roughly a fiftieth of Pearl Street's eventual capacity, but it was enough to light Rogers' own house, the paper and pulp company's building and the nearby Vulcan Paper Mill. The second generator started in November 1882 in its own small building on Vulcan Street, which is why the system is remembered as the Vulcan Street Plant.","Appleton is often called the first hydroelectric central station in the United States serving private customers. Using water power was cheap once the wheel was built, but it had its own problems. If the river ran low, the lights dimmed. With such simple equipment, the brightness of the lamps could rise and fall with the speed of the water. Today the site is recognised as a historic landmark by American engineering societies."]},{"heading":"Fuses, Sockets and a Meter Made of Zinc","paragraphs":["What made Edison's stations special was not any single machine. It was the fact that every piece had been designed to fit together. Imagine following the electricity from Pearl Street to a lamp on an office desk in 1882. It leaves the dynamo at about 110 volts of direct current (DC), meaning it flows in one direction only. It travels through thick copper bars called feeders to junction points under the streets, then through smaller mains along each block. From the mains, a service wire carries it into the building, through a meter, past a fuse, along wires in the walls, through a switch, into a socket and finally into the lamp.","Each of those parts had to be invented or improved. Edison's company developed a screw-in socket and lamp base, very like the screw base that ordinary bulbs still use today in many countries. It made safety fuses: a short piece of soft lead wire designed to melt if too much current flowed, breaking the circuit before wires could overheat and start a fire. It made switches, junction boxes and insulating materials. And it made sure every lamp worked at the same voltage, so customers could add or remove lamps without upsetting anyone else's lights.","Then there was the question of payment. A gas company could measure gas with a meter, but how do you measure something invisible flowing through a wire? Edison's answer used chemistry. Each customer had a meter containing a small jar with a zinc solution and zinc plates. A tiny, fixed fraction of the customer's current was sent through the jar. As it flowed, it carried zinc atoms from one plate to the other, a process called electrolysis, which we first met with Nicholson and Carlisle in Part 4. The more electricity the customer used, the more zinc moved.","Once a month, a company employee visited each building, took out the plates and carried them back to the office, where they were carefully weighed. From the change in weight, the company worked out how much electricity had been used and sent the bill. It was clever but awkward: customers could not read the meter themselves, and they had to trust the company's scales. Within a few years, engineers such as Elihu Thomson developed meters with spinning parts and dials that customers could read directly. Today, electricity is sold by the kilowatt-hour, the energy used by a 1,000-watt appliance running for one hour.","To save copper, Edison's company also developed a three-wire system, which supplied two circuits of 110 volts using three wires instead of four. The first central station to use it opened in Sunbury, Pennsylvania, on 4 July 1883. By the late 1880s, Edison companies had built stations in many towns across the United States and in Europe, and isolated plants were lighting hundreds of factories, mills, hotels and ships."]},{"heading":"Rivals in the Dark: Arc Lights, Gas and the Law","paragraphs":["Edison's stations were not the only electric businesses of the early 1880s, and it would be unfair to tell the story as if they were. Arc-light companies had got going even earlier. Charles Brush's arc lamps, which had lit Cleveland's Public Square in 1879 (Part 7), were soon being sold to towns across America. In 1879 the California Electric Light Company in San Francisco began selling arc lighting from a central station using Brush dynamos, one of the first companies anywhere to sell electricity as a service. In December 1880, Brush lit a stretch of Broadway in New York with arc lamps powered at around 3,500 volts.","Arc systems were good at one job: flooding streets, squares, factories and railway stations with brilliant light. They ran at high voltages, with many lamps strung one after another along a single wire, like beads on a necklace. That is called a series circuit. If one lamp failed, special switches had to bypass it so the whole string did not go dark. But arc lamps were far too fierce and too dangerous for a sitting room. Edison's low-voltage incandescent system, with each lamp wired side by side (in parallel) so it could be switched on and off by itself, was aimed at a different market: indoor light for homes, offices and shops.","The biggest rival of all, though, was gas. Gas companies had spent seventy years laying pipes under city streets, and almost every town of any size had a gasworks. Gas light was familiar and, at first, cheaper than electric light. Gas companies fought back hard. In the late 1880s the Austrian chemist Carl Auer von Welsbach invented the gas mantle, a little fabric sleeve soaked in metal salts that turned into a fragile mesh and glowed brightly when heated by a gas flame. His improved version of 1891, made mostly of thorium and cerium oxides, gave a strong white light and went on sale in 1892. Mantles made gas lamps much brighter for the same amount of gas, and they kept gas streetlights in use in many towns until electric lighting spread in the early 1900s.","In Britain, electricity faced another possible obstacle: the law. Parliament passed the Electric Lighting Act in 1882 to control the new industry. It allowed local councils to buy out private electricity companies after 21 years. Many people at the time blamed this rule for scaring investors away, and British central stations grew slowly for several years. Historians still argue about how much the law really mattered: some point instead to a burst of over-excited investment in electricity shares in 1882, followed by a slump. Either way, the rules were loosened in 1888, stretching the buyout period to 42 years and treating companies more fairly, and a wave of new British stations followed.","So in the early 1880s, the future of electric light was far from certain. Electricity was new, expensive and limited to small districts. Its rivals were powerful, cheap and everywhere. What electricity had on its side was the quality of its light: clean, bright, safe and steady. Customers who tried it rarely wanted to go back."]},{"heading":"The One-Mile Problem","paragraphs":["Edison's direct-current system had a serious weakness, and the engineers knew it from the start. Every wire has some resistance, so as current flows along it, some energy is lost as heat in the wire itself, just like the filament heating we met in Part 11, only much less. The longer the wire, the more is lost. And because Edison's system sent electricity at the same low voltage, about 110 volts, that customers used in their lamps, it needed a large current to deliver a useful amount of power, and large currents waste a lot of energy in the wires.","There were only two ways to keep the losses down. One was to use very thick copper conductors, which have less resistance. But copper was expensive, and the further you went, the thicker and costlier the cables had to be. The other was to keep the wires short. In practice, a station like Pearl Street could only serve customers within roughly one mile, or about 1.5 kilometres. Beyond that, the voltage dropped so much that lamps at the far end would glow dim and orange while lamps near the station shone brightly.","That meant a big city would need a power station every mile or so, each sitting right in the middle of a crowded neighbourhood, each burning coal and filling the streets with smoke. Land in city centres was expensive. And what about suburbs, farms and small towns spread across miles of countryside? With Edison's system, lighting them would mean building countless small stations, or cables so thick they would cost a fortune.","There was also the question of where power could be made. Some of the best sources of energy, such as great waterfalls and rushing rivers, were far from the cities that needed electricity. Appleton was lucky to have its river right in the middle of town. But no DC system of the early 1880s could carry the power of a distant waterfall many miles to a city. To use those natural power sources, someone would need a way to send electricity much, much further.","So, by the mid-1880s, engineers in Europe and America were asking the same question. Was there a way to send electricity across many miles without losing most of it on the way? Some of them believed the answer lay in a different kind of current: one that did not flow steadily in one direction, but wiggled back and forth, many times every second."]}],"deepDive":"Why was Edison's DC system limited to about a mile? The physics comes down to two simple formulas. Power delivered equals voltage times current (P = VI), and the power lost as heat in a wire equals the current squared times the wire's resistance (P = I squared R). To deliver, say, 10 kilowatts at 110 volts you need about 91 amps. If the copper feeder to a customer half a mile away has a resistance of just 0.1 ohm for the round trip, the loss is about 91 squared times 0.1, or roughly 830 watts, already over 8 percent, and the voltage at the far end sags by about 9 volts, enough to make carbon lamps visibly dimmer. Double the distance and both the loss and the voltage drop double; to keep them the same you must double the copper's cross-section, so the weight of copper needed grows roughly with the square of the distance served. Edison's engineers fought this with clever tricks. The feeder-and-main design, devised by Edison around 1880, used heavy feeders that ran without branches from the station to points in the network, so voltage stayed fairly even along the mains. The three-wire system, patented in 1883 and also developed independently by John Hopkinson in Britain, effectively doubled the transmission voltage to 220 volts between the outer wires while keeping 110 volts at each lamp, cutting copper needs by roughly 60 percent compared with a two-wire system. Historians also debate which station deserves the title of 'first'. Godalming (1881) supplied public streetlights and some private customers, but used a mix of arc and incandescent lamps and closed in 1884. Holborn Viaduct (January 1882) was Edison's first public central station but mainly lit streets and ran at a loss. Pearl Street (September 1882) is usually called the first commercial central power station in the United States, and it was profitable within a few years. Appleton (September 1882) is often listed as the first hydroelectric central station in the US. Each was a 'first' in a slightly different sense, which is a good reminder that big inventions usually have several birthdays.","funFact":"Edison's giant 'Jumbo' dynamos were named after Jumbo the elephant, a London Zoo favourite whom the showman P.T. Barnum bought for his American circus in 1882, the same year the first Jumbo dynamos went to work.","check":{"q":"How did Edison's first electricity meters measure how much electricity a customer used?","a":"Current moved zinc between plates in a jar, and the plates were weighed each month","wrong":["A worker counted how many hours each lamp was switched on","A spinning dial showed the number of volts used","Customers wrote down their own use in a notebook"]},"sources":[{"title":"Pearl Street Station","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_Street_Station"},{"title":"Holborn Viaduct power station","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holborn_Viaduct_power_station"},{"title":"Godalming","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godalming"},{"title":"Vulcan Street Plant","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vulcan_Street_Plant"},{"title":"Edison Illuminating Company","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edison_Illuminating_Company"},{"title":"Electricity meter","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity_meter"},{"title":"War of the currents","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_currents"},{"title":"Charles F. Brush","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_F._Brush"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/12-1.jpg","alt":"1880s engraving of the dynamo room in Edison's Pearl Street power station, New York, with workers beside the giant machines.","file":"Edision PowerPlant, Pearl Street NYC.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edision_PowerPlant,_Pearl_Street_NYC.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/12-2.jpg","alt":"An Edison dynamo from 1889 or earlier, built by the Edison Machine Works, now in a Tokyo science museum.","file":"Edison dynamo, 1889 or earlier, 125 volts, 200 amps - National Museum of Nature and Science, Tokyo - DSC07386.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edison_dynamo,_1889_or_earlier,_125_volts,_200_amps_-_National_Museum_of_Nature_and_Science,_Tokyo_-_DSC07386.JPG","author":"Daderot","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/12-3.jpg","alt":"Hearthstone in Appleton, Wisconsin: in 1882 it became the first home lit by the Vulcan Street hydroelectric plant.","file":"Appleton June 2026 007 (Hearthstone Historic House Museum).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Appleton_June_2026_007_(Hearthstone_Historic_House_Museum).jpg","author":"Michael Barera","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3863},{"n":13,"title":"AC, DC and the Magic of Transformers","when":"Science + 1881 – 1886","summary":"Some electricity flows one way, like a river. Other electricity rocks back and forth, 50 or 60 times every second! The back-and-forth kind has a secret helper called a transformer, which can make electricity strong enough to travel for miles and then gentle enough to light your lamp.","sections":[{"heading":"One Way, or Back and Forth?","paragraphs":["At the end of the last part, Thomas Edison's shiny new power station at Pearl Street was humming away in New York. It worked. Lamps glowed, customers paid their bills, and newspapers wrote excited stories. But there was a catch hiding inside the copper cables under the street: the electricity could only travel about a mile before too much of it leaked away as heat. To understand why, and how engineers solved it, we need to learn about two different kinds of electric current. This part is a bit more science-heavy than the others, so get ready to think like an engineer.","The first kind is called direct current, or DC. In a DC circuit the electric charge flows around the loop in one direction only, always the same way, like a river that runs steadily downhill to the sea. A battery gives DC. So does a solar panel. So did Edison's big Jumbo dynamos at Pearl Street, which pushed current out through the cables to the lamps and back again, always in the same direction.","The second kind is called alternating current, or AC. In an AC circuit the charge does not travel steadily around the loop. Instead it rocks back and forth, first one way, then the other, then back again, over and over. Imagine a long rope stretched across a playground. If you pull the rope steadily toward you, that is like DC. If you shove it forward and yank it back again and again, the rope wiggles in place but still carries your effort to the person at the other end. That is like AC. Energy still gets delivered, even though nothing travels very far.","How fast does AC wiggle? Today, in Britain, Europe, China, India, Australia and much of the world, the current changes direction so that it completes 50 full back-and-forth cycles every second. In the United States, Canada, Mexico and some other countries it completes 60 cycles a second. Scientists measure this in hertz (Hz), a unit named after the German physicist Heinrich Hertz, whom you met when he made the first radio waves. So European sockets are 50 Hz and American ones are 60 Hz. Because each cycle has a forward push and a backward push, the current in a European lamp actually stops and reverses 100 times every second. It happens far too fast for your eyes to notice.","In the early days there was no agreement at all. Some early lighting systems ran at about 133 cycles a second, others at 125, others at 66 or 25. One study counted ten different frequencies in use in London alone as late as 1918. Lower frequencies were good for motors but made lamps flicker, because the glowing filament had time to cool down a tiny bit every time the current dropped to zero. Engineers slowly settled on 50 and 60 as a good compromise, and the rest of the world was split between them, mostly depending on which companies sold the first equipment.","You might think that a current that keeps changing its mind would be useless. Surely the steady one-way flow is better? For a battery-powered torch it is. But as you are about to see, the wiggling kind had a hidden superpower. It could be squeezed up to very high voltages and then let down again, and that made all the difference for sending electricity across long distances."]},{"heading":"Spinning Magnets Make Wiggles","paragraphs":["Here is a surprise: AC is actually the natural kind of electricity for a generator to make. To see why, go back to Michael Faraday's great discovery of 1831, electromagnetic induction. Faraday found that when a magnet moves near a coil of wire, or a coil moves near a magnet, a current flows in the wire. The important word is moves. A magnet sitting still next to a coil does nothing. It is the change in magnetism passing through the coil that pushes the charge along.","Now picture a magnet spinning round and round inside a ring of wire coils, which is roughly what happens inside a modern generator. As the north pole of the magnet sweeps toward a coil, the magnetism through that coil grows, and current is pushed one way. As the north pole sweeps past and the south pole comes round, the magnetism through the coil flips, and current is pushed the other way. One full turn of the magnet gives one forward push and one backward push. Keep spinning, and you get current that swings back and forth smoothly, in a wave shape that mathematicians call a sine wave. A spinning generator makes AC all by itself.","The very first hand-cranked generator, built by the young French instrument maker Hippolyte Pixii in Paris in 1832, did exactly this. Turning the handle spun a horseshoe magnet past two coils, and the current came out alternating. At the time, scientists did not want wiggling current. They wanted steady current like a battery's, because that was what they knew how to use for experiments such as electrolysis. So a clever switching device called a commutator was added. It flipped the connections every half-turn so that the current always left the machine in the same direction. Most generators for the next fifty years, including Edison's dynamos, used commutators to turn their natural AC into DC.","Commutators worked, but they had drawbacks. They were made of copper segments rubbed by brushes of copper or carbon, and they sparked, wore out and needed constant care. They also became harder to build as machines grew bigger and voltages rose. An AC generator, often called an alternator, can skip the commutator and simply send its wiggling current straight out to the wires.","How fast the current wiggles depends on how fast the generator spins and how many magnetic poles it has. Many early lighting alternators had eight poles and spun at about 2,000 turns a minute, which works out at roughly 133 cycles per second. Today a big power station generator in Europe with just two poles spins at exactly 3,000 turns a minute to produce 50 Hz, and in America at 3,600 turns a minute for 60 Hz. Every generator connected to the grid has to spin in perfect step with all the others, like a giant team of rowers pulling together.","So the AC idea was not new in the 1880s, and no single person invented it. Alternating current had been coming out of generators since Pixii's day. What was missing was a reason to want it and a way to use it. That reason turned out to be a problem with wires."]},{"heading":"Why Wires Waste Power","paragraphs":["No ordinary wire is perfect. Even copper, one of the best conductors there is, has some resistance, which means it holds back the flow of charge a little. As the current pushes through, the moving electrons keep bumping into the vibrating atoms of the metal, and some of the electrical energy turns into heat. You can feel this yourself: a phone charger cable or a laptop power lead often gets slightly warm. In a toaster or the filament of an old light bulb this heating is the whole point. In a long cable carrying power across a city, it is pure waste.","Now comes the key piece of science. The amount of heat wasted in a wire does not just go up with the current. It goes up with the current multiplied by itself, the current squared. Double the current, and the heat loss becomes about four times bigger. Triple the current, and it becomes nine times bigger. Ten times the current means a hundred times the waste. Engineers write this as a tiny formula, loss equals I squared times R, where I stands for current and R for the resistance of the wire.","There is a second rule to put next to it. The power delivered to a lamp or a motor, measured in watts, equals the voltage times the current. A 100-watt bulb could be fed by 100 volts and 1 amp, or by 10 volts and 10 amps, or by 1,000 volts and one tenth of an amp. Each gives the same 100 watts. This means there are two ways to send a lot of power: a big current at a low voltage, or a small current at a high voltage.","Put the two rules together and something wonderful happens. Imagine you want to send 10,000 watts, enough for a hundred old 100-watt bulbs, along a cable that has a resistance of half an ohm. At 100 volts you need a current of 100 amps. One hundred squared is 10,000, and half of that is 5,000 watts lost as heat in the cable. Half your power never reaches the customers. Now send the same 10,000 watts at 10,000 volts. You need only 1 amp. One squared is one, and half of that is half a watt lost. The waste has almost vanished, simply because the voltage went up and the current went down.","This is exactly why Edison's 110-volt DC system could only reach customers within about a mile of the power station. To carry more power farther at such a low voltage, he would have needed enormously thick copper cables, and copper was expensive. In fact, the cost of copper for the cables was one of the biggest worries in Edison's own calculations. A city lit the Edison way would have needed a power station every mile or so, each burning coal right in the middle of crowded neighbourhoods.","Of course, nobody wanted 10,000 volts running into their living room. A voltage that high could jump through the air and would be deadly to touch. Lamps and motors needed safe, low voltages. So engineers wanted something seemingly impossible: very high voltage on the long journey, and low voltage at the end. They needed a machine that could change voltage up and down like a gearbox changes the speed of a bicycle. That machine existed, but it only worked with alternating current."]},{"heading":"The Transformer: A Gearbox for Electricity","paragraphs":["A transformer is one of the simplest important machines ever made. It has no moving parts at all. At its heart is a core of iron, usually a ring or a rectangle shape. Around one side of the core is wound a coil of insulated wire called the primary coil. Around the other side is wound a second, separate coil called the secondary coil. The two coils do not touch each other. There is no wire connecting them. And yet electrical energy jumps from one to the other.","Here is how. When alternating current flows in the primary coil, it turns the iron core into an electromagnet. Because the current keeps reversing, the magnetism in the core keeps growing, shrinking and flipping, many times a second. The iron guides this changing magnetism around the core and through the secondary coil. And as Faraday discovered, changing magnetism passing through a coil makes a current flow in it. So the secondary coil produces its own alternating current, at the same frequency as the first.","The magic is in the number of turns. If the secondary coil has ten times as many turns of wire as the primary, the voltage coming out is about ten times higher, and the current is about ten times lower. That is a step-up transformer. Swap the coils around, with fewer turns on the secondary, and you get a step-down transformer that lowers the voltage and raises the current. The power going in and the power coming out stay almost the same; a good transformer wastes only a tiny fraction as heat. It is like a gearbox: you can trade speed for strength, but you do not get extra energy for free.","Now you can see why a transformer cannot work with steady DC. Remember Faraday's famous experiment with two coils on an iron ring in August 1831? He found that the second coil only gave a quick flick of current at the moment he connected or disconnected the battery. While the current was steady, nothing happened. Steady current makes steady magnetism, and steady magnetism induces nothing. A transformer fed with DC would just sit there, getting warm. AC, which is changing all the time, keeps the transformer working every instant. Faraday's ring was, in fact, the first transformer, but it took fifty years for anyone to turn it into a practical machine.","With transformers, a whole new plan becomes possible. Generate AC at a power station. Use a step-up transformer to raise the voltage to thousands of volts. Send it along fairly thin wires for many miles, losing very little. Then, near the homes and shops, use step-down transformers to bring it back to a safe low voltage for lamps. This is still exactly how electricity reaches you today. Those grey boxes on poles or green metal cabinets on the pavement in your street are often step-down transformers, and the faint hum you sometimes hear from them is the iron core vibrating 100 or 120 times a second as its magnetism flips.","The question in the early 1880s was who could build a transformer good enough, cheap enough and safe enough to light a town. Several teams in different countries raced toward the answer at almost the same time."]},{"heading":"Gaulard and Gibbs and the Secondary Generator","paragraphs":["The first team to make real headlines was an unlikely pair. Lucien Gaulard was a young French engineer, born in Paris in 1850, full of ideas and eager to make his name. John Dixon Gibbs was an English businessman and engineer, about sixteen years older, who had the money and connections to help turn ideas into products. Together, in the early 1880s, they built a device they called a secondary generator. Today we would call it a transformer.","They first exhibited it in London in about 1881 or 1882 (accounts differ) and applied for patents in 1882. Their design had a straight bundle of iron wires at its centre, with coils wound around it. Because the iron did not form a closed loop, engineers call this an open-core design. Much of the magnetism leaked out into the surrounding air instead of being guided neatly from one coil to the other, which made it less efficient than it could have been.","Gaulard and Gibbs also arranged their system in an unusual way. The primary coils of all their secondary generators were connected one after another, in a long chain called a series circuit, like old-fashioned Christmas tree lights. Each device had about the same number of turns on both coils, so it did not raise or lower the voltage very much. To adjust how much power a lamp got, you slid the iron core in or out of the coils. The trouble with a series chain is that everything depends on everything else: switch one group of lamps on or off, and the voltage for all the others changes too.","Many British engineers were not impressed, and the device was criticised at first. But in 1884 Gaulard and Gibbs took their system to an exhibition in Turin, in northern Italy, and there it shone. They built a demonstration line tens of kilometres long, much farther than any DC line of the time, running at high voltage and lighting lamps along the route. It proved that AC could carry power across real distances, and the system was adopted for electric lighting. One of the scientists who studied their device closely was a Turin professor named Galileo Ferraris, who will turn up again in the next part.","Sadly, the story did not end happily for Gaulard. His patents were challenged in court by other engineers, including the young British inventor Sebastian Ziani de Ferranti, who argued that the ideas were not truly new. The patents were overturned. Gaulard, who had put everything into his invention, became very unwell and died in a Paris hospital in November 1888, at just 38 years old. Historians still remember him as one of the true pioneers of AC power, even though others made the money from the ideas he helped launch."]},{"heading":"Three Friends in Budapest","paragraphs":["While Gaulard and Gibbs were showing off their open-core device, a team in Budapest, the capital of Hungary, was working on something better. The Ganz Works was a big engineering company famous for making railway wheels and machinery. It had started making electric lighting equipment in 1878 and had installed more than fifty lighting systems in Austria-Hungary by 1883. Its electrical department was run by Károly Zipernowsky, an engineer born in 1853.","On 1 July 1883 a talented 22-year-old named Ottó Bláthy joined Zipernowsky's team, and soon a third engineer, Miksa Déri, was working with them too. Together they became known as the ZBD team, from the first letters of their surnames: Zipernowsky, Bláthy and Déri. They studied the Gaulard and Gibbs design and set out to improve it, and each brought a different strength.","Bláthy's big idea was the closed core. Instead of a straight bundle of iron with magnetism leaking out of the ends, the iron should form a complete loop, a ring or a rectangle, so that almost all the magnetism stays trapped inside the iron and passes through both coils. This is exactly the shape of Faraday's old ring of 1831. Zipernowsky's big idea was to connect the transformers side by side, in parallel, instead of in a long series chain. In a parallel system each transformer and each lamp gets the same steady voltage, and switching one on or off does not upset the others. Déri carried out many of the experiments that proved the ideas worked.","The Ganz factory shipped its first high-efficiency transformer on 16 September 1884. In early 1885 the team improved the iron core even further by making it from thin sheets, called laminations, stacked together, which cut down on wasted heat caused by swirling currents inside solid iron. They filed their patents in 1885. According to later accounts, the ZBD transformers were several times more efficient than the open-core devices of Gaulard and Gibbs. The ZBD engineers are also widely credited with making the word 'transformer' popular for this kind of machine, though the word had been used before.","Their system was shown off at the Budapest National Exhibition in May 1885, where it lit a large display of lamps from a high-voltage supply. The next year, in 1886, Ganz engineers installed a ZBD system for public lighting in Rome. Very soon Ganz transformers were being sold across Europe. Ottó Bláthy went on to a long career of inventions, including an AC electricity meter in 1889, which solved another problem for AC companies: how to measure what customers used so you could send them a bill.","The basic design of almost every power transformer in the world today, from the giant ones at power stations to the little ones inside old phone chargers, still follows the plan of the three friends from Budapest: a closed iron core, laminated to reduce losses, with coils connected in parallel."]},{"heading":"Lights Along Main Street","paragraphs":["Across the Atlantic, one businessman was paying close attention. George Westinghouse, born in New York State in 1846, had already become rich from inventing the railway air brake in 1869, a system that let a driver stop a whole train at once using compressed air. He was bold, full of energy, and not afraid of a fight. In 1884 he had started to get involved in electric lighting, and he hired a gifted young engineer named William Stanley, born in Brooklyn in 1858.","In 1885 a young associate named Guido Pantaleoni told Westinghouse about the Gaulard and Gibbs transformer he had seen in Europe. Westinghouse imported several of the devices and some Siemens AC generators to study in Pittsburgh. Then he moved fast. He founded the Westinghouse Electric Company in Pittsburgh in 1886, and that February he bought the American rights to the Gaulard and Gibbs patents for 50,000 dollars, a fortune at the time.","William Stanley's job was to redesign the European transformer so it worked well in America. In December 1885 he moved his work to Great Barrington, a small town in the Berkshire hills of western Massachusetts where his family had a home. There he built improved transformers with soft iron cores, and an AC system to go with them.","On 20 March 1886 Stanley switched it on. A generator sent alternating current at about 500 volts along wires running roughly 4,000 feet, a little over a kilometre, down the town's Main Street. At the shops and offices, step-down transformers brought the voltage down to about 100 volts, safe enough for indoor lamps. Lights came on in 23 businesses along the street. Imagine being a shopkeeper in a quiet New England town that spring evening, watching a steady electric glow fill your shop window, powered from a generator out of sight down the road. It was the first complete AC lighting system with transformers in the United States.","Great Barrington was only a small demonstration, but it proved the whole plan: generate, step up, send, step down, use. That summer, Westinghouse tested the system in Pittsburgh with a new alternator designed by Stanley, and by the autumn of 1886 Westinghouse, Stanley and another engineer, Oliver Shallenberger, had installed the first commercial AC lighting system in the country, in Buffalo, New York. Orders poured in. By the end of 1887, Westinghouse had 68 AC power stations, while Edison's companies had 121 DC stations. The newcomer was catching up fast.","AC still had problems to solve. There was not yet a good AC motor, so AC was mostly useful for lighting, while DC could easily run factory machines and streetcars. And high voltages really were dangerous if wires were badly installed. But the advantages were huge: one AC power station could now light a whole town from miles away."]},{"heading":"Two Views of the Future","paragraphs":["Thomas Edison watched all this with growing alarm. He had spent years and a great deal of money building his DC system: the dynamos, the underground cables, the meters, the fuses, the sockets, the whole careful design. His meters only worked with DC, and his companies owned the key patents. Many investors had trusted him. Now a rival was offering a completely different kind of electricity.","Edison also had a genuine worry that should not be dismissed. His DC system used about 110 volts, which can give a nasty shock but rarely kills. Early AC systems sent thousands of volts along wires, often hung on poles above crowded streets, sometimes with poor insulation. In the 1880s there were few safety rules and few standards. Edison believed that high-voltage AC wires would kill people, and he said so, loudly and often. He was not entirely wrong that badly installed high-voltage lines were dangerous. But he was also defending his business, and he greatly underestimated how safely AC could be handled with good transformers, insulation and rules.","George Westinghouse saw things differently. To him, AC was simply the future. With transformers, a single big power station could serve a whole city, or many towns, from far away, perhaps from a waterfall or a coal mine. Thin wires could replace fat copper cables. Electricity could become cheaper and spread much farther than Edison's system would ever allow. He was ready to bet his company on it.","Both men were brilliant, stubborn and convinced they were right. Both had armies of engineers, lawyers and investors behind them. One believed AC was dangerous. The other believed it was the future. Between them stood millions of dollars, thousands of jobs, and the question of how the whole world would get its electricity. The stage was set for one of the most famous battles in the history of technology: the War of the Currents."]}],"deepDive":"The physics behind the AC victory comes down to two simple relationships. Power delivered is P = V x I (voltage times current), while power lost as heat in a conductor is P_loss = I squared x R. For a fixed amount of power, raising the voltage by a factor of ten cuts the current by ten and the resistive loss by a hundred, using the same wire. Even if engineers then use a thinner, cheaper wire, the losses still fall a great deal. This is why modern transmission lines run at hundreds of thousands of volts; over a 160 km line carrying 1,000 megawatts, losses at 765 kilovolts can be around 1 percent, compared with about 4 percent at 345 kilovolts. Overall, the United States loses roughly 5 percent of its electricity between power station and customer. An ideal transformer obeys Vp/Vs = Np/Ns: the voltage ratio equals the ratio of turns on the primary and secondary coils, and because energy is conserved, the current changes by the opposite ratio. Real transformers lose a little energy in the copper windings and in the iron core. Core losses come partly from eddy currents, small whirlpools of current induced in the iron itself, which is why the ZBD team switched to thin insulated laminations in 1885, and partly from hysteresis, the energy needed to flip the iron's magnetism back and forth every cycle. Large modern transformers can exceed 99 percent efficiency. Historians argue about credit. Faraday's 1831 ring was physically a closed-core transformer, but he used only on-off pulses and never developed the turns-ratio idea. Gaulard and Gibbs demonstrated long-distance AC with an open core and series connection; the Ganz engineers contributed the closed core, lamination and, crucially, parallel connection, which made constant-voltage distribution practical; Stanley and Westinghouse turned these into a commercial American system. Patent battles (Gaulard's patents were overturned after challenges led by Ferranti and others) show how hard it can be to say who invented a technology that many people improved step by step. Frequency choice was also messy: early systems ran anywhere from about 16 to 133 cycles per second before 50 and 60 Hz won out.","funFact":"A transformer has no moving parts, and the two coils inside it never touch, yet energy jumps from one to the other through the changing magnetism in the iron core, and the hum you can hear from big transformers is that core vibrating twice per cycle, 100 times a second in Europe or 120 in North America.","check":{"q":"Why does a transformer not work with steady direct current (DC)?","a":"Because a transformer needs changing magnetism to induce a current, and steady DC makes steady, unchanging magnetism.","wrong":["Because DC is too weak to pass through copper wire","Because DC flows too fast for the iron core to keep up","Because transformers were only allowed to be sold to AC companies"]},"sources":[{"title":"Transformer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformer"},{"title":"Alternating current","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternating_current"},{"title":"Electric power transmission","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_power_transmission"},{"title":"Utility frequency","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utility_frequency"},{"title":"Lucien Gaulard","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucien_Gaulard"},{"title":"Ottó Bláthy","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ott%C3%B3_Bl%C3%A1thy"},{"title":"William Stanley Jr.","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Stanley_Jr."},{"title":"War of the currents","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_currents"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/13-1.jpg","alt":"One of the first transformers, made by Déri, Bláthy and Zipernowsky in Budapest in 1885, on display in a museum.","file":"DBZ trafo.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DBZ_trafo.jpg","author":"Zátonyi Sándor, (ifj.)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/13-2.jpg","alt":"Electrical pioneers in the 1910s: William Stanley stands fourth from left at the back; Thomas Edison sits in front.","file":"Dr. R.B. Owens, J.J. Carty, Frank Sprague, Wm. Stanley, Sam'l Insull, Dr. Walton Clark, T.A. Edison and wife, Chevalier Van Rappard, Dr. R. Tait McKenzie LCCN2014699091.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr._R.B._Owens,_J.J._Carty,_Frank_Sprague,_Wm._Stanley,_Sam%27l_Insull,_Dr._Walton_Clark,_T.A._Edison_and_wife,_Chevalier_Van_Rappard,_Dr._R._Tait_McKenzie_LCCN2014699091.jpg","author":"Bain News Service, publisher","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/13-3.jpg","alt":"Diagram of a transformer: primary and secondary coils wound on one closed iron core, which carries the magnetism between them.","file":"Ideal transformer representation.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ideal_transformer_representation.png","author":"Electrical Classroom","license":"CC BY 4.0"}],"words":4303},{"n":14,"title":"The War of the Currents","when":"1884 – 1896","summary":"Thomas Edison wanted the world to use DC electricity. George Westinghouse and the inventor Nikola Tesla bet on AC. Their fight got noisy and sometimes nasty, but in the end AC lit up a giant world's fair and carried the power of Niagara Falls to a whole city.","sections":[{"heading":"From Smiljan to Edison's Workshop","paragraphs":["On 10 July 1856 a baby boy named Nikola Tesla was born in Smiljan, a small village in the mountains of what is now Croatia. At the time the area was part of the Austrian Empire, and Tesla's family were Serbs; his father was an Orthodox priest. Nikola was a brilliant, imaginative child who later claimed he could picture machines in his head so clearly that he could build and test them in his mind before touching a single tool.","In 1875 he went to study engineering at the technical college in Graz, in Austria. There he saw a DC machine whose brushes and commutator sparked fiercely, and, by his own later account, he became convinced that a motor could be built without them. He left Graz in 1878 without finishing his degree, and in 1880 he went to Prague, where he attended lectures without being formally enrolled. He never earned a university degree, though he read and taught himself constantly.","In 1881 Tesla moved to Budapest to work for the city's new telephone exchange, run by the Hungarian inventor Tivadar Puskás, and he rose to be its chief electrician. According to Tesla's own story, told many years later, the great idea came to him in 1882 while he was walking in a Budapest park with a friend: a magnetic field that spins round and round, made by alternating currents, which could drag a motor along with it. He said he sketched the idea in the sand with a stick. We only have his word for the scene, but the idea itself was real and became his most important invention.","Later in 1882 Puskás found him a job in Paris with the Continental Edison Company, which installed Edison lighting systems in Europe. Tesla worked on improving dynamos and motors and fixing lighting plants. In June 1884 he sailed to New York to work at the Edison Machine Works, which built dynamos for Edison's power stations. He arrived with very little money, ready to make his mark in the land of opportunity.","Tesla worked for Edison's company for only about six months, solving problems with generators and lighting installations. Edison and Tesla were very different people. Edison was a practical tinkerer who believed in testing thousands of possibilities, while Tesla preferred to work things out in his head and with mathematics. Edison had already bet everything on direct current. Tesla was dreaming of alternating current.","Why Tesla left is not entirely clear. Many years later, in his autobiography, Tesla told a story that he had been promised 50,000 dollars for redesigning the company's dynamos, and that when he finished, he was told the offer had been a joke. This story is famous, but it comes only from Tesla's own account written decades later, and historians point out that the company did not have that kind of spare cash. His own diary from the time simply records a short goodbye to the Edison Machine Works. So it is best to treat the 50,000-dollar joke as a story told by Tesla, not as a proven fact.","Next, investors helped Tesla start his own company, Tesla Electric Light and Manufacturing, which built an arc lighting system in Rahway, New Jersey. But in 1886 the investors decided to run an electric utility instead, pushed Tesla out, and kept the patents he had signed over to them. He was left almost penniless. For a while, during a hard winter, he took repair jobs and even worked digging ditches for about 2 dollars a day.","His luck turned in April 1887, when two backers, Alfred S. Brown and Charles Peck, helped him set up the Tesla Electric Company in a laboratory in New York. At last he had the time and tools to build the motor he had been imagining for years."]},{"heading":"A Motor with a Spinning Field","paragraphs":["Remember the big weakness of early AC from the last part? There was no good AC motor. Lights worked fine on AC, but factories, mills and streetcars needed motors, and those ran on DC. If AC was going to win, it needed a motor of its own.","Tesla's answer was the induction motor, and the idea behind it is beautiful. Imagine a ring of electromagnets arranged around a circle. Feed one set of coils with an alternating current, and a second set with another alternating current that is out of step with the first, peaking a quarter of a cycle later. As the two currents rise and fall in turn, the magnetism inside the ring does not just flip back and forth. It swings smoothly round and round, like the beam of a lighthouse. This is a rotating magnetic field, and no part of the motor has to move to create it.","Inside the ring sits the rotor, a simple drum of iron with copper bars or wires. The spinning magnetism sweeps across the copper and, by Faraday's induction again, makes currents flow in it. Those currents turn the rotor into a magnet too, and it is dragged around after the rotating field, chasing it but never quite catching it. There are no brushes, no sparking commutator, and no electrical connection to the spinning part at all. It is tough, simple and almost maintenance-free. Using several AC currents out of step with each other like this is called a polyphase system, from Greek words meaning many phases.","Tesla filed patents on his motor and polyphase system in 1887, and they were granted in May 1888. On 16 May 1888 he gave a lecture and demonstration to the American Institute of Electrical Engineers in New York, showing his motors running. Engineers were impressed, including those working for George Westinghouse.","Tesla was not alone. In Turin, the Italian physicist Galileo Ferraris had been experimenting with rotating magnetic fields since about 1885, and on 11 March 1888 he presented a paper on the idea to the Royal Academy of Sciences in Turin, two months before Tesla's lecture. Ferraris built working induction motors but never patented them, believing ideas like this should be free for all. In Germany, the Russian-born engineer Mikhail Dolivo-Dobrovolsky, working for the AEG company in Berlin, soon developed improved three-phase motors and transformers from 1889 onward. Like many great inventions, the induction motor had several parents.","Westinghouse moved quickly. In July 1888 his company licensed Tesla's patents. Tesla and his partners received 60,000 dollars in cash and stock, plus a royalty of 2.50 dollars for every horsepower of AC motors sold. Westinghouse also hired Tesla as a consultant in Pittsburgh for a year at 2,000 dollars a month, and took an option on Ferraris's motor design too, to be safe. The work in Pittsburgh was frustrating at first. Tesla disagreed with other engineers, his early motors only ran well at one fixed speed, which made them no good for streetcars, and it took years of work by Westinghouse engineers, especially Benjamin Lamme, to make induction motors practical. But AC now had a future beyond lighting."]},{"heading":"Warnings and Wires","paragraphs":["The Edison side fought back with words first. In February 1888 Edison's company published an 84-page booklet titled simply 'A Warning'. It was sent to newspapers and to customers of rival companies. It argued that Westinghouse and others were using Edison's patents without permission, and that high-voltage AC was a deadly danger to the public, while Edison's low-voltage DC was safe.","The danger was not imaginary. In the 1880s the streets of New York were crowded with a tangle of overhead wires on poles: telegraph wires, telephone wires, fire alarm wires and electric lighting wires, some carrying thousands of volts with poor insulation. Several workers were killed. The most shocking case came on 11 October 1889, when a telegraph lineman named John Feeks grabbed a telegraph wire that had accidentally become crossed with a high-voltage AC lighting line. He was killed high up on a pole above a busy Manhattan street. Newspapers called the fear that followed the Electric Wire Panic, and the city ordered many overhead wires to be cut down and moved underground.","In November 1889 Edison wrote a magazine article in the North American Review attacking AC and saying he would never use it while he was in charge. Many ordinary people agreed that high-voltage wires were frightening. Westinghouse's side replied that the real problem was careless installation, and that AC could be made safe with proper insulation, underground cables and transformers that kept the voltage low inside homes and shops. In the long run, the Westinghouse argument proved right: with good safety rules, AC was not more dangerous to customers than DC. But in 1889 the question was not yet settled.","It is worth being fair to both sides. Edison really did believe high voltages were dangerous, and early AC systems were often installed in risky ways. Westinghouse really did believe AC could be made safe and would bring cheaper power to more people. Both men also had huge sums of money at stake. Behind the arguments about safety lay a fierce business battle over patents, customers and the future of an entire industry."]},{"heading":"Harold Brown and the Electric Chair","paragraphs":["The darkest chapter of the War of the Currents was led not by Edison himself but by an engineer named Harold P. Brown. Brown was largely self-taught and had worked on arc lighting before moving to New York in 1887. On 5 June 1888 he wrote a letter to the New York Post calling AC lines a deadly public menace and demanding strict limits on AC voltage.","To prove his point, Brown staged public experiments in which animals were killed with electricity. In July 1888, at Columbia College in New York, he showed that a dog survived shocks of direct current but was killed by a lower voltage of alternating current. Later that year, at Edison's laboratory in West Orange, New Jersey, he killed calves and a horse with AC for a group of doctors from the New York Medico-Legal Society. These cruel demonstrations upset many people even at the time, and they were not fair science either: the dog at Columbia had already been given a series of DC shocks, up to 1,000 volts, before the AC was used, so it was not a careful comparison. Edison's laboratory supplied space and equipment, although Brown denied being employed by Edison. In August 1889 a New York newspaper, the Sun, printed letters taken from Brown's office suggesting he had been taking directions, and money, from Edison's company and its ally Thomson-Houston.","At the same time, New York State was looking for a new way to carry out the death penalty, one that people hoped would be quicker than hanging. A Buffalo dentist named Alfred Southwick had suggested electricity years earlier, and in 1888 the state passed a law making electrocution its method of execution. Brown became a consultant on the design of the electric chair and helped choose AC for it, then quietly obtained Westinghouse AC generators to power it, without Westinghouse's permission. Many historians think Brown and his supporters hoped the public would come to link Westinghouse's AC with death.","The first person sentenced to the electric chair was William Kemmler, who had been convicted of a murder in Buffalo in 1889. George Westinghouse, not wanting his equipment used this way, is widely believed to have quietly paid for a top lawyer, William Bourke Cockran, to appeal the case, arguing that electrocution was a cruel and unusual punishment. The appeals failed. On 6 August 1890, at Auburn Prison in New York, Kemmler was executed. The process went badly: the first jolt did not end his life, and a second, stronger jolt was needed, so the whole procedure took about eight minutes. The New York Times reported it under the headline 'Far Worse Than Hanging'. Westinghouse is reported to have said that they would have done better using an axe.","The electric chair did not destroy AC, as some had hoped. Most people could see the difference between a machine built to kill and the wires that lit their homes. But the whole episode showed how ugly a business fight could become when fear, money and pride were mixed together."]},{"heading":"175 Kilometres of Power: Lauffen to Frankfurt","paragraphs":["While Americans argued, Europe gave the world a spectacular demonstration of what AC could really do. In 1891 the city of Frankfurt in Germany held an International Electrotechnical Exhibition from 16 May to 19 October. The organisers had a bold aim: to bring electric power to the show from a waterfall on the river Neckar at Lauffen, about 175 kilometres away. Nobody had ever sent serious amounts of power that far.","The project was led by the German engineer Oskar von Miller, who later founded the famous Deutsches Museum in Munich. The equipment was built by two companies: AEG of Berlin, where Mikhail Dolivo-Dobrovolsky had been developing three-phase AC, and the Swiss firm Maschinenfabrik Oerlikon, where the engineer Charles E. L. Brown worked with Dolivo-Dobrovolsky on the project. Three-phase AC uses three alternating currents, each one third of a cycle out of step with the others, which is the most efficient way to send power and run induction motors. It is still the standard on almost every power grid in the world.","At Lauffen, a water turbine turned a three-phase generator of about 300 horsepower. Transformers raised the voltage to many thousands of volts (accounts give figures from about 8,000 to 15,000 volts), and in later tests as high as 25,000 volts, far higher than anything Edison's system used. The power then travelled along overhead wires strung across the German countryside, using about 60 tonnes of copper wire only 4 millimetres thick. At Frankfurt, transformers stepped the voltage back down.","The entrance to the exhibition had a sign glowing with 1,000 light bulbs that announced the power transmission from Lauffen, and inside, an electric pump drove an artificial waterfall. About 1.2 million visitors came. Imagine standing in front of that waterfall and realising that the river water tumbling at Lauffen, 175 kilometres away, was making this water fall in Frankfurt. Official tests found that about 75 per cent of the power generated at Lauffen arrived in Frankfurt. That was proof that long-distance three-phase AC transmission worked.","There was one small lesson too. The Lauffen line ran at 40 cycles per second, and engineers noticed lamps flickering slightly. AEG soon chose 50 cycles per second as its standard, and that choice spread across much of Europe, which is one reason European sockets run at 50 Hz today."]},{"heading":"Money Troubles and a New Company","paragraphs":["The War of the Currents was also a war of money, and both sides felt the strain. In 1890 a financial crisis, set off by the near-collapse of Barings Bank in London, swept through the business world. Banks called in their loans, and Westinghouse's company ran short of cash. Its lenders demanded cuts. Tesla helped his friend by agreeing in 1891 to give up the royalty on his motor patents, which could have been worth a fortune. Years later, in 1897, Westinghouse bought the patents outright for a lump sum of 216,000 dollars.","Edison was losing control of his own companies. In 1889 his various electrical businesses had been merged into Edison General Electric, with the financier Henry Villard as president, and Edison no longer held the majority. By 1890 Edison told Villard he wanted to leave the lighting business. Meanwhile another strong company, Thomson-Houston, led by Charles Coffin and built on the inventions of Elihu Thomson, was selling both AC and DC systems.","On 15 April 1892 the great banker J. P. Morgan helped arrange a merger: Edison General Electric and Thomson-Houston became a single company called General Electric, usually known as GE. Thomson-Houston's managers took charge, and Edison's name disappeared from the company title. He reportedly learned of the deal only shortly before it was announced. The new GE controlled roughly three quarters of the American electrical business, and, importantly, it embraced AC as well as DC. Edison himself turned his energy to other inventions.","So by 1892 the war was no longer really Edison against Westinghouse. It was two giant companies, GE and Westinghouse, both selling AC systems and competing for the biggest contracts in the world."]},{"heading":"The White City and the Mighty Falls","paragraphs":["The first great prize was the World's Columbian Exposition, a huge world's fair held in Chicago from 1 May to 30 October 1893 to mark 400 years since Christopher Columbus reached the Americas. In May 1892 Westinghouse won the contract to light it, underbidding General Electric by a wide margin. He made almost no profit, but the fair was a stage seen by millions.","There was a snag: GE owned Edison's light bulb patent, so Westinghouse could not use Edison-type bulbs. His engineers quickly designed a different lamp, sealed with a ground-glass stopper, based on an older patent by Sawyer and Man. It worked, though the lamps did not last long and teams of workers had to keep replacing them. Westinghouse also built twelve big 1,000-horsepower AC generators for the fair.","When the fair opened, more than 200,000 lamps lit up its gleaming white buildings, which gave it the nickname the White City. Fountains glowed, searchlights swept the sky, and in the Electricity Building visitors could see Tesla's induction motors and AC equipment. Tesla himself visited in August and gave dazzling demonstrations, including the Egg of Columbus, in which a rotating magnetic field made a copper egg spin and stand up on its end. More than 27 million visits were made to the fair. For many Americans it was their first sight of a whole city lit by electricity, and it was powered by AC.","The second prize was even bigger: Niagara Falls. For years people had dreamed of harnessing the enormous power of the falls. In 1890 an international commission, chaired by the famous British scientist Lord Kelvin, studied many proposals but could not choose one. Finally, in 1893, the Cataract Construction Company, led by Edward Dean Adams, decided on a polyphase AC system, persuaded in part by the engineer George Forbes. Westinghouse won the contract to build the generators, based on Tesla's polyphase system and refined by Benjamin Lamme, while GE won contracts for the transmission line and transformers. Both rivals were now working on the same AC project.","The Adams Power Plant began running on 25 August 1895 with giant 5,000-horsepower generators, each producing AC at 25 cycles per second. Then, on 16 November 1896, power from the falls reached the city of Buffalo, about 20 miles (around 32 kilometres) away, stepped up to high voltage for the journey. Electricity made by falling water could now help light and power a big city. The plant soon grew, and Niagara became a symbol of the electric age. AC had shown it could power whole cities from far away."]},{"heading":"Who Really Won?","paragraphs":["AC clearly won the battle to power big electricity grids. Its ability to be stepped up and down by transformers made it the only practical way, with the technology of the 1890s, to send large amounts of power over long distances. Within a few decades, nearly every new power system in the world used AC, and the three-phase system shown at Frankfurt became standard almost everywhere.","But DC never disappeared. Look around you. Your phone, tablet, laptop and torch all run on batteries, which give DC. Computers, LED lights and televisions use DC inside, and the small power adapters you plug into the wall turn AC into DC for them. Solar panels produce DC. Electric cars store DC in their batteries. And today, very long high-voltage DC lines, a technology you will meet later in this story, carry huge amounts of power across countries and under the sea, because modern electronics can now do the voltage-changing that only transformers could manage in Edison's day.","Old DC systems also lasted far longer than you might guess. Some Broadway theatres in New York used DC until 1975. Consolidated Edison, the company that grew out of Edison's original New York business, still had about 4,600 DC customers in 1998, many of them old buildings that still used DC to run their elevators. By 2006 there were just 60. Finally, on 14 November 2007, Con Edison switched off its last direct current service, 125 years after Pearl Street started up. The few remaining customers were given machines that turn AC into DC on site.","One more myth needs clearing up. People often say that Edison electrocuted an elephant named Topsy to prove AC was dangerous. In fact, Topsy was killed at Coney Island in 1903 by the people who owned her, more than ten years after the War of the Currents was over and long after Edison had left the electric lighting business. A film company connected to Edison recorded it, but it had nothing to do with the battle between AC and DC.","In the end, the War of the Currents was not a story of heroes and villains. Edison built the first complete electric lighting system and was right that badly installed high voltages were dangerous. Tesla, Ferraris and Dolivo-Dobrovolsky gave AC its motors. Gaulard, Gibbs, the Ganz engineers and Stanley gave it transformers. Westinghouse had the courage to bet his company on it. Each side pushed the other to do better, and the whole world benefited.","With the argument settled, engineers turned to a much bigger task. Power lines began to march out from the cities, across fields, forests and mountains, linking power stations together into networks that would one day stretch across entire countries."]}],"deepDive":"Historians today tend to see the War of the Currents less as a duel between geniuses and more as a contest between technical systems, patents and finance. Edison's 110-volt DC network was elegant inside a dense district, and he had a complete package of lamps, meters, fuses and underground cables. Its weakness was physics: resistive losses rise with the square of current, so low-voltage distribution needed huge copper conductors or many local power stations. AC's advantage was the transformer, but in 1886 AC lacked a practical motor and a meter. Both gaps closed quickly: Oliver Shallenberger's induction meter (1888), Tesla's and Ferraris's induction motors (1888), and Dolivo-Dobrovolsky's three-phase machines (1889 to 1891). The Lauffen to Frankfurt test in 1891 delivered about 75 percent of the generated power over roughly 175 km, convincing engineers worldwide. Credit for the induction motor is genuinely shared. Ferraris presented his rotating-field work in Turin on 11 March 1888 but did not patent it; Tesla's US patents, granted in May 1888, became commercially decisive because Westinghouse bought them. Tesla's 1882 park vision and the 50,000-dollar 'joke' come from his own recollections written decades later and cannot be checked. Harold Brown's animal experiments and his role in the electric chair are well documented, as is support from Edison's laboratory and company, but historians debate how far Edison personally directed him. Edison's safety fears had a real basis: overhead high-voltage lines in New York caused several deaths, including lineman John Feeks in October 1889. But AC could be, and soon was, made safe through insulation, underground cables and step-down transformers. Business forces ended the war more than any demonstration. The 1890 financial panic weakened Westinghouse, and Edison lost control of his companies before J. P. Morgan's 1892 merger created General Electric, which promptly sold AC too. In the mid-1890s GE and Westinghouse reached a patent-sharing agreement, and Niagara became a joint project, with Westinghouse generators and GE transmission equipment. The early 25 Hz Niagara frequency and 40 Hz at Lauffen also show that standards, not just currents, were being fought over; the 50 and 60 Hz standards came out of these years.","funFact":"Con Edison, the company that grew out of Edison's original New York business, did not switch off its last direct current customers until 14 November 2007, 125 years after the Pearl Street station started up.","check":{"q":"In 1891, how far did engineers send three-phase AC power from Lauffen to the exhibition in Frankfurt?","a":"About 175 kilometres","wrong":["About 1 kilometre","About 1,750 kilometres","About 17 kilometres"]},"sources":[{"title":"War of the currents","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_currents"},{"title":"The War of the Currents: AC vs. DC Power","publisher":"U.S. Department of Energy","url":"https://www.energy.gov/articles/war-currents-ac-vs-dc-power"},{"title":"Nikola Tesla","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikola_Tesla"},{"title":"Galileo Ferraris","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galileo_Ferraris"},{"title":"International Electrotechnical Exhibition","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Electrotechnical_Exhibition"},{"title":"William Kemmler","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Kemmler"},{"title":"World's Columbian Exposition","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World%27s_Columbian_Exposition"},{"title":"Adams Power Plant Transformer House","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adams_Power_Plant_Transformer_House"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/14-1.jpg","alt":"Nikola Tesla at about age 34, photographed around 1890 by Napoleon Sarony in New York.","file":"Tesla circa 1890.jpeg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tesla_circa_1890.jpeg","author":"Napoleon Sarony","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/14-2.jpg","alt":"Tesla's working induction motor model, used to show the ideas in his 1887–1888 motor patents.","file":"Moteur à induction.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moteur_%C3%A0_induction.jpg","author":"Romain Ramier","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/14-3.jpg","alt":"A huge alternating-current generator inside the Edward Dean Adams Power Plant at Niagara Falls, New York.","file":"Photocopy of photograph Photographer and date unknown GENERATOR - Edward D. Adams Station Power Plant, Niagara River and Buffalo Avenue, Niagara Falls, Niagara County, NY HABS NY,32-NIAF,3-5.tif","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photocopy_of_photograph_Photographer_and_date_unknown_GENERATOR_-_Edward_D._Adams_Station_Power_Plant,_Niagara_River_and_Buffalo_Avenue,_Niagara_Falls,_Niagara_County,_NY_HABS_NY,32-NIAF,3-5.tif","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3921},{"n":15,"title":"Wiring the World: The Grid","when":"1889 – 1960s","summary":"Power stations got huge, and long wires on tall steel towers began linking them together into a giant web called the grid. Britain, America, Russia and Japan all raced to bring electricity to cities and farms. For the first time, families far out in the countryside could flip a switch and see the light come on.","sections":[{"heading":"A Young Man's Giant on the Thames","paragraphs":["Picture London in 1887. The city is the biggest in the world, with millions of people, smoky chimneys and gas lamps flickering along the streets. Electricity has arrived, but only in little patches. A theatre here, a rich man's house there, a few streets in the City lit by a small power plant tucked into a basement. Each little plant can only reach the buildings close by. If you want electric light, you basically need to live next door to a steam engine.","That year a new company called the London Electric Supply Corporation, usually shortened to LESCo, was formed. It had started with a single fashionable art gallery, the Grosvenor Gallery on Bond Street. The gallery's owners had been so impressed by the electric displays at the 1881 Paris exhibition that they installed their own generators, and soon they were selling spare power to their neighbours. Demand grew so fast that the little gallery plant could not keep up. LESCo needed something far bigger.","The engineer they hired was astonishingly young. Sebastian Ziani de Ferranti had been born in Liverpool on 9 April 1864, so he was only about 23 when LESCo asked him to design their new station. He had already been running his own electrical business since his late teens. His plan was bold, even shocking to many experts: instead of putting a power station in the middle of the crowded city, he would build an enormous one several miles away, on cheap land beside the River Thames at Deptford, and send the electricity into central London along cables.","Why Deptford? Coal for the boilers could arrive by boat straight up the river, and water for the steam engines was right there. Building a huge, smoky plant out of the centre also avoided complaints from people who did not want a giant coal-burning factory next door. It was the same reason many big power stations were later built at the edge of cities or out in the countryside.","The secret ingredient was high voltage. Remember from earlier in this story that sending power at high voltage and low current wastes far less energy as heat in the wires. Ferranti's generators produced alternating current at about 2,500 volts, and transformers then stepped it up to about 10,000 volts for the journey into town. Nothing like that had been tried on such a scale. At the far end, more transformers lowered the voltage again so it was safe enough to use in shops and homes.","Because no one made cables for such a high voltage, Ferranti designed his own: copper tubes, one inside the other, separated by layers of paper soaked in wax, laid in short sections alongside a railway line into London. He also had to account for losses along the way. Engineers later explained that the cables lost roughly a tenth of the power from end to end, so the station was designed to send out about 11,000 volts in order to deliver about 10,000 at the gallery. Some people say this is one reason British transmission voltages ended up in multiples of eleven, such as 11,000, 33,000 and 132,000 volts.","Construction began in 1888 and the first power flowed in about 1889, with the station fully at work by 1891. Its two giant main alternators of 1889 were driven by steam engines of about 1,500 horsepower each. For ten years, from 1891 to 1901, Deptford was the largest power station in Britain.","It was not a smooth success. There were breakdowns at the station and at the Grosvenor Gallery substation, long shutdowns and customers who got fed up and left. In 1891 Ferranti was replaced as chief engineer. Later managers rebuilt the plant with new machinery, and then the supply became reliable. But the big idea was right, and it was copied around the world: one huge central power station, far from the customers, linked to them by high-voltage AC lines. That is still the basic shape of electricity supply today. Ferranti went on to have a long career, became President of the Institution of Electrical Engineers and was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society in 1927."]},{"heading":"Parsons and the Spinning Wheel of Steam","paragraphs":["A power station is really a machine for spinning things. Faraday showed back in 1831 that if you move a magnet and a coil of wire past each other, electricity flows. So every big generator needs something to turn it, fast and steadily, day and night. In the 1880s that something was usually a reciprocating steam engine: huge pistons sliding back and forth, connected by rods and cranks to a turning wheel. These engines were heavy, shook the ground, needed constant oiling and could not spin very fast.","Charles Parsons had a better idea. He was born in London on 13 June 1854, the youngest son of a famous astronomer, the Earl of Rosse, whose family home was Birr Castle in Ireland. Young Charles grew up surrounded by workshops, because his father had built one of the largest telescopes in the world there. He became an engineer, and in 1884 he built a machine that changed power stations forever: the modern steam turbine.","Think of a pinwheel. Blow on it and it spins. A steam turbine is a very clever, very strong version of that. High-pressure steam from a boiler rushes through rings of fixed blades that aim it, then hits rings of moving blades attached to a shaft, making the shaft whirl. Parsons' trick was to let the steam push through many sets of blades one after another, giving up a little of its energy at each stage instead of all at once. That way the turbine could run smoothly and efficiently, and it turned in one continuous motion instead of the jerky back-and-forth of pistons.","His first turbine of 1884 spun at about 18,000 revolutions per minute, which is hundreds of turns every second, and drove a dynamo that made about 7.5 kilowatts, roughly enough today for a few electric kettles. It was not very efficient yet, but it worked. In 1890 the Forth Banks power station in Newcastle upon Tyne became the first power station in the world to make electricity with turbine-driven generators. By 1899 Parsons had built a turbine generator of one megawatt for the town of Elberfeld in Germany.","Parsons also loved to show off his invention in style. In 1897 Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee was celebrated with a great review of the Royal Navy at Spithead, near Portsmouth. Suddenly a small, sleek boat called Turbinia, powered by Parsons' turbines, came racing between the lines of warships at about 34 knots, around 63 kilometres per hour. The Navy's fastest ships of other kinds could only manage about 27 knots, and no one could catch her. Before long, turbines were powering warships and ocean liners, as well as power stations.","The growth in size was incredible. During Parsons' own lifetime, the most powerful single turbine units grew around ten thousand times bigger than his first one. Today each of the two main turbines at the new Hinkley Point C nuclear power station in England is rated at about 1,600 megawatts. That is more than two hundred thousand times the output of the 1884 machine.","Most of the world's power stations still use turbines. In a coal, gas or nuclear station, heat boils water into steam, and the steam spins a turbine. In a hydroelectric dam, falling water spins the turbine directly, and in a wind farm the wind does the job. Even Deptford was eventually rebuilt with Parsons turbines. So when you switch on a light, there is a very good chance that somewhere a spinning shaft is turning because of the idea Charles Parsons had in 1884."]},{"heading":"What Is a Grid, Anyway?","paragraphs":["The word grid makes people think of the squares on graph paper, and that is close. An electrical grid is a giant network, a web of wires that links many power stations to millions of users so that electricity can flow from wherever it is made to wherever it is needed. You can think of it as a road system for electricity, with motorways, main roads and little lanes.","It starts at the power station, where a generator makes electricity. Right next door is a step-up transformer that raises the voltage very high, often hundreds of thousands of volts. The power then travels along the motorways of the grid: high-voltage transmission lines, usually hung from tall steel towers called pylons. As the US Energy Information Administration explains, higher voltage makes long-distance transmission more efficient and cheaper, which is the same lesson Ferranti put to work at Deptford.","Next come substations, fenced-in yards full of transformers, switches and thick metal bars. There the voltage is stepped down to a lower level for the main roads, the distribution lines that run along streets on wooden poles or under the pavement. Finally, a smaller transformer, perhaps a grey box at the end of your road or a can-shaped one up a pole, lowers the voltage once more to what your home uses, about 230 volts in Britain and most of Europe, or about 120 volts in North America. Then it comes through a meter, into your fuse box and out of your sockets.","Here is the part that surprises most people. A grid has almost no storage. Electricity is being made at the very same moment it is being used. When millions of people switch on their kettles at the same time, power stations somewhere must produce more power within seconds. If they do not, the generators slow down very slightly, and the frequency of the AC (50 times a second in Britain, 60 in North America) starts to drop. Engineers in control rooms watch the frequency like a heartbeat. If it falls too far, equipment can switch off to protect itself, and that can lead to blackouts.","British grid engineers even plan for television. When a hugely popular programme or football match finishes, millions of people get up at once to make a cup of tea, and demand jumps sharply. Controllers watch the TV schedule and have extra power ready to go at the right moment. It is one of the oddest jobs in engineering: predicting the nation's tea breaks.","This is why linking many power stations together is so useful. If one station breaks down, others on the grid can cover for it. Stations can be built where fuel or water is easiest to get, not right next to the customers. Cheaper, more efficient stations can run most of the time, while smaller ones help out only at busy hours. And everyone shares the cost of spare capacity instead of each town needing its own backup.","Grids can be enormous. The United States, apart from Alaska and Hawaii, is covered by just three main grids, called interconnections: the Eastern, the Western and a separate one for most of Texas. Together they have thousands of miles of high-voltage lines and millions of miles of local lines, linking thousands of power plants to hundreds of millions of customers. But in the 1890s none of this existed. Building it took decades of engineering, money and political argument, as the rest of this chapter shows."]},{"heading":"Britain Joins the Dots","paragraphs":["By the 1920s Britain had electricity, but it was a mess. There was one shining example in the north-east, where the engineer Charles Merz's Neptune Bank power station near Newcastle had opened in 1901 with modern three-phase high-voltage supply for a whole region. Almost everywhere else, power came from a patchwork of hundreds of small local companies and town councils. Many ran their own little power stations. They used different voltages, some supplied DC and some AC, and the AC ones did not all use the same frequency. A vacuum cleaner bought in one town might not work properly if you moved to the next.","This was wasteful. Small stations were expensive to run, and each one had to keep spare machines in case of breakdowns. The government decided to act. The Electricity (Supply) Act of 1926 set up a new body, the Central Electricity Board, with a giant task: to link the best power stations across the country with a single high-voltage network, which people called the national gridiron, soon shortened to the grid.","The plan was to build lines running at 132,000 volts, a huge voltage for the time, all synchronised at 50 cycles per second. The best and most efficient stations would be selected to feed the grid, while the smaller, older ones would gradually close. Places using odd frequencies had to change over, which meant altering or replacing a great deal of equipment in factories and homes.","The first grid tower went up near Edinburgh in July 1928. Then the pylons began marching across the countryside, thousands of them, steel lattice towers carrying the wires high above fields, rivers and hills. Their design was picked through a competition, with advice from the architect Sir Reginald Blomfield, because people worried about how they would look. Some writers and nature lovers hated them. Others found them thrilling, like giant metal walkers striding toward the future, and in the 1930s a group of poets were even nicknamed the Pylon Poets for writing about them.","The work was finished in September 1933, ahead of schedule and on budget, which is rare for any giant engineering project. At first the grid ran as a set of regional grids, with links between them that were not used all the time. Then, one night in October 1937, engineers unofficially switched the regions together so that the whole system ran as one. It worked, and by 1938 Britain's grid was operating as a true national system.","The effect on ordinary people was dramatic. In 1920 about 750,000 consumers in Britain used electricity. By 1938 the number had grown to about 9 million. Power was cheaper and more reliable, and towns that had never had a supply could now be connected to the grid instead of needing a power station of their own.","The grid kept growing. After the electricity industry was nationalised by the Electricity Act of 1947, engineers added even higher-voltage lines at 275,000 volts. By 1960 demand was rising faster than anyone had predicted, so they chose 400,000 volts for a new backbone. The first 400,000-volt section, at West Burton, was switched on in June 1965, the beginning of what was called the Supergrid. Those lines still carry much of Britain's power today."]},{"heading":"America: Bright Cities, Dark Farms","paragraphs":["In the United States, private companies built power systems quickly in towns and cities, where many customers lived close together. Running a line down a city street might connect hundreds of homes. Out in the countryside it was a different story. A farm might be a mile or more from its nearest neighbour, so a power company could spend a fortune on poles and wire and connect only a handful of customers. Most companies decided it simply was not worth it.","The result was a huge gap. By the mid-1930s most homes in American cities had electricity, roughly nine out of ten by common estimates, but in 1935 fewer than 11 out of every 100 farms did. Farms in some European countries were said to be far better connected. Imagine life on an unelectrified farm in Kansas or Tennessee. Water had to be pumped by hand or by windmill and carried to the house in buckets. Cows were milked by hand by the light of a kerosene lantern before dawn. Clothes were scrubbed on a washboard and pressed with irons heated on a stove. Food was kept cool in a cellar or in an icebox with a block of ice, if you could get ice at all. When the sun went down, the work got harder and the reading stopped.","President Franklin D. Roosevelt's government made rural electricity part of its New Deal, the set of programmes created to fight the Great Depression. On 18 May 1933 Congress created the Tennessee Valley Authority, or TVA. The Tennessee Valley was one of the poorest parts of the country. In 1933 the average rural family there earned about 639 dollars a year, some families lived on around 100 dollars, around 30 per cent of people suffered from malaria, and the soil was being washed away by erosion. The TVA's job was to build dams that would control floods, make rivers easier to navigate and generate cheap hydroelectric power for a region covering Tennessee and parts of six neighbouring states.","Work on its first dam, the Norris Dam, began on 1 October 1933. In its first eleven years the TVA built 16 hydroelectric dams. Cheap power attracted factories and jobs. But the dams also flooded valleys where people lived. More than 125,000 people, roughly 15,000 families, had to leave their homes, along with some cemeteries and small towns. Historians still debate whether they were treated fairly. It is a reminder that big projects that help many people can also bring real losses to others.","On 11 May 1935 Roosevelt set up the Rural Electrification Administration, or REA, and in 1936 Congress passed the Rural Electrification Act. Instead of waiting for power companies, the REA lent money cheaply to groups of farmers who formed electric cooperatives, little companies owned by their own customers. The loans could pay for power lines, wiring inside houses and even appliances, and they could be paid back over as long as 25 years.","The cooperatives found clever ways to cut costs. Private companies had said rural lines would cost 1,500 to 2,000 dollars per mile. By 1939 REA borrowers were building them for under 825 dollars a mile. By 1943 the programme had lent about 466 million dollars and built about 380,000 miles of line, serving more than a million consumers. Farm electrification rose from about 11 per cent in 1935 to almost 97 per cent by 1960. Families who had lived by lantern light remembered the night the lights first came on for the rest of their lives."]},{"heading":"Taming a River: The Hoover Dam","paragraphs":["Meanwhile, in the hot deserts of the American Southwest, an even bigger project was rising out of a canyon of the Colorado River on the border between Nevada and Arizona. The Hoover Dam was built between 1931 and 1936. It controlled floods, stored water for farms and cities, and generated electricity on a scale few had imagined.","The numbers are mind-boggling. The dam stands about 221 metres tall, taller than a 60-storey building. It contains about 2.5 million cubic metres of concrete, enough, people often say, to build a two-lane road from one side of the United States to the other. At the busiest time, in July 1934, 5,251 people worked on it at once, in fierce heat. Building it was dangerous: 112 deaths were reported as connected with the project. Behind the dam, the river filled a vast new reservoir, Lake Mead, which covers about 640 square kilometres when full.","President Roosevelt dedicated the dam on 30 September 1935, and the first generators began producing power in 1936. Today it has 17 main generating units. Water drops down four giant pipes, called penstocks, and falls as much as about 180 metres before hitting the turbines. Each turbine turns a shaft attached to a rotor, a ring of electromagnets that spins inside fixed coils of copper wire, which makes electricity exactly as Faraday's induction predicted. Together they can produce about 2,080 megawatts, and in an average year they make about 4 billion kilowatt-hours, enough for roughly 1.3 million people.","That power was shared out by agreement between Nevada, Arizona and Southern California, including Los Angeles, helping those cities and their factories to grow. The Hoover Dam became a symbol of the electric age: a wall of concrete that turned a wild river into a power station."]},{"heading":"Around the World: Plans, Plugs and Two Frequencies","paragraphs":["Other countries also saw electricity as the key to the future. Few made that clearer than the new Soviet Union. In February 1920, just after years of war and revolution, a State Commission for the Electrification of Russia was set up, known by its Russian initials as GOELRO. Led by the engineer Gleb Krzhizhanovsky, around 200 scientists and engineers drew up a plan to rebuild the country around electric power.","The GOELRO plan, approved in December 1920, called for a network of 30 regional power stations, including ten large hydroelectric stations. The goal was to produce 8.8 billion kilowatt-hours of electricity a year, compared with only 1.9 billion in 1913. The Soviet leader Vladimir Lenin summed it up in a famous slogan: 'Communism is Soviet power plus the electrification of the whole country.' Electric bulbs in village homes were nicknamed 'Ilyich's lamps', after Lenin, whose full name was Vladimir Ilyich Lenin. Big stations such as Volkhov and the Dnieper dam were built, and the output goal was reached in 1931. The plan also became the model for the Soviet Union's later Five-Year Plans.","Japan's story shows how early choices can last a very long time. In 1895 a company in Tokyo bought generators from the German firm AEG, built to run at 50 cycles per second. Around the same time, a company in Osaka bought generators from the American firm General Electric, built for 60 cycles. Other utilities followed their neighbours, and the country ended up split in two. To this day, eastern Japan, including Tokyo, runs at 50 hertz, and western Japan, including Osaka, Kyoto and Hiroshima, runs at 60 hertz.","The two halves cannot simply be joined with a wire. Power has to pass through special converter stations that change it into DC and back again. At the time of the 2011 disaster their total capacity was only about 1 gigawatt. After the great earthquake and tsunami of March 2011, when power stations in the east were badly damaged, the west could not send enough electricity across to help, and some areas suffered blackouts. Japan has since been building bigger links to carry more power between the two halves.","Most of the rest of the world made a similar choice of frequency to its early suppliers. Europe, Africa, most of Asia and Australia mostly use 50 hertz, while North America and some other countries use 60 hertz. That is why travellers often need a plug adapter, and sometimes a voltage converter, when they go abroad."]},{"heading":"A Web Across the Planet","paragraphs":["Step back and look at the whole picture. In 1882 the world's most famous power system, Pearl Street in New York, served a few hundred lamps within about a mile. By the 1960s, grids stretched across entire countries, carrying power hundreds of kilometres at hundreds of thousands of volts. In many countries the pylons and substations had become such a normal part of the landscape that people stopped noticing them.","This giant machine was built by many people: young engineers like Ferranti who dared to try high voltage, inventors like Parsons who made generators spin faster and bigger, government planners in London, Washington and Moscow, the line workers who climbed poles in all weather, and the farmers who formed cooperatives so their own families could have light. It is sometimes called the largest machine ever built, because everything on a grid, from the turbines in a power station to the toaster in your kitchen, is connected and must work together every second.","But a grid is just a set of wires until someone plugs something in. The real story is what happened inside homes, factories and streets once the power arrived. So let us step through the front door of a house in the 1920s or 1930s, find the new switch by the door, and see how plugging in changed everyday life."]}],"deepDive":"Why build one grid instead of many separate power systems? Engineers point to three big advantages. The first is economies of scale: a large, efficient station makes each kilowatt-hour more cheaply than a small one, so it pays to build big and share the output. The second is diversity of demand: a factory, a school and a family home use most power at different times, so when thousands of customers are combined the peak demand is much smaller than the sum of each customer's own peak. That lets a grid build fewer power stations in total. The third is reserve sharing: every system needs spare capacity in case a generator fails, and connected systems can share one pool of spare machines instead of each keeping its own. The catch is that every generator on an AC grid must turn in perfect step, or synchronism, with all the others. If supply falls short of demand, all the machines slow down together and the frequency drops; if supply exceeds demand, it rises. Modern grids hold frequency within a narrow band, in Britain normally within about 1 per cent of 50 hertz, by constantly adjusting generation. Big shared grids also have a weakness: a fault can spread, which is why engineers use protective relays and circuit breakers to isolate trouble quickly, a topic Part 17 returns to. Historians also argue about who should own such a system. In Britain the 1926 Act created a public board to run transmission while generation stayed with many owners, and the industry was nationalised in 1947. In the United States, private utilities, public bodies like the TVA and customer-owned cooperatives all ended up existing side by side, and the REA's success showed that rural electrification needed government help because private companies found it unprofitable. Critics of projects such as the TVA point to the families displaced by reservoirs; supporters point to flood control, cheap power and jobs in one of the poorest regions of the country. Both sets of facts are true, and weighing them is part of the history.","funFact":"Japan still runs on two different electrical frequencies, 50 hertz in the east and 60 hertz in the west, because in the 1890s Tokyo bought German generators and Osaka bought American ones.","check":{"q":"In the mid-1930s, roughly what share of American farms had electricity?","a":"About 1 in 10 (around 10 per cent)","wrong":["About 9 in 10","About half","None at all"]},"sources":[{"title":"Deptford Power Station","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deptford_Power_Station"},{"title":"Charles Algernon Parsons","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Algernon_Parsons"},{"title":"National Grid (Great Britain)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Grid_(Great_Britain)"},{"title":"Delivery to consumers (Electricity explained)","publisher":"US Energy Information Administration","url":"https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/electricity/delivery-to-consumers.php"},{"title":"Rural Electrification Administration (REA) (1935)","publisher":"The Living New Deal","url":"https://livingnewdeal.org/glossary/rural-electrification-administration-rea-1935/"},{"title":"Tennessee Valley Authority","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennessee_Valley_Authority"},{"title":"Hoover Dam power FAQs","publisher":"US Bureau of Reclamation","url":"https://www.usbr.gov/lc/hooverdam/faqs/powerfaq.html"},{"title":"GOELRO","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GOELRO"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/15-1.jpg","alt":"Ferranti's Deptford power station beside the River Thames in London, shown in an 1889 engraving.","file":"Powerstation deptford 1889.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Powerstation_deptford_1889.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/15-2.jpg","alt":"Charles Parsons' steam-turbine boat Turbinia racing at full speed, photographed in 1897 or 1898.","file":"Turbinia At Speed.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turbinia_At_Speed.jpg","author":"Alfred John West","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/15-3.jpg","alt":"A Rural Electrification Administration lineman climbs a power pole in Hayti, Missouri, in July 1942.","file":"A Rural Electrification Administration cooperative lineman at work in Hayti, Missouri.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Rural_Electrification_Administration_cooperative_lineman_at_work_in_Hayti,_Missouri.jpg","author":"Arthur Rothstein","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4274},{"n":16,"title":"Switched On: How Life Changed","when":"1879 – 1960s","summary":"Once electricity arrived, it changed almost everything. Electric trams replaced horses, lifts carried people up skyscrapers, and machines like vacuum cleaners, fridges and washing machines took over heavy chores at home. Radio, cinema, bright city streets and even X-rays that could see inside the body all ran on electricity.","sections":[{"heading":"All Aboard the Electric Train","paragraphs":["Imagine visiting the Berlin trade exhibition in 1879. Among the machines and stands you spot a crowd queuing for a ride. A tiny locomotive, not much bigger than a garden shed on wheels, is pulling open carriages full of laughing visitors around a short track. There is no smoke, no hissing steam, no coal. The power comes through an extra rail on the ground. This was the world's first electric passenger train, built by Werner von Siemens and his company, Siemens and Halske.","Siemens knew it was more than a fairground ride. In May 1881 his company opened the world's first electric tram service in Gross-Lichterfelde, a suburb of Berlin. In 1882 he even tested an electric bus called the Elektromote, which took its power from overhead wires, an early ancestor of the trolleybus.","Across the Atlantic, the man who made electric streetcars a real success was Frank Julian Sprague, a former US Navy officer who had worked briefly with Thomas Edison. In 1887 he took on a job that many thought impossible: to build a complete electric tram system in Richmond, Virginia, a hilly city with steep slopes and sharp curves. After trials in late 1887, regular service began on 2 February 1888 with 10 streetcars.","Each car drew power at 450 volts from an overhead wire, through a pole on its roof, and had two electric motors. The big cars had 40 seats and could carry up to 100 passengers, cruising at about 12 kilometres per hour with a top speed around 24. By June 1888 the system had 40 cars running on about 19 kilometres of track. In September 1888 officials from Boston came to inspect it and decided to build a similar system at home.","What happened next was one of the fastest changes in transport history. Before electricity, most city trams were pulled by horses. Horses were slow, needed huge stables, got tired, fell ill and left tonnes of manure in the streets every day. By 1895, only about seven years after Richmond, the United States had almost 900 electric street railways with nearly 18,000 kilometres of track, and horse-drawn street railways had almost disappeared within a little over a decade. Electric trams let cities spread outwards, because workers could now live in new suburbs and ride into town every morning.","Electricity went underground too. Early underground railways, like London's first lines from 1863, used steam engines, which filled tunnels with smoke. On 18 December 1890 the City and South London Railway opened to the public. It ran deep below the streets in round iron tunnels, the first successful deep-level tube railway in the world, and its trains were pulled by electric locomotives. The first line was about 5 kilometres long with six stations, and every journey cost a flat twopence. Its small, windowless carriages were nicknamed padded cells, but people loved the speed: about 5.1 million passengers rode it in its first year. Nearly every big city's metro since then has run on electricity."]},{"heading":"Going Up: Lifts and Tall Buildings","paragraphs":["Before lifts, which Americans call elevators, there was a limit to how tall a useful building could be. Nobody wanted to climb ten flights of stairs to reach an office, so the top floors of tall buildings were cheap and often empty. Lifting goods with ropes and pulleys was old, but carrying people safely was another matter, because everyone feared what would happen if the rope snapped.","Elisha Otis solved the fear first. In 1852 he introduced a safety brake that would grab the guide rails if the lifting cable broke, and in 1854 he showed it off dramatically at the New York Crystal Palace exhibition. In 1857 the first passenger lift was installed at 488 Broadway in New York, in the Haughwout Building. These early lifts were powered by steam engines and later by water pressure, called hydraulic lifts.","Electricity made lifts faster, simpler and easier to put into any building. In 1880 Werner von Siemens, busy as ever, built the first electric lift in Germany. Frank Sprague, fresh from his Richmond trams, developed electric lifts too, which could go faster and carry larger loads than hydraulic or steam ones. He installed 584 of them before selling his company to the Otis Elevator Company in 1895.","Together with strong steel frames, electric lifts made the skyscraper possible. In the early 1900s New York and Chicago began to rise upward, with buildings of 20, 40 and then more than 100 storeys. The Empire State Building, finished in 1931 with 102 storeys, depended completely on its banks of electric lifts. Without electricity there could be no modern city skyline."]},{"heading":"Factories Without Belts","paragraphs":["Step inside a big factory in the 1880s and you would hear a deafening clatter and feel the floor trembling. Somewhere in the building was a single huge steam engine. It turned a long iron rod called a line shaft that ran along the ceiling of every workshop. From the line shaft, dozens of leather belts dropped down to each machine below, flapping and slapping as they spun. If the steam engine stopped, every machine in the factory stopped. If you wanted to use just one machine, the whole engine still had to run.","These belt systems were also dangerous. A loose sleeve or a ponytail caught by a moving belt could drag a worker into the machinery. The rooms were often dim, because the windows had to fit around the shafts, and gas lamps or oil lamps were a fire risk in buildings full of cotton, wood or oil.","Electric motors changed all of that, though it took several decades. At first factories simply replaced the big steam engine with one big electric motor, keeping the belts. Then engineers realised they could give each machine its own small motor. This was called unit drive. Now a machine could be switched on and off by itself, placed anywhere on the floor, and moved when needed. Factories no longer had to be built around the line shaft. They could be laid out to follow the order of the work, one step after another, which made assembly lines much easier to design.","Electric motors were several times more efficient than small steam engines, because the power came from big, efficient central power stations and there was much less energy lost to friction in shafts and belts. The amount of mechanical power available to each American worker grew enormously, from about half a horsepower for each worker in the 1870s to about five horsepower by 1950. By about 1925, half of the power used by industry came from central power stations rather than a factory's own engines.","Electric light changed factories too. Bright, steady lamps meant fewer accidents and less eye strain, and they made it much easier to run a night shift. Some factories began to run around the clock, which helped produce more goods but also meant that more people worked through the night. Electricity made products cheaper, from cars to clothing to tinned food, and that would soon fill people's homes with new things to buy, many of them electric."]},{"heading":"The Electric Servants Move In","paragraphs":["Now walk into a home in about 1900, before most houses had electricity. Housework was exhausting. On washing day, which usually took the whole day, water had to be carried and heated on a stove, clothes were soaked, scrubbed on a ridged washboard, rinsed, wrung out by hand or through a mangle, and hung up to dry. Then came ironing with solid metal irons, called sad irons, heated on the stove. You kept several, swapping a hot one for the one that had gone cold. Carpets were dragged outside and beaten with a stick. Food had to be bought almost every day because there was no good way to keep it cold. Most of this work was done by women and girls, or by servants in richer homes.","Electric appliances started to appear surprisingly early, even before many homes could use them. In the early 1880s Schuyler Wheeler in New York designed an electric fan, and in 1882 Philip Diehl made one of the first electric ceiling fans. Also in 1882, on 6 June, Henry W. Seeley of New York patented an electric iron. It weighed almost 7 kilograms and took a long time to heat up, and of course you needed electricity at home to use it, which very few people had. Irons with a thermostat, a switch that keeps the temperature steady, arrived in the 1920s, so you no longer had to guess whether you were about to scorch your shirt.","The vacuum cleaner has a famous story. In London around 1901 an engineer named Hubert Cecil Booth watched a demonstration of a machine that tried to clean by blowing dust away, which mostly just moved it around. He wondered whether sucking would work better. To test it, he reportedly put a handkerchief over the seat of a restaurant chair, pressed his mouth to it and sucked hard. When he looked, the cloth was dirty on the underside. Booth patented his cleaner in 1901. His first big machine, nicknamed Puffing Billy, was actually driven by a petrol engine and pulled through the streets by horses. It parked outside a building while long hoses went in through the windows. Booth soon made electric versions, and in the following decades smaller electric vacuum cleaners that one person could push around a room became common.","Refrigerators took longer. Before them, many families had an icebox, a wooden cupboard lined with metal, where a block of ice delivered by the iceman kept food cool for a day or two. Some of the first electric home refrigerators, made by Fred Wolf in Fort Wayne, Indiana, from about 1913, sat on top of an ordinary icebox. Kelvinator and Frigidaire both started selling refrigerators in 1918, but they were very expensive. In 1922 one model cost 714 dollars, more than a Ford Model T car at about 476 dollars. In 1927 General Electric launched the Monitor-Top, with its round machinery perched on top like a hat, for 525 dollars. Over a million were made. Refrigerators meant less shopping, less food going bad, and fewer people falling ill from spoiled food.","Electric washing machines were being advertised in American newspapers by 1904. Many early ones were a tub with an electric motor that swished the clothes about, plus a powered wringer, two rollers that squeezed out the water, which could catch fingers and was quite dangerous. Sales grew fast: about 800,000 electric washers were sold in the United States in 1928. By 1940, about 60 per cent of the 25 million American homes wired for electricity had an electric washing machine. In 1937 the Bendix company introduced the first automatic home washing machine. Early versions had to be bolted to the floor because they shook so much when spinning.","These machines did not get rid of housework, but they changed it. Jobs that had taken a whole day could take an hour or two. Historians point out that standards also rose, so people started washing clothes more often and expecting cleaner homes. Still, electric appliances gave millions of people, especially women, more time and less heavy lifting. They also changed how houses were built, with sockets in every room. In the United States about 70 per cent of homes were electrified by 1930, mainly in towns and cities, while many farms, as we saw in the last part, had to wait longer."]},{"heading":"Voices, Music and Moving Pictures","paragraphs":["Electricity did not only do work. It brought entertainment and news into homes, and it made new kinds of entertainment possible.","The telephone, patented by Alexander Graham Bell in 1876, carried voices as electrical signals along wires. By the early 1900s, telephone wires were strung through every city, and switchboard operators, most of them young women, connected calls by plugging cords into sockets.","Radio came next. Radio waves are the invisible electromagnetic waves first produced by Heinrich Hertz, which we met in Part 6. On the evening of 2 November 1920, the Westinghouse company broadcast the results of the American presidential election from East Pittsburgh. Perhaps a thousand people listened. It was not the only station on the air that night, and historians still argue about which station deserves to be called the first true broadcaster, but the event helped start a radio craze. Within a few years millions of families gathered around radio sets in the evening for news, music, comedy and sport. In Britain the BBC began broadcasting in 1922.","The cinema depended on electricity too. A projector needed a very bright lamp to throw images onto a large screen, and electric motors to run the film smoothly. In 1927 the film The Jazz Singer became famous as one of the first feature films with synchronised talking scenes, using electrical sound recording and loudspeakers. Picture palaces with thousands of seats opened in cities around the world, glowing with electric signs outside.","Television combined many of these ideas. In January 1926 the Scottish inventor John Logie Baird demonstrated a working television system in London, using a spinning disc. Electronic television, using cathode-ray tubes, soon proved better. The BBC began a regular high-definition television service from Alexandra Palace in London in 1936, although only a few thousand people had sets at first. After the Second World War, television spread rapidly, and by the 1960s it had become the centre of the living room in many countries. Every one of these inventions, from the telephone to the television, needed a steady supply of electricity, either from a battery or, increasingly, from the grid."]},{"heading":"The Night Comes Alive","paragraphs":["For all of human history, night had been dark, and dangerous. Gas streetlights had helped, but they were dim. Electric lighting turned city streets into places where people could walk, shop and meet late into the evening.","New York's Broadway became famous for its blazing lights and was nicknamed the Great White Way. In 1904 Longacre Square was renamed Times Square, after the New York Times moved its headquarters there, and about three weeks later the first electric advertisement appeared on a building there. By the 1920s advertisers were spending tens of millions of dollars a year on giant signs that flashed and moved using thousands of bulbs. One huge sign for Wrigley's Spearmint chewing gum, possibly the biggest electric sign of its time, cost 9,000 dollars a month to rent.","Even celebrations changed. In 1904, 1905 and 1906, New Year was marked with fireworks from the roof of the Times building, but city officials stopped them because of the fire danger. So on 31 December 1907 the famous ball drop began instead: a lit ball lowered down a pole at midnight. It still happens every year, though today the ball is covered in LEDs.","Electric light made roads safer, helped police and changed shops, which could now light up their windows for people passing by in the evening. Night life grew, with restaurants, dance halls, cinemas and theatres open late. Sports could be played after dark under floodlights. Shift workers, nurses, bakers and printers could work through the night in good light. Little by little, the old rhythm of going to bed soon after sunset faded away. That brought freedom and fun, but, as later parts of this story show, it also had costs, including the bright glow that now hides the stars from most city dwellers."]},{"heading":"Seeing Inside the Body","paragraphs":["Some of the most important electric inventions were in medicine and science. One of the most amazing came from a German physicist named Wilhelm Röntgen at the University of Würzburg.","On 8 November 1895 Röntgen was experimenting with a glass tube from which most of the air had been pumped out, called a Crookes tube. When a high voltage was applied, an electrical discharge passed through it. He had covered the tube with black cardboard so no light could escape. Yet across the room, a screen painted with a chemical called barium platinocyanide began to glow. Something invisible was coming out of the tube, passing through the cardboard and lighting up the screen. Not knowing what it was, he called it X, the letter scientists use for an unknown, and the name X-rays stuck.","Röntgen worked secretly for weeks to study the rays. He found that they passed through paper, wood and flesh but were blocked by bones and metal. One of the first X-ray photographs he made, dated 22 December 1895, shows the hand of his wife, Anna Bertha, with her bones and her ring clearly visible. His first paper appeared on 28 December 1895. The news spread around the world within weeks, and doctors were soon using X-rays to find broken bones and swallowed objects, and bullets in wounded soldiers. In 1901 Röntgen received the first ever Nobel Prize in Physics. He refused to patent his discovery, because he wanted everyone to be able to benefit from it.","At first nobody realised that X-rays could also be harmful in large doses, and some early researchers were injured before safety rules were developed. Today doctors use them carefully, with protective screens and the smallest dose needed.","Hospitals filled with other electrical equipment too. Electric lights made operating theatres far brighter than gas or candles ever could. In the early 1900s the Dutch physiologist Willem Einthoven built a sensitive instrument that could record the tiny electrical signals of the beating heart, the electrocardiogram or ECG, and he won the Nobel Prize for it in 1924. It is a fitting link back to Galvani's twitching frogs: our bodies really do run on electrical signals. In 1928 the Drinker respirator, an electrically powered iron lung, helped people breathe when their muscles were paralysed. Electricity had become part of saving lives."]},{"heading":"Selling the Electric Dream","paragraphs":["Having electricity in the house was one thing. Persuading people to use it was another. In the early days many homes were wired only for lights, with perhaps one socket, and some families plugged appliances into the light fitting hanging from the ceiling. Electricity was still expensive, and lots of people did not trust it. Would it give you a shock? Would it set the curtains on fire? Was it worth the money?","Power companies had a strong reason to win them over. A power station built for evening lighting sat mostly idle during the day, while its owners still had to pay for the machines. Every iron, fan, washing machine or refrigerator used during daylight hours meant more income from the same equipment. So electricity companies and appliance makers began advertising heavily. Newspapers and magazines filled with pictures of smiling families and spotless kitchens. Showrooms let people try appliances before buying them, and many families paid in small monthly instalments, a system known in Britain as hire purchase.","Some of the most effective campaigners were women engineers. In Britain the Electrical Association for Women was founded in 1924, with the engineer Caroline Haslett as its first director. It ran cookery demonstrations, published advice, and taught women how electricity worked and how to use it safely, arguing that electric power could free women from drudgery. Similar groups and demonstration homes appeared in other countries.","Houses began to be designed differently. Builders added more sockets, wired kitchens for electric cookers, and left space for a refrigerator. Exhibitions showed off all-electric homes where everything, from the kettle to the heating, ran from the mains. By the 1950s and 1960s, in many richer countries, a house without a fridge, a television and a vacuum cleaner was starting to seem old-fashioned. The demand from all these homes in turn pushed power companies to build ever bigger stations and grids, the giant system described in the last part. Homes and the grid grew together, each one driving the other.","Think how much changed in a single lifetime. A child born in 1880 grew up with candles, oil lamps, horse-drawn trams and washing day by hand. If that child lived to 1960, they could ride an electric underground train, take a lift to the top of a skyscraper, store food in a refrigerator, listen to the radio, watch television and have an X-ray at the hospital. Electricity had moved from being a scientific wonder to something people barely noticed, until it stopped working.","And that is the warning at the end of this part. All this power came with hidden costs. Wires could give deadly shocks and start fires. Power stations poured out smoke. And when a grid failed, whole cities could be plunged into sudden darkness. The next part looks at the dark side of the electric age."]}],"deepDive":"Economic historians have long puzzled over a strange delay. Electric motors were available in the 1880s, yet American factory productivity did not jump dramatically until the 1920s. The economist Paul David famously compared this to computers, which spread through offices for years before they clearly raised productivity. His explanation was that simply swapping a steam engine for an electric motor changed little. The real gains came only when factories were redesigned from scratch around unit drive: single-storey buildings, machines placed in the order of the work, better light and ventilation, and flexible layouts. That required new buildings, new managers and new habits, which took a generation. By the time central power stations supplied about half of industrial power in the mid-1920s, the transformation was well under way. Historians also debate the household story. Ruth Schwartz Cowan, in her book More Work for Mother (1983), argued that electric appliances did not reduce the total hours many housewives spent on housework as much as advertisers promised, because expectations of cleanliness rose, servants disappeared from middle-class homes, and tasks once sent out to laundries returned to the home. Other researchers have found that appliances did free up time and helped more women take paid jobs, especially after the Second World War. Both effects can be true at once, and they varied between rich and poor families and between countries. There was also a business logic behind the appliance boom: power stations built for evening lighting stood half idle by day, so utilities promoted daytime loads such as irons, washers and motors to spread their fixed costs, a measure engineers call the load factor. Finally, electrification spread very unevenly. In 1930 about 70 per cent of American homes had electricity, yet most farms did not, and in many parts of the world the electric revolution described in this part did not reach ordinary homes until much later in the twentieth century, or has still not arrived. The changes were real and enormous, but they did not arrive for everyone at the same time.","funFact":"Hubert Cecil Booth's first big vacuum cleaner of 1901, nicknamed Puffing Billy, was not electric at all: it ran on a petrol engine and was pulled through London's streets by horses.","check":{"q":"Which city had the first big successful electric tram system, opened by Frank Sprague in 1888?","a":"Richmond, Virginia","wrong":["London, England","Paris, France","Chicago, Illinois"]},"sources":[{"title":"Richmond Union Passenger Railway","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richmond_Union_Passenger_Railway"},{"title":"Werner von Siemens","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Werner_von_Siemens"},{"title":"City and South London Railway","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_and_South_London_Railway"},{"title":"Elevator","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elevator"},{"title":"Electrification","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrification"},{"title":"Hubert Cecil Booth","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hubert_Cecil_Booth"},{"title":"Refrigerator","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Refrigerator"},{"title":"Wilhelm Röntgen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilhelm_R%C3%B6ntgen"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/16-1.jpg","alt":"Frank J. Sprague, the engineer whose electric streetcars began running in Richmond, Virginia, in 1888.","file":"Frank J. Sprague - NYSPPM 3 126.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frank_J._Sprague_-_NYSPPM_3_126.jpg","author":"Unknown photographer","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/16-2.jpg","alt":"A big wheeled vacuum-cleaner cart from Hubert Booth's British Vacuum Cleaner Company, now in London's Science Museum.","file":"Booth's vacuum cleaner, Science Museum, London.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Booth%27s_vacuum_cleaner,_Science_Museum,_London.jpg","author":"Matt Brown","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/16-3.jpg","alt":"\"Hand with Rings\": Röntgen's 1895 X-ray of his wife Anna Bertha's hand, showing bones and her ring.","file":"First medical X-ray by Wilhelm Röntgen of his wife Anna Bertha Ludwig's hand - 18951222.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_medical_X-ray_by_Wilhelm_R%C3%B6ntgen_of_his_wife_Anna_Bertha_Ludwig%27s_hand_-_18951222.jpg","author":"Wilhelm Röntgen","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3754},{"n":17,"title":"Shocks, Smoke and Blackouts","when":"1880s – today","summary":"Electricity is a wonderful helper, but it can bite. In this part we meet the clever gadgets that stop shocks and fires, visit the nights when whole cities went dark, and see how smoky power stations, glowing skies and missing power lines became problems people are still working to fix.","sections":[{"heading":"A Helper With a Bite","paragraphs":["Imagine New York City in the late 1880s. Look up from almost any busy street corner and you would see a black spider web of wires strung on tall wooden poles: telegraph wires, telephone wires, wires for fire alarms and stock tickers, and, newest of all, the thick wires that carried current for arc lamps. Some poles held dozens of crossbars. Wires sagged, snapped in storms and draped over one another. When an old telegraph wire touched a high-voltage lighting wire, the harmless one could suddenly become deadly.","In 1889 a telegraph repairman named John Feeks climbed a pole in lower Manhattan to work on the tangle and touched a wire that had become live. He died up there among the wires while a crowd watched from the street below. It was one of several deaths that shocked the city, and it pushed New York to finish a job it had already begun: moving electric wires off poles and into tunnels under the streets. It was an early lesson that the new force lighting up cities could also kill.","Why is electricity dangerous at all? Your body is not a good conductor like copper, but it is not a perfect insulator like rubber either. It is mostly salty water, and salty water lets current through. Your nerves and heart also run on tiny electrical signals of their own, as Galvani's twitching frog legs hinted back in the 1780s. When an outside current flows through you, it can jam those signals.","The amount of current matters much more than the voltage alone. Scientists measure it in milliamps, or thousandths of an amp. Roughly speaking, about 1 milliamp flowing through the body is enough to feel as a tingle. Around 10 milliamps can make muscles clench so hard that a person cannot let go of the wire. Somewhere between about 30 and 100 milliamps passing through the chest for a second or so can throw the heart out of its steady rhythm, which can be fatal. A household light bulb uses hundreds of milliamps, so the current in an ordinary wall socket is far more than enough to hurt or kill.","Electricity can also start fires. Any wire carrying current warms up a little, because the moving charge bumps against the atoms of the metal. If too much current flows, for example because too many heaters are plugged into one socket, or because two bare wires touch and make a short circuit (a shortcut with almost no resistance), the wire can glow red-hot and set wood, cloth or plaster alight. In the early years of electric lighting, many buildings had wires simply tacked along wooden walls with thin cloth insulation. Insurance companies were worried, and with good reason.","So, almost from the moment electricity entered buildings, engineers began a second race alongside the race to make better bulbs and bigger power stations: the race to make electricity safe. It is a race that never really finished. Every year, new rules, new tests and new devices make homes a little safer than before."]},{"heading":"Fuses, Breakers and the Third Pin","paragraphs":["The very first safety device was beautifully simple. Engineers noticed that a thin wire heats up faster than a thick one carrying the same current. So why not put a deliberately thin, weak piece of wire into a circuit, on purpose, as a sacrifice? If the current ever grew dangerously large, the weak link would melt first and break the circuit before the main wiring could catch fire. That weak link is called a fuse.","Telegraph engineers were already using little meltable wires and strips of foil to protect their equipment by the 1860s. Edison's lighting system of the 1880s relied on fuses too, and Edison received a patent for a fuse as part of his electrical distribution system in 1890. For about a hundred years, the fuse box was a familiar sight in homes. When a fuse 'blew', the lights went out, and somebody had to find a torch, open the box and fit a new fuse or a fresh piece of fuse wire.","Fuses have a weakness: they can only work once. Worse, a frustrated person might replace a blown fuse with a thicker wire, or even a coin, which defeats the whole point. Engineers wanted a switch that would flip itself off when the current grew too big, and that could simply be switched back on afterwards. This is the circuit breaker. Edison sketched the idea in an 1879 patent application, but the small household breaker really arrived in 1924, when the German company Brown, Boveri and Cie patented a miniature circuit breaker based on the work of the engineer Hugo Stotz.","A modern household breaker has two clever tricks inside. The first is a strip made of two different metals stuck together. When too much current flows for a while, the strip warms up; one metal expands more than the other, so the strip bends, and as it bends it trips a catch that pops the switch open. That handles slow overloads, such as too many heaters on one circuit. The second trick is a little electromagnet, the same invention Sturgeon and Henry built in the 1820s and 1830s. A sudden huge current, such as a short circuit, makes the electromagnet pull hard and snap the switch open almost instantly.","Fuses and breakers protect wires, but they are not sensitive enough to protect people. A breaker might let 20 amps flow before it trips, while a fraction of an amp through the body can be deadly. Two more inventions fill that gap. The first is the earth wire, also called the ground wire. In many plugs it is the third pin. It connects the metal case of an appliance, such as a toaster or washing machine, to the earth. If a loose live wire inside ever touches the case, current rushes down the earth wire instead of waiting for a person to touch the metal, and the large current trips the breaker or blows the fuse.","The second is a device with many names: residual-current device (RCD) in Britain and much of the world, or ground-fault circuit interrupter (GFCI) in North America. It works like a careful accountant. In a healthy circuit, every bit of current that flows out along the live wire comes back along the neutral wire. The device constantly compares the two. If even a few thousandths of an amp are missing, that current must be leaking somewhere it should not, perhaps through a person to the ground, so the device cuts the power in a few hundredths of a second.","Power companies first used such devices in the 1950s to catch people who were stealing electricity by sneaking current past their meters. In South Africa, Henri Rubin built a sensitive version in the mid-1950s, and in 1961 the American professor Charles Dalziel developed a transistorised ground-fault interrupter with the Rucker Manufacturing Company. Today, a typical personal-protection GFCI in the United States trips at about 5 milliamps, and RCDs elsewhere often trip at 30 milliamps. Many lives have been saved by that little test button you may have noticed on bathroom sockets or in the fuse box."]},{"heading":"The Lab Above the Fire Station","paragraphs":["Let's travel to Chicago in 1893. The city is hosting the World's Columbian Exposition, a gigantic fair that, as Part 14 told, was lit by Westinghouse's alternating current. There is an entire Palace of Electricity, packed with glowing lamps and humming machines. All this new wiring makes insurance companies nervous. If one of the fair's dazzling buildings catches fire, who pays?","The Chicago fire insurers sent a young electrical inspector named William Henry Merrill, who had studied electrical engineering at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology and worked as an inspector in Boston, to check the electrical installations. Merrill saw that the world needed something new: a place where products could be tested scientifically before people bought them, so that dangerous designs never reached homes in the first place.","Merrill stayed in Chicago. With about 350 dollars' worth of equipment, he set up a small laboratory on the third floor of a fire insurance patrol station. Funding came from insurance groups. His lab signed its first test report on 24 March 1894. It became known as Underwriters Laboratories, or UL (an underwriter is a person or company that agrees to pay if something goes wrong, which is what insurers do).","UL's testers did things to products that ordinary customers never would. They overloaded wires until they smoked, set fire to materials to see how fast flames spread, and dropped, bent and soaked appliances. Their first published safety standard, in 1903, was actually for tin-clad fire doors rather than for an electrical gadget. In 1906 they started a label service, and those labels grew into the small UL mark you can still find stamped on the plugs and cables of many appliances today.","The safety rules that come out of all this history are simple enough for anyone to remember. Never poke anything into a socket except a proper plug. Keep electrical things away from water. Do not overload sockets with lots of adaptors. Pull plugs out by the plug, not the cable. Stay far away from fallen power lines and never climb electricity pylons or fly kites near overhead wires."]},{"heading":"The Night the Lights Went Out","paragraphs":["It is a cold Tuesday evening, 9 November 1965, just after five o'clock. In New York City, millions of people are leaving work. Trains are full. Office towers glitter. Then, at about 5:27, the lights begin to flicker, dim and die. Within minutes, Manhattan, the Bronx, Queens and most of Brooklyn are dark. Underground, more than 800,000 subway riders are stuck in stopped trains.","The trouble had started about ten minutes earlier and hundreds of kilometres away, at the Sir Adam Beck No. 2 hydroelectric station at Queenston, Ontario, near Niagara Falls. A protective relay, a small automatic switch meant to disconnect a power line if it became dangerously overloaded, had been set to trip at too low a level. When the power flowing through the line crept above that setting, the relay switched the line off, even though nothing was really wrong.","The electricity that had been flowing along that line did not simply vanish. It rushed onto the neighbouring lines, which suddenly carried far more than they were designed for. Their own protective switches tripped too, pushing the load onward again. Like a row of dominoes, the failure spread across the linked-up grid. Within minutes, parts of Ontario and nine American states, covering about 80,000 square miles (roughly 200,000 square kilometres), had lost power. More than 30 million people were in the dark, some of them for up to 13 hours.","What happened next surprised many people. New Yorkers mostly stayed calm. People directed traffic with torches, strangers shared candles and sandwiches, and the full moon helped light the streets. New York police received only five reports of looting, the lowest crime count for any night in the city's records. In Syracuse, a hospital's backup generator failed to start, and surgeons finished operations by the light of flashlights. Newspapers later claimed that a baby boom followed nine months later, but careful studies found no such thing; it is a popular myth.","Yet blackouts kept happening, because grids kept growing. On the hot afternoon of 14 August 2003, just after 4:10, an even bigger blackout struck the north-eastern United States and Ontario. This time the chain began in Ohio. Power lines carrying heavy summer loads warmed up, stretched and sagged into trees that had not been trimmed, and each touch tripped the line off. At the same time, a software bug had silently frozen the alarm system in the control room of the power company FirstEnergy, so operators did not realise for over an hour that their lines were failing.","Without warnings, nobody acted in time. The failure cascaded across eight states and Ontario, and about 50 to 55 million people lost power. Hundreds of generating units at around 265 power plants shut down to protect themselves. Water pumps stopped, so Detroit had to tell about 4 million people to boil their drinking water. Some areas got power back in a couple of hours; parts of New York City and Toronto waited until 16 August. After investigations, the United States made its grid reliability rules compulsory instead of voluntary, with fines for companies that broke them."]},{"heading":"Smoke Over the City","paragraphs":["Most of the electricity of the twentieth century came from burning coal. A coal power station works like a giant kettle: coal burns in a furnace, the heat boils water into high-pressure steam, the steam spins a turbine like the ones Charles Parsons invented (Part 15), and the turbine turns a generator that uses Faraday's induction to make current. It is a brilliant system, but the smoke from all that burning coal has to go somewhere.","In December 1952, London found out what happens when the wind stops. On 5 December, a large area of high air pressure settled over the city. The air went still, and a layer of warmer air sat on top of colder air near the ground like a lid on a pot. Weather scientists call this a temperature inversion. It was bitterly cold, so Londoners piled more coal on their home fires, and much of the coal sold for homes after the Second World War was cheap, low-grade and full of sulfur. Coal-burning power stations inside the city, such as Battersea and Bankside, added their smoke, and so did factories, steam trains and buses.","With no wind to carry it away, the smoke mixed with the fog and built up day after day. The Met Office later estimated that about 1,000 tonnes of smoke particles poured into the air each day, along with hundreds of tonnes of sulfur dioxide, a choking gas. The fog turned a dirty yellowish colour. Londoners called this kind of fog a 'pea-souper'. In some places people could see only about 30 centimetres in front of them. Buses, trams and taxis stopped running; only the Underground railway kept going, and ambulances struggled to find their way. The smog crept indoors, and cinemas and concerts were cancelled because audiences could no longer see the screen or stage.","The smog lifted on 9 December, but the damage was done. Thousands of people, especially the elderly, babies and those with weak lungs or hearts, became seriously ill. The government's estimate at the time was about 4,000 extra deaths and around 100,000 people made ill. Later researchers, studying hospital and death records for the weeks and months afterwards, have suggested the true number of deaths may have been 10,000 to 12,000. It is remembered as the worst air pollution disaster in British history.","The Great Smog changed the law. Britain's Clean Air Act of 1956 created smoke-free zones in cities and helped families switch from open coal fires to cleaner fuels such as gas. New power stations were built farther from city centres. Both of those famous London power stations were eventually closed: Bankside became the Tate Modern art gallery, and Battersea, with its four white chimneys, became shops, offices and flats.","Moving smoke away from cities created a new problem farther away. Sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from tall chimneys drift for hundreds of kilometres. Up in the clouds they react with water to form weak sulfuric and nitric acid, which falls as acid rain. The Scottish chemist Robert Angus Smith noticed acidic rain around smoky Manchester as early as 1852 and coined the term 'acid rain' in 1872. In the 1960s and 1970s, scientists found that lakes in Scandinavia, North America and elsewhere were becoming so acidic that fish could no longer breed; most fish eggs fail to hatch when the water drops below a pH of about 5. Forests on mountain tops were damaged, and stone buildings and statues slowly dissolved.","Coal-fired power plants were found to produce about two-thirds of the sulfur dioxide in the air, so they became the target. Engineers fitted 'scrubbers', huge towers that spray a mixture of water and limestone through the exhaust gas so that the sulfur is captured before it reaches the chimney. In the United States, the 1990 Clean Air Act amendments set a total limit on sulfur dioxide and let companies buy and sell permission to pollute below that cap. Sulfur pollution fell sharply in the decades that followed, and many lakes and forests have slowly begun to recover. Acid rain is one of the best examples of an environmental problem that people noticed, measured and largely fixed."]},{"heading":"The Invisible Gas and a Warming World","paragraphs":["Scrubbers can catch sulfur, and filters can catch soot. But there is one product of burning coal, oil and gas that no simple filter removes: carbon dioxide, or CO2. Every atom of carbon in the fuel joins with two atoms of oxygen from the air as it burns. Burning a tonne of coal produces more than two tonnes of carbon dioxide, because the oxygen adds so much weight. This invisible gas is not poisonous at the levels in the air, which is why for most of history nobody worried about it.","Carbon dioxide does have one important property, though. It lets sunlight pass through on the way in but traps some of the heat that the Earth gives off on the way out, a bit like a blanket. This is called the greenhouse effect. A natural amount of it keeps our planet warm enough to live on. But since the 1800s, people have been adding huge amounts of extra carbon dioxide to the air, mostly from burning fossil fuels, and the Earth has been warming as a result. Scientists measure the effects in hotter summers, melting glaciers, rising sea levels and changing weather patterns.","Electricity is a big part of that story. Studies of where greenhouse gases come from find that making electricity and heat is the largest single source among all human activities. In 2024, coal still produced about a third of the world's electricity, more than any other single source. Coal is also the most carbon-heavy way to make power. Figures from American power plants found that coal released roughly 1 kilogram of CO2 for every kilowatt-hour of electricity, about twice as much as natural gas.","Notice something important here, though. The bulb itself does not burn anything and makes no smoke. The pollution happens back at the power station. That means the same lamp can be cleaner or dirtier depending on where its electricity comes from. Powered by wind, sunlight, water or nuclear energy, it releases almost no carbon dioxide while it runs. This idea, that the plug is only as clean as the power station behind it, will matter a great deal in the final parts of this Deep Dive."]},{"heading":"Where Did the Stars Go?","paragraphs":["There is another kind of pollution caused by electricity, and you can see it every clear night, or rather, you can see what it hides. Look up from a big city and you might spot the Moon, a few bright planets and a handful of the brightest stars. The rest of the sky glows a dull orange or grey. That glow is light pollution: artificial light shining where it is not needed, and especially upward into the sky, where it scatters off dust and water droplets in the air and makes a glowing dome over towns called skyglow.","A team of scientists made a world atlas of night-sky brightness in 2016 using satellite measurements. They found that more than 80 percent of the world's people live under light-polluted skies. About one-third of humanity can no longer see the Milky Way, the faint, shimmering band of billions of stars that every human ancestor could see on a dark night. That includes about 80 percent of North Americans and around 60 percent of Europeans. In Singapore, the most light-polluted country studied, the whole population lives under skies so bright that night never becomes truly dark.","People are not the only ones affected. Many animals use the natural pattern of light and dark to know what to do. Baby sea turtles hatch on beaches at night and find the sea by crawling toward the brightest horizon, which on a natural beach is the moonlit and starlit ocean. Bright hotels, roads and streetlights behind the beach can lure them the wrong way, toward danger, instead of toward the waves.","Migrating birds, which often fly at night and navigate partly by the stars, can be drawn toward brightly lit towers, offshore platforms and city skyscrapers, where many crash into glass or circle until they are exhausted. Some cities now run 'lights out' campaigns during migration seasons. Insects are attracted to lamps in vast numbers and die there, which affects the bats and birds that eat them, and the night-blooming plants that rely on moths to carry their pollen. Light at night can also upset human sleep, because our bodies use darkness as a signal that it is time to rest.","The good news is that light pollution is one of the easiest kinds of pollution to fix: when you switch a light off, the pollution disappears instantly. Lights can be shielded so they shine only downward, dimmed late at night, and switched on only when someone is near. France cut its light pollution substantially over a few years with national rules. Around the world, some places have been named dark-sky parks and reserves, where lighting is controlled so visitors can see the stars as people did centuries ago."]},{"heading":"Still Waiting for the Switch","paragraphs":["So far this part has been about the problems of having too much electricity. But for many people on Earth, the problem is having none at all. Picture a village far from any power line. When the sun sets at about six o'clock, darkness falls. A family might light a kerosene lamp, the same kind of smoky flame from Part 1, or use a candle or a cheap torch. Children try to do their homework in that dim, flickering glow. A clinic cannot keep vaccines cold in a refrigerator, and a nurse might deliver a baby by the light of a phone screen.","There has been real progress. In 1998, more than 1.5 billion people in the world had no electricity at home. By 2015 that number had fallen below 1 billion, and it kept falling. In the early 2020s, estimates put the number of people still without electricity at somewhere around 700 to 760 million, depending on the year and how access is counted. That is still roughly one person in every ten or eleven on the planet.","Most of them live in sub-Saharan Africa, which is home to about three-quarters of all the people in the world without power. There, the share of people with electricity has been rising, but for many years the population grew so fast that the total number of people without power kept going up anyway. In some countries, only a small fraction of schools have electricity at all, which makes it much harder to use computers, run lights for evening classes or pump clean water.","Today, some of the fastest progress comes from small solar panels on roofs and village-sized 'mini-grids', which can bring light to places that big power lines may not reach for years. Combined with the efficient lamps you will meet in the next part, a single small solar panel can now light a home, charge a phone and run a radio.","Shocks, fires, blackouts, smog, acid rain, rising carbon dioxide, glowing skies and the unfair gap between those with power and those without: electricity has brought plenty of problems along with its gifts. But engineers and scientists have been fighting back on two fronts. On one front, they have been inventing lights that give far more brightness for far less energy. On the other, they have been finding cleaner ways to make power in the first place. The next part begins the story of the first front, with a strange red glow seen at a Paris motor show in 1910."]}],"deepDive":"Why does one tripped line sometimes black out a whole region? A power grid must keep supply and demand balanced every instant, because it has almost no storage of its own. Its 'heartbeat' is the frequency, 50 or 60 cycles per second. If demand suddenly exceeds supply, the huge spinning generators slow slightly and the frequency drops; if supply exceeds demand, they speed up. Protective systems watch for dangerous frequencies, voltages and currents and disconnect equipment to stop it being damaged. That protection is essential, but it creates the risk of a cascade: when one line or generator drops out, its load shifts instantly to its neighbours, which may then overload and trip, shifting the load again. Investigations into the 1965 and 2003 blackouts both found that the first failures were small (a mis-set relay; lines sagging into trees plus a frozen alarm system) and that the disaster grew because operators lacked a clear picture of the whole system. Modern grids use wide-area monitoring that measures conditions many times a second, plans for the loss of any single major component, and deliberately disconnects some customers ('load shedding') to save the rest. Historians and epidemiologists still debate the true toll of the 1952 London smog. The official figure of about 4,000 deaths counted only the weeks around the event; later analyses of excess deaths through early 1953 suggested 10,000 to 12,000, although officials at the time blamed part of the later rise on an influenza outbreak, a claim many researchers now doubt. Similar debates surround air pollution today: because fine particles harm health slowly, death tolls are estimated statistically rather than counted one by one, so honest estimates come with wide ranges. Measurements of light pollution have their own quirk: many satellites cannot detect the blue part of the light from white LEDs well, so ground observations suggest skyglow may be rising faster than satellite records show. Finally, the access figures for electricity also depend on definitions: does a household with a small solar kit that runs two lamps count as 'having electricity'? International agencies now use tiered measures that record how much power a home has, for how many hours a day, and how reliably.","funFact":"During the great Northeast blackout of 9 November 1965, New York police recorded only five reports of looting, the lowest crime count for any night in the city's records.","check":{"q":"What small fault started the great Northeast blackout of 9 November 1965?","a":"A wrongly set protective relay at a hydroelectric station near Niagara Falls tripped a power line","wrong":["A lightning strike on the Empire State Building","A squirrel chewing through a cable in Manhattan","Edison's old Pearl Street dynamos breaking down"]},"sources":[{"title":"Northeast blackout of 1965","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northeast_blackout_of_1965"},{"title":"Northeast blackout of 2003","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northeast_blackout_of_2003"},{"title":"Great Smog of London","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Smog_of_London"},{"title":"UL (safety organization)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UL_(safety_organization)"},{"title":"Residual-current device","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Residual-current_device"},{"title":"Acid rain","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acid_rain"},{"title":"Light pollution","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light_pollution"},{"title":"Access to Energy","publisher":"Our World in Data","url":"https://ourworldindata.org/energy-access"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/17-1.jpg","alt":"Nelson's Column in London fading into thick smog in December 1952.","file":"Nelson's Column during the Great Smog of 1952.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nelson%27s_Column_during_the_Great_Smog_of_1952.jpg","author":"N T Stobbs","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/17-2.jpg","alt":"Crowds of New Yorkers walk home along a street near the Queensboro Bridge during the big 2003 blackout, when subways stopped running.","file":"2003 New York City blackout.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2003_New_York_City_blackout.jpg","author":"User:Glitch010101","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/17-3.jpg","alt":"A NASA satellite view of Britain, Ireland, France and their neighbours at night, glowing with city lights.","file":"Northwestern Europe at night by VIIRS.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northwestern_Europe_at_night_by_VIIRS.jpg","author":"NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4358},{"n":18,"title":"Beyond the Filament: From Neon to LED","when":"1907 – today","summary":"Old light bulbs were really tiny heaters that happened to glow. In this part, inventors find new ways to make light: glowing gases in neon signs, buzzing tubes on ceilings, curly bulbs, and finally the LED, a crystal smaller than a grain of rice that shines without getting hot. The last piece of the puzzle was a bright blue light that took scientists nearly thirty years to make.","sections":[{"heading":"A Heater That Happens to Glow","paragraphs":["Hold your hand near an old-fashioned incandescent bulb that has been on for a few minutes, and you will feel the problem straight away. It is hot. As Part 11 explained, the bulb makes light by heating a thin tungsten filament to around 2,500 °C, and most of the energy comes out as invisible heat rather than visible light. Roughly nine-tenths of the electricity going into a classic bulb is wasted as heat.","Lighting engineers measure how good a lamp is with a unit called the lumen, which is the amount of visible light it gives out. Divide the lumens by the watts of electricity it uses, and you get its efficacy. A typical household incandescent bulb manages only about 15 or 16 lumens per watt. In Part 17 we saw that every watt wasted at home means more coal, oil or gas burned at a power station somewhere. So for more than a hundred years, inventors searched for a way to make light without making a red-hot wire first.","There turned out to be another way. Instead of heating something until it glows, you can give energy directly to atoms or electrons and let them release it as light. When an electron in an atom is given extra energy, it jumps to a higher 'energy level', a bit like a ball kicked up onto a higher step. When it drops back down, it gives out the extra energy as a tiny packet of light called a photon. The size of the drop decides the colour of the light: small drops make red light, bigger drops make green, and bigger ones still make blue or violet.","This kind of light is sometimes called 'cold light', because the thing that glows does not have to be hot. You have seen it in nature. Fireflies make it with chemicals, and the northern lights glow when fast particles from the Sun crash into oxygen and nitrogen high in the sky. In this part we follow three families of 'cold light' that humans learned to make with electricity: glowing gases, glowing powders and glowing crystals."]},{"heading":"Georges Claude and the Red Glow of Paris","paragraphs":["Scientists had been making gases glow since the 1850s. Glassblowers such as Heinrich Geissler in Germany sealed a little gas inside a glass tube, pumped out most of the air, and fixed a metal electrode at each end. When a high voltage was connected, the gas inside lit up with an eerie glow. These Geissler tubes were beautiful toys for laboratories and lecture halls, but they were too dim and short-lived for real lighting.","In 1898, the British chemists William Ramsay and Morris Travers discovered a new gas hiding in the air. They separated it by cooling air until it turned liquid and then letting the different parts boil off one at a time. When they put a sample in a glowing tube, it blazed a brilliant red-orange. They named it neon, from the Greek word for 'new'.","Neon was rare and expensive, until a French engineer named Georges Claude came along. In 1902 Claude invented an improved way of turning air into liquid on an industrial scale, so that oxygen and nitrogen could be sold to factories and hospitals. He and his partner Paul Delorme founded a company called Air Liquide, which still exists today. Separating huge amounts of air left Claude with a by-product: small amounts of neon. What could he do with it?","Claude found the answer by building long glass tubes filled with neon at low pressure, with specially designed electrodes that would last a long time. At the Paris Motor Show in December 1910, he showed two huge neon tubes glowing red. Visitors had never seen light like it: bright, colourful and strangely calm, with no flame and no hot filament. Claude soon realised the best use was not lighting rooms but advertising. A glass tube could be heated and bent into letters, words and pictures.","How does a neon tube work? The high voltage pulls electrons from the electrodes and speeds them along the tube. They crash into neon atoms, kicking the atoms' own electrons up to higher energy levels. When those electrons fall back down, they release photons of red-orange light, because that is the colour of neon's particular energy steps. Other gases have different steps and so different colours. Mercury vapour glows blue, helium a pinkish yellow, and argon a pale lavender. Sign makers also learned to coat the inside of tubes with glowing powders to make even more colours, although people still call almost all such signs 'neon'.","Claude patented his design, and his company, Claude Neon, sold signs around the world. In 1923 a car dealer named Earle C. Anthony bought two neon signs from Claude for his Packard showroom in Los Angeles. People said they literally stopped traffic, as drivers slowed down to stare. Within a few years, neon spread across American cities, and later it became the trademark of places such as Las Vegas, with its giant glowing cowboys and flashing arrows. Claude's main patent ran out in 1932, and then many companies could make neon signs.","Claude's own later life was less happy. He was a brilliant inventor who also tried to make electricity from the temperature difference in the ocean, but during the Second World War he publicly supported France's collaboration with Nazi Germany, and afterwards he was sent to prison. Like several people in this story, he is remembered for both his inventions and his mistakes."]},{"heading":"The Buzzing Tube on the Ceiling","paragraphs":["Neon signs are wonderful for shouting 'OPEN' or 'HOTEL', but red light is no good for reading a book. To light offices, schools and kitchens, engineers needed a glowing gas that made white light. The answer came from a gas you might not expect: mercury, the silvery liquid metal once used in thermometers.","In 1901, the American inventor Peter Cooper Hewitt patented a lamp that sent current through mercury vapour. It was bright and efficient, but its light was a ghostly blue-green that made people's faces look ill. The reason is that mercury vapour gives off much of its energy as ultraviolet light, which our eyes cannot see, and only a little as visible blue and green.","The clever trick was to use that invisible ultraviolet. Certain powders, called phosphors, absorb ultraviolet light and glow with visible light of their own, a process called fluorescence. You may have seen it under a 'black light' at a party, where white shirts glow bright blue. Way back in 1859, Edmond Becquerel had experimented with coating gas tubes with glowing materials. In 1926 the Frenchman Jacques Risler patented fluorescent coatings on neon tubes. Then, in 1927, three German inventors, Friedrich Meyer, Hans Spanner and Edmund Germer, invented a low-pressure metal-vapour lamp along these lines, although their own design was never made in factories.","General Electric in the United States, which already owned other key patents, put all the pieces together. A fluorescent tube is a long glass tube coated on the inside with phosphor powder and filled with a little mercury vapour and argon gas. Electrons flowing along the tube excite the mercury atoms, which give off ultraviolet light; the phosphor coating turns that ultraviolet into white light. GE began selling fluorescent lamps in 1938, and the next year it paid 180,000 dollars for the Meyer, Spanner and Germer patent application, to protect its position.","A fluorescent tube needs a helper called a ballast. Once the gas inside starts conducting, it lets more and more current through, and without something to limit it the lamp would destroy itself in a moment. Old ballasts were heavy coils of wire on iron, which is why older fluorescent lights often buzzed and flickered as they started up.","Even with their buzz, fluorescent tubes were a giant improvement. They gave about 50 to 100 lumens per watt, several times better than an incandescent bulb, and lasted many thousands of hours. During and after the Second World War they spread through factories, offices, schools, hospitals and shops. If you have ever sat in a classroom under long white light panels, you have probably studied by fluorescent light."]},{"heading":"Twisting the Tube: The Curly Bulb","paragraphs":["There was one place fluorescent tubes did not fit: the ordinary lamp socket at home. A tube more than a metre long will not go into a bedside lamp. In 1973, an oil crisis made energy suddenly expensive in many countries, and engineers began asking whether a fluorescent lamp could be squeezed down to the size of a normal bulb.","At General Electric's lighting laboratory in Ohio, an engineer named Edward Hammer had a neat idea. Instead of folding a tube, why not twist it into a spiral, like a curly pasta shape or a soft-serve ice cream? In 1976 he built a spiral compact fluorescent lamp, or CFL, that could screw into a normal socket. It worked. But GE decided not to sell it: building new factories to make it would have cost about 25 million dollars, and coating the inside of a twisted tube evenly with phosphor was difficult. Hammer's original prototype is now kept by the Smithsonian Institution in Washington, D.C.","Other companies chased the same goal. In 1980, Philips sold a compact fluorescent lamp that screwed into an ordinary socket, but it was big, heavy, flickered noticeably and took about three minutes to warm up to full brightness. In 1985 Osram brought out a CFL with an electronic ballast, which removed much of the flicker and slow starting. From about 1995, companies started making and selling spiral CFLs very like Hammer's design.","A CFL used about one-quarter to one-third of the electricity of an incandescent bulb giving the same light, and lasted roughly 6,000 to 15,000 hours instead of about 1,000. Over its life, it could save its owner far more in electricity than it cost to buy. Millions of homes switched over in the 1990s and 2000s.","CFLs had drawbacks, though. Many took a while to reach full brightness, some could not be dimmed, and their light could look harsh. Each one also contained a few milligrams of mercury, a poisonous metal, so broken or old CFLs had to be handled and recycled carefully. Even so, where electricity came from coal, which also releases mercury, a CFL could lead to less mercury pollution overall by saving so much power. CFLs turned out to be a bridge: an important improvement that would soon be overtaken by something much better."]},{"heading":"A Crystal That Shines: How an LED Works","paragraphs":["The 'something much better' is the LED, short for light-emitting diode. To understand it, we need a special kind of material called a semiconductor. Copper is a conductor: its electrons move easily. Rubber is an insulator: its electrons stay put. A semiconductor, such as silicon or gallium arsenide, sits in between, and scientists can control exactly how well it conducts by adding tiny traces of other elements, a process called doping.","Doping can make two different kinds of semiconductor. One kind, called n-type, has a few spare electrons that are free to wander (n for negative). The other kind, called p-type, is missing some electrons. Each missing electron leaves a gap called a 'hole', and holes behave as if they were little positive charges that can move around too (p for positive). A diode is made by joining a p-type layer to an n-type layer. The boundary between them is called the junction.","Connect a battery the right way round, and electrons are pushed from the n-side toward the junction while holes are pushed from the p-side toward it. At the junction, an electron meets a hole and drops into it. Remember the ball falling down a step? The electron falls from a higher energy level to a lower one, and in the right materials it releases the energy it loses as a photon of light. Millions upon millions of these tiny drops every second make a steady glow.","The height of the step is called the band gap, and it is fixed by the material the crystal is made from. That is why each LED shines in one particular colour: gallium arsenide gives invisible infrared, gallium arsenide phosphide gives red, and gallium nitride, as we will see, gives blue. There is no filament to heat up and burn out, nothing to break except a tiny chip, and very little energy is wasted as heat. An LED chip is often smaller than a grain of rice, and it lights up instantly when switched on."]},{"heading":"From Carborundum to Little Red Dots","paragraphs":["The first person known to have seen this effect was an English radio engineer named Henry Joseph Round. In 1907, while working for Guglielmo Marconi's wireless company, he was testing crystals of carborundum (silicon carbide) used to detect radio signals. When he passed current through one, he noticed it gave off a faint yellowish glow. He wrote a short note about it in the magazine Electrical World. It is the first known report of light from what we would now call a light-emitting diode, but Round was busy with radio and never followed it up.","In the 1920s, a young, largely self-taught Russian technician named Oleg Losev, working at a radio laboratory in Nizhny Novgorod, studied the same glow much more carefully. Around 1924 he saw light coming from carborundum crystal contacts and published his results from 1927 onwards. He argued that the glow was not caused by heat, and he even linked it to the new science of quantum physics. Losev's work was largely forgotten for decades. Sadly, he died of starvation in January 1942, aged 38, during the siege of Leningrad in the Second World War.","The modern LED was born in the early 1960s, when companies were exploring new semiconductors. In 1962, engineers at Texas Instruments patented a gallium arsenide diode that gave off strong infrared light, the invisible kind used today in television remote controls.","The same year, on 9 October 1962, Nick Holonyak Jr., a 33-year-old scientist at General Electric's laboratory near Syracuse, New York, showed a device made of gallium arsenide phosphide that glowed visibly red. At very low temperatures it worked as a laser; at room temperature it shone as a red LED, the first practical visible one. Holonyak had been the very first graduate student of John Bardeen, one of the inventors of the transistor. In 1963 he told the magazine Reader's Digest that his kind of light would one day replace the ordinary light bulb. Many people found that hard to believe. It took about fifty years, but he was right.","Early red LEDs were dim and very expensive. In the 1970s, companies such as Monsanto and Hewlett-Packard found ways to make them cheaply, for less than five cents each. Soon little red dots and glowing red numbers appeared everywhere: on pocket calculators, digital watches, clock radios, and the 'on' lights of televisions and stereos. Scientists soon made yellow, orange and green LEDs too. But one colour stubbornly refused to appear brightly, and without it, LEDs could never make white light."]},{"heading":"The Great Blue Puzzle","paragraphs":["Why did blue matter so much? Our eyes see white when red, green and blue light are mixed together. Without a bright blue LED, there could be no white LED lamps and no full-colour LED screens. But blue light needs a big energy drop, a wide band gap, and the materials that had one were extremely hard to make into good crystals. Through the 1970s and 1980s, many big companies tried and gave up.","The most promising material was gallium nitride. It was so difficult to grow as a smooth, perfect crystal that most researchers abandoned it. Isamu Akasaki, a Japanese scientist, did not. He began working on gallium nitride in the late 1960s at the Matsushita research institute in Tokyo and carried on after moving to Nagoya University in 1981. There, with his young student Hiroshi Amano, he spent years growing crystals on slices of sapphire. In 1985 they found the trick of first laying down a thin 'buffer layer' grown at a lower temperature, which let a high-quality gallium nitride crystal form on top.","Next came an even harder problem. Gallium nitride naturally behaved as n-type, but an LED also needs a p-type layer. In 1989, Akasaki and Amano managed to make p-type gallium nitride by adding magnesium and then treating the crystal with a beam of electrons. They had made the first gallium nitride LED with a proper p-n junction, glowing in blue and ultraviolet.","Meanwhile, in the small city of Tokushima on the island of Shikoku, another researcher was attacking the same problem almost alone. Shuji Nakamura worked for Nichia, a modest chemical company that made phosphors. With a small budget, he built and modified his own crystal-growing equipment. He found a simpler way to make p-type gallium nitride, by heating the crystals, that was suitable for mass production, and he made layers containing indium to tune the colour. In 1993 Nichia began producing the first commercial high-brightness blue LEDs, and the world's lighting companies were astonished.","Nakamura later said his company had paid him a bonus of only 20,000 yen, about 180 dollars, for his key patent. He sued Nichia, and in 2005 the two sides settled for about 840 million yen, then the largest payment a Japanese company had made to an employee for an invention. By then Nakamura had moved to the University of California, Santa Barbara, where he still works. His blue-violet semiconductor lasers, developed from the same materials, later made Blu-ray discs possible.","In 2014, Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura shared the Nobel Prize in Physics 'for the invention of efficient blue light-emitting diodes which has enabled bright and energy-saving white light sources'. It was unusual for a Nobel Prize to honour something so practical, sitting in billions of homes, but the committee felt it was a gift to the whole world.","How does a blue LED make white light? Most white LED bulbs contain blue LED chips covered with a layer of yellow phosphor, the same kind of glowing powder used in fluorescent tubes. Some of the blue light passes straight through, and the rest is turned into yellow. Blue mixed with yellow looks white to our eyes. Changing the phosphor recipe makes the light 'warm white' like an old bulb or 'cool white' like daylight. Screens on phones and televisions use red, green and blue in tiny dots instead."]},{"heading":"Goodbye, Old Bulb","paragraphs":["Once efficient white LEDs existed, the old incandescent bulb's days were numbered. Governments saw a way to save enormous amounts of electricity. Cuba replaced its bulbs and banned their sale in 2006 and 2007. Australia stopped shops selling ordinary incandescent bulbs from November 2009. The European Union began phasing them out in September 2009, starting with frosted bulbs and the brightest 100-watt clear bulbs, then lower wattages over the next three years. China began restricting them in 2012, Brazil fully banned them by 2016, and in the United States the sale of most ordinary incandescent bulbs ended on 1 August 2023.","The numbers explain why. Good LED lamps today can produce well over 100 lumens per watt, with some reaching 150 or more, compared with about 15 for an incandescent bulb. That means an LED needs roughly a fifth or less of the electricity to give the same light, using around 75 to 85 percent less energy. A good white LED can be expected to last 30,000 to 50,000 hours, compared with about 1,000 hours for an old bulb. Leave one on for eight hours every day, and it could keep going for more than ten years.","LEDs changed far more than bedside lamps. Traffic lights, car headlights, bicycle lights, torches, stadium floodlights, Christmas lights, aquarium lights and the screens of phones, laptops and giant stadium scoreboards all use them. Because they are tiny and work well from batteries, LEDs combined with small solar panels can now bring safe, smokeless light to homes far from any power line, replacing kerosene lamps, just as Part 17 described.","There is a twist, though. Because LED light is so cheap to run, some places simply use more of it, lighting buildings, signs and car parks all night that were once dark. And the bluish-white light of some LED streetlights scatters strongly in the sky, which can make skyglow worse. Many towns now choose warmer-coloured LEDs, shield them so the light points down, and dim them late at night. A better bulb is a powerful tool, but people still have to decide how to use it.","Look back over this part and you can see a hundred-year journey: from Geissler's flickering tubes to Claude's red signs, from Hewitt's ghostly mercury lamp to fluorescent tubes and Hammer's curly bulb, from Round's faint yellow glow to Holonyak's red dot and the blue LEDs of Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura. Each step gave more light for less energy. Yet even the most efficient lamp in the world still needs electricity from somewhere. A better bulb is only half the answer. The other half is where the electricity comes from, and that is where we go next: to the power of falling water, blowing wind and shining sunlight."]}],"deepDive":"The colour of an LED comes straight from quantum physics. The energy of a photon (E) is linked to its wavelength (λ) by E = hc/λ, where h is Planck's constant and c the speed of light. In convenient units, the wavelength in nanometres is about 1,240 divided by the energy in electronvolts. Gallium arsenide has a band gap of about 1.4 eV, giving infrared light near 870 nm. Gallium nitride's gap is about 3.4 eV, which corresponds to ultraviolet near 365 nm; mixing in indium narrows the gap so that indium gallium nitride emits blue around 450 nm, the colour used in most white LEDs. That wide gap is exactly why blue was hard. Gallium nitride crystals grown on sapphire were full of defects, because the two materials' atoms are spaced differently, and making the material p-type was blocked by hydrogen atoms that bonded to the magnesium dopant. Akasaki and Amano's low-temperature buffer layer and electron-beam treatment, and Nakamura's discovery that simple heating (annealing) drives out the hydrogen, solved these problems. Remarkably, gallium nitride LEDs shine brightly even though their crystals still contain huge numbers of defects, which would ruin many other semiconductors; scientists still debate exactly why, with one leading explanation involving tiny indium-rich regions that trap electrons and holes before they can reach the defects. Efficiency figures need care. 'Efficacy' in lumens per watt depends on the human eye, which is most sensitive to green-yellow light, so the theoretical maximum for good-quality white light is roughly 250 to 350 lm/W, not infinite. The US Department of Energy has set long-term targets in this range for LED packages. Economists also debate the 'rebound effect' (sometimes called Jevons' paradox): when light becomes cheaper, people may use more of it, so total energy savings can be smaller than the efficiency gain suggests. Historical studies show that the price of a given amount of light has fallen enormously since the days of candles, and the amount of light people use has risen dramatically alongside it. Even so, most studies find that switching to LEDs still produces large overall savings in electricity used for lighting.","funFact":"Shuji Nakamura said his employer originally paid him a bonus of only 20,000 yen, about 180 US dollars, for the blue LED patent that later helped win him a Nobel Prize.","check":{"q":"Why was the bright blue LED so important for lighting?","a":"Blue light mixed with a yellow phosphor (or with red and green) makes white light, so it made white LED lamps possible","wrong":["Blue light is the only colour that does not produce any heat","Blue LEDs were needed to make neon signs glow red","Blue light lets electricity travel along wires without resistance"]},"sources":[{"title":"Neon lighting","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neon_lighting"},{"title":"Fluorescent lamp","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fluorescent_lamp"},{"title":"Compact fluorescent lamp","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_fluorescent_lamp"},{"title":"Light-emitting diode","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light-emitting_diode"},{"title":"Nick Holonyak","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nick_Holonyak"},{"title":"Isamu Akasaki","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isamu_Akasaki"},{"title":"Shuji Nakamura","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuji_Nakamura"},{"title":"LED Basics","publisher":"US Department of Energy","url":"https://www.energy.gov/eere/ssl/led-basics"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/18-1.jpg","alt":"\"Vegas Vic\", a famous giant neon cowboy sign, glowing over Fremont Street in Las Vegas.","file":"Fremont Street 4.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fremont_Street_4.jpg","author":"Antoine Taveneaux","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/18-2.jpg","alt":"A glowing spiral compact fluorescent lamp (CFL), which uses far less electricity than an old filament bulb.","file":"Spiral fluorescent lamp.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spiral_fluorescent_lamp.jpg","author":"Alphax","license":"CC BY 2.5"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/18-3.jpg","alt":"Three light-emitting diodes glowing red, green and blue.","file":"RBG-LED.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RBG-LED.jpg","author":"PiccoloNamek","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":3859},{"n":19,"title":"Power from Sun, Wind and Water","when":"1839 – today","summary":"Rivers, wind and sunshine can all spin or push electricity into our wires without burning anything. Giant dams, windmills taller than skyscrapers and shiny solar panels now make almost a third of the world's electricity. Even Edison's old direct current is back, racing through cables under the sea!","sections":[{"heading":"Falling Water: From a Lamp at Cragside to the Three Gorges","paragraphs":["Every power station you have met in this story so far has needed something to turn its generator. Faraday showed that if you spin a coil of wire near a magnet, electricity flows. The hard question has always been: what does the spinning? At Pearl Street it was steam from burning coal. But there is another, much older muscle on our planet that never needs to be dug out of the ground: falling water.","Water wheels had ground grain and hammered iron for well over a thousand years before anyone attached one to a dynamo. Then, in 1878, the inventor and industrialist William Armstrong used water from the lakes on his estate at Cragside in Northumberland to drive a small turbine, which powered a single arc lamp in his picture gallery. It is usually counted as the world's first hydroelectric scheme. A few years later, in 1882, a little plant on the Fox River in Appleton, Wisconsin began making about 12.5 kilowatts, enough for a handful of buildings. You met both of these pioneers earlier in our story, and they were tiny. The next step was enormous.","At Niagara Falls, where the whole of four Great Lakes drains over a cliff, engineers built the Edward Dean Adams power station. It began running on 25 August 1895 with Westinghouse alternating-current generators, each rated at 5,000 horsepower, designed using ideas from Nikola Tesla and the Westinghouse engineer Benjamin Lamme. Transformers raised the voltage to about 20,000 volts so the power could travel to Buffalo, Lockport and Tonawanda. When the electricity reached Buffalo in November 1896, it proved two things at once: alternating current could carry power over long distances, and a river could light a city.","How does a hydroelectric station actually work? Imagine a dam as a wall that holds back a deep lake called a reservoir. Near the bottom of the dam, big pipes called penstocks let water rush downhill. The water slams into the curved blades of a turbine, which is a kind of super-efficient water wheel, and spins it very fast. The turbine is connected by a thick steel shaft to a generator, where magnets whirl past coils of copper wire and Faraday's induction does the rest. Two things decide how much power you get: how much water flows each second, and how far it falls. Engineers call that height the 'head'. A small amount of water falling a long way can be just as useful as a huge river falling a short way.","Today the biggest power station of any kind on Earth is a hydroelectric dam: the Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze River in Hubei province, central China. Construction began in December 1994, the first generators started in 2003, and the full plant was running by 2012. The concrete wall stands roughly 180 metres high and stretches about 2.3 kilometres across the valley. Inside are 32 main generators of 700 megawatts each, plus two smaller ones that power the dam itself, for a total of 22,500 megawatts. In an average year it produces somewhere around 95 to 100 terawatt-hours of electricity, and in its record year of 2020 it made about 112.","Other giants follow close behind: Baihetan, also in China, at about 16,000 megawatts, and Itaipu on the border between Brazil and Paraguay at about 14,000. Altogether, hydropower makes roughly 14 to 15 percent of the world's electricity. For more than a century it made more than all the other renewable sources added together, until wind and solar together finally overtook it in 2024. It has a special talent, too. Operators can open or close the gates and change the output within minutes, so dams are excellent at filling gaps when demand suddenly jumps.","Big dams have a serious cost, and it is fair to say so. The Three Gorges reservoir flooded towns, farms and ancient sites, and somewhere between about 1.1 and 1.4 million people had to move to new homes. Around the world, dams are estimated to have displaced tens of millions of people. Dams also block fish from swimming upriver, trap the mud and sand that rivers normally carry to the sea, and in hot countries rotting plants in a reservoir can release methane, a gas that warms the planet."]},{"heading":"Catching the Wind: Blyth, Brush and the Giants","paragraphs":["Windmills are old too. People in Persia were using wind to grind grain more than a thousand years ago, and the Netherlands became famous for windmills that pumped water off low-lying land. But making electricity from the wind had to wait for Faraday's generator, and it happened, as so often in this story, in two places at almost the same moment.","In July 1887 James Blyth, a professor at Anderson's College in Glasgow, built a strange wooden machine in the garden of his holiday cottage in Marykirk, in the Scottish county of Kincardineshire. It stood on a tripod and had four arms about 13 feet (4 metres) long covered in canvas sails, like a small windmill. Instead of grinding flour, it drove a dynamo using a rope wrapped around a flywheel. The dynamo charged accumulators, which are rechargeable batteries, and those lit the cottage. In a moderate breeze it could light ten small bulbs. Blyth's cottage is usually counted as the first house in the world lit by wind-made electricity.","Blyth later offered his spare electricity to light the main street of Marykirk. The story goes that the villagers said no, because they thought electricity was 'the work of the devil'. That tale has been repeated for over a century, so treat it as a much-loved local story rather than a carefully recorded fact. Blyth did not give up. In 1895 a Glasgow firm built an improved version of his turbine that gave emergency power to the hospital and asylum in Montrose, and it kept working for many years.","Meanwhile, across the Atlantic, Charles Brush, the arc-lighting inventor you met lighting up Cleveland's Public Square in 1879, was building a monster in his own back garden on Euclid Avenue in Cleveland. Finished in 1888, Brush's wind turbine had a wheel about 17 metres (56 feet) across, packed with 144 thin blades, sitting on top of an 18-metre tower. All those blades made it turn slowly but powerfully. Inside, the wind turned a 12-kilowatt dynamo that charged a roomful of batteries in his basement. Those batteries ran around 100 incandescent bulbs, a few arc lamps and motors in his laboratory. The machine worked for years, until about 1900, and was finally abandoned once Cleveland's own power stations could do the job more cheaply.","In Denmark, a teacher named Poul la Cour took the idea further. In 1891 he built a wind turbine at the Askov Folk High School, and by 1895 his windmill was lighting the whole village of Askov. He trained students to build and look after wind turbines, and Denmark became the country most famous for wind power. In 1957 a Danish engineer called Johannes Juul built the Gedser turbine, with three blades facing into the wind, which became the model for almost every big wind turbine built since.","A modern wind turbine works on the same idea as Brush's, just far bigger and smarter. The wind pushes on long, gently twisted blades shaped like aeroplane wings. They turn a central hub slowly, perhaps 10 to 15 times a minute for a big machine. In many designs a gearbox speeds up that rotation for the generator; others use giant 'direct-drive' generators with no gearbox at all. Everything sits in a box called the nacelle at the top of the tower, and motors turn the whole head to face the wind. There is a natural limit, too: no turbine can ever capture more than about 59 percent of the energy in the wind, because if it stopped the air completely, no new air could flow through. This is called the Betz limit.","And the size! An adult blue whale, the largest animal that has ever lived, can be around 30 metres long. Today's biggest onshore turbines have single blades well over 100 metres long, and the largest offshore machines, built in China, have rotors about 310 metres across, with blades roughly 150 metres long, and can produce about 26 megawatts each. One of those blades is longer than four blue whales nose to tail. Because the wind is stronger and steadier at sea, many of these giants stand in wind farms offshore, in places like the North Sea, where Britain and Denmark now get a large share of their electricity from the wind."]},{"heading":"Sunlight into Electricity: From a Teenager's Lab to Outer Space","paragraphs":["Hydro and wind both work by spinning a generator. Solar cells are different. They have no moving parts at all. Light goes in, electricity comes out, silently. The story of how anyone discovered this begins in Paris in 1839, in a laboratory run by the physicist Antoine César Becquerel. His son Edmond was just 19 years old. Edmond put silver chloride into an acidic liquid, dipped in two platinum electrodes, and shone light on the setup. A small voltage and current appeared, and they changed when the light changed. He had found the photovoltaic effect, from 'photo' meaning light and 'volt' after Alessandro Volta.","For decades the effect was a curiosity. In 1883 the American inventor Charles Fritts in New York coated the element selenium with a very thin layer of gold and made what is often called the first solid solar cell. It worked, but it was only about 1 percent efficient, meaning that 99 percent of the sunlight's energy was wasted. That was far too poor to power anything useful, though Fritts was bold enough to imagine rooftops of solar panels one day.","The real breakthrough came from a telephone company. At Bell Telephone Laboratories in New Jersey, the engineer Daryl Chapin was trying to find a way to power telephone equipment in remote, humid places such as the tropics, where ordinary batteries quickly failed. He tested selenium and found it hopeless. Meanwhile, two of his colleagues, the chemist Calvin Fuller and the physicist Gerald Pearson, were experimenting with specially treated silicon, the same element found in sand. They discovered that their silicon made electricity when light fell on it. The three teamed up, and on 25 April 1954 Bell Labs showed off its 'solar battery'. It was about 6 percent efficient, roughly six times better than Fritts' cell. A strip of these cells could deliver roughly 60 watts for every square metre of bright sunshine, and newspapers were thrilled by the idea of power pouring straight out of the Sun. The team described their work in a scientific paper the very next month.","How does a silicon cell do it? A solar cell is a thin sandwich of two slightly different kinds of silicon. One layer has a few extra electrons mixed in; the other has a few 'holes', places where an electron is missing. Where the two layers meet, called the p-n junction, a built-in electric field forms, a little like a slope. When a particle of light, called a photon, hits the silicon, it can knock an electron loose, leaving a hole behind. The slope pushes the electron one way and the hole the other. If you connect the two sides with a wire, the electrons flow round the circuit, through your lamp or phone charger, and back again. It is the LED from Part 18 running in reverse: an LED turns electricity into light, while a solar cell turns light into electricity.","At first, solar cells were fantastically expensive, far too costly for houses. But there was one customer who did not mind the price: space. On 17 March 1958 the United States launched Vanguard 1, a shiny aluminium ball only about 15 centimetres across and weighing about 1.5 kilograms. It carried six small solar cells powering a radio transmitter. Its ordinary battery-powered transmitter fell silent after about 20 days, but the solar-powered one kept sending signals for more than six years, until 1964. Vanguard 1 was the first satellite with solar power, and it is still circling the Earth today, the oldest human-made object in orbit. Since then, nearly every satellite and space station, including the International Space Station with its huge golden-brown wings, has run on sunlight.","Back on Earth, efficiency slowly climbed. Ordinary rooftop panels today turn around 20 percent of sunlight into electricity, the best silicon cells in laboratories reach about 26 to 27 percent, and special stacked cells used under concentrated light have passed 47 percent. But the biggest change was not efficiency. It was price, as you will see at the end of this chapter."]},{"heading":"Heat from Inside the Atom: Nuclear Power","paragraphs":["In the 1930s and 1940s, scientists learned that the nuclei of certain heavy atoms, such as uranium, could be split apart. This splitting is called fission. When a uranium nucleus splits, it releases a burst of energy and a couple of tiny particles called neutrons, which can fly off and split more nuclei, which release more neutrons, and so on. If this chain reaction is carefully controlled, it produces a steady, enormous amount of heat from a very small amount of fuel.","Here is a surprise: a nuclear power station is, at heart, a fancy kettle. The heat from fission boils water (or heats it under pressure), the steam spins a turbine, and the turbine spins a generator, exactly like the coal-fired stations you met in Part 15, which used Charles Parsons' steam turbine. The difference is the fire. Instead of burning coal, which pours carbon dioxide into the air, the reactor makes heat from splitting atoms, which releases almost no carbon dioxide while it runs.","The first nuclear power plant to send electricity into a public grid was at Obninsk, a science town about 100 kilometres south-west of Moscow in the Soviet Union. Its reactor, called AM-1, was connected to the grid in late June 1954. It was small, producing about 5 megawatts of electricity, enough for a small town, but it showed the idea worked. Remarkably, it kept running in one form or another until 2002. Two years later, on 17 October 1956, Queen Elizabeth II opened Calder Hall in Cumbria, England, the first full-scale nuclear power station. It was built mainly to produce plutonium for Britain's nuclear weapons, but it also sent large amounts of electricity into the grid. Calder Hall ran for nearly 47 years, closing in 2003.","Nuclear power spread to many countries during the 1960s and 1970s. Today it provides roughly a tenth of the world's electricity, and in France it supplies the majority. Its supporters point out that it runs day and night whatever the weather, and that it is one of the lowest-carbon ways to make electricity. Together, renewables and nuclear now produce almost 40 percent of the world's electricity.","But nuclear power has real problems too, and people argue about it strongly. The used fuel stays dangerously radioactive for thousands of years, so it must be stored very safely, and countries are still building the deep underground stores meant to hold it for good. Nuclear plants are also very expensive and slow to build. And when things go badly wrong, the results can be serious.","In the early hours of 26 April 1986, during a badly designed safety test, reactor number 4 at the Chernobyl power plant, near the town of Pripyat in what is now Ukraine, ran out of control and exploded. It was the worst nuclear accident in history. Two workers died on the night, and 28 more, mostly firefighters and plant staff, died from radiation sickness within a few months. Radioactive dust spread across Europe, and around 135,000 people were eventually moved away from their homes for good.","Twenty-five years later, on 11 March 2011, a magnitude 9.0 earthquake shook the sea floor off north-eastern Japan. About 50 minutes later a tsunami up to about 14 metres high swept over the sea wall at the Fukushima Daiichi plant, knocking out the backup power that kept the reactors cool. Fuel in several reactors overheated and melted, and hydrogen explosions blew apart reactor buildings. More than 160,000 people left the area. The earthquake and tsunami together killed about 18,000 people along the coast, but very few, if any, deaths have been linked to radiation from the plant; the stress and disruption of the evacuation caused much more harm. After Fukushima, Germany decided to close all its nuclear plants, while other countries, such as France, China and the United Kingdom, kept building. Whether nuclear power should be part of the clean-energy future is still one of the great debates of our time."]},{"heading":"Saving Sunshine for a Rainy Day: Storing Electricity","paragraphs":["Coal, gas and nuclear plants can run whenever you like. The Sun and wind cannot be switched on. The Sun sets every evening, clouds roll in, and some days the wind simply stops. Remember from Part 15 that a grid must match supply and demand every single second. So if more and more of our power comes from wind and sunshine, we need ways to save electricity when there is too much and release it when there is too little.","The oldest and still the biggest way to do this is called pumped storage hydroelectricity. It needs two lakes, one high up a hill and one at the bottom. When electricity is cheap and plentiful, perhaps on a windy night, huge reversible machines pump water up to the top lake. When everyone gets home, switches on the oven and turns on the television, the water is let back down through the same machines, which now work as turbines and generators. You get back about 70 to 80 percent of the energy you put in. The first known pumped storage plant opened in 1907 near Schaffhausen in Switzerland. The largest today is at Fengning in China, at about 3,600 megawatts. In Wales, the Dinorwig station is hidden inside a mountain in an old slate quarry; when its machines are already spinning, it can go from zero to full power, about 1,700 megawatts, in well under a minute. Pumped storage still makes up the great majority of the world's grid storage.","The newcomer is the battery, and in particular the lithium-ion battery that is almost certainly inside the phone or tablet nearest to you. Its story is a relay race of its own. In the 1970s, during an oil crisis, the British-born chemist M. Stanley Whittingham, working for the oil company Exxon, made a rechargeable battery in which lithium ions slipped in and out of layers of titanium disulfide. In 1980 John Goodenough's team at the University of Oxford found that lithium cobalt oxide made a much more powerful positive side. Then in 1985 Akira Yoshino in Japan replaced the risky lithium metal on the other side with a form of carbon, making the battery far safer than designs that used pure lithium metal, which can catch fire. Sony began selling lithium-ion batteries in 1991.","In 2019 the three men shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry. John Goodenough was 97 years old, the oldest person ever to win a Nobel Prize. Since 1991 the price of lithium-ion cells has fallen by about 99 percent. That is why electric cars became affordable, and why power companies now build whole fields of shipping-container-sized battery packs next to solar and wind farms. These can soak up the midday sunshine and release it in the evening rush. By 2025 there were roughly 267 gigawatts of grid battery storage around the world, and the number is shooting upward every year."]},{"heading":"Edison's Current Gets a Second Chance","paragraphs":["In Part 14 you watched alternating current win the War of the Currents, mostly because transformers made it easy to raise AC to high voltages for long journeys. Thomas Edison's direct current seemed to have lost for good. But engineers kept thinking about DC, because for some jobs it has real advantages.","The trick was converting electricity back and forth. You can use transformers to step AC up to a very high voltage, then use special equipment called a converter to turn it into DC, send it hundreds or thousands of kilometres, and turn it back into AC at the other end. An early attempt came from the Swiss engineer René Thury, whose system in Italy, working by 1889, used chains of spinning machines to make high-voltage DC. It worked but wasted energy and needed constant repairs.","The modern era began in Sweden. Engineers at the company ASEA, led by Uno Lamm, spent years developing mercury-arc valves, glass and steel devices that could switch huge currents in one direction only. In 1954 they opened a link from Västervik on the Swedish mainland to the island of Gotland in the Baltic Sea: a cable 98 kilometres long, lying on the sea floor, carrying 20 megawatts at 100,000 volts of DC. It is counted as the world's first fully commercial high-voltage direct current, or HVDC, connection.","Why use DC under the sea? A long AC cable acts a bit like a giant Leyden jar from Part 3. Every time the current reverses, which is 50 times a second in Europe, the cable has to be charged up again, and that wastes so much of the cable's capacity that beyond several dozen kilometres an undersea AC cable becomes very inefficient. DC flows steadily one way, so this problem disappears. Over very long distances on land, HVDC lines also lose less energy than AC lines of the same voltage, roughly 3.5 percent per 1,000 kilometres compared with about 6.7 percent. And DC can link two grids that are not in step with each other, even ones running at different frequencies, like Japan's 50-hertz east and 60-hertz west.","Today HVDC is everywhere you cannot see it. Cables under the North Sea carry Norwegian hydropower to Britain and send British wind power back. In China, the Zhundong to South Anhui line carries power more than 3,000 kilometres at 1,100,000 volts, from coal and wind in the far west to cities in the east. Edison would probably smile: his current did not win the war, but it found a job no one else could do."]},{"heading":"The Great Price Crash","paragraphs":["For most of the twentieth century, solar and wind power were thought of as nice ideas that were simply too expensive. Then something extraordinary happened. The price of solar panels fell, and fell, and kept on falling. In 1976 a solar module cost about 106 US dollars for each watt of power it could make. By 2019 it cost about 38 cents, a drop of more than 99.6 percent. Between 2010 and 2019 alone, the price of electricity from new solar farms fell by about 89 percent, and electricity from wind by about 70 percent.","Why? Mostly because of a pattern called a learning curve. Every time the world's total number of solar panels doubled, factories got better at making them, machines got faster, materials got cheaper, and the price of panels fell by about 20 percent. The same thing happened with wind turbines and with batteries. The more we built, the cheaper they got, and the cheaper they got, the more we built. In many parts of the world, a new solar or wind farm is now the cheapest way to make electricity that has ever existed.","In 2023, renewable sources, mainly hydro, wind and solar, produced about 30 percent of all the world's electricity for the first time, with wind and solar together making about 13 percent. Coal is still the biggest single source, and in many countries it is still growing, so there is a long way to go. But the direction of travel has changed. On sunny afternoons, some places, such as South Australia and California, now sometimes get almost all their power from the Sun and wind.","Think how far we have come. Edmond Becquerel's tiny voltage in 1839, Fritts' 1 percent gold-and-selenium cell, Blyth's canvas sails in a Scottish garden, Brush's 144-bladed wheel, a 6 percent silicon cell built for telephones, a little ball in space: each step looked small at the time. Put together, they are changing how a whole planet makes its electricity. So here is a question to carry into the final part of our story: when you are grown up, what will your electric world look like?"]}],"deepDive":"Why did solar get so cheap so fast while nuclear did not? Energy economists point to modularity. A solar farm is built from millions of identical, factory-made panels, and a battery park from thousands of identical cells, so manufacturers repeat the same process billions of times and keep finding small improvements. Our World in Data, using decades of price data, estimates a learning rate of about 20 percent for solar modules: each doubling of all the panels ever made cut the module price by roughly a fifth, from about 106 dollars per watt in 1976 to about 0.38 dollars by 2019. Onshore wind shows a learning rate of about 23 percent, and battery prices have followed a similarly steep curve, falling about 97 percent in three decades. Large one-off projects such as nuclear stations or giant dams are built only a few at a time, often to new designs, and costs have frequently risen rather than fallen. The catch with cheap solar and wind is that their output is variable, which engineers describe with the 'capacity factor': a solar farm might produce, averaged over a year, only 10 to 25 percent of its maximum rating, because of night, clouds and seasons, while a nuclear plant often runs above 80 percent. Grid planners therefore talk about 'firming' renewables with storage, long-distance HVDC links that move power from where the wind is blowing to where it is not, flexible hydro, and demand that can shift in time. Grid studies suggest that grids with under about 40 percent variable renewables mostly need short-term storage, but at about 80 percent, storage lasting many hours to days becomes essential. There is also a materials debate: batteries need lithium, cobalt and nickel, and mining them has environmental and human-rights costs, which is one reason researchers are working on cobalt-free chemistries such as lithium iron phosphate and on sodium-ion batteries. Finally, large hydro is renewable but not impact-free: estimates of people displaced by dams worldwide run to the tens of millions, and tropical reservoirs can emit methane. 'Renewable' and 'harmless' are not the same word, and good energy planning compares the full costs of every option.","funFact":"Vanguard 1, launched in 1958 with six solar cells, is still orbiting the Earth today, making it the oldest human-made object in space.","check":{"q":"Where was the first fully commercial high-voltage direct current (HVDC) link opened in 1954?","a":"Between mainland Sweden and the island of Gotland","wrong":["Between Niagara Falls and Buffalo","Across the English Channel from France to England","From the Three Gorges Dam to Shanghai"]},"sources":[{"title":"Three Gorges Dam","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three_Gorges_Dam"},{"title":"Hydroelectricity","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydroelectricity"},{"title":"History of wind power","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_wind_power"},{"title":"Daryl Chapin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daryl_Chapin"},{"title":"Vanguard 1","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanguard_1"},{"title":"HVDC Gotland","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HVDC_Gotland"},{"title":"Why did renewables become so cheap so fast?","publisher":"Our World in Data","url":"https://ourworldindata.org/cheap-renewables-growth"},{"title":"Global Electricity Review 2024","publisher":"Ember","url":"https://ember-energy.org/latest-insights/global-electricity-review-2024/"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/19-1.jpg","alt":"The Three Gorges Dam on China's Yangtze River, the world's biggest power station.","file":"ThreeGorgesDam-China2009.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ThreeGorgesDam-China2009.jpg","author":"Source file: Le Grand Portage Derivative work: Rehman","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/19-2.jpg","alt":"Charles Brush's giant many-bladed wind turbine in Cleveland, which lit his house from 1888.","file":"Wind turbine 1888 Charles Brush.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wind_turbine_1888_Charles_Brush.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/19-3.jpg","alt":"The backup of Vanguard 1, the small ball satellite with antennas and solar cell panels, at a Smithsonian museum.","file":"Vanguard 1 satellite (flight backup) - Udvar-Hazy Center.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vanguard_1_satellite_(flight_backup)_-_Udvar-Hazy_Center.JPG","author":"Daderot","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4387},{"n":20,"title":"The Electric Future — and the Whole Story","when":"Today – the future","summary":"Tomorrow's cars, heaters and even light bulbs will all talk to the grid and run on electricity. Scientists are trying to copy the Sun's power in tiny bright flashes, and to bring light to millions of families who still have none. And every time you flip a switch, you are joining a relay race that started with a piece of rubbed amber!","sections":[{"heading":"When the Wires Learn to Talk: The Smart Grid","paragraphs":["Picture the electricity grid your great-grandparents knew. Power flowed one way only: from a big power station, along high-voltage lines, through substations and transformers, down the street and into the house. Once a month or so, a person came round to read the meter, a little box with spinning dials, and the family got a bill. The grid had no idea who was using what, minute by minute. Engineers at the power station simply watched the frequency and the demand and turned generators up or down to keep everything balanced, just as you saw in Part 15.","That old one-way grid is now changing into what engineers call a smart grid. The big idea is that information, not just electricity, flows in both directions. Smart meters in homes send readings automatically, often every half-hour or even every few seconds, so the power company can see exactly when people use electricity, and families can see it on a little screen or a phone app. Sensors along the power lines can spot a fault, such as a tree branch falling on a wire, and switch the power around the broken section automatically. Engineers call this 'self-healing', and it can turn a long blackout into a flicker.","Electricity can now flow both ways, too. Millions of homes have solar panels on their roofs. On a sunny afternoon, when the family is out at school and work, those panels make more power than the house needs, and the spare electricity flows backward out of the house and into the street's wires for the neighbours to use. A house can be a customer in the evening and a tiny power station at lunchtime.","A smart grid also helps with the problem you met in Part 19: the Sun and wind cannot be switched on when we want them. Instead of only changing supply to match demand, a smart grid can gently nudge demand to match supply. This is called demand response. Electricity can be cheaper when there is plenty of wind and sunshine and more expensive at the busy evening peak, so a smart washing machine, dishwasher or car charger can wait and run at the cheapest, cleanest time. Some power companies can even ask thousands of air conditioners or water heaters to pause for a few minutes during an emergency, and nobody notices.","Then there are smart bulbs, the great-great-grandchildren of Swan's and Edison's lamps. Inside is an LED like the ones in Part 18, plus a tiny radio chip. You can dim them, change their colour from warm candle-orange to cool daylight-blue, set them to switch off at bedtime, or ask a voice assistant to turn them on. They can talk to each other and to other devices in the house. Imagine explaining that to Joseph Swan, who had to fight just to keep a carbon thread glowing for a few hours.","Smart grids have critics, and their worries are fair. A meter that records your electricity use every few minutes can reveal when you are home, asleep or away on holiday, so privacy matters. Anything connected to a network can be attacked by hackers, so cybersecurity is a huge concern for power companies. Engineers today spend as much time thinking about computer security as Edison's team spent thinking about fuses."]},{"heading":"Electric Everything: Cars, Heat Pumps and the Plug-In World","paragraphs":["Here is a twist in our story. For a hundred years, electricity mostly ran lights, motors, machines and gadgets, while cars ran on petrol and most homes were heated by burning gas, oil, coal or wood. Now, all over the world, people are switching those jobs over to electricity too. Engineers call this electrification, and it means the grid will need to carry much more power in the future than it does today.","Electric cars are not new. Inventors were building electric carriages in the 1830s, and in 1881 the French inventor Gustave Trouvé showed a small electric vehicle in Paris. By 1897 electric taxis were driving around London and New York. They were quiet and did not smell, but their heavy lead-acid batteries ran out quickly, and cheap petrol and Henry Ford's mass-produced cars soon pushed them aside. For most of the twentieth century, electric cars were a curiosity.","What brought them back was the lithium-ion battery from Part 19, which got lighter, safer and much cheaper. In 2025, about 21 million plug-in electric cars were sold around the world, more than a quarter of all new cars. In some countries, such as Norway, almost every new car sold is now electric. China builds and buys more electric cars than anywhere else.","Electric cars are also much more efficient. A petrol engine turns only about 15 percent of the energy in its fuel into movement; the rest mostly escapes as heat from the engine and exhaust pipe. An electric car gets roughly three quarters or more of the energy in its battery to the wheels. Its motor is a direct descendant of Faraday's spinning wire from 1821 and Tesla's induction motor from 1888.","The other big change is the heat pump. A heat pump is basically a refrigerator working backwards. A fridge pulls heat out of its cold inside and dumps it into your kitchen through the coils at the back. A heat pump pulls heat out of the outdoor air, the ground or a lake, even on a cold day, and pumps it into your house. A special liquid called a refrigerant soaks up warmth outside and boils into a gas, an electric compressor squeezes that gas so it gets hotter, and the hot gas gives up its heat inside the house before going round again.","The amazing part is that a heat pump does not make heat; it moves it. For every unit of electricity it uses, a good air-source heat pump can deliver around three or four units of heat into the home. An ordinary electric heater can only ever give one unit of heat for one unit of electricity. Heat pumps provide only about a tenth of the world's heating today, but in the United States they now outsell gas furnaces, and many countries are encouraging them as a way to cut pollution. Add electric cars, electric buses, electric trains and electric factories, and you can see why many experts say the twenty-first century will be even more electric than the twentieth."]},{"heading":"Big Ideas in the Laboratory: Starlight, Supercold Wires and Giant Batteries","paragraphs":["The Sun makes its energy by fusion: squeezing tiny hydrogen nuclei together so hard and so hot that they join and release energy. Scientists have dreamed for over seventy years of doing this on Earth. Fusion fuel can be made from forms of hydrogen found in seawater and from lithium, it releases no carbon dioxide, and it cannot run away in a chain reaction the way a fission reactor can. The problem is that it needs temperatures of many millions of degrees, hotter than the centre of the Sun.","On 5 December 2022, at the National Ignition Facility, or NIF, at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory in California, scientists fired 192 giant laser beams at a target smaller than a pea. The lasers delivered 2.05 megajoules of energy into a small gold cylinder, which turned it into X-rays that crushed a tiny frozen fuel capsule inside. For a split second the capsule produced 3.15 megajoules of fusion energy, more than the laser energy that went in. Scientists call this 'ignition', and they had been chasing it for decades.","It was a real milestone, but it is important to understand what it did not do. The lasers themselves needed far more electricity from the grid than the experiment released, and NIF fires only a few shots a day; a power plant would need many shots every second, for years. Meanwhile, in southern France, about 35 countries, organised as seven members (China, the European Union, India, Japan, Russia, South Korea and the United States), are building ITER, a giant ring-shaped machine called a tokamak that holds super-hot gas in place with powerful magnets. Its goal is to produce 500 megawatts of fusion heat from 50 megawatts of heating, ten times more out than in. Its current plan is to begin full fusion experiments in the late 2030s. Fusion power stations, if they work, are probably still decades away.","Another dream is the superconductor. In 1911, the Dutch physicist Heike Kamerlingh Onnes cooled mercury to about minus 269 degrees Celsius, just above the coldest possible temperature, and found that its electrical resistance vanished completely. Not just small: zero. A current set going in a superconducting loop can keep flowing with no battery at all. In 1986 and 1987, scientists discovered new materials, ceramic compounds containing copper, that become superconductors at about minus 180 degrees Celsius, warm enough to be cooled with cheap liquid nitrogen. Superconducting magnets already sit inside every hospital MRI scanner and inside particle accelerators. Engineers have also built short test power cables from superconductors, which can carry several times more power than copper cables of the same size, but the cooling makes them expensive. A superconductor that worked at room temperature would transform the grid. Every few years someone claims to have found one, and so far every claim has failed when other scientists checked it.","The third big idea is storage on a giant scale. Engineers are building ever larger battery parks, and testing new chemistries such as sodium-ion batteries, which use cheap sodium from salt instead of lithium. Others are experimenting with storing energy as heat in hot sand or rocks, as compressed air in underground caves, or by using spare electricity to split water into hydrogen, Nicholson and Carlisle's experiment from Part 4 now done with whole wind farms. Nobody knows yet which ideas will win, which is exactly what makes this such an exciting time to be a young scientist."]},{"heading":"Power for Everyone: The Last 666 Million","paragraphs":["It is easy to think everyone in the world has electricity. They do not. According to the World Bank and its partners, in 2023 about 666 million people still had no electricity at home. That is more than the populations of the United States, Brazil and Germany put together. About 565 million of them, roughly 85 percent, live in sub-Saharan Africa. In countries such as South Sudan, Chad, Burundi and Malawi, most families still live without power.","There has been real progress. In 2010 well over a billion people had no electricity, so hundreds of millions have been connected since. India alone brought power to many millions of rural homes during the 2010s. But the last people are the hardest to reach. They often live in villages far from any big power line, scattered across mountains, forests or deserts, and many are poor, so building a long high-voltage line to reach them can cost far more than they could ever pay back.","Imagine being a child in one of these homes. When the Sun sets, around six in the evening near the equator, your family might light a kerosene lamp, like the ones in Part 1, which is smoky, smelly and a fire risk, or a candle, or a cheap battery torch. Doing homework is hard. The local health clinic may not be able to keep vaccines cold in a fridge, or run a light for a baby born at night. A mobile phone, which many families do have, might need a long walk to a shop with a generator just to be charged.","This is where the price crash you saw in Part 19 matters most. Solar panels, LEDs and lithium batteries have become so cheap and so efficient that a single small panel on a roof, charging a battery, can now run several bright LED lamps, charge phones and power a radio or small television. These are called solar home systems. In East Africa and elsewhere, families can often pay for them in small amounts using their mobile phones, a bit at a time, like topping up a phone plan.","For whole villages, engineers build mini-grids: a little local grid, often running on a field of solar panels with batteries and sometimes a small hydro turbine or diesel backup, wired to houses, shops, schools and clinics nearby. By 2022 mini-grids served about 48 million people worldwide. It is the story of Godalming and Pearl Street from Part 12 happening again, only this time powered by sunshine. Between 2020 and 2022, small local renewable systems like these provided more than half of all new electricity connections in sub-Saharan Africa, and experts expect off-grid solar to be the cheapest way to reach about four in ten of the people who still have no power.","Bringing electricity to everyone is one of the United Nations' Sustainable Development Goals, with a target date of 2030. On current trends the world will probably miss it, but every year the numbers move in the right direction. Some of the most important electrical engineering of the twenty-first century is not happening in famous laboratories, but on rooftops in villages most of us will never visit."]},{"heading":"What You Can Do: Be a Careful, Curious Electrician of Life","paragraphs":["You do not have to wait until you are grown up to be part of this story. Start with saving energy. Every unit of electricity you do not waste is one that no power station has to make. Switch off lights when you leave a room. Turn off games consoles and computers properly instead of leaving them on standby all night. If your family is buying new bulbs, choose LEDs, which, as you saw in Part 18, use about 75 percent less energy than old incandescent bulbs and last many times longer. Shut the fridge door quickly, and dry clothes on a line when the weather is good.","Next, stay safe. Electricity is just as powerful as it was when Georg Wilhelm Richmann was killed in 1753 studying lightning. Never poke anything into a socket. Keep water and electrical things apart: no phones charging in the bath, and no touching switches with wet hands. Never climb pylons, trees near power lines, or fences around substations, and never fly a kite or a drone anywhere near overhead lines. If you ever see a fallen power line, stay well away and tell an adult to call the emergency services. And when a thunderstorm comes, follow the old safety saying, 'When thunder roars, go indoors.' Remember that Franklin's kite was never actually hit by lightning; if it had been, he would very likely not have survived.","Most importantly, stay curious. Michael Faraday left school at about 13 with almost no maths. He was a bookbinder's apprentice who read the science books he was binding, went to Humphry Davy's lectures with free tickets, wrote out beautiful notes, and asked for a job. He went on to discover electromagnetic induction, the idea behind every generator on Earth, and he started the Royal Institution Christmas Lectures for young people, which still run today. You can do what he did: ask questions, test ideas with simple experiments, write down what you see, and do not be afraid to be wrong. Edmond Becquerel was 19 when he discovered the photovoltaic effect. Curiosity has no minimum age."]},{"heading":"The Whole Story, from Amber to LEDs","paragraphs":["Before we end, let us walk back through the whole journey, like strolling through the rooms of a museum, one gallery for each great step.","In the first room, people sit around oil lamps, tallow candles and later gas jets, squinting at their work and worrying about fire. In the next, an ancient Greek, traditionally said to be Thales, rubs a lump of amber, the stone the Greeks called elektron, and watches bits of straw leap up to it. Centuries later, William Gilbert, the Queen's physician, gives the effect a name in 1600. Otto von Guericke spins a ball of sulfur to make sparks, Stephen Gray sends electricity along a thread more than 200 metres long, and Charles du Fay finds that there are two kinds of charge.","In the third room, a jar of water gives Pieter van Musschenbroek the shock of his life: the Leyden jar can store electricity. Benjamin Franklin names positive and negative charge and plans his famous experiments, and in 1752 a French team and Franklin's kite show that lightning is electricity. Then, in Bologna, a frog's leg twitches, Luigi Galvani and Alessandro Volta argue politely, and in 1800 Volta builds his pile of zinc and copper discs: the first battery and the first steady electric current.","Next door, Humphry Davy makes a blazing arc between two charcoal rods, Hans Christian Ørsted watches a compass needle swing beside a wire in 1820, and Michael Faraday makes a wire spin round a magnet in 1821. On 29 August 1831, Faraday winds two coils on an iron ring and discovers electromagnetic induction. Motion can make electricity. Maxwell turns his ideas into equations, and Hertz catches invisible radio waves.","Then electricity goes to work. Telegraph wires carry messages across countries and under oceans. Dynamos by Siemens and Gramme make strong, steady current, and Yablochkov's candles and Brush's arc lamps light up Paris and Cleveland. Dozens of inventors, from de la Rue and de Moleyns to Lodygin, Woodward and Evans, chase a gentle glowing filament, and Sprengel's pump gives them the vacuum they need. In 1878 and 1879 Joseph Swan in Newcastle and Thomas Edison's team at Menlo Park both get carbon filaments to glow. Swan lights his home, Cragside and the Savoy Theatre, while Edison's team, including Lewis Latimer, builds a whole system of lamps, sockets, fuses, meters and generators.","In the next gallery, Pearl Street Station hums into life in New York on 4 September 1882. But direct current cannot travel far, so transformers, the Ganz engineers, Stanley, Westinghouse and Tesla bring alternating current, and the War of the Currents rages until Niagara's power reaches Buffalo in 1896. Ferranti, Parsons and many others link power stations into grids that reach right across countries. Lifts, trams, vacuum cleaners, fridges, radios and X-ray machines change daily life. Then come the costs: shocks, smog, blackouts, light pollution and a warming climate. Engineers fight back with neon and fluorescent tubes, then with the LED, finally made white by the blue LEDs of Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura. And in the newest room, dams, wind turbines, solar cells, nuclear plants, batteries and HVDC cables reshape the way the world makes its power."]},{"heading":"A Relay Race Across the Centuries","paragraphs":["Look at that list again and notice something. There is no single inventor of electricity, and there is no single inventor of the light bulb. Thales did not finish the job, and neither did Franklin, Volta, Faraday, Swan, Edison or Tesla. Each of them picked up the baton from someone earlier, ran their stretch of the race, and handed it on.","Notice, too, how many countries took part: ancient Greece, England, Scotland, Germany, the Netherlands, France, Italy, Denmark, America, Russia, Serbia and Croatia, Hungary, Belgium, Sweden, Japan, China and many more. Some of the runners were famous professors. Others were a dyer, a bookbinder's apprentice, a pharmacist, a draftsman whose parents had escaped slavery, a teenager working in his father's laboratory. Some made fortunes, and some died poor. Some, like Galvani and Volta, or Edison and Swan, argued fiercely, then turned out to have each been partly right, or ended up working together.","Many great ideas arrived in two places at once: Kleist and Musschenbroek with the Leyden jar, Faraday and Henry with induction, Swan and Edison with the bulb, Tesla and Ferraris with the induction motor, Blyth and Brush with wind power. That is not a coincidence. When enough people share their discoveries, write them down and publish them, the next step becomes possible for anyone clever and hard-working enough to take it. Science is a conversation that lasts for centuries.","Now, wherever you are reading this, find a light switch. Put your finger on it. Before you press it, think for a moment about what is waiting on the other side: copper wires inside the wall, a fuse or circuit breaker, a meter, a cable under the street, a transformer, a substation, high-voltage lines striding across the hills, a spinning turbine or a field of solar panels, and behind them all, two and a half thousand years of curious people asking 'what happens if...?'","Click. The room fills with light, instantly, silently, without a flame. For one second, remember the Greek with his amber, Franklin with his kite in the storm, Volta with his stack of coins, Faraday with his iron ring, Swan and Edison with their glowing threads, and all the engineers, miners, line workers and scientists whose names never made it into the history books. They made that light possible. And somewhere, perhaps reading these very words, is the person who will carry the baton next."]}],"deepDive":"Engineers sometimes say the twenty-first century challenge is 'electrify everything, and clean the electricity'. The logic is about efficiency as much as pollution. An internal-combustion car turns only around 15 percent of its fuel energy into motion, while an electric car delivers well over three quarters of its battery energy to the wheels; a heat pump with a coefficient of performance (COP) of 3 to 4 delivers three or four units of heat per unit of electricity, while burning gas can never give more heat than the fuel contains. So switching to electricity can cut total energy demand even before the power is clean. The difficulty is the grid. If cars and heating move to electricity, peak demand could rise sharply, especially on cold, still winter evenings when solar output is zero and wind may be low. Planners therefore combine several tools: more transmission (including HVDC) to share power between regions, storage from minutes (batteries) to seasons (perhaps hydrogen or large hydro), smart charging and demand response that shift flexible loads, and firm low-carbon sources such as hydro, geothermal or nuclear. Fusion is the most hyped possibility. The December 2022 NIF result reached a target gain of about 1.5 (3.15 MJ out for 2.05 MJ of laser energy), but the lasers drew vastly more energy from the grid, and a power plant would need high gain, repetition many times per second, and materials that survive intense neutron bombardment for years. Magnetic-confinement machines like ITER aim for Q = 10 but are not designed to make electricity. Most physicists therefore do not expect fusion to make a large contribution to world electricity before the second half of this century, although private companies are attempting faster timelines. Superconductors raise another debate: high-temperature superconducting cables exist, but cryogenic cooling and cost confine them to special cases, and claims of room-temperature superconductivity, such as the much-publicised LK-99 material in 2023, have not survived independent testing. Finally, energy access remains the fairness question: with about 666 million people still unconnected in 2023, the goal of universal access by 2030 looks likely to be missed, and decentralised solar is now central to most plans to close the gap.","funFact":"When the National Ignition Facility achieved fusion ignition in December 2022, its 192 laser beams were all focused on a target only about the size of a peppercorn.","check":{"q":"About how many units of heat can a good air-source heat pump deliver for every unit of electricity it uses?","a":"About three or four","wrong":["Exactly one, like an electric heater","About one half","About fifty"]},"sources":[{"title":"Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory achieves fusion ignition","publisher":"Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory","url":"https://www.llnl.gov/article/49306/lawrence-livermore-national-laboratory-achieves-fusion-ignition"},{"title":"Tracking SDG 7: The Energy Progress Report 2025","publisher":"World Bank","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/energy/publication/tracking-sdg-7-the-energy-progress-report-2025"},{"title":"ITER","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ITER"},{"title":"Superconductivity","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superconductivity"},{"title":"Smart grid","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smart_grid"},{"title":"Heat pump","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heat_pump"},{"title":"Electric car","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_car"},{"title":"Mini-grid","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mini-grid"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/electricity/20-1.jpg","alt":"Inside the National Ignition Facility's target chamber, with the pointed target positioner and many laser ports.","file":"National Ignition Facility's target chamber.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Ignition_Facility%27s_target_chamber.jpg","author":"Lawrence Livermore National Security","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/20-2.jpg","alt":"Workers fit solar panels on the roof of Nyanza Primary School in Uganda as pupils watch.","file":"Nyanza.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nyanza.jpg","author":"Lior Sperandeo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/electricity/20-3.jpg","alt":"A plug-in car charging at a public street charging point beside a canal in Amsterdam.","file":"Amsterdam car charging station, for two cars, Oudezijds Voorburgwal, 2 July 2014.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amsterdam_car_charging_station,_for_two_cars,_Oudezijds_Voorburgwal,_2_July_2014.jpg","author":"AxelBoldt","license":"CC0"}],"words":3797}],"words":81195};
